// Scenario Lab asset library — Handbook of Research Methods in Social and Personality Psychology, 3rd ed. (Reis, West & Judd, 2024)
const _AL_research_methods = {"id":"research_methods","title":"Handbook of Research Methods in Social and Personality Psychology, 3rd ed. (Reis, West & Judd, 2024)","chapters":[{"id":"rm00","title":"Introduction","pages":"1-3","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"The Third Edition of the Handbook","visual_frame_description":"You stand at a bookshelf in a campus office, holding the thick third edition while the faded 2000 and 2014 volumes sit open on the desk. A browser on the desk screen shows the Cambridge University Press website listing freely available chapters from the second edition.","player_mission":"Your mission is to introduce a newcomer to the Handbook of Research Methods in Social and Personality Psychology by explaining why a third edition exists, what it promises, and how it differs from the earlier editions. Walk through the book's purpose, the decision process behind topic selection, and what readers can expect from the current chapters.","key_concepts":["The Handbook of Research Methods in Social and Personality Psychology is now in its third edition.","The first two editions of the handbook were published in 2000 and 2014.","The first two editions of the handbook played an important role in widening access to and utilization of cutting-edge methods in the field.","The science of personality and social psychology never sleeps when it comes to developing new and improved research methods.","The third edition was designed to capture some of the most influential and promising new methodological advances in the field.","The third edition covers both traditional methodological topics that have seen advances in recent years and novel approaches of recent vintage.","The editors chose content they believed would be most relevant to the largest proportion of new scholars in the field.","Topics omitted from the third edition are not necessarily less important than the ones included.","With the help of Cambridge University Press and the consent of the authors, several still-timely chapters from the second edition were made freely available on the publisher's website.","The freely available chapters from the second edition should be considered essential elements in a survey of the field's methodological state of the art.","The freely available chapters from the second edition might well be incorporated into a methods-course syllabus."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Methods and Theory: Two Sides of the Same Coin","visual_frame_description":"You sit across a table from a skeptical senior scholar, a shiny coin resting between you with 'method' on one face and 'theory' on the other. Over your shoulder, a chalkboard carries Lewin's dictum and a chart of relationship research timelines marked by multilevel modeling breakthroughs.","player_mission":"Your mission is to explain how methods and theory feed each other, using Greenwald's observations about Nobel awards and Lewin's famous dictum as anchors. Show how new methods open doors to questions and how a good theoretical question should lead the researcher, not a trendy method.","key_concepts":["Methods are the bread and butter of science: more than just defining what is possible, they suggest what can be learned.","Much of what appears in journals today asks questions that could not have been imagined, much less investigated, by earlier researchers.","Tony Greenwald's 2012 observation that there is a much greater frequency of Nobel science awards for contributions to method than for contributions to theory still rings true.","Methods open the door to questions, and questions make it possible to advance theory.","The methods in the handbook are not intended to replace theories, but rather to provide a guide to optimally test them.","Most chapters in the handbook toggle between theory and method, presenting them as two sides of the same coin.","The history of psychology is full of examples of theories advancing with the advent of new methods, and vice versa.","Relationships scientists were able to develop and test complex theories of how partners influence one another over time once advancements were made in multilevel modeling generally, and dyadic analysis more specifically.","Those advances in multilevel modeling and dyadic analysis happened out of necessity, once scholars realized the importance of handling issues such as nonindependence of data properly.","Without properly handling nonindependence of data, it was easy to make mistakes in drawing conclusions from the data.","Leading with a good theoretical question, especially one that may lead to useful applications, is good practice.","Methodological approaches change all of the time, but, to quote Lewin's well-known dictum, there is nothing so practical as a good theory."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Taking Measurement Into the Field","visual_frame_description":"You sit in a sunlit park café, auditing a researcher's field kit: a smartwatch recording cardiovascular data, a phone app with dashboards, and a tablet streaming live heart-rate traces from a participant jogging past the fountain. A map pins study sites from the classroom to the operating room.","player_mission":"Your mission is to audit how psychophysiologic measurement has moved out of the laboratory and into everyday settings. Catalogue what new tools enable researchers to measure, where the measurements now happen, and what big questions the age of massive data has made testable.","key_concepts":["Advances in psychophysiologic measurement have enabled researchers to move beyond the lab to the field to collect cardiovascular data using watches and phone apps.","Questions such as how basic stressors impact people's health across multiple social contexts and in multiple countries can now be studied in a more ecologically valid manner.","With psychophysiologic tools being widely available, more and more researchers can now think even bigger about the contexts in which they can study physiological processes, from the classroom to the operating room.","Since the prior edition of the handbook, the age of massive data began, a point made clearly in Kosinski's chapter in this handbook.","Scholars can now test big questions with huge multinational samples, such as how emotions spread throughout social networks.","Massive data now allow scholars to test questions such as who we are attracted to and what gives rise to misinformation contagion.","Parts I and II of the handbook introduce readers to basic issues in the science and design of research.","Parts I and II of the handbook might be considered what you need to know before starting research.","Parts III and IV of the handbook introduce the reader to new methodological and statistical procedures while considering the role of theory development in their application.","The handbook urges readers to ask whether a proposed method will be the best available to test their question.","The handbook urges readers to ask whether an analysis strategy will allow them to ask what they need to ask to advance their theory."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Navigating the Handbook's Architecture","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a large four-column wall chart, each column a part of the handbook color-coded to a stage of a researcher's journey, from a student's first lecture hall to a laboratory bench where data analysis is underway. Arrows show Parts I and II as a fixed path and Parts III and IV as modular detours.","player_mission":"Your mission is to map the four parts of the handbook onto the stages of a researcher's journey, from first-year graduate student to data analyst. Explain what each part covers, in what order it should be read, and why the later parts are organized by breadth rather than sequence.","key_concepts":["When deciding how to organize the handbook, the editors adopted the perspective of a new researcher with little experience conducting or running studies.","The handbook is designed to guide the reader from the earliest stages of thinking about research, when foundational issues of ethics and replication are top of mind, through the design, implementation, and analysis stages.","The handbook deliberately starts out broad, addressing big-picture issues relevant to all new scholars interested in the study of personality and social psychology, regardless of topic.","The first part of the handbook, foundational issues in psychological science, covers basic issues around ethics in conducting research, replication, how to conduct team science, and how to conduct research that captures multicultural and multinational samples.","The editors recommend reading the first part of the handbook first, and it requires no prior knowledge or expertise in conducting experimental research.","The second part of the book, basic methodological considerations, covers the basics of design and validity, beginning with essential concepts of validity and experimentation.","The second part of the book moves on to quasi-experimental design and field methods, which take the researcher out of the lab and into uncharted real-world territory.","The third part of the handbook assumes a basic knowledge of experimental method and design, along with consideration of ethics and replication.","In Part III, the authors provide a deep dive into several popular methodological approaches at the forefront of cutting-edge research.","Part III chapters need not be read in order, but rather can be turned to as the researcher begins to think through different methodological options.","The fourth and final part is aimed at the reader who has designed a study and collected the data, and is now ready for data analysis."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Keeping Up in an Era of Rapid Change","visual_frame_description":"You sit at a curved control desk in a dim data center, wall screens streaming live feeds of AI-generated survey items, R and Python code windows, and neural-net training curves. A junior researcher in the chair beside you watches a chatbot draft a results section, looking both awed and adrift.","player_mission":"Your mission is to advise a novice researcher on how to stay afloat as methodological and statistical advances accelerate. Weigh the promise of generative AI, machine learning, and data-driven approaches against the risk of running code without understanding it, and defend why the classic building blocks still anchor the field.","key_concepts":["One challenge all researchers face is that methodological and statistical advances are happening at a rapid, and perhaps expanding, pace.","The three editors struggled to create a handbook that is both cutting-edge in the approaches covered and likely to stand the test of time.","The editors' goal is to expose the reader to as many methods as possible while also covering the tried and true principles of methodological skill building.","As the handbook was being finalized, the advent of generative AI happened.","AI tools will shape not only the types of question scientists can ask, but also how they go about developing materials, collecting and analyzing data, and even writing up scientific findings.","Issues of ethics will surely come up as generative AI tools develop.","Machine learning and computational modeling, topics covered in Part IV, allow scholars to examine dynamic processes that unfold naturally over time.","Using data-driven statistical approaches can help circumvent some of the ethical issues around questionable research practices that have entered the zeitgeist since the prior edition of the handbook.","Most analyses these days are conducted using packages in R, Python, MPlus, and SPSS, many of which can be run with little knowledge of what is going on under the hood.","It can be all too easy to run code without knowing what it does, and fighting this temptation is essential as you develop your methodological and analytical skills.","The classic issues of psychometrics and study design, such as principles of construct validity and scale construction, are essential to learn during this time of constant change.","Classic knowledge of psychometrics and design will anchor your understanding of methodological developments and give you a more critical eye when encountering something new."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm01","title":"Chapter 1: The Romance of Research Methods","pages":"4-14","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Scrub the Lineage of Method: From Aristotle's Logic to the Baconian Turn","visual_frame_description":"A time-scrubber playhead glides along a hall of ideas: Aristotle lecturing on logic, Archimedes at his lever, Bacon's Novum Organum on a printing press, and a campfire circle of ancestors. Definition cards of romance from Lexico and Merriam-Webster float past, while a guitar strums Eddie van Halen's chasing sounds in the background.","player_mission":"Your mission is to scrub through the history of scientific method that Banaji traces back to Aristotle, Archimedes, and Francis Bacon, decoding what she means by the romance of research methods. Then walk the PSP chase itself: define what personality and social psychologists study and why it is among the hardest stuff to chase and bring back for others to see.","key_concepts":["Banaji opens her essay with dictionary definitions of romance: a quality or feeling of mystery, excitement, and remoteness from everyday life (Lexico), and an attraction or appeal to the emotions (Merriam-Webster).","Banaji's use of the word romance has nothing to do with romantic love or falling in love with science, but is meant to capture a profound attraction to the scientific method based on uncontainable curiosity.","Epistemological inquiry, an understanding of how knowledge may be acquired and what constitutes a claim to knowledge, connects researchers across time and place who engage in the production of knowledge.","The research methods psychologists use every day can be traced at a deep level at least to Aristotle's teachings of logic and systematic observation and to Archimedes' dare about a lever long enough.","Francis Bacon's Novum Organum pushed out dogma and ushered in the modern idea of the scientific method.","Bacon's idea of an imposed inductive process is not intuitive; it is an acquired taste that most people in the world have not had the opportunity or inclination to develop.","Scientists are rebels by nature or training, dissatisfied with things as they are and committed to expressing their curiosity in a manner that is set up to prove their strongest beliefs wrong.","Banaji describes the work of research as chasing, echoing guitarist Eddie van Halen, who said innocently of his guitar virtuosity, I was just chasing sounds.","Personality and social psychology (PSP) is the science of understanding humans as individual selves and as social beings, and nothing in this chase has a clearly marked path or an end point.","What PSP studies can never be seen directly because it has no physical format, being mental representation: perception and memory, the process of inference, and the thoughts and feelings unfolding in complex social contexts.","Murray Gell-Mann captured the difficulty of the field with the comment, think how hard physics would be if particles could think.","In psychology's data, behavior is the dry stuff while neural activity and physiology are the wet stuff, and both types of data are indirect proxies of what researchers ultimately wish to know about the mind."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Dialogue on the Grandma Question: Transparency and Shared Method","visual_frame_description":"A Yale seminar room with a colleague across the table raising the grandmother question while a projection screen scrolls an old NSF conference poster, laptops running experimental software, and journal policy notices removing word limits, with open-science badges floating over the papers.","player_mission":"Your mission is to stage a debate with a Yale colleague who asks why anyone's grandmother should care about a dissertation, and to defend deep expertise in abstract methods while demanding transparency. Then reconstruct the NSF laptop conference where colleagues ran one another's computerized experiments, and audit how open-science and journal policies encourage sharing procedures across labs.","key_concepts":["William James phrased the psychologist's unique problem as the knower is also the known, while comedian Emo Philips noted that the brain tells us it is the most wonderful organ in the body, then made us realize who was telling us this.","Because the thing being understood is also the thing doing the understanding, psychology needs established methods of research and protections built into them that will keep us honest.","Banaji urges the goal of developing methods that can be shared across laboratories, methods whose components are transparent, and methods whose data from experiments are available for anybody to analyze.","There is no alternative to a diversity of scientists using the same method to examine the same questions.","Banaji intervened when colleagues asked why Suzie's grandmother should care about a thesis, asking instead why that should be a requirement of the work of a Ph.D. thesis.","Banaji agrees researchers should make what they do as transparent and their thinking and writing as lucid as possible, but their work is rooted in technically sophisticated methods developed by generations of scientists.","At an NSF-supported conference in the years when laptops were newly invented, participants brought their computerized experimental procedures for each other to try and served as actual subjects in each other's experiments.","Running one another's experiments revealed where labs were united in understanding, where differences arose, and the extra little instructions and technical nuances that could account for disparities in the data.","The study of implicit cognition uniquely offered the experience of being subjects in one another's experiments, and the processes being tapped proved reliable and robust enough that certain variations did not seem to matter at all.","The open-science movement encourages the dual goals of greater sharing of procedures and materials and greater transparency.","Journal policies have changed for the better, especially the removal of word limits for methods and results sections of empirical reports that are available with full details in supplemental materials.","The more accepted and routine a research procedure becomes, the more invisible it becomes to the expert, which is why versions of procedures must be handed over to other labs whose intuitions differ from our own."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Audit the Sleeper Effect: Where Theory Obstructs Progress","visual_frame_description":"An audit room with a timeline of sleeper effect studies across different labs, each tagged for whether the effect appeared; a red flag drops on the content-cue dissociation theory while a rejection letter reading does not sufficiently advance theory is stamped, and a chip flips between Lewin's dictum and Greenwald's 2012 variation.","player_mission":"Your mission is to audit the rise and fall of the sleeper effect research program, from Hovland, Lumsdaine, and Sheffield (1949) to the Greenwald et al. (1986) verdict of unnecessary inefficiency. Then audit the placement-of-theory lesson: where theory must be set aside in the research cycle, the Lewinian and Greenwald dicta, Kuhn's normal science, and the rejection-letter formula your work does not sufficiently advance theory.","key_concepts":["The sleeper effect is the result that a persuasive message produced greater attitude change over time rather than immediately.","The interpretation of the sleeper effect claimed it occurred because of a dissociation between the content of the message and the discounting cue, such as a low-credibility source.","The sleeper effect was first reported by Hovland, Lumsdaine, and Sheffield in 1949, and dozens of experiments across different labs followed without a clear understanding of when the effect appeared and when it did not.","Greenwald and colleagues (1986) concluded that the effort to study the sleeper effect had been unnecessarily inefficient and meandering because scientists were mired in the theory of differential rates of fading in memory of the message relative to the discounting cue.","Watching seasoned research scientists stumble on the sleeper effect taught Banaji that theory was deeply important but that where it drives research and where it needs to be set aside required explicit recognition.","Banaji concluded that the lesson was the placement of theory: reducing the burden of theory in the design and testing phases of the research cycle may ultimately, even more effectively, advance theory.","Anthony Greenwald (2012) framed the lesson as a variation on the original Lewinian adage: there is nothing so theoretical as a good method.","The common misunderstanding of the 1986 conclusion is that theory is unimportant, when the authors' actual point concerned the placement of theory in the research cycle.","Under normal science in the Kuhnian sense, experiments simply build on previous work, and Banaji's early papers were competent and well executed yet rejected with the sentence your work does not sufficiently advance theory.","In cover letters to journals such as JPSP, Banaji offered that a paper was a report of empirical discoveries that offered no notable advance to theory, and no editor ever desk-rejected a paper on those grounds.","Banaji's spouse, an engineer and computer scientist by training, asked in the 1980s why a publication in her field was treated as a reward rather than a report.","The situation in publishing is far better today than four decades ago, which Banaji credits to methods having gained in strength and to research procedures utilized by multiple labs rather than a single lab."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Matrix Match: Tool Revolutions versus Concept Revolutions","visual_frame_description":"A wall matrix with two columns labeled CONCEPT and TOOL; cards fly into place, among them Kuhn's The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, Popper's logic of discovery, solar power, genetic engineering, worldwide connectivity, Thurstone's 1928 paper, a reaction-time implicit measure, a hotel towel card, and a vaccine syringe, while a counter ticks six versus twenty at the top.","player_mission":"Your mission is to build a two-column matrix contrasting Dyson's tool-based revolutions with concept-based revolutions championed by Kuhn and Popper, and to drop each evidence card into its cell: the six concept revolutions versus twenty tool revolutions, the sun, the genome, and the internet, Thurstone's measurement of attitudes, implicit cognition, and the COVID-era demonstration of humble methods. Then place the persuasion card: individuals and communities versus mandates.","key_concepts":["Freeman Dyson (2015) pointed out that Thomas Kuhn's The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1970) did the disservice of elevating the status of theory above all else in science.","Philosophers of science such as Karl Popper (1959) further reinforced theory fixation with brilliant writing focused on concept-based revolutions.","Dyson's view, which Banaji is sympathetic to, is that revolutions in methods or tools are more commonplace than revolutions in theory and more likely to produce both advances in science and salubrious effects on society.","Dyson counted six major concept-based revolutions in the physical sciences, including Freud among them, against twenty tool-based revolutions.","In The Sun, the Genome, and the Internet (2000), Dyson focused on solar energy, genetic engineering, and worldwide connectivity as technologies that each ushered in numerous scientific discoveries while aiding a more just and equitable society.","The humble methods of psychology, the simple experiment that introduces a manipulation and seeks to understand subjective experience by asking what people think and feel and why, have the potential to inform, educate, and change a society for the better.","L. L. Thurstone's 1928 paper Attitudes Can Be Measured introduced the method of equal-appearing intervals for attitude measurement and bristled at the orthodoxy that attitudes, beliefs, and opinions simply could not be measured.","Banaji paid homage to Thurstone with her essay Implicit Attitudes Can Be Measured (2001), and applications of new methods to implicit cognition have given a clearer view of the nature of attitudes and beliefs, conscious and less conscious.","Van Bavel and colleagues (2020) published a COVID-era review of knowledge on threat perception, emotion and risk taking, social norms, and inequality, whose subtext was that intense focus on research methods lets us take the simplest thing, how a question is posed, and show its formulation is not simple at all.","Goldstein and Cialdini (2008) showed that hotel guests can be motivated to skip fresh linen for the sake of environmental conservation when the question is asked in the right, counterintuitive way.","During the pandemic, false beliefs about vaccines and masks, spread by social influence, cost thousands more lives than necessary, showing that physical and biological technologies are of little use unless mind and behavior co-operate.","In freer, more democratic societies, persuasion at the level of individuals and communities with free access to social media is the harder job, compared with a place where millions of people in a city will dutifully line up to take a vaccine when ordered."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Science of Methods","topic":"Boss Level: The Romance of a Lifetime, Becoming a Methodologist","visual_frame_description":"A boss arena on a railway platform in India: a station bookstall with five volumes of the 1968 Handbook of Social Psychology, a train window showing the countryside passing, and then a hall of giants, Milgram, Festinger, Mischel, and others, with a medieval map of Here be dragons unfurling overhead while Feynman's The Pleasure of Finding Things Out glows on a pedestal at the end.","player_mission":"Your mission is to confront the final boss, the question of whether you will join the tribe of researchers and develop an identity with the enterprise of measurement itself. Retrace Banaji's origin story from the railway station in India to the train ride to Secunderabad, weigh the classics that prove human intuition cannot be trusted, and deliver your verdict on why a chapter on research methods is the romance of a lifetime.","key_concepts":["Banaji entered graduate school in social psychology at Ohio State without background in the field, having taken mostly psychophysics and learning theory and having done one experiment on family members using a memory drum.","On a trip in India, Banaji bought five volumes of the Handbook of Social Psychology (1968), edited by Gardner Lindzey and Elliot Aronson, at a railway station after bargaining the price down to a dollar a volume.","On the long train ride from New Delhi to Secunderabad, the only chapter Banaji could grasp was Elliot Aronson's chapter on research methods in social psychology, while the theory chapters made no sense to her whatsoever.","The descriptions of methods and the dramas that made up the experiments were fascinating and mind-blowing to Banaji, who could not believe a tribe of people had the nerve to measure the most complex mental and social processes under controlled conditions.","When Banaji got off that train she knew with a rare conviction what she had to do, even though the chances of success were slim, and she credits a chapter on research methods as the reason she became a social psychologist.","Banaji focused disproportionately in her career on a method that did not exist when she entered the field, and understanding and honing it was among the most intellectually fulfilling experiences of her life.","The great romance of research methods is the belief that the mind can be measured.","The classics of the field offer the most incontrovertible evidence of why we must remain skeptical of human intuition: Milgram's experiments, Latane and Darley's bystander research, Festinger's self-deceptive rationalizations, and Gilbert and Wilson's evidence that we are no good at knowing what our future selves will desire.","The map of the discipline resembles medieval maps with entire unexplored regions marked with the warning Here be dragons, because in PSP the questions for study themselves are being discovered.","Methods of research, as creations of limited information processors called humans, can lead us to wrong answers, but iterating through imperfections and failures is what makes for the romance.","Studying research methods gives the knowledge to choose the right tool and the confidence to bring order to a mess of data, and also brings the many joys of finding things out, echoing Richard Feynman's The Pleasure of Finding Things Out, first published in 1999.","Banaji suggests developing an identity not necessarily with a single method but with the enterprise of measurement itself, to experience the romance of creating and improving the very instruments of exploration and discovery."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm02","title":"Chapter 2: Ethical Issues in Psychological Science: Studying Humans, Analyzing Data, Publishing Findings","pages":"17-43","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Research Ethics","topic":"Scrub the History of Ethical Abuse: From Tuskegee to the Nuremberg Code","visual_frame_description":"A scrubber playhead glides along a dark timeline of research scandals: a 1932 Alabama clinic door labeled 'bad blood,' a hypobaric chamber and ice bath from the Nazi camps, an 1840s operating theater without anesthetic, Milgram's shock generator, and the Stanford basement jail with its prison cells. As each event passes, a Green commission report, the Nuremberg Code scroll, and the Belmont report rise out of the timeline to light up the next era of protection.","player_mission":"Your mission is to scrub through the history of human research ethics, tracing how each scandal - the Tuskegee syphilis study, Nazi medical experiments, J. Marion Sims' gynecology, Milgram's obedience shocks, and Zimbardo's Stanford prison - spurred positive developments in participant protection. Then reconstruct the Nuremberg trials, the Green commission's three-point code, and the National Research Act that produced the Belmont report, and place every event on the timeline of ethical reform.","key_concepts":["Science is a social process: to engage in science, psychologists and other scientists must systematically collect data, analyze and draw conclusions from their data, and share their results with other scientists and the public (Merton, 1942).","In 1932, the U.S. Public Health Service began the Tuskegee study of the course of syphilis in about 600 African American men in Alabama, of whom roughly 400 had latent syphilis and 200 served as comparison controls, and the men were told it was a study of 'bad blood.'","When penicillin became an accepted treatment about fifteen years after the Tuskegee study began, it was withheld from the participants, and researchers convinced local doctors not to treat the men, so that by 1972, when the story reached the media, dozens of men had died and many wives and children had been infected (Reverby, 2012).","From 1933 until the end of World War II, Nazi doctors carried out medical experiments on prisoners, including mass sterilization of Jews, exposure to high altitudes without oxygen, freezing prisoners to develop hypothermia treatments, experimental bone-grafting, and exposure to mustard and phosgene gas (Mitscherlich & Mielke, 1949).","Modern gynecology began in the 1840s with the Alabama practice of James Sims, who invented the speculum and pioneered a surgical technique to repair tears between the uterus and bladder, but the foundation of this medical research was the use of enslaved women who were operated on without anesthetic (Ojanuga, 1993).","In the early 1960s, men recruited from southern Connecticut for a 'scientific study of memory and learning' were actually in Stanley Milgram's famous obedience experiments, where they came to believe they were delivering painful electric shocks to a man in another room, and although no actual shocks were delivered, subjects were observed to sweat, tremble, stutter, groan, and dig their fingernails into their flesh (Milgram, 1963).","In 1971, Zimbardo's Stanford prison experiment recruited male college students with a newspaper advertisement offering fifteen dollars a day (about 110 dollars today) to participate in a 'psychological study of prison life,' and the guards, with encouragement from the researchers, engaged in verbal abuse, humiliation, and sleep deprivation of the prisoners.","In the Stanford prison experiment, the 'prisoners' continued submitting in large part because they thought they would lose their pay, and by the fifth day Zimbardo had released four out of ten prisoners because of 'extreme depression, disorganized thinking, uncontrollable crying and fits of rage' (Zimbardo et al., 1973, 48).","The Nuremberg trials after World War II tried Nazi medical researchers who had subjected concentration camp prisoners to unnecessary surgery, ice baths, simulated high altitudes, and chemical and biological warfare agents, and it was suggested that scientists behaved unethically 'because their scientific curiosity submerged their morals' (Ivy, 1949, 132).","When the Nazi doctors pointed to ongoing American research at an Illinois prison in which malaria was injected into prisoners, physician A. C. Ivy hastily formed the 'Green commission,' which developed a three-point code of medical research ethics requiring informed consent, rigorous experiments on animals preceding human experimentation, and research carried out by qualified scientists trained to minimize injuries, disability, or death (Green, 1948).","Ivy's testimony at the Nuremberg trials was adopted by judges and medical experts in framing the Nuremberg Code, the pioneering international ethical code for medical research, whose first principle, informed consent, is the central organizing factor worldwide in ethical review of both medical and behavioral sciences.","In the United States, a systematic governmental approach to research ethics began in 1974 with the National Research Act, which created the National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects of Biomedical and Behavioral Research, and in 1978 this group released the Belmont report, named after the Belmont Conference Center in Elkridge, Maryland."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Research Ethics","topic":"Dialogue on Informed Consent: Disclosure, Comprehension, and Voluntariness","visual_frame_description":"A seminar room dialogue scene with two chairs facing each other, while a consent form scrolls on the wall between them listing research purposes, procedures, duration, risks, benefits, confidentiality, and voluntary participation. A balance scale tips as cards labeled disclosure, comprehension, and voluntariness appear, and a browser window opens and closes to show an online participant exiting without debriefing.","player_mission":"Your mission is to stage a dialogue with a skeptical colleague about what informed consent really requires, walking through the three core components of information, comprehension, and voluntariness, and the U.S. federal regulations that govern consent documents. Then debate the hard cases: deception and debriefing, waivers of consent, competence in vulnerable populations, and whether payment can ever become coercion.","key_concepts":["Informed consent is a process of communication between the researcher and the prospective participant in which explicit agreement to participate is sought after disclosure of important details about the study, including procedures, risks, and benefits.","In U.S. federal regulations that govern IRBs, consent documents must include the explicit statement that the study involves research, along with descriptions of the purposes, the procedures, the expected duration, the risks or discomforts and benefits, the extent to which confidentiality is maintained, and a statement that participation is voluntary throughout, since discontinuation can occur at any time (45 CFR 46.116).","The three core components of consent are information (disclosure of key elements of the research, including procedures, risks, and benefits), comprehension (presumed competence of the participants to make the decision whether or not to participate), and voluntariness at every stage of research participation (Gupta, 2013).","To be informed enough to properly give consent, potential participants must know what will happen in the research, such as stress, pain, embarrassment, or potential emotional triggers, and while not every fact must be revealed, a specific description of what will occur is necessary as it relates to potential harm in the research experience.","Scientists are sometimes reluctant to disclose too much because research hypotheses may be revealed and change participants' responses, and the use of deception or omission of information may therefore enhance the internal and external validity of research (see Korn, 1997).","U.S. federal guidelines stipulate that informed-consent documents with incomplete disclosure, or even waivers of informed consent, may be approved by IRBs if the research involves no more than minimal risk, if the deception does not 'adversely affect the rights and welfare of the subjects,' if the research could not 'practicably be carried out' without deception, and if full debriefing follows.","Informed-consent forms themselves may never contain false information, and the ethical guidelines of the APA further suggest that participants be permitted to withdraw their data once they have been informed of the deception they faced.","Because online participants can end a study session simply by closing their browsers, it may be impossible for researchers to ensure that debriefing has occurred, and an online research participant upset by negative false feedback may simply shut down, with any residual ill effects never countered with information from the researchers.","Decision-making capacity for consent is the ability to understand the information provided, including procedures, risks, and benefits, so that an informed decision about participation can be made, and it requires cognitive and emotional ability and the freedom to decide without undue pressure (Biros, 2018).","Many kinds of participants do not have presumed competence, including minors, people who are institutionalized or imprisoned, people with mental illness or temporary emotional disturbance, participants who are intoxicated, and students in classrooms where the instructor or TA is conducting the research.","The Nuremberg Code (1947) declares that the voluntary consent of the human subject is absolutely essential, requiring that the person have legal capacity to consent, be so situated as to exercise free power of choice without force, fraud, deceit, duress, or other constraint or coercion, and have sufficient knowledge and comprehension to make an understanding and enlightened decision.","Compensation in some studies may be too high to truly allow for a voluntary decision to participate, as in the example of 500 dollars to complete a five-minute survey, and while federal regulations do not give clear guidance on compensation levels, minimizing the possibility of coercion or undue influence is key (45 CFR 46.116)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Research Ethics","topic":"Audit the Belmont Principles: Respect for Persons, Beneficence, and Justice","visual_frame_description":"An audit room with three giant filing cabinets labeled RESPECT, BENEFICENCE, and JUSTICE, each receiving stamped documents while an IRB review board deliberates at a long table. A large world map flickers with dots concentrated in North America to illustrate WEIRD samples, while a scale weighs risks against benefits and a red stamp slams down on sample exclusions that lack justification.","player_mission":"Your mission is to audit the Belmont report's three foundational principles - respect for persons, beneficence, and justice - and check each research practice card against them, from autonomy and informed consent to risk-benefit analysis and equitable representation. Then audit the enforcement machinery: IRBs, the Common Rule (45 CFR 46), its 2018 update, and the WEIRD-sample problem that threatens the justice principle.","key_concepts":["The Belmont report provided a foundation of ethical guidelines for human-subjects research, identifying three fundamental principles: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice (National Commission, 1978).","The Belmont report is distinguished from other medically grounded research ethics statements often invoked in publishing, such as the Helsinki declaration, by its explicit applicability to behavioral research.","Respect for persons requires that prospective research participants be recognized as 'autonomous agents' capable of thinking about their own goals and making deliberative decisions about what shall or shall not happen to them, and this respect is the key justification for requiring informed consent.","The Belmont report and U.S. federal regulations recognize that not every person is capable of self-determination, so younger children, people with cognitive or mental disabilities, the temporarily incapacitated, and institutionalized people or prisoners not only require extra protection but at times may not be considered autonomous agents.","Beneficence means 'doing or producing good,' and in the context of research it means we must maximize potential benefits and minimize possible risks or harms to participants.","Any potential harm to participants - physical, emotional, social, reputational, or legal - should receive careful scrutiny by IRBs, and harms include loss of confidentiality and privacy, an increasing concern with the use of online methods to collect data.","Ethical research may involve a certain amount of risk, but these risks must be compared to the potential benefits to the participant and/or to science or society, and career advancement, payment, and/or credit toward course requirements are not considered benefits.","The principle of justice obliges equitable representation in research, such that the burdens and benefits of research are fairly distributed, requiring inclusion in research as well as protection from being burdened with the risks of research.","The Belmont report states that the selection of research subjects needs to be scrutinized to determine whether some classes of people, such as particular racial and ethnic minorities or persons confined to institutions, are being systematically selected simply because of their easy availability, their compromised position, or their manipulability, rather than for reasons directly related to the problem being studied.","Recruitment of research participants in social and personality psychology is heavily skewed toward easy-to-access samples, such as middle-class college students and online samples who are often North American English speakers or otherwise from Western, educated, industrialized, rich, and democratic (WEIRD) cultures (Henrich et al., 2010).","A case in point of injustice is the underrepresentation of women in cardiovascular clinical trials, despite the fact that cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death for women, and the basic design of everyday objects such as the size of cellphones and the seating alignment in cars is also based on men's bodies (Criado-Perez, 2021).","In the U.S., the Office for Human Research Protections in the Department of Health and Human Services administers the 'Common Rule' (45 CFR 46), which regulates human subjects' protections and details ethical conduct of human research, and the Common Rule was updated in 2018 in a process overseen by social psychologist Susan Fiske."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Research Ethics","topic":"Matrix Match: Data Ethics versus Publishing Reforms","visual_frame_description":"A wall matrix with two columns labeled DATA ETHICS and PUBLISHING ETHICS; cards fly into place, among them a locked server rack, Rosnow and Rosenthal's five principles, a promise to destroy raw data from an old consent form, Simmons's p-hacking graph, the twenty-one-word statement, an open data repository, an OSF preregistration page, and a Stage 1 registered report. A verdict banner scrolls non-reproducibility percentages across the top.","player_mission":"Your mission is to build a two-column matrix sorting the chapter's reforms into Data Ethics (how data are stored, analyzed, and shared) and Publishing Ethics (how results are reported), placing each evidence card in its cell: confidentiality, the five principles of ethical data analysis, the APA's prohibition of errors of omission, p-hacking, disclosure statements, open data, preregistration, and registered reports. Then weigh the evidence on whether each reform actually works in practice.","key_concepts":["Ethical principles in research involve both the avoidance of harm to participants by respecting their confidentiality and the duty to share the results of research completely with other scientists, and these issues carry through the entire research process, from planning to reporting.","The APA's Ethical Principles in the Conduct of Research with Human Participants (Standard 6.02) states that psychologists maintain confidentiality in creating, storing, accessing, transferring, and disposing of records, and that they use coding or other techniques to avoid the inclusion of personal identifiers.","Because identification of participants through combinations of personal information is sometimes possible in surprising ways with access to online resources, researchers should apply the latest anonymization protocols and keep data under password protection, ideally offline or encrypted, prior to the release of carefully anonymized data (Narayanan & Shmatikov, 2008).","Rosnow and Rosenthal (2011) describe five ethical principles of data analysis and reporting: analyses should be transparent, informative, precise, accurate, and grounded, meaning the method is appropriate to the hypotheses and the analyses appropriately test the stated hypothesis.","Since at least the fourth edition (1994), the APA publication manual has prohibited errors of omission, stating that 'psychologists do not omit troublesome observations from their reports so as to present a more convincing story,' a regulation linked to the ethical code's Standard 5.01a, Avoidance of False or Deceptive Statements.","Simmons et al. (2011) gave the name 'p-hacking' to the technique of gaming the unwritten rule that strong evidence consisted of significant results supporting the hypothesis across multiple studies, and showed that, applied creatively, it could boost a patently null population finding into a false-positive rate of about 60 percent.","Bem's (2011) article demonstrating precognitive phenomena was based on nine supportive studies in the highly selective journal JPSP, but subsequent investigation revealed that those nine studies were only the best results selected from scores of attempted studies (Engber, 2017).","Simmons et al. (2011) advocated a 'twenty-one-word statement' (later revised to eighteen words) that authors could affirm in their articles: 'We report how we determined our sample size, all data exclusions (if any), all manipulations, and all measures in the study.'","The drive to require open data and materials is one of the most universally adopted changes to social psychology publishing in the past decade, and default open-data policies materially increase provision of open data links, from negligible rates of about 1 percent to 87 percent and 55 percent in two pairs of comparable journals (Nuijten et al., 2017).","Responses to requests for data from those who publish in psychology journals have rarely topped 50 percent, with Wicherts et al. (2006) receiving data sets from only 27 percent of requests, Vanpaemel et al. (2015) from 38 percent, and Hardwicke and Ioannidis (2018) from 32 percent.","Preregistration, the practice of registering methods and analyses in a document available through a public repository before running a study, has been proposed in psychology since 2011 to counteract selective data analysis that increases the false-positive problem, and online tools such as the Open Science Framework and aspredicted.org have greatly facilitated the practice.","A registered report is simply a preregistration that is peer-reviewed as a journal article, with introduction, method, and, in place of results, an analysis plan, and if editors and reviewers deem the Stage 1 manuscript suitable, authors are guaranteed 'in-principle acceptance' regardless of results, provided the final Stage 2 version faithfully follows the registration plan (Chambers & Tzavella, 2022; Nosek & Lakens, 2014)."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Research Ethics","topic":"Boss Level: Pending Ethical Issues and the Future of Research Ethics","visual_frame_description":"A boss arena shaped like a tribunal table with case folders labeled with the pending questions, each folder glowing with unresolved options on either side. Behind the tribunal, a wall mural shows the three phases of science - collecting data, analyzing it, and sharing results - while badges and disclosure statements rain down over journals, and a gavel slams down on the final verdict: accuracy, access, dignity, justice, representation, and respect.","player_mission":"Your mission is to confront the final boss, the unresolved ethical dilemmas listed in Table 2.2, and decide where you stand on each: whether citizens have a duty to participate in research, whether results must generalize across cultures and groups, whether open review should replace anonymity, and which economic model for publishing is most ethical. Then deliver your verdict on whether transparency norms can be internalized, and defend the chapter's summary claim that a more ethical science is a better science.","key_concepts":["One pending ethical question is whether social implications, such as those of race and gender differences, should be considered when evaluating proposed and completed research, a question that hinges on whether egalitarian values should be seen as foundational to the profession or merely a political standpoint that should not impede research.","Whether participation in ethically vetted and publicly disseminated research should be considered a duty of citizens is contested because research is a regulated public good, but the argument, more prevalent in Europe than in the U.S., has to be weighed against potential backlash against the profession and the unreliability of coerced data.","Presenting research done on samples of convenience as universal knowledge can be said to discriminate against groups whose omission is part of a wider social marginalization, raising the pending question of whether there is an ethical duty to ensure that research results and participation generalize across cultures, groups, and genders.","Because psychological research still lags in representation of many different populations based on class, race and ethnicity, geography, and language, a pending question is whether the principle of justice can be met when there is de facto exclusion of understudied populations (Strauss et al., 2021).","Pending questions about peer review include whether authors should know the identity of reviewers, whether readers should know the identity and comments of reviewers and editors, and how the culture of peer review can be improved to increase constructiveness and sensitivity, perhaps replacing adversarial error-finding with a more assistance-oriented style.","The explosion of open-access journals has included the birth of journals that offer neither independent peer review nor the likelihood of rejection, a model described as 'predatory,' but the exact distinction between author processing fees (ethical) and a 'pay-for-publication' scheme (unethical) has yet to be drawn.","Subscription models of traditional publishing have been criticized for earning publishers huge profit margins from the volunteer labor of authors, reviewers, and editors, while open access with high publication fees merely shifts the burden and profit source from readers to researchers.","Fraud is a red herring in the decision to adopt policies toward greater openness, because its occurrence is nothing new and its prevalence seems to be relatively low, and a regime constructed to routinely prevent and detect falsified results through constant surveillance and audit would be costly, impractical, and ultimately hateful to researchers (Fanelli, 2009).","One recent study found that questionable research practices such as selective reporting have become stigmatized among U.S. psychologists, and another Dutch survey found self-reported use of questionable practices was lower the more the scientific norms of openness were endorsed, suggesting researchers' ethical standards have been sensitized to these issues since 2011 (Fox et al., 2022; Gopalakrishna et al., 2022).","The primary guiding principles of ethical conduct in publishing are the companion principles of transparency and completeness, with transparency achieved by data sharing (often with analytic code), by disclosures of data practices such as variables measured and participants excluded, and by forthright accounts of how the research was conducted.","Concerns about anonymity and confidentiality, especially in the context of data sharing, continue to grow in importance and difficulty, and while data sharing is characterized as ethical, it is just as much an ethical imperative to simultaneously protect participants' privacy.","The chapter's summary concludes that a more ethical science is a better science, because it offers accuracy, access, dignity, justice, representation, and respect, values that go hand in hand with the knowledge, techniques, theories, and methods that are the more proximate goals of science."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm03","title":"Chapter 3: Replication in Social and Personality Psychology","pages":"45-66","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Replication","topic":"Scrub the Replication Crisis: From the Replicandum to the OSC and Camerer Projects","visual_frame_description":"A scrubber playhead glides along a timeline of replication science: a laboratory bench with disgust stimuli boosting immune reactions, a balloon rising into the sky instead of falling, the LIGO gravitational-wave detector, and a drosophila lab culture bottle labeled 'paradigm.' The playhead then sweeps into a chart room where the OSC's hundred 2008 papers shrink to 36 percent significant replications, and a Nature/Science journal rack counts only thirteen significant replications out of twenty-one studies.","player_mission":"Your mission is to scrub through the history of replication science, tracing how the replicability criterion rose to prominence and then collided with the reality of the Open Science Collaboration (2015) and Camerer et al. (2018) large-scale replication projects. As you scrub, sort the chapter's key ideas into their proper era: the naive belief that every finding must be replicable, the existence proofs and universal proofs distinction, moderator and enabling conditions, the paradigm notion, the paradoxes of exact replication, and the disappointing results of the two flagship projects.","key_concepts":["When politicians, managers, investors, physicians, and principals and agents in law, education, economy, and ecology praise their decisions as 'science-based' or 'evidence-based,' they allude not merely to the existence of any empirical evidence but to well-established, stable, unequivocal, and replicable evidence.","The replicandum can be any outcome of an empirically assessed relationship between two or more variables, observed in any domain, independent of the research objective and regardless of the methods and procedures used to measure and manipulate variables.","Schaller's (2014) finding that disgust elicited by discrete stimuli facilitates adaptive immune reactions is a mere existence proof of the possibility of using disgust as a catalyst of the immune system, and even when the same stimulus is not constantly effective, the very possibility of such a class of stimulus-based interventions is of fundamental significance.","Prominent examples of striking existence proofs include the demonstration of repressed memory recovered twenty years after a rape experience (Loftus, 1993), false confessions (Kassin, 2008), the possibility of reversing the influence of a fluency cue (Unkelbach, 2006), and exerting volitional control over one's heart rate through biofeedback (Williamson & Blanchard, 1979).","Universal rules do exist, as regression to the mean is as inevitable as death and paying taxes (Campbell & Kenny, 1999), yet even universal laws like 'what goes up must fall down' cannot be replicated universally, because the balloon may move up into the sky rather than falling down when other causal factors such as the specific gravity of the gas it contains come into play.","Whether formerly observed results can be replicated depends not only on competing causal influences in a multicausal world but also on the catalytic role of boundary conditions, such as slow-wave sleep creating ideal conditions for anti-prejudice interventions (Feld & Born, 2015), oxygen being an essential enabling condition for burning a house (Hilton & Slugoski, 1986), and good mood strengthening priming effects (Storbeck & Clore, 2008).","A prominent class of moderator conditions is the availability of highly sensitive and reliable measurement procedures, as the LIGO group received a Nobel Prize in 2017 for demonstrating gravitational waves a hundred years after Einstein predicted them, whereas many earlier attempts had failed just because measurement devices were not sensitive enough.","A paradigm (Fiedler et al., 2012) is a conventionalized set of approved, auspicious conditions under which a set of findings has been shown to be most demonstrable, just as drosophila constitutes a paradigm within which the multicausal complexity of genetic research is reasonably well understood and tied to standard moderator conditions.","Paradigms constitute at least partial attempts to deal with the Quine-Duhem notion that purely theoretical assumptions are always tested in conjunction with auxiliary assumptions, for example about the operationalization of the theoretical variables (Earp & Trafimow, 2015).","The term 'exact replication' denotes a replication study that re-establishes exactly the same conditions as a previously published 'original' study, yet it relies on two unreflected assumptions: that a replication failure renders the assumption of exact replication false, and that earlier studies are invariantly 'original' whereas historically later studies are always 'replications.'","In the Open Science Collaboration (2015) project, which attempted to exactly replicate a sample of a hundred (eventually shrunk to ninety-eight) findings published in 2008 in Psychological Science, JPSP, and JEP: Learning, Memory, and Cognition, only 36 percent of all replications were statistically significant, although the estimated statistical power was over 0.90 on average.","The OSC (2015) replication effect size (M = 0.197, SD = 0.257) was on average 47 percent of the effect size obtained in the original studies (M = 0.403, SD = 0.188), and both the proportion of significant replications and the replication effect sizes were regularly lower in the fifty-five social psychological studies (25 percent) than in the forty-two cognitive studies (50 percent)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Replication","topic":"Dialogue on Replication Success: Significance, Shrinkage, and the Exact-Replication Paradox","visual_frame_description":"Two researchers face each other across a desk while a scatterplot of 100,000 simulated replication effect sizes spreads across the wall, showing a cloud of points densely clustered below the original effect size and a red significance threshold that only a thin tail of points crosses. A regression line tilts toward the origin as cards labeled sampling error, measurement error, and reliability drop onto the plot, and a mirror splits one card reading 'EXACT REPLICATION' into two impossible halves.","player_mission":"Your mission is to stage a dialogue between a researcher who insists that a failed replication must produce a significant result to count as success and a critic who argues from Stanley & Spence's (2014) simulations that regressive shrinkage makes significant replications mathematically unlikely. Walk through the expected distribution of replication results, the reliability and measurement-error story, Savage's principle of insufficient reason, Bayesian posterior odds, and the paradox that no replication can ever be exactly 'exact' or purely non-conceptual.","key_concepts":["An enlightening simulation study by Stanley & Spence (2014) demonstrated that even under ideal conditions, given a valid research hypothesis, fully correct data handling, reasonable reliability, and realistic sample sizes, the expected distribution of 100,000 simulated replications shows considerable variation as a consequence of normal sampling error and measurement error.","Given a true population effect size of r = 0.30 and a reliability of 0.70, 99 percent of all replicated effect sizes vary from r = 0.03 to r = 0.46 when the sample size per cell is 40, and the variation is hardly smaller (from less than r = 0.05 to more than r = 0.45) when the sample size is 80.","It would be fully unjustified to expect that replication results must be significant or similarly strong as the true size of an effect, and the Stanley & Spence (2014) results reflect a logically necessary property of the empirical world rather than any peculiarity of psychological phenomena, unwanted practices, publication bias, or exploitation of researcher degrees of freedom (Simmons et al., 2011).","The distributions of simulated effect sizes reflect regressive shrinkage, because the simulated effect sizes are not distributed symmetrically around the true effect size and the greatest part of replication results must be less extreme than the true effect size.","It is logically unwarranted to base a replication project on the null hypothesis that the expected replication effect size is the same as the 'original' effect size, because if the effect size observed in an 'original' study is +0.30 (or -0.30), the true effect size can be expected to be less extreme, since the error term in the equation r(observed) = r(true) + e tends to be positive when the observed r is high but negative when the observed r is low (Fiedler & Prager, 2018).","According to Savage's (1954) principle of insufficient reason, in the absence of prior information about the true effect size, a large (small) observed effect size can be brought about by a wide (small) range of smaller true effect sizes but only a small (wide) range of true effect sizes larger than the observed one, so the true effect size is likely to be smaller in absolute value than the observed effect size.","Because regressive shrinkage is a function of reliability, state-of-the-art replication research must assess or estimate the reliability of dependent variables, and as a rule, if reliability is 0.75 or 0.50, the expected replication effect size is only three-quarters or one-half of the previously obtained observed effect size, respectively.","Essential for all research, not just replications, is checking on the effectiveness of the manipulation of the independent variable: if a predicted influence of a change in X causing a change in Y is not borne out, one must distinguish a failure to replicate the change in Y although the X condition was properly established from a failure to establish the X premise, and simply reporting a failure to observe the change in Y regardless of the X premise renders replication research equivocal and ultimately worthless (Fiedler et al., 2021).","The very possibility of a replication failure renders the ideal of an exact replication unrealistic, and since replication results must not be 'exact,' the fundamental distinction between exact and conceptual replication is called into question, so it seems hard to see how any replication can be not conceptual.","If the arbitrary role assignment of 'original' and 'replication' is reversed, the common finding of weaker effect sizes in the (later) replication studies gives rise to an opposite pattern of stronger results in (earlier) replications than in (later) originals, and if two exact replications only differ in temporal precedence, the puzzling question arises whether effect sizes should regularly decrease over time.","In a prediction market where participants could bet on which of the Camerer et al. (2018) studies would replicate, the studies' market values were highly correlated (r = 0.76 and r = 0.84 respectively, for two prediction tasks) with the dichotomous criterion of replication significance, so betting strongly predicted which studies were replicable.","In Bayesian odds notation, the posterior probability odds that one hypothesis rather than another is true given a finding are equal to the product of the likelihood ratio of a diagnostic design and the prior odds, which implies that posterior odds can only be high when the prior odds are high on a priori grounds."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Replication","topic":"Audit Replication Methodology: Power, Reliability, Manipulation Checks, and the Table 3.2 Standards","visual_frame_description":"An audit room with two giant dossiers, one stamped OSC 2015 and the other CAMERER 2018, lying open on a desk while an inspector ticks and crosses items on a wall ledger: replicandum specified, reliability estimated, regressive shrinkage controlled, manipulation checks passed, simulation run. A power gauge reads 0.92 for the OSC sample, and a calculator tape streams expected replication sizes computed from a reliability of 0.70, while a red stamp smashes down on both dossiers: 'NO MANIPULATION CHECKS.'","player_mission":"Your mission is to audit two flagship replication projects, the Open Science Collaboration (2015) and Camerer et al. (2018), against the state-of-the-art methodological standards of replication research. Check each project against the audit ledger: the explicable replicandum, expected effect strength with regressive shrinkage, simulations of replicated effect sizes, reverse regression analysis, manipulation checks, theoretical constraints, and auxiliary, statistical, and inferential assumptions, then apply the full Table 3.2 recommendation checklist to determine which standards the projects violated.","key_concepts":["Without any deliberation, both the OSC (2015) and the Camerer et al. (2018) projects presupposed that the reference set of replication studies (the denominator in proportional estimates of replication success) includes all published findings, disregarding the possibility that not all empirical research may be replicable.","With regard to other standards of state-of-the-art replication methodology, neither the paradox of 'exact replication' (that a replication failure cannot be exact) nor the distinction between non-replication and moderator effects were taken into account by the large-scale projects.","The Stanley & Spence (2014) insight that measurement error and sampling error alone may considerably reduce replication success did not motivate any attempts to estimate the expected (less than 100 percent) replication rate, regressive shrinkage was not controlled for, and intended high-quality replication studies were not contingent on manipulation checks and reliability checks as a precondition to distinguish replication failures from failures to operationalize the theoretical variables.","Completely different conclusions about the replicability of behavioral research might have been obtained if the reference set had been reduced to the core set of genuine replication candidates, if measurement and sampling error and the necessary impact of regressive shrinkage had been considered, and if only replication studies with a successful manipulation check had been included.","In the Camerer et al. (2018) project on twenty-one behavioral-science articles published between 2010 and 2015 in Nature and Science, aiming at 90 percent power to detect at least 50 percent of the original effect sizes, only 62 percent of the highly powered replications (thirteen out of twenty-one) yielded significant results in the expected direction, and the average replication effect size of these thirteen studies was roughly 75 percent of the effects reported in the original studies.","It is always possible to assess a manipulation's effectiveness between participants, in separate conditions attached to the previous study design, so it is not justified to omit manipulation checks merely because they can interfere with the experimental procedures (Hauser et al., 2018; Polivy & Doyle, 1980).","The first recommendation for high-quality replication research in Table 3.2 is to explicate the replicandum, that is, to specify what subset of empirical results can be realistically expected to be replicable, and why, as not all indices of a construct such as construal level can be expected to be equally related to the focal variable.","Researchers should estimate the expected strength of the replication results, taking regressive shrinkage and reliability into account, and run simulations of replicated effect sizes (Stanley & Spence, 2014), rather than making 'replication success' contingent on statistical significance.","Given a relatively large effect in a replication study, the size of the original study can be expected to be smaller, which is why researchers should consider reverse regression analysis in evaluating replication outcomes.","For an optimal manipulation check, researchers should not only assess whether a manipulation affects the focal construct (e.g., distance) but also whether it affects other factors than that construct, and they should beware of auxiliary assumptions, for instance that an instruction to imagine a distal versus proximal episode succeeds in inducing a distance mindset as a priming treatment.","Constraints on statistical assumptions require explicating in what statistical index a predicted finding should be found (an average measure, percentile, range or maximal score, a consensus rate, etc.), and constraints on inferential assumptions require sound assumptions about what design factors can be considered random factors allowing for generalizing inferences (Trafimow, 2022).","In the OSC (2015) sample, the proportion of significant replications was only 25 percent for social psychological studies versus 50 percent for cognitive studies, with replication power around 0.92 and replication effect sizes of 0.140 versus 0.278, while the effect sizes in the original studies were 0.340 and 0.493, respectively."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Replication","topic":"Matrix Match: Replication Successes versus Replication Failures across Paradigms","visual_frame_description":"A wall matrix with two columns, one labeled FAILED TO REPLICATE showing a pen held between teeth, a crossed-out letter 'e,' and an unsolvable geometric puzzle, and the other labeled REPLICATED showing a time-travel instruction screen and a door-in-the-face compliance chart. Cards fly into each column: Hagger 2016 with d = 0.04, Vohs 2021 with a Bayes factor of 4.4 favoring the null, Wagenmakers 2016 with d = 0.03 across seventeen labs, and Sanchez 2021 with d = 0.276 in a preregistered replication.","player_mission":"Your mission is to build a two-column matrix sorting the chapter's paradigm debates into FAILED REPLICATIONS and SUCCESSFUL REPLICATIONS, placing each evidence card in its cell: the Hagger et al. (2016) and Vohs et al. (2021) multi-lab ego-depletion projects, the Wagenmakers et al. (2016) facial-feedback replication and the Many Smiles Collaboration, and the successful construal-level theory replications by Sanchez et al. (2021), Soderberg et al. (2015), and Bhatia & Walasek (2016). Then weigh, for each cell, how manipulation checks, theoretical constraints, and procedural choices decided the outcome.","key_concepts":["The strength model of self-control posits that the exertion of self-control is a limited resource comparable to a muscle that gets tired (Baumeister et al., 2007), and following a plethora of original studies on ego depletion and support from meta-analyses (Dang, 2018; Blazquez et al., 2017), recent research attempting to replicate the most frequently cited findings yielded rather disappointing results.","The registered multi-lab replication of an ego-depletion experimental setting conducted by Hagger et al. (2016) in twelve different labs with a total of 1,775 participants yielded a very small effect size with a 95 percent confidence interval that included zero (d = 0.04, 95% CI [-0.07, 0.15]), although a meta-analysis of effect sizes across all labs yielded a small but significant depletion effect (Z = 2.10, p = 0.036, d = 0.10, 95% CI [0.01, 0.19]).","As Friese et al. (2019) noted, ego-depletion research has relied on an inflated variety of independent and dependent variables and combinations of both, yielding variable results, and since the theory does not strictly constrain what measures are best suited to validate the core of the theoretical idea, it might be premature to dismiss the theory upon one (more or less) arbitrary operational test.","In the Vohs et al. (2021) multi-lab replication project, thirty-six participating labs freely chose to implement one of two procedures (the letter 'e' crossing task with endurance on unsolvable geometric puzzles, or a story-writing task with the Biber cognitive estimation test), with a total sample of 3,531 participants, and meta-analytically no significant performance difference was found between conditions (d = 0.06, 95% CI [-0.02, 0.14]).","Bayesian analysis in the Vohs et al. (2021) project indicated that the data were 4.4 times more likely under the null hypothesis than under the ego-depletion hypothesis, while a subsequent analysis without the preregistered exclusions (which removed nearly one-third of participants) showed a small but statistically significant ego-depletion effect (d = 0.08, 95% CI [0.01, 0.15]) with the corresponding Bayes factor indicating only 1.33 times more likelihood under the null.","According to the facial feedback hypothesis, facial motor activity determines or moderates subjectively experienced emotions, and in the seminal study by Strack et al. (1988) participants who simulated a smiling facial expression by biting on a pen with their teeth rated cartoons as more funny (5.14 versus 4.32 on a ten-point scale) than did participants who had held the pen between their lips.","The multi-lab direct replication of the Strack et al. (1988) experiment coordinated by Wagenmakers et al. (2016), using video-based instructions and videotaping participants across seventeen labs with a total of 1,894 participants, yielded no significant effect (d = 0.03, 95% CI [-0.11, 0.16]), and the authors conceded that this failure need not invalidate the facial feedback hypothesis, as it may be peculiar to the ineffectiveness of the pen-in-mouth procedure.","A meta-analysis by Coles et al. (2019) of a broad range of facial feedback research provided no support for the claim that participants' awareness of the manipulation and videorecording altered findings, as videorecording produced no overall difference in results across studies.","The Many Smiles Collaboration (Coles et al., 2022) found that evidence for the pen-in-mouth procedure is unclear because analyses yielded both significant and nonsignificant results depending on one-sided versus two-sided testing and inclusion criteria, and even when the pen-in-mouth manipulation produced facial feedback effects they were small compared to larger effects of facial mimicry or voluntary facial action.","Construal-level theory relies on two fundamental assumptions: that psychological distance constitutes a general construct with convergent results for measures of temporal, spatial, social, and hypothetical distance (Trope & Liberman, 2010), and that distal events tend to be encoded in terms of more abstract verbal and mental representations than proximal events.","Sanchez et al. (2021) conducted a preregistered conceptual replication of the temporal distance - linguistic abstraction relation, and after excluding thirty-five participants, an analysis of the remaining sample of n = 485 valid participants successfully replicated the CLT prediction that thinking about the distant future induced more abstract descriptions than thinking about the near future (d = 0.276, 95% CI [0.097, 0.455]).","Further evidence for the replicability of CLT was provided by a meta-analysis by Soderberg et al. (2015) supporting a positive relationship between abstractness and psychological distance (d = 0.493, 95% CI [0.40, 0.58]), and by Bhatia & Walasek (2016) using 'big data' from huge language corpora, who found more concrete words in tweets referring to temporally proximate dates and increasing concreteness in New York Times articles on elections as temporal proximity increased."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Replication","topic":"Boss Round: Theoretical Constraints and the Unpacking Effect as the Route to Predictably Replicable Science","visual_frame_description":"A boss arena shaped like a giant set of theater scales: on one pan a stack of 'sexy findings' manuscripts from top journals tips the balance, while on the other pan a toolbox labeled theoretical constraints with items like regression to the mean, classical conditioning, and the Weber-Fechner law weighs heavily downward. An unpacking diagram of Japanese cars splitting into Toyota, Datsun, Mitsubishi, Honda, and Suzuki subcategories overflows with overestimated frequency estimates, and a banner unfurls the twofold mistake: weak replication methodology plus journalistic 'sexy finding' criteria.","player_mission":"Your mission is to defeat the final boss, the notion that replicable findings grow out of surprising, 'sexy' research detached from theory, by reconstructing the chapter's positive program. Master the unpacking effect and its regressive-shrinkage foundation, show how theoretical constraints (regression, classical conditioning, Weber-Fechner law) make findings predictably replicable, explain the Bayesian logic of why no robust findings can grow out of unexpected ideas, and deliver the chapter's twofold diagnosis of the replication crisis to claim victory.","key_concepts":["The unpacking effect (Tversky & Koehler, 1994; Rottenstreich & Tversky, 1997), also called the category-split effect (Fiedler et al., 2009), is the sublinear rule that the prevalence of a superordinate category is smaller than the summed prevalence of two or more subcategories, so that the summed estimated prevalence of all Toyota, Datsun, Mitsubishi, Honda, and Suzuki cars on American streets is by magnitudes higher than the estimated prevalence of Japanese cars on American streets.","Unpacking a category into two or more subcategories results in massive overestimation of frequentist quantities, often amounting to 100 percent overestimation or more of the unpacked category, and it applies to cost calculation (Kruger & Evans, 2004) and to evaluative or utilitarian judgments (Morewedge et al., 2007).","Unpacking effects do not occur with 100 percent certainty, because just as all perfectly valid rules (like the law of gravitation) may be overshadowed by other causal factors, unpacking can be countered by deliberate control or correction processes when participants notice the upcoming bias or deliberately check the additive category structure using paper and pencil.","When overshadowing influences are controlled experimentally, a normal unpacking effect follows inevitably from a logically necessary rule, namely regressive shrinkage (Fiedler & Krueger, 2012; Fiedler et al., 2009).","Because regression is as inevitable as death and paying taxes (Campbell & Kenny, 1999), there are strong a priori constraints to assume that whenever the correlation between two variables X and Y is less than perfect, plotting Y as a function of X must yield a regression slope of less than unity, so judgments of an objective attribute X must show regressive shrinkage that underestimates high X values and overestimates low X values, granting a few auxiliary assumptions.","Regressive judgments have been found in many domains, such as frequency and risk estimates (Combs & Slovic, 1979) and student performance evaluations (Fiedler & Walther, 2004), and because the strength of regressive shrinkage increases with unreliability, regression effects can be expected to increase when task conditions decrease judgment reliability, such as small sample size, cognitive load, or lack of knowledge.","Larger sample size in majorities than in minorities is sufficient to explain illusory correlations (Costello & Watts, 2019; Fiedler, 1991), because granting the same high rate of positive behavior in a larger majority as in a smaller minority, the lesser regression of larger samples implies more positive judgments of majorities than of minorities.","Validating the regression account, Fiedler et al. (2009) found that unpacking effects get stronger when cognitive load reduces the reliability and increases the regressive shrinkage of frequency judgments, and the unpacking effect can be easily reframed as a prototypical regression effect in which a high estimate of a superordinate category is underestimated relative to a sum of more infrequent subcategories that are all overestimated.","Quantifying theoretical constraints involves a trade-off between theoretical assumptions, which must be resistant to counteracting influences of auxiliary assumptions about competing causal factors, statistical assumptions, and inferential assumptions (Trafimow, 2022), and the art of clever experimentation consists in constructing diagnostic research designs that isolate the focal influences from the potential interference of auxiliary, statistical, and inferential conditions.","In Bayesian terms, 'sexy' findings published in the most prestigious outlets must be characterized by particularly low prior odds, and because posterior odds are a multiplicative function of a likelihood ratio and the prior odds, no robust findings can grow out of completely unexpected ideas detached from cumulative science.","The most prestigious and selective outlets, with rejection rates of 90 percent or higher, seem to selectively publish unexpected, miraculous, sexy, groundbreaking, and completely novel findings, and in this regard high-ranking publications are selectively biased against cumulative science.","The authors' tentative solution to the replication crisis is the hypothesis that its undeniable truth lies in a twofold mistake: the weakness and underdeveloped methodology of replication research, and most importantly the intrinsic failure of behavioral scientists to give more weight to genuinely scientific theoretical priors than to journalistic criteria ('sexy findings'), such that increasing a focus on theoretical constraints promises a dramatic increase in the rate of successful replications."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm04","title":"Chapter 4: Realizing the Promise of Diverse and Interdisciplinary Team Science","pages":"68-83","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Team Science","topic":"The Benefits of a Team Science Approach","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen scene: on the left, a laboratory full of similar-looking researchers clustered around one whiteboard; on the right, a wide round table where researchers of varied backgrounds, fields, and nations exchange methods and questions. Floating between the two panels, a ticking gauge labeled 'collective toolkit' rises as the diverse team speaks.","player_mission":"Audit the four benefits of team science described in section 4.1: the opportunity to diversify the field, expanded questions and methodologies, and the people power for more ambitious projects. For each benefit, identify the factual claims and named examples the authors cite, then rank which benefit makes the strongest case for building diverse interdisciplinary teams.","key_concepts":["Psychology is a team science in that people from many walks of life come together to explore the inner workings of the mind and how those internal dynamics are affected by external forces.","Many areas of psychology benefit from channeling the 'wisdom of crowds' that comes from working together in teams (Larrick et al., 2012).","In a diverse and inclusive team science model, people can use their unique experiences and training to advance the research process more efficiently and expand the collective toolkit of methods and approaches available for tackling important psychological questions.","Research teams continue to be overwhelmingly white and wealthy, as are the participants recruited into research studies to examine psychological processes (Dupree & Kraus, 2022; Roberts et al., 2020).","In a sample of research articles about race published in top social psychology journals, 92 percent of papers were edited by white editors in chief, 72 percent were written by white authors, and 66 percent of participants were white (Roberts et al., 2020).","A team science approach has the potential to address the lack of diversity by creating new opportunities for expanding access to research and for creating more roles on teams that increase the representation of people who have been traditionally excluded from knowledge production processes in the field.","Because 'homophily' is a strong force, it is actually quite easy to build teams that lack diversity (McPherson et al., 2001).","Faculty tend to admit graduate students who share their background characteristics, collaborate with people who are already in their homogeneous networks, and cite researchers who are similar to them (Posselt, 2016; Newman, 2001; Greenwald & Schuh, 1994).","There is a 'bonus' that comes from diversity: new ways of thinking, new questions, and new ideas that can advance knowledge (Page, 2019).","The Psychological Science Accelerator is 'a distributed network of laboratories designed to enable and support crowdsourced research projects' (Moshontz et al., 2018).","In its first project, the Psychological Science Accelerator tested the generalizability of the valence-dominance model of face perception by collecting data from over 11,000 participants in forty-one countries through a large-scale collaboration with over 200 researchers (Jones et al., 2021).","Even small teams can benefit from the ethos of team science, because as long as team members possess different skills and backgrounds, they can work together in a way that capitalizes on those unique strengths and improves the efficiency and outcomes of their collaboration."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Team Science","topic":"Four Models of Team Science","visual_frame_description":"A round-table talk show: four chairs labeled 'Lab,' 'Cross-lab,' 'Cross-discipline,' and 'Cross-sector,' each occupied by a researcher silhouette, with a table in the middle listing Table 4.1's four principles of team science. A moderator projects each model's reach as a network diagram behind the speakers.","player_mission":"Host a debate among four lab directors, each defending one of the four models of team science: lab teams, cross-lab teams, cross-discipline teams, and cross-sector teams. For each model, present its composition, scale, benefits, and characteristic challenges, and press the panel on when one model should be chosen over another.","key_concepts":["The chapter discusses four common models of team science: lab teams, cross-lab teams, cross-discipline teams, and cross-sector teams.","Lab teams are groups of students, postdocs, and faculty working on common issues and topics, and they are small-scale collaborations.","Lab teams may not live up to their full potential under the traditional model of academic research in which principal investigators or lab directors set the research agenda, select their own preferred methods, and train their students to follow in their academic footsteps.","Thinking of labs as teams can help us recognize the contributions of members whose work may not always be acknowledged, such as research assistants, administrators, or interns (Allen et al., 2019).","Cross-lab teams are often made up of researchers from different universities, typically within the same or related fields, who come together to conduct research on a shared interest.","Cross-lab teams include large-scale collaborations that often produce replication studies or more rigorous tests of ideas in the field, such as the Psychological Science Accelerator and the Many Labs replication projects (Klein et al., 2018).","Cross-lab teams allow researchers to test a question across several different contexts (e.g., regions, countries, languages), creating opportunities to assess generalizability and cultural variation.","Conducting cross-lab research at a larger scale often requires a great deal of organization given challenges such as agreeing on research methods, navigating multi-institutional review board protocols, and finding times for team members to meet across time zones.","Cross-discipline teams involve researchers from various fields or disciplines who likely have different academic backgrounds and methodological skills from one another.","Cross-discipline teams are typically broader in terms of the skills and goals of the team members, so it is often important for them to be aligned in their goals for a given project, as there may be different standards across fields to adhere to.","Cross-sector teams typically involve academics working in partnership with companies, schools, nonprofits, technology firms, government agencies, or other organizations to answer questions of mutual interest to all parties (Yudkin et al., 2021).","Cross-sector partnerships can provide researchers with access to real-world data that are otherwise difficult to obtain, but cross-sector teams consist of people who might have drastically different goals."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Team Science","topic":"Forming and Growing Teams","visual_frame_description":"A whiteboard covered in three sticky-note clusters labeled 'Goals,' 'Skills,' and 'Voices,' with a facilitator drawing a dotted line from an empty skill slot to a door marked 'Collaboration Zone.' Off to one side, a clock ticks through a premortem imaginary failure scenario, and a checklist shows paid positions and SPSP networking platforms.","player_mission":"Act as a team-building consultant running the three assessment questions from section 4.3.1: What are your team's goals? What skills do your team members have (or need)? What voices are represented on the team? Produce a concrete expansion plan that closes skill gaps, runs a project premortem, and recruits underrepresented collaborators through the routes the authors list.","key_concepts":["To engage in team science, we first need to form teams or expand and improve our current teams.","The chapter offers three questions that researchers should discuss with one another early on and often: (1) What are your team's goals? (2) What skills do your team members have (or need)? (3) What voices are represented on the team?","Researchers' goals might vary depending on their background, personal interests, skills, career stage, and long-term career goals, with some researchers more drawn to basic research and others interested in applications.","Researchers should take time to reflect on their personal goals regularly throughout their careers, because personal reflection can help them decide whether a given project is a good investment of time and resources before beginning to work on it.","Team members should meet to discuss their goals for the project and what they each hope to get out of it, including what they hope to learn and what the concrete outputs will be (e.g., papers, conference presentations, or other forms of dissemination).","When collaborating in diverse teams, especially in terms of career stage and field, it is important to consider that not all outputs are valued equally across different contexts; for example, for early-career researchers in psychology departments, publications in 'top' journals are typically valued more highly than publications in other outlets.","While authorship conversations can be difficult at times, the authors recommend having them early in order to set expectations for all members of the team (Eggert, 2011).","To divide tasks efficiently, the team should have an understanding of each person's capacities and how they would like to use them in a particular project, and teams can start by creating a list of the skills needed for the project at hand.","To generate a skills list, teams might broadly map out each phase of a project, such as idea generation, sample selection, research design, data collection, data analysis, and dissemination, and then fill in each phase with the required skills.","Teams might assess where there may be gaps in their collective skill set by performing a study 'premortem,' imagining a scenario in which their project fails to meet its intended aims (Klein, 2007).","As a matter of ethical concern, teams should strive to represent a diversity of experiences and identities as much as possible, because academic psychology has systematically excluded people of color, low-income people, and other marginalized groups.","Researchers can expand teams by creating paid positions specifically focused on recruiting people from underrepresented groups, for example by writing funding into existing grants or working with programs like SPSP's Social-Personality Undergraduate Research (SPUR) Program or the NSF's Research Experiences for Undergraduates."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Team Science","topic":"The Logistics of Working in Teams","visual_frame_description":"A control-room dashboard with four panels: 'Roles & Leadership' showing an org chart, 'Communication' showing email and Slack threads with an agreed cadence, 'Research Process' showing a community partnership flowchart, and 'Conflict' showing a mediation staircase ending at a university ombudsperson's office door.","player_mission":"Take charge of the operational handbook for a large, newly formed research team. Establish roles and a leadership structure, set communication norms, adapt the research process for a community partner organization, and write a conflict-resolution protocol that handles authorship disputes and power dynamics fairly.","key_concepts":["Each person on the team should have a clear set of tasks that they will be responsible for, and it can be useful to establish specific roles, especially regarding leadership.","The larger the team, the more critical it is to have people who are in charge of high-level co-ordination of the project, such as assigning responsibilities, assessing the progress of team members, setting up meetings, and establishing a timeline.","Regardless of the scope of the project or the size of the team, it is critical to have a clear leadership structure and to make sure that all team members are aware of and in agreement on that structure.","Although teams may require a few members to take on leadership roles, all team members should ideally feel comfortable asking questions and contributing ideas.","In a team science model, leaders should instead seek out opportunities to include others in the decision-making process and provide rationale for any major decisions.","Team members should all agree on a mode or modes of communication and set expectations about when and how often to communicate, for example whether to meet monthly over Zoom and whether progress updates should go to leaders or the whole team.","In many cases of collaboration, the steps of the research process closely resemble those of a more traditional, small-scale project, with teams assembling already having a specified research question and hypothesis.","Partner organizations have an intimate knowledge of their processes and of the communities they serve that they can contribute to the collaboration, so partners (and participants) may feel excluded from the research process if academics approach them with a preset agenda that has no room for their goals.","Whereas many researchers are interested in answering theoretical questions, organizations are typically far more driven by practical questions, which may result in a misalignment of goals on the team.","Conflict can arise at any stage of the process, from deciding on authorship order to interpreting findings, and these conflicts often stem from poor communication, unclear expectations, or misalignment of goals.","When conflict arises, the best course of action is often to facilitate an open discussion, which authors can do by approaching one another one-on-one, calling for a group discussion, or trying to bring in a neutral party to aid in the conversation.","More junior members of the team may not feel as comfortable advocating for themselves, so more senior team members should be acutely aware of power dynamics and, when necessary, create anonymous ways for team members to share their concerns, such as involving a university ombudsperson."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Team Science","topic":"The Promise of Team Science","visual_frame_description":"A stadium stage with the African proverb 'If you want to go fast, go alone; if you want to go far, go together' projected behind the speaker. As the talk progresses, a wall of solo publication counts collapses and is rebuilt as a mosaic of shared contributions, labeled 'access, inclusion, communication, roles, cooperative decisions.'","player_mission":"Deliver the closing keynote: synthesize why diverse, interdisciplinary team science matters and what it requires beyond adding more people. Lay out the core principles from Table 4.1 and the field-level norm changes (hiring, promotion, credit) needed for team science models to become more commonplace, ending with the impact case.","key_concepts":["Team science models can be implemented in social and personality psychology in a number of ways, from lab teams to cross-sector teams.","Regardless of the size or scope of the collaboration, we can benefit from expanding our teams to include people from different backgrounds, disciplines, and perspectives.","Working in teams provides us with a larger toolkit and greater capacity to address bigger and more impactful questions.","Conducting team science is not just about working with a larger number of people, but also about implementing certain principles in our work, including promoting access and inclusion as we build our teams, creating open and honest channels of communication, and establishing clear roles and expectations for team members.","By engaging in this type of science, we maximize our potential for learning by tackling more ambitious research questions and by engaging with team members who think differently about scientific and social issues.","In order for team science models to become more commonplace in social and personality psychology, we must also push to change the norms in our field to place greater value on collaborative work instead of primarily focusing on individual accomplishments.","Changing field norms requires assessing people's contributions to science in a more holistic way, for example by changing our hiring and promotion practices to value different types of contribution to projects rather than counting first-author publications.","Most researchers in social and personality psychology seek to have an impact in their careers, whether on the field itself or in the world more broadly.","Diverse team science can bring us closer to this goal by helping us add nuance to our questions, expand our methods and theories, and improve the validity and generalizability of our research.","The benefits of a team science approach are not automatic; creating larger teams does not mean that those teams will necessarily be better or more diverse, and increasing diversity requires deliberate efforts.","Team science approaches create new opportunities for expanding access to research, and these collaborations can help us increase our toolkit to include methods like field studies that many of us may not be exposed to in our traditional training (Paluck & Cialdini, 2013).","Engaging in interdisciplinary collaborations can also be mutually beneficial, because just as psychologists can learn new methods from other researchers, researchers from other fields can also expand their toolkits through partnership with psychologists."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm05","title":"Chapter 5: A Cross-Cultural Method in Social and Personality Psychology: The Cultural Imagination","pages":"85-113","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Cross-Cultural Methods","topic":"Why Culture? The Theoretical Contributions of a Cross-Cultural Approach","visual_frame_description":"A three-panel museum gallery titled 'The Cultural Imagination.' Panel one shows a magnifying glass hovering over a classic laboratory result labeled 'fundamental attribution error,' panel two shows a balance scale weighing 'Confucianism' against 'collectivism,' and panel three shows a globe with pride expressions radiating from thirty-six nations, while a caption reads 'dispositions, situations, and larger cultural contexts.'","player_mission":"Audit the three theoretical contributions of a cross-cultural approach laid out in section 5.1.1: exposing hidden theoretical assumptions, testing theories, and identifying psychological universals. For each contribution, cite the key studies (Miller, the lexical approach, Gurven et al.) and assess what each one reveals about how cross-cultural comparison refines psychological theory.","key_concepts":["Social psychology is often defined as the scientific study of the ways in which the actual or imagined presence of others influences one's thinking, feeling, and behavior (Aronson et al., 2010).","Personality psychology is often defined as the scientific study of characteristic patterns of thinking, feeling, and behavior (Funder, 2019).","The chapter advocates the utility of cultural imagination, the ability to see human behaviors as a function not just of their dispositions or immediate situations but also of larger cultural contexts, echoing C. Wright Mills's notion of the sociological imagination (1959).","The fundamental attribution error is the tendency to overestimate the extent to which people's behavior is due to internal, dispositional factors while underestimating the role of situational factors (Ross, 1977).","Miller (1984) found that 45% of American adults' explanations for deviant behaviors were general dispositions and 14% were contextual, whereas among Indian adults the pattern was reversed (15% general dispositions and 32% contextual), showing that the fundamental attribution error was not observed among Indian participants.","Failure to replicate the results of published studies has become a hot methodological issue over the last decade and is now widely considered an issue of research credibility in personality and social psychology (e.g., Vazire et al., 2022).","Cultural and cross-cultural psychologists were among the earliest personality and social psychologists to routinely test the replicability of published studies in other cultures (see Markus et al., 1996 for an early review).","Cross-cultural studies provide additional macro explanations for the fundamental attribution error, such as holistic worldviews, social role orientations, and a belief in karma, explanations that intra-psychic accounts like perceptual salience and self-serving motivation cannot provide.","The lexical approach is one of the most stringent tests of the big-five factor model because researchers rely on the dictionary words of a respective culture rather than on a scale developed in the U.S. or Europe (Saucier & Goldberg, 2001).","Polish researchers extracted 1,811 person-descriptive adjectives from a Polish dictionary, selected the 290 most representative, and factor analysis of high-school students' self-ratings resulted in a five-factor solution similar to the original one (Szarota, 1996).","Korean researchers using the lexical approach found a best-fit three-factor solution (extraversion, agreeableness, and conscientiousness) plus a truthfulness factor, which was eventually incorporated into the HEXACO model of personality (Hahn et al., 1999; Lee & Ashton, 2018).","Gurven et al. (2013) administered a translated big-five scale to Tsimane adults, forager-farmers in the Bolivian Amazon, and factor analysis produced only two factors (prosociality and industriousness), with very low internal consistency for the traditional five factors (alphas ranging from 0.07 to 0.63)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Cross-Cultural Methods","topic":"Testing Theories Across Cultures: Triangulation and the Quest for Universals","visual_frame_description":"A circular debate table with four spokes radiating from a center labeled 'psychological universal.' Each spoke leads to a distinct strategy: a triangle diagram with three cultural labels, three Turkish occupational groups on one coastline, a Fijian village facing a Canadian campus, and a globe wrapped in a forty-one-country network. A banner overhead reads 'the strongest form of external validity.'","player_mission":"Moderate a panel of four methodologies, each defending a strategy for testing theory cross-culturally: the triangulation strategy of adding a third culture that differs on only one dimension, the just-minimal-difference approach of Uskul et al., extreme-culture sampling, and large-scale multi-nation data collection. Force the panel to confront third-variable explanations and to state when each strategy best supports claims about psychological universals.","key_concepts":["A cross-cultural study that fails to replicate an original study gives rise to additional theorizing, whereas a successful replication provides an opportunity for further generalizability (Norenzayan & Heine, 2005).","Bagozzi et al. (1999) found that the frequency of positive emotional experiences was strongly negatively associated with the frequency of negative emotional experiences among Americans, whereas the two were only weakly inversely associated among South Koreans and Chinese.","To test whether Confucianism or collectivism explains East Asian emotion structure, the triangulation strategy requires a third group (C) that differs from group A (the U.S.) and group B (East Asians) in one dimension but not the other (Norenzayan & Heine, 2005).","Latin Americans are similar to the U.S. in terms of the Judeo-Christian tradition but differ in individualism/collectivism, making them a useful third group in triangulation designs.","Schimmack, Oishi, et al. (2002) conducted a cross-cultural study involving all three cultural groups and found that East Asians were different from both Latin and North Americans in the structure of emotion, confirming that Confucianism rather than collectivism seems responsible for the weak positive-negative emotion correlation among East Asians.","An elegant extension of the triangulation strategy is the just-minimal-difference approach employed by Uskul et al. (2008), which compares groups that differ minimally on the dimension of interest while matching on potential third variables such as language, education, and political systems.","Uskul et al. (2008) sampled tea farmers, fishers, and herders within the eastern Black Sea region of Turkey and found that herders, the most independent of the three in their everyday lives, were more analytic and less holistic than farmers and fishers.","Tracy and Matsumoto (2008) coded spontaneous facial expressions and behaviors of Judo players photographed at the moment they won or lost Olympic matches and found that most athletes across the thirty-six nations showed pride expressions such as head-tilt back, smile, and raised arm.","One way to show universality is to collect data from a variety of cultures, as Tracy and Matsumoto (2008) did, and another way is to sample an extreme culture, one as different from the original sample as possible.","Tracy et al. (2013) compared Fijian villagers, who live in a highly hierarchical culture in which low-status individuals are not supposed to show pride expressions, with Canadian college students and showed that pride was associated with high social status in both cultures.","A research strategy that tests a claim in an extreme culture helps increase confidence in identifying a psychological universal, the strongest form of external validity.","Even with the triangulation strategy, cross-cultural comparisons are still filled with third-variable explanations such as language, education, and political systems."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Cross-Cultural Methods","topic":"Steps 1-2: Selecting Samples and Preparing Materials","visual_frame_description":"A consultant's desk covered in route cards: a PSA network diagram, a World Values Survey yearbook, a single flag planted on a remote island, and a U.S.-Japan silhouette pair, with a red banner labeled 'sampling bias' overhead. Beside the desk, a conveyor belt carries a scale from English through Thai translation, back-translation, reviewer comments, adjudication, and a pretest clipboard before it reaches the study.","player_mission":"Act as a preparation consultant for a researcher planning a first cross-cultural study. Audit the sample-selection route (multi-culture networks, large representative datasets, a single maximally different culture, hypothesis-driven choices, or within-country groups) and the material-preparation pipeline (emic versus etic approach, situation sampling, team translation, back-translation, and pilot testing), then issue a concrete prep checklist with named studies and bias warnings.","key_concepts":["If you have no a priori hypotheses, the authors recommend sampling as many cultures as you can, and the Psychological Science Accelerator (PSA), a distributed laboratory network, can help researchers collect data from participating sites around the world.","As of the chapter's writing, the Psychological Science Accelerator has 1,328 members representing eighty-four countries, and Jones et al. (2021) used the PSA network to test whether the valence-dominance model of face processing would emerge in forty-one countries.","A major advantage of assembling your own cross-cultural collaboration team is the ability to design the study to fit your exact needs, but a major disadvantage is the potential for sampling bias, as it can be very difficult to collect data representative of respective cultures.","Robert Levine devised an ingenious data collection approach by recruiting and training international students returning to their home countries over the summer, which allowed him to make a major contribution to the literature on helping and the pace of life without any major grants.","To address sampling bias, many social scientists turn to large-scale representative datasets that are freely available, such as the World Values Survey (the oldest ongoing comprehensive international survey, started in 1981 and now covering more than 120 countries), the European Social Survey, the Latinobarometro, the Arab Barometer, and the Afrobarometer.","For researchers who have not conducted any cross-cultural studies before, a good alternative to a multiculture study is to choose one sample that is most different from your convenient sample, like Tracy et al.'s (2013) choice of Fijian villagers.","Joining forces with a collaborator familiar with the literature, the local culture of interest, and relevant data collection methods significantly contributes to the quality of a study, as illustrated by Chentsova-Dutton and Dzokoto's (2014) study of interoceptive awareness in the U.S. and Ghana.","If researchers already have hypotheses regarding cultural differences, they can choose samples that are the best testing grounds, such as Kitayama et al.'s (2000) choice of the U.S. and Japan, or Günsoy et al.'s (2020) choice of Turkey and the southern U.S. (cultures of honor) versus the northern U.S. (a culture of dignity).","A cross-cultural study does not need to be conducted in different national contexts: Triandis et al. (1984) discovered a cultural script of simpatía among Hispanic Navy recruits, Cheryan and Monin (2005) studied identity denial among Asian American students, and other researchers have compared religious groups in shared spaces and social classes within single countries.","The emic approach is an idiographic perspective in which all the materials and concepts to be tested are originally conceived in the culture to be studied (like the lexical approach), whereas the etic approach translates a scale developed elsewhere, such as the North American PANAS emotion scale, into the local language.","Kitayama et al. (2000) used the emic approach to study emotions, including terms such as fureai (feeling close) and shitashimi (friendly feelings) that are not typically included in scales developed in North America, and the situation-sampling method asks participants in each culture to generate situations that evoke a particular emotion or experience.","The back-translation process, in which the translated materials are translated back into the original language by another person (Brislin et al., 1973), helps researchers identify obvious mistranslations and difficult items, although the Japanese translation of 'excited' as koufun shows that back-translation can still hide connotation problems, and Lomas (2016) collected 216 untranslatable well-being-related words across diverse languages."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Cross-Cultural Methods","topic":"Steps 3-4: Ethics, Data Collection, and Measurement Equivalence","visual_frame_description":"A control-room dashboard with four panels: 'Ethics' showing IRB stamps and a preregistration badge for each site, 'Timeline' showing one-month versus six-month collection bars, 'Bias' showing three alarm lights labeled construct, method, and item bias, and 'Equivalence' showing a chain of configural-metric-scalar models being tightened, with an item response curve display and a Bayesian density plot on the side.","player_mission":"Take charge of the operational control room for a multiculture study. Secure ethics approval and preregistration at every site, plan realistic data-collection timelines across platforms, then run the measurement-equivalence gauntlet: construct, method, and item bias; response styles; reliability reporting; MG-CFA; IRT/DIF; and the Bayesian ROME approach. Issue the go/no-go verdict for mean comparisons and hypothesis testing.","key_concepts":["Before starting the data collection, it is important to have institutional review board (IRB) or ethics approval and to preregister your study, and in cross-cultural research it is generally expected that you secure ethical approval from all data collection sites, with procedures checked carefully as local ethical requirements may vary.","Cross-cultural studies are challenging because very few countries have data collection platforms similar to those in the U.S. (e.g., departmental participant pools, MTurk), and if you anticipate completing data collection in three months at your home institution, you should expect it to take twice or three times as long elsewhere.","Construct bias is conceptual and refers to the construct under investigation having a different meaning for participants from different cultural groups, and suggestions to achieve construct equivalence include in-depth understanding of each cultural context and developing study materials simultaneously in different cultures (also called decentering).","Method bias results from differences in the methods employed to collect data in different cultural contexts, including differences in sampling procedures, familiarity with study materials such as Likert scales, response styles, and administration modes such as paper-and-pencil versus online surveys.","Response styles refer to a systematic tendency to use certain categories of the response scale independent of the item content, with commonly discussed biases including acquiescent responding, extreme responding, and moderacy bias.","Reference group effects occur when participants evaluate themselves in comparison to members of their own cultural group, which results in different standards being used across cultural groups (Heine et al., 2002).","Item bias occurs when an item has a different psychological meaning across cultures, derived from poor item translation, items with culture-specific interpretations, or items with ambiguous connotations, such as when members of a cultural group respond to an item with social desirability.","Researchers should report a reliability coefficient such as Cronbach's alpha for each of the key variables in each cultural sample, and if alpha is very low for one sample (e.g., below 0.60), it is important to check item-level correlations within the scale to identify problematic items.","For a formal test of measurement equivalence, researchers should use a series of multiple-group confirmatory factor analyses (MG-CFA), starting with a configural invariance model in which the same items are indicators of the factor across samples and moving to a metric invariance model in which factor loadings are constrained to be the same across samples.","In Oishi (2006), MG-CFA of the Satisfaction with Life Scale between the U.S. and China showed that item 5 was biased, with a standardized factor loading of 0.38 in the Chinese sample versus 0.71 in the U.S. sample, justifying its removal from both samples for the final mean comparisons.","Item response theory (IRT) differential item functioning (DIF) analyses are a far more stringent test of measurement equivalence than MG-CFA, and in Oishi (2006) DIF identified far more biased items (only item 2 equivalent), with a latent mean difference of d = 0.71 versus d = 1.18 based on the five original items.","Zhang et al. (2022) developed a Bayesian region of measurement equivalence (ROME) approach that computes highest posterior density intervals and compares them with a predetermined range of practically zero values, and in an empirical example found that items flagged as non-equivalent by traditional MG-CFA had a negligible effect on the mean difference."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Cross-Cultural Methods","topic":"Future Directions and Conclusions: The Underutilized Toolkit","visual_frame_description":"A stadium stage where a toolbox labeled 'methods' unfolds into eight compartments, each lighting up in turn: a dictionary, a handshake, a Starbucks cafe, a phone pinging with surveys, a Twitter feed, a credit card, a brain scan, and a children's storybook. Behind the speaker, a globe labeled 'cultural imagination' absorbs four contribution badges: hidden assumptions, expanded explanations, theory building, and universals.","player_mission":"Deliver the closing keynote on the underutilized methods for cross-cultural research: linguistic analyses, behavioral experiments, systematic behavioral observations, experience sampling, social media data, bank records, neuroimaging, and cultural artifacts. End with the four unique contributions of a cross-cultural approach and the meaning of cultural imagination for the field.","key_concepts":["For conceptual questions such as what happiness is and whether the target concept differs in connotations across cultures, it is useful to use free associations: Uchida and Kitayama (2009) used multidimensional scaling to show that positive hedonic experience overlapped with personal achievement among Americans but almost completely with social harmony among Japanese.","Oishi et al. (2013) collected the dictionary definitions of happiness in thirty countries and found that twenty-four of them had the main definition of happiness as good luck or fortune.","Kashima and Kashima (1998) examined pronoun drop, whether it is acceptable to drop 'I' and 'you,' across thirty-nine languages and seventy-one cultures and found that pronoun drop was more common in less individualistic countries.","Cohen et al. (1996) had southern and northern male participants bumped into by a confederate who called them 'asshole' and found that following the insult, southern participants showed higher cortisol levels, greater cognitive priming for aggression, higher testosterone, and firmer handshakes.","Herrmann et al. (2008) replicated Fehr and Gächter's (2002) public-goods game across a pool of societies and unexpectedly discovered antisocial punishment in samples from Greece, Oman, Saudi Arabia, and Russia, where some participants reduced the earnings of those who contributed more than the punisher did.","Talhelm et al. (2018) set up chairs that blocked an aisle in Starbucks and observed that customers in wheat-growing northern China more often moved the chairs to pass through, whereas those in rice-growing southern China squeezed through without moving them.","Keller et al. (2010) videotaped free-play situations between mothers and their babies and found that mothers from Berlin tended to focus on the cultural model of independence, whereas Delhi mothers tended to focus on the cultural model of autonomous relatedness.","Wheeler et al. (1989) conducted the first cross-cultural event-sampling study in Hong Kong and the U.S. and found that social interactions in Hong Kong were more likely to be in groups (29.2%) than were social interactions in the U.S. (16.7%).","Oishi et al. (2004) conducted a cross-cultural experience sampling study in India, Japan, and the U.S. and found that Japanese participants reported less positive affect when alone than Americans did, but being with friends increased Japanese participants' positive moods more than it did Americans'.","Golder and Macy (2011) text-analyzed Tweets from 2.4 million individuals in eighty-four countries and found that diurnal patterns of positive affect were fairly similar across different cultures.","Adams et al. (2009) asked Japanese and American participants to complete the Reading the Mind in the Eyes test during fMRI and found that the posterior superior temporal sulcus was more strongly activated when participants were seeing in-group faces than when seeing out-group faces.","The chapter concludes that a cross-cultural approach uncovers hidden assumptions about theory, expands the range of potential theoretical explanations, contributes to theory building, and helps establish the universality of behaviors (e.g., mate preferences; Buss, 1989), highlighting the utility of cultural imagination, the ability to see human behaviors not just as the result of dispositions or immediate situations but also as the result of larger cultural contexts."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm06","title":"Chapter 6: Research Design and Issues of Validity","pages":"115-134","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Research Design","topic":"Validity as a Property of Conclusions: Research Purpose and the Types of Validity","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory cleanroom where four glowing specimen jars labeled description, prediction, causal inference, and explanation sit on a bench, each connected by a pipeline to a distillation column labeled 'validity.' Beside the column, three nested sieves labeled internal, external, and construct sift a stream of research conclusions, while a technician sorts sampling cards into random-sampling and random-assignment trays.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundations: classify the four purposes of social psychological research (description, prediction, causal inference, and explanation), match each purpose to its governing validity concern, and distinguish internal, external, and construct validity as defined by Campbell and by Cook and Campbell. Also flag how complex manipulations can confound an independent variable and separate methodological confounds from procedural artifacts.","key_concepts":["Validity refers to the best available approximation to the truth or falsity of propositions (Cook & Campbell, 1979, p. 37), and it is the statements, inferences, or conclusions we wish to draw from the results of empirical research that can be subject to validation, not research per se.","The goals of empirical research in social psychology can be differentiated into four broad categories: description, prediction, causal inference, and explanation.","Survey research such as opinion polling and health surveys is conducted for the purpose of describing characteristics of a specific population, and for such descriptive purposes random sampling from the defined population is the gold standard for valid generalization.","In experimental research, random assignment of participants to the different experimental treatments or conditions serves as a counterpart to random sampling from a population, so that prior to treatment the randomly assigned subgroups can be considered equivalent random samples from the same unknown population.","For drawing valid conclusions about whether two or more variables are correlated, the sample of observations must produce a sufficient degree of variation on the measures of interest, since a restricted sample would invalidate assessment of the relationship, as when running a study at a Mensa meeting to assess the correlation between intelligence and liberalism.","From the utilitarian perspective of causation (Collingwood, 1940; Cook & Campbell, 1979; Gasking, 1955; Mackie, 1974), the purpose of the search for cause-effect relationships is to identify agents that can be controlled or manipulated to bring about changes in an outcome, so the goal of research is to establish causal connections, not to explain how or why they occur.","Research undertaken for the purpose of explanation reflects the essentialist conceptualization of causation and has the goal of determining not only whether causation exists, but also how, why, and under what conditions it occurs.","It was in connection with research intended to establish cause-effect relationships that Campbell introduced the now-classic distinction between internal and external validity (Campbell, 1957; Campbell & Stanley, 1963).","Internal validity refers to the truth value that can be assigned to the conclusion that a cause-effect relationship between an independent variable and a dependent variable has been established in the context of a specific research setting.","External validity in Campbell's original terminology referred to the generalizability of the causal finding, that is, whether the same cause-effect relationship would be obtained across different participants, settings, and methods.","Cook and Campbell (1979) added the term construct validity to refer to the extent to which a causal relationship could be generalized from the methods and operations of a specific study to the theoretical constructs and processes they were meant to represent, and this tripartite distinction organizes the chapter.","Any operation meant to represent a causal construct may be associated with variation in more than one hypothetical state, any one of which may be the true causal variable, which is what experimentalists are referring to when they talk about confounding the independent variable."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Research Design","topic":"The Third Variable Problem: Debating Causal Inference in Social Psychology","visual_frame_description":"A split courtroom stage: on the left an experimenter holds a deck of random-assignment cards, while on the right a field researcher points to a scatterplot of marriage and suicide rates in colonial America. Between them a translucent figure labeled C casts shadows on both X and Y, and two branching overhead pathways are marked 'mediation' and 'moderation.'","player_mission":"Moderate a debate between an experimentalist and a correlational field researcher over the third variable problem. Push them to explain spurious correlation, self-selection, selective attrition, and reactivity, then force both to distinguish mediators from moderators and to defend whether random assignment or the one-by-one ruling out of threats better protects causal inference.","key_concepts":["The essence of internal validity is establishing that variation in an effect or outcome (the dependent variable) has been produced by changes in the level or intensity of the independent variable and not by some other causal force or forces.","When the relationship between X and C is not causal but X and C are correlated, causes of variation in Y can be misattributed to X when they were actually produced by C, a pattern referred to as a spurious correlation between X and Y that underlies the dictum that correlation does not prove causation.","The inverse relation between marriage rates and suicide rates in colonial America could not be interpreted unambiguously as causal because a hidden third factor, the economy, was related both to a decrease in marriage rates and to an increase in suicides, so the conclusion that marriage deters suicide would have low internal validity.","In social psychological research, many potentially problematic third variables are associated with self-selection, because if persons choose for themselves what experiences they will be exposed to, we cannot tell whether the outcome was influenced by the treatment or by correlated individual differences in personality or aptitudes.","Random assignment of participants to different levels of the manipulated factor rules out many potential third-variable threats to causal inference, particularly self-selection, in one operation, whereas correlational or quasi-experimental studies must rule out such threats one by one, even if they can sometimes still justify causal inferences (Rohrer, 2018).","In mediated relationships, a third variable is part of the causal chain linking X to Y, so that varying X causes variations in C, which in turn cause changes in Y, as when marriage exposes a couple to a wider circle of friends and family, reducing social isolation, which in turn may mitigate depression.","In many experiments the mediator is measured rather than manipulated, so its role in the causal chain is correlational, and many mediational analyses fail to test alternative models, leaving questions about whether relationships have been misspecified (Bullock & Green, 2021; Fiedler et al., 2011).","Moderator variables augment or block the causal process, so the causal link is between X and Y but the observed relationship is qualified by levels of variable C, and moderation is assessed statistically as an interaction between X and C in predicting Y (Baron & Kenny, 1986; Marsh et al., 2011).","In the Sivacek and Crano (1982) study, Michigan college students were largely opposed to a proposed law to raise the drinking age, but the relation between their negative attitudes and their willingness to work to defeat the law was weak because vested interest, operationalized by the age of the respondent, moderated the attitude-behavior link.","Reactivity effects, which arise because social psychological research involves social interaction and subjects usually are aware that they are participants, include demand characteristics (Orne, 1962), experimenter expectancies (Rosenthal, 1966), and evaluation apprehension (Rosenberg, 1969).","Demand characteristics that are inherent in the experimental manipulations themselves confound the conceptual interpretation of the causal effect and compromise the construct validity of the independent variable, whereas procedural artifacts such as instructions delivered in ways that vary systematically across conditions threaten the internal validity of causal interpretations.","Selective attrition threatens internal validity when variations in the independent variable are inadvertently correlated with extraneous variables, as when subjects refused to participate in a severe but not a mild shock condition and the overrepresentation of brave or foolhardy subjects in the high-shock group could have caused outcome differences (Zhou & Fishbach, 2016)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Research Design","topic":"From Construct to Operation and Back Again: Auditing the Translation Chain","visual_frame_description":"An assembly-line inspection bay where a conveyor belt carries abstract constructs stamped into concrete operations, with two checkpoints marked Link 1 and Link 2. At the end, a QA panel displays a multitrait-multimethod grid that lights green only where same-trait cells outrank same-method cells, and a stamp reads 'conceptual replication required.'","player_mission":"Act as a validity auditor for a theory-testing experiment. Walk the full translation chain: Link 1 from the theoretical cause to its operationalization and Link 2 from the operations back to the hypothetical effect, apply Rakover's four difficulties, judge the need for conceptual versus exact replication, and rate convergent and discriminant validity, ending with a verdict on whether mono-operation bias or ambiguous mediation undermines the construct interpretation.","key_concepts":["Theories are stated in terms of abstract concepts and hypothetical constructs that cannot be observed or measured directly, so to be subject to empirical testing, theoretical constructs must be translated from the abstract to the more concrete, from concepts to operations that can be observed and replicated.","The conduct of theory-testing research has a progressive nature in which the first link is the stage of translating initial theoretical concepts into empirically testable hypotheses and specific operations, and the second link is the process of inference from empirical results back to the level of theoretical concepts, which then become the starting point for the next cycle of research.","Link 1 refers to the inferential link between the operational definition of the independent variable in an experiment and the corresponding causal concept at the theoretical level, whereas Link 2 refers to the analogous link between the hypothetical effect and the actual response measure assessed in an experiment.","Rakover (1981) claimed that all the links between theory and data are problematic because there are no standardized operations that correspond closely to our hypothetical constructs, and he identified four major difficulties in connecting theory and data: the problems of stimulus and response validity and the problems of unknown range of stimulus variation and unknown range of measurement.","The unknown-range problems refer to the failure to specify precisely what levels of the independent variable are expected to be causally significant and over what range of outcomes, which makes it difficult to determine whether a failure to confirm a predicted relationship represents a failure of theory or a failure of operation, and sensitivity analysis is a useful method to assess the likelihood of this possibility.","Much experimental work in social psychology has been criticized for providing no information about the construct validity of the dependent measure (Flake et al., 2017), even less attention is paid to the construct validity of specific experimental operations (Chester & Lasko, 2021), and manipulation checks are not always adequate to establish stimulus validity (Ejelov & Luke, 2020; Gruijters, 2022).","The purposes of conceptual replication, which employs methods that are as different as possible from the original study to operationalize the same underlying conceptual variables, are distinct from those of exact replication, which duplicates the methods and procedures of a specific study to determine whether the findings are robust and reproducible (Nosek et al., 2022).","In the Aronson and Mills (1959) experiment, cognitive dissonance was induced by having female participants read embarrassing, obscene passages in the guise of an initiation test, but the complex social situation had many potential interpretations, including the possibility that reading obscene materials generated sexual arousal that carried over to reactions to the group discussion (Zillman, 1983).","Gerard and Mathewson (1966) conceptually replicated the initiation experiment using electric shocks justified as a test of emotionality and a group discussion about cheating in college, thereby eliminating sexual arousal as a concomitant of the operationalization, and they confirmed the original finding that people who underwent painful shocks to join a dull group found the group more attractive.","Cook and Campbell (1979) asserted that the most serious threat to the construct validity of any program of research comes from a mono-operation bias, the tendency to use only a single operation or measure to represent a particular theoretical construct, and it takes conceptual replication across multiple different operationalizations of the same construct to establish construct validity.","Convergent validity is established when different operations representing the same underlying theoretical construct produce essentially the same results, and discriminant validity is established when operations representing the construct of interest show the predicted effect whereas other operations that do not reflect the theoretical construct do not have similar effects.","Zanna and Cooper (1974) broke the deadlock between dissonance theory and self-perception theory by manipulating the proposed mediator itself: when the motivational effects of arousal were blocked through misattribution, attitude change following counterattitudinal behavior did not occur, demonstrating that the presence of arousal was necessary to produce the typical attitude change effect."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Research Design","topic":"The Many Faces of External Validity: Robustness, Ecological Validity, and Relevance","visual_frame_description":"A three-column holographic matrix floating in a dark room, each column labeled robustness, ecological validity, and relevance, with rotating icons of laboratories, coffee shops, and airplane cockpits. A control strip at the bottom toggles research purposes, and the matrix reweights which column glows, while a small plaque reads 'the quest for external validity is a search for moderators.'","player_mission":"Run the three-way generalizability matrix. For each of the three faces of external validity, generate the defining question, the acceptance test, and the failure mode, then assign each form to the research purposes (descriptive, utilitarian, theory-testing) where it matters most, ending with Mook's verdict on the generality of findings versus the generality of conclusions.","key_concepts":["External validity refers to the question whether an effect, along with its underlying processes, that has been demonstrated in one research setting will be obtained in other settings, at different times, with different research participants, and with different research procedures.","External validity is not a single construct; it is concerned with a whole set of questions about generalizability, and the chapter discusses three of the most important forms: robustness, ecological validity, and relevance.","Robustness refers to whether a particular finding is replicable across a variety of settings, persons, and historical contexts, and some findings appear fragile, obtainable only under highly controlled conditions in a specific context, whereas others hold up despite significant variations in the conditions under which they are tested.","Technically, robustness is demonstrated if a research study is conducted with a random sample of participants from a broadly defined population in a random sampling of settings, but such designs are usually impractical and not cost-effective, so this form of generalizability is more often established by repeated replications in systematically sampled settings and types of research participants.","Critics argue that the restriction of populations and settings characteristic of laboratory research means that social psychology has a narrow database on which to draw conclusions about human nature, and historically the majority of experimental research has been conducted with the so-called WEIRD population of North American and Western European participants (Henrich et al., 2010; Sears, 1986).","Studies conducted in psychology laboratories are not any less generalizable to the world at large than any other specific type of person or setting, and the real issue is the overrepresentation of a specific type of participant and setting across many studies that all share the same limitations on external validity.","Cook and Campbell (1979) made it clear that all questions of external validity, or generalizability, are implicitly questions about interactions between the independent variable (treatment) and contextual variables such as participant selection, history, and research setting, so the quest for external validity is essentially a search for moderators that limit or qualify the cause-effect relationship.","Ecological validity concerns whether the conditions of an experiment match or map onto the real-world situations that inspired the research, and the concept derives from Brunswik's (1956) advocacy of representative design, in which research is conducted with probabilistic samplings of participants and situations.","For most social psychologists, ecological validity is too restrictive a conceptualization of generalizability for research designed to test causal hypotheses, and an experimental setting may have little mundane realism but still capture psychological processes that are highly representative of those underlying events in the real world (Aronson et al., 1998).","Relevance in its broader meaning refers to whether findings are potentially useful or applicable to solving problems or improving quality of life, and it does not necessarily depend on the physical resemblance between the research setting and the setting of application, as perceptual research on eye-hand coordination in artificial laboratory settings proved valuable to the design of airplane instrument panels.","External validity must be evaluated with respect to the purposes for which research is being conducted: when the research agenda is fundamentally descriptive, ecological validity may be essential; when the purpose is utilitarian, robustness of an effect is particularly critical; and when the purpose is testing explanatory theory, construct validity is more important than other forms of external validity.","Mook (1983) argued that the importance of external validity has been exaggerated in the psychological sciences, drew an important distinction between the generality of findings and the generality of conclusions, and held that it is understanding the processes themselves, not the specific finding, that has external validity."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Research Design","topic":"Optimizing Validity: Trade-Offs, Complementarities, and Cumulative Theory Testing","visual_frame_description":"A grand auditorium with a balance beam suspended over the stage, one pan labeled lab and the other field, held level by a set of complementary weights. Behind the speaker, a map of a research program connects many small studies into a single constellation labeled 'validity as a product of cumulative theory testing,' and an equation glows on the back wall: abstract enough to generalize, precise enough to test.","player_mission":"Deliver the final synthesis on optimizing validity: reconcile Campbell and Stanley's precedence claim with the trade-offs between lab and field settings and between isolation and construct validity, then lay out the complementarity principle for research programs and end with the ultimate challenge that constructs be abstract enough to generalize yet precise enough to test.","key_concepts":["Campbell and Stanley (1963) took the position that internal validity is the sine qua non of experimental research and takes precedence over questions of external validity, and controversies about the relative importance of different validity concerns have ensued among research methodologists ever since.","It is possible to conduct a single research study with the goal of maximizing internal validity, but questions of external validity and construct validity can rarely be addressed within the context of a single research design and require systematic, programmatic studies that address a particular question across different participants, operations, and research settings.","It is patently unfair to expect that any single piece of research has high internal, external, and construct validity simultaneously, and it is more appropriate to require that programs of research be designed in ways that address validity issues of all types.","It is a common assumption that laboratory research achieves high internal validity at the expense of external validity, whereas research in natural field settings is associated with greater external validity at the cost of more threats to internal validity, but there is no invariable association between the setting in which research is conducted and its degree of internal, external, or construct validity.","Conducting research in a naturalistic context does not by itself confer external validity, just as conducting an experiment in the laboratory does not itself confer internal validity, and even if the setting is highly representative, ecological validity is suspect if the research introduces conditions into the setting that do not occur spontaneously.","It is the complementarity of field and lab as research settings that contributes to validity, not the characteristics of either setting alone, and the convergence of results in the two settings greatly enhances our confidence in the findings from both sets of operations.","The field version of a basic research paradigm cannot be expected to be simply a transplanted replication of the laboratory operations, because researchers have considerably less control in the field setting with respect to extraneous sources of variation and manipulation checks may prove impossible, so the results of a field experiment as a single isolated study would be difficult to interpret without the parallel of conceptually related laboratory experiments.","Laboratory experiments are inherently artificial in the sense that causal variables are isolated from their normal contextual variation, and isolation and control are the essence of testing causal hypotheses with a high degree of internal validity, but isolation does not necessarily jeopardize external validity if the experimental situation has psychological realism.","Greenwood (1982) called the problem of the artificiality of alteration the stickiest issue of validity, because bringing a variable into the laboratory changes its nature, although the issue applies to any context in which a phenomenon is observed, since psychological experiences are never the same from one time or place to another.","The ultimate challenge for valid theory building in social psychology is that theoretical constructs must be abstract enough to generalize across a range of contexts and specific manifestations, yet precise enough to permit empirical testing.","Validity is never the achievement of a single research project but the product of cumulative theory testing and application, because each empirical demonstration is inevitably limited to a specific context and participant sample and subject to multiple interpretations, but each time a theoretical proposition is tested in a new setting or with new operations, a contribution is made to the overall picture.","Although much current research involves multiple independent variables and their interactive effects on multiple dependent variables in the context of complex multivariate causal models, the distinctions among internal validity, construct validity, and external validity as different criteria for evaluation are just as applicable to multivariate, multilevel research as they are to interpreting the simplest bivariate relationship."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm07","title":"Chapter 7: Experimental Design","pages":"136-158","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Experimental Design","topic":"Statistical Power and Research Design","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a whiteboard covered with power curves and a sample-size calculator. A bar chart shows the median power of social psychological studies dropping below fifty percent, while a warning panel flashes Type M and Type S error labels next to a low-power study's inflated effect estimate.","player_mission":"You are a study designer who must plan a new experiment. Compute how statistical power depends on alpha, sample size, and effect size, and explain why underpowered studies are dangerous. Decide how to allocate a fixed number of participants across conditions so each planned hypothesis test is as powerful as possible.","key_concepts":["Statistical power is the probability that a study will attain a statistically significant result for a particular effect, assuming that effect has a specific nonzero size in the population.","Researchers usually aim to achieve power of 80 percent or better for the effects they test.","For a given effect in a design, power depends on the chosen alpha or significance level (conventionally 0.05), the number of participants or other units, and the assumed size of the effect.","An a priori power analysis indicates the required number of participants given an estimated effect size, which may come from a similar study, a meta-analysis, or a domain-wide meta-analysis.","For an already completed study with fixed N, a sensitivity analysis can indicate the smallest effect size the study would have been able to detect.","Best practice today is to conduct a power analysis to select the sample size before running a study, to ensure the study is adequately powered.","As long ago as 1962, Cohen found that published social psychological studies had a median power of only 0.46 to detect a medium-sized effect, meaning researchers had less than an even chance of finding a significant result.","A low-powered study gives imprecise and highly variable estimates of effects, and a significant result from it is very likely to represent a Type M error (overestimating the effect's magnitude) or even a Type S error (getting the wrong sign).","Running several low-powered studies is dangerous because one may pop up as significant by chance alone even when the procedural variations being tested make no real difference.","The way a fixed number of participants is allocated across conditions affects power, and no single allocation is optimal for all hypotheses simultaneously.","Factors other than participant number that affect power include the choice of within-participant versus between-participant designs, covariates or blocking factors, reliable measurement of dependent variables, and fixed versus random treatment of factors."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Experimental Design","topic":"Independent Variables and Factors","visual_frame_description":"You sit at a drafting table covered with factor cards labeled between and within, next to a diagram of participants flowing into groups. A scale of manipulation strength runs along the wall, and a panel contrasts one scenario about a fixed set of accident scenarios with a random sample of scenarios drawn from a larger population.","player_mission":"You are a researcher designing the independent variables for an experiment on persuasion and person perception. Choose whether each factor is between-participant or within-participant, decide whether its levels are fixed or random, and justify the extremity and number of levels you select for each manipulation.","key_concepts":["An experimental design always has at least two factors: one corresponding to participants (or other randomly assigned units) and at least one other independent variable.","A between-participant factor means distinct groups of participants are exposed to the different levels of the factor, with participants randomly assigned to those groups.","A within-participant factor means each participant is exposed to more than one level of the factor, and in an experimental design the order of exposure is what is randomly assigned.","Randomization means that differences between groups on the dependent variable cannot readily be attributed to alternative causes like differences in group composition.","With a fixed factor, the levels used in a study are selected for their intrinsic theoretical or practical interest, and conclusions apply only to those levels.","With a random factor, the levels used in the experiment are regarded as a sample from a larger population of levels, and the participants factor in a typical experiment is the obvious example.","As a guideline for deciding between fixed and random treatment, a factor is fixed if one would leave its levels the same when replicating the study, and random if one would sample other similar levels instead.","Reusing the same materials across studies is dangerous, as the risky shift literature showed: the effect was specific to particular decision problems and was later reconceptualized as group polarization.","Ecological design emphasizes constructing manipulations that match treatments found in everyday life, such as sampling accident scenarios from police reports.","Strong manipulations can create larger effect sizes and higher statistical power, but some effects of everyday life, like the death of a close relative, cannot be ethically or practically reproduced as manipulations.","For an ordered continuum, a typical design uses just high and low levels to maximize power for a linear effect, while more levels can reveal nonlinear or U-shaped effects at a cost in power.","Dichotomizing a continuous variable throws away valid variance and reduces power, and Maxwell and Delaney showed it can even make another factor appear significant when it is not."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Experimental Design","topic":"Relations among Factors: Crossing, Nesting, and Counterbalancing","visual_frame_description":"You review a wall of factor-relation diagrams: crossed grids, nested trees, and a tangle labeled confounded. A timeline of conditions scrolls past, with carryover effects leaking between adjacent cells, while a checkerboard counterbalancing grid shows how stimulus sets can be swapped across participant groups.","player_mission":"You are a design auditor reviewing a proposed experiment on memory for expectation-consistent versus inconsistent behaviors. Classify each pair of factors as crossed, nested, or confounded, evaluate the within-participant design for carryover and order effects, and recommend counterbalancing or other corrections before the study is approved.","key_concepts":["Multiple factors in a design can be crossed, nested, or confounded, and crossed factors include each level of one factor in combination with each level of the other.","Nested factors involve multiple levels of a subsidiary factor that occur uniquely within a given level of a more encompassing factor, and confounded factors are conceptually distinct variables whose effects cannot be empirically disentangled.","A between-participant design nests participants within all other factors, while a within-participant design crosses one or more factors with participants, and a mixed design has both kinds of factors.","Power is often greater for within-participant manipulations because differences among participants, the largest source of variance, inflate the error term only for between-participant effects.","Carryover effects occur when a participant's response in a given condition depends on experimental conditions experienced previously, for example because previous stimuli serve as anchors for reactions to the next stimulus.","Participants in a within-participant design see more than one condition and are better able to guess the hypotheses, creating demand characteristics that can bias their responses.","The most important reason for including crossed factors is to test a theoretically predicted interaction, since theories that predict a specific interaction pattern rather than a simple main effect gain more in credibility when confirmed.","A single dissociation shows one manipulation affecting one measure but not another, and a full double-dissociation design manipulates both constructs to rule out alternative explanations like insensitive measures.","Tests of interactions generally have much lower power than tests of main effects: detecting a predicted interaction in a two-by-two design typically requires quadrupling the sample size compared with detecting the original main effect.","When factors entering an interaction are measured rather than manipulated, power is further reduced by few cases with extreme values on both factors and by measurement unreliability.","Mathematical transformations can create or remove ordinal interactions, so claims about the presence or absence of an interaction are weak unless the applicability of transformations can be ruled out on theoretical grounds.","Adding crossed factors that represent theoretically irrelevant sources of variance, termed covariates (if continuous) or blocking factors (if categorical), reduces error variance and increases statistical power."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Experimental Design","topic":"Dependent Variables: Pretesting and Item Selection","visual_frame_description":"You arrange blocks on a measurement matrix: a single gold-standard item, a stack of Likert items awaiting averaging, and a column of items coded as a within-participants factor. A warning light labeled pretest sensitization flickers next to a timeline that shows where the pretest is placed in the session.","player_mission":"You are building the dependent measures for an attitude change study. Decide whether to include a pretest, choose how many items to use, and select a strategy for combining them, weighing power and reliability against the risk of pretest sensitization for each measurement choice.","key_concepts":["Pretesting participants with a measure identical or related to the dependent variable can increase power when the pretest is used as a covariate or to create blocks of participants with similar pretest scores.","If the covariate's within-groups correlation with the dependent variable is r, the experiment's error variance decreases by approximately a factor of 1 minus r squared in either a randomized-blocks or covariance design.","A covariate with a correlation of 0.70 with the dependent variable would cut error variance roughly in half, doubling the study's power.","Pretests are common in studies using physiological measures with large individual differences, such as measuring blood pressure reactivity while a participant relaxes to collect a baseline.","The main reason pretests are often not used is concern about pretest sensitization, because participants who have completed a pretest may respond differently to a manipulation than unpretested participants.","Pretest sensitization can be made less likely by administering the pretest in a separate session unconnected in participants' minds with the main experiment, such as a mass pretesting session at the start of an academic term.","Using a single item selected from pilot testing minimizes the participant's time and effort but may lead to low reliability, although a single gold-standard measure is appropriate in some areas, such as the cardiac measure of pre-ejection period.","The most common approach is to average or sum several items with the same response scale, which gains power by increasing reliability and increases construct validity by more broadly sampling the content domain.","Treating item as an explicit within-participant factor corresponds to systematically varying a contextual factor and permits confidence about generality if the effect holds across items, but items should then be treated as a random factor requiring a large number of items.","Analyzing multiple dependent variables separately should be avoided if they are parallel measures of the same construct, because conflicting results would be uninterpretable, but separate analyses are reasonable for measures of distinct constructs.","When measures of multiple conceptual variables are included and hypotheses concern their causal and mediational interrelationships, the data can be analyzed in a multiple-group structural equation framework."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Experimental Design","topic":"Confounds, Artifacts, and Studying Mediation","visual_frame_description":"You command a control room with panels monitoring every study session: a cover-story integrity gauge, an experimenter blindness switch, and an attrition tracker that charts who drops out in each condition in real time. A wall map shows a causal chain X to M to Y, with dashed lines marking the untestable assumptions that a single study cannot verify.","player_mission":"You are the chief methodologist in charge of a high-stakes online experiment on stereotypes and decision making. Protect the study against demand characteristics, experimenter expectancies, social comparison effects, and differential attrition, and then plan a multi-study strategy that can support a mediation claim about the causal chain.","key_concepts":["Demand characteristics arise because participants' perceptions of the purpose of the research may influence their responses, and a coherent believable cover story can at least ensure all participants hold the same view of the purpose.","Keeping experimenters or confederates unaware of each participant's condition prevents unconscious biases from causing subtle differential treatment, whereas merely keeping them unaware of the hypotheses offers no real protection.","Participants who see treatments as differentially desirable may react with compensatory rivalry or demoralization, and mood effects from manipulations are a related potential confound that experimenters often assess and statistically check.","Differential treatment-related attrition can falsely produce differences between conditions, and even equal attrition rates across conditions are a problem because the remaining subpopulations may no longer be equivalent.","Zhou and Fishbach recommend inducing less motivated participants to drop out before random assignment and collecting demographics and personality measures before assignment so analyses can show which participants drop out.","Reporting attrition, both overall and per condition, is essential for online studies, where large proportions of participants may drop out or fail minimal data quality checks.","A typical mediation design manipulates X and measures both the dependent variable Y and one or more putative mediators M, and statistical evidence for mediation is traditionally obtained from several separate regression equations.","Experimental manipulation assures that X causally precedes both M and Y, but the design does not establish causal priority between the latter two variables.","Standard mediation analysis requires strong assumptions, including that all unobserved causes of M are uncorrelated with unobserved causes of Y, and these assumptions generally are not empirically testable within a single study.","Mediation cannot generally be demonstrated with a single statistical procedure or a single study, but can be firmly supported only by convergent patterns of results from multiple studies, often from multiple research teams.","In social psychology today the most important unit of research is not the individual study but the research program, a series of conceptually related studies developing a common theoretical theme.","The strategic maxim proposed is that more smaller studies are often better than fewer larger ones, provided they possess adequate power, because a series of small studies is stronger in construct and external validity than a single massive study."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm08","title":"Chapter 8: Quasi-Experimental Designs","pages":"160-191","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Quasi-Experimental Design","topic":"What Is a Quasi-Experiment and the Validity Framework","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a lecture screen with three study-type cards and arrows showing how assignment to conditions occurs in each one. A validity diamond labeled statistical conclusion, construct, external, and internal validity spins beside a causal-inference meter that moves as you test the association, direction, and isolation conditions.","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor preparing students to distinguish study types. Classify studies as randomized experiments, quasi-experiments, or nonexperimental studies based on how assignment to the independent variable occurs, and apply the four types of validity from Cook and Campbell (1979) to a set of study descriptions. Use the association, direction, and isolation conditions to judge how strong a causal claim each design can support.","key_concepts":["A quasi-experiment is a type of study that attempts to mimic the objectives and structure of traditional randomized experiments, and like an experiment its goal is to evaluate the causal effect of an independent variable (IV) on a dependent variable (DV) of interest.","The primary distinction between quasi-experiments and experiments is that condition assignment is randomized in experiments whereas it is not randomized in quasi-experiments.","Quasi-experiments are typically used in contexts where it is not possible to randomly assign participants to conditions because of practical constraints or ethical concerns.","Unlike nonexperimental studies, assignment to levels of the IV in a quasi-experiment is usually not solely the result of natural variation but is typically based on some researcher-specified criterion that is at least somewhat under the researcher's control or understood by the researcher.","Even when natural variation has determined assignment in a quasi-experiment, the threshold value for assignment is at minimum known by and often determined by the researcher, and variation above and below the threshold has no implications for assignment.","Quasi-experiments are frequently conducted in field settings, because these settings sometimes make random assignment unfeasible or unethical, and they often address applied research questions.","The Cook and Campbell (1979) framework conceptualizes the validity of a study in terms of statistical conclusion validity, construct validity, external validity, and internal validity.","Statistical conclusion validity is the accuracy of a conclusion regarding a relation between or among variables in a study, and methodologists have typically not assumed a general advantage of any study type on this dimension.","Construct validity is the degree to which operationalizations of IVs and DVs reflect their intended constructs, since no single operationalization is likely to perfectly reflect its intended construct.","External validity is the degree to which the results of a given study can be generalized to other contexts and populations, and it must be judged in the context of the researchers' goals.","Internal validity refers to the inferential strength with which an observed relation among variables can be interpreted as causal in nature.","Bollen (1989) suggested that internal validity is high to the extent that one can build a compelling case for having satisfied three conditions: association, direction, and isolation, where the condition of isolation is achieved by removing the distorting effects of all other causal influences on the DV."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Quasi-Experimental Design","topic":"Threats to Causal Assumptions in Quasi-Experiments","visual_frame_description":"You face a wall of time-lapse panels showing an intervention study across a school year, with ghost arrows labeled history, maturation, and attrition poking at the outcome line. A deck of threat cards sits on your desk, each one marked with how it undermines the condition of isolation.","player_mission":"You are a research consultant reviewing a school-based intervention study in which assignment was not random. Diagnose which internal validity threats, including history, maturation, selection, attrition, testing, and instrumentation effects, could explain the observed outcome changes. Recommend design modifications that would make each identified threat implausible before the study is rerun.","key_concepts":["Each of the threats to causal assumptions in quasi-experiments can be understood as undermining the condition of isolation.","A history threat is any influence that occurs between participants' exposure to an IV and the assessment of the DV that might shape participants' outcome responses.","Maturation effects refer to endogenous changes in participants across time, either immediately, such as participants becoming tired after exposure to an intervention, or in the longer term, such as child participants developing greater cognitive skills as they age.","Selection threats arise when participants' judgments or actions determine their assignment to conditions, something that always occurs when the IV reflects naturally occurring variation on a measure.","Attrition threats arise when participants drop out of a study at systematically different rates, or for systematically different reasons, in a way associated with their standing on the IV.","Testing effects capture shifts in participants' understanding, interpretation, or subjective view of measures, whereas instrumentation effects refer to shifts in the measurement instrument itself, such as a gradual loss of sensitivity in a physical measurement tool.","Random assignment makes history and maturational effects unlikely because control and intervention participants should be equally susceptible to external events and endogenous changes.","A key advantage of the experiment is that random assignment mitigates the risk that selection can occur at different rates or for different reasons depending on condition.","Attrition can be an issue even in experiments, because if dropout rates are substantial but at comparable levels across conditions, attrition could still threaten isolation if the psychological factors producing attrition in the two conditions are different and exert a causal impact on the DV.","If quasi-experimental condition assignment is based on properties of the participants that influence attrition, those properties might exert influences on the DV that may be incorrectly attributed to the IV.","If the criterion that determines condition assignment is associated with the direction or magnitude of testing or instrumentation effects, internal validity can be threatened in quasi-experimental and nonexperimental studies."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Quasi-Experimental Design","topic":"Nonequivalent Comparison Group Designs and Statistical Control","visual_frame_description":"You sit before a balance scale with an intervention school on one side and a control school on the other, labeled not equivalent. A covariate tree grows beside it with propensity scores glowing as matched pairs light up, and a data grid shows classrooms nested inside schools to flag nonindependent observations.","player_mission":"You are a design auditor evaluating a district's new academic skills program tested with an intervention group and a control group of schools. Inspect the covariate set and the propensity score analysis for the assumption of strong ignorability, decide whether a pretest of the DV was included, and judge how the hierarchical structure of the data was handled. Recommend propensity score stratification or a switching replication design where the evidence is weak.","key_concepts":["Nonequivalent comparison group design is a family of quasi-experimental designs in which researchers acknowledge that a control group and an intervention group are not equivalent because of some nonrandom assignment of participants to conditions.","A crucial limitation of the nonequivalent comparison group design is the assumption of strong ignorability, the idea that all possible variables that might make the control and intervention groups non-equivalent have somehow been captured and addressed.","The simplest approach to debiasing the causal estimate is to include covariates that might be associated with the IV, by measuring the alternative causal variables and including them in statistical analyses.","Research suggests that it is often better to include relatively diverse domains of covariates, more domains of covariates, and more covariates within each domain, for example child cognitive skills, child demographics, child social skills, teacher demographics, and home environment.","Propensity scores are composite variables calculated from several other measured variables that are intended to capture variance that could conceivably be confounded with membership in conditions.","A propensity score represents the probability that a person will be in one condition rather than another given that person's observed scores on a set of covariates selected to predict the person's condition.","Detected differences in propensity score distributions by condition can be adjusted in numerous ways, such as stratifying the sample by propensity score quintiles.","Propensity scores can only reduce biases insofar as the measures used to calculate them are internally reliable, and to a degree the sheer volume of measures can help counteract the deleterious effect of unreliable measures.","The most basic design is the posttest-only nonequivalent-groups design, in which the control and intervention groups' scores on the DV are compared without measuring the DV at any other timepoint, and selection and attrition factors could prompt artificial differences between the groups.","Designs that employ a pretest of the DV are termed pretest-posttest comparison group designs, and selection biases are less likely to drive results in these designs because control-versus-intervention selection differences that affect the DV are accounted for by the pretest measure, although history effects remain possible.","In switching replication with treatment removal designs, treatment assignment is reversed over time so that a causal effect can be triangulated, making it increasingly implausible that maturity or history effects would account for the shifting patterns on the DV.","When data are hierarchical, such as observations nested within schools, neighborhoods, or wards, statistical conclusion validity is undermined if this nonindependence is not modeled with multilevel modeling, and statistical power is also influenced by the degree of nonindependence."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Quasi-Experimental Design","topic":"Regression Discontinuity Designs","visual_frame_description":"You gaze at a scatterplot of aptitude scores against scholarship rates with a vertical cutoff line and a substantial jump in the regression line at the boundary. A model-fitting panel lets you toggle linear and curvilinear functional forms, while a power meter warns that the design needs roughly three to four times as many participants as an experiment.","player_mission":"You are an evaluator for a scholarship program that awards certificates of merit to students scoring at or above a cutoff on an aptitude measure. Estimate the intervention's causal effect from the discontinuity at the cutoff, check whether the functional form of the selection-outcome relation is correctly modeled, and judge whether the design has adequate statistical power. Consider adding a pretest or an exclusively control sample to strengthen the estimates.","key_concepts":["Researchers can estimate the causal effect of the IV using a regression discontinuity design in cases where interventions are allocated strictly based on a cutoff point on a known variable, as when psychiatric patients are assigned therapy given a particular level of depression.","The regression discontinuity design capitalizes on the fact that although condition assignment is not random, the basis of assignment is known: participants' level on a quantitative assignment variable.","The relation between the selection variable and the DV is assumed to show a consistent, though not necessarily linear, pattern across levels of the selection variable, which is called the functional form.","Any discontinuity in the relation between the selection variable and the DV that occurs at the cutoff point determining inclusion in the experimental versus control groups is assumed to represent the causal impact of the intervention.","The first published regression discontinuity study was by Thistlewaite and Campbell (1960), in which 5,126 students received certificates of merit and another 2,848 did not, and their outcomes were tracked six months later.","In the basic linear implementation, the causal effect of the intervention is estimated through a regression equation in which an indicator variable is scored 0 if the participant is in the control group and 1 if the participant is in the experimental group.","The regression discontinuity design can demonstrate the causal impact of the IV even in the presence of nonlinearity in the relation between the selection variable and the DV, so long as the relation is correctly modeled.","A widely discussed limitation of the design is the loss of statistical power relative to experiments, with common estimates that regression discontinuity studies need about 2.75 or three to four times more participants to reach a comparable level of statistical power.","Regression discontinuity designs technically only provide evidence of an average causal effect at the level of the selection variable that forms the cutoff point, and thus are limited in their ability to generalize across levels of the selection variable.","The regression discontinuity with control group design adds a second sample that is exclusively a control group, which helps to determine the functional form between the selection and outcome variables in the absence of the intervention and increases statistical power.","Supplementing the regression discontinuity design with a pretest of the DV has been demonstrated to improve researchers' ability to extrapolate causal effect estimates beyond the cutoff point.","Methodologists have often proposed regression discontinuity as the gold standard for quasi-experiments, and when optimal designs have been compared against randomized experiments they often produce very similar results, suggesting an almost complete elimination of bias."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Quasi-Experimental Design","topic":"Interrupted Time Series Designs","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a long timeline chart of weekly outcome data with a vertical intervention marker and a slope that visibly shifts at the break point. Side panels show an ARIMA parameter console, an A-B-A toggle that withdraws the intervention, and a second grey control-group line tracking flat beside the experimental line.","player_mission":"You are the lead analyst for a hospital policy change that took effect on a known date, with weekly outcome measurements collected before and after. Conduct a segmented regression analysis, model autocorrelation with ARIMA, and identify whether the intervention's effect takes step, pulse, or ramp form. Strengthen the design by adding a control series, an A-B-A phase, or multiple baselines before delivering the causal verdict.","key_concepts":["Interrupted time series designs use multiple measurements of a DV assessed on a regular basis, and researchers assess whether there is a disruption in the relation between time and the DV at the timepoint when an intervention occurs.","In the most basic version of this design there is no true control group, because observations collected when the intervention is not occurring are the control condition being compared against observations collected during the intervention.","The classic tool for analyzing interrupted time series data is segmented regression analysis, and at its most basic level this form of analysis matches the equations used for regression discontinuity analysis.","Autocorrelation refers to the idea that observations closer to one another in time will not be entirely independent of one another, and it can take the form of negative autocorrelation or the more common positive autocorrelation.","Autocorrelation can be modeled with varying levels of sophistication using ARIMA (autoregressive integrated moving average) models, whose three basic parameters are p, d, and q, each of which can be as low as zero.","An intervention's effect can be understood as having a step form, representing a sudden sustained change in the outcome variable, a pulse form, representing a sudden but temporary change that reverts back to baseline, or a ramp form, representing a change in the slope that gradually takes place across time.","The most basic interrupted time series design is called an A-B design, and toggling the intervention on and off produces the A-B-A design, in which alternative explanations like history effects are increasingly implausible because an external event would have to begin and end exactly with the intervention.","History and maturation explanations become decreasingly plausible as the number of pre-intervention measures increases, because the pattern of change in the outcome measure prior to the intervention is explicitly modeled.","Adding an intervention-irrelevant DV, such as attitudes toward school cafeteria food when studying attitudes toward science, helps show that an intervention did not simply capitalize on a general shift in circumstances on that date.","The comparative interrupted time series (CITS) design measures a control group whose observations are collected across the same time period as the intervention group but which does not receive the intervention, and there is wide consensus that CITS methods are superior to simple ITS designs.","In a multiple-baseline design, multiple groups eligible to receive an intervention are allocated different dates to receive it, and the mere effect of time is ruled out by showing that whichever group is not receiving the intervention does not demonstrate any pronounced increases.","Collecting many pre-intervention measurements of the DV is important, with at least fifty recommended by Glass et al. (1975), though some have questioned the evidentiary basis for this assertion."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm09","title":"Chapter 9: Field Research Methods","pages":"193-219","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Field Research","topic":"What Is Field Research? Naturalism, Online Research, and the Advantages of the Field","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a lecture screen with four naturalism meters labeled participants, context, treatment, and outcome measures, each tilting between low and high. A classifier panel flashes the Harrison and List taxonomy cards, while a globe on the side shows field settings from factories to postwar communities where randomized interventions take place.","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor preparing a lesson that defines field research. Assess the naturalism of a set of study designs along the four dimensions of participants, context, treatment, and outcome measures, and classify each design as a lab experiment, artefactual field experiment, framed field experiment, or natural field experiment. Then judge whether online research counts as field research and weigh the advantages of the field for theory testing.","key_concepts":["Field research is defined as research with a high degree of naturalism, meaning the researcher gathers evidence in contexts where the individual decisions, social interactions, and group dynamics of interest naturally take place.","The naturalism of a study can be assessed with four considerations: whether the participants resemble the general actors who ordinarily encounter the phenomenon, whether the context resembles the context of interest, whether the treatment resembles the intervention of interest, and whether the outcome measures resemble the actual outcomes of interest.","A study that has a high degree of naturalism on some dimensions but a low degree of naturalism on others may often still be considered field research.","Harrison and List (2004) classify experiments high in naturalism in participants alone as artefactual field experiments (also known as lab-in-the-field experiments), experiments high in naturalism in participants, treatment, and outcome measures as framed field experiments, and experiments high in naturalism on all dimensions as natural field experiments.","Research that recruits participants through crowdsourcing platforms such as Amazon Mechanical Turk and Prolific often resembles traditional laboratory experiments in that the research paradigms have a low degree of naturalism.","Other types of online research, such as varying the messages or advertisements people are exposed to on social media sites, take place in highly naturalistic settings and can be evaluated as field research with the same four naturalism dimensions.","Field research is not synonymous with applied research, because while some field research tests the direct application of existing theory, other field research investigates basic or exploratory questions in an ideal context for building and refining theory.","Ecological validity refers to how well a study's variables and findings can be generalized to a real-world context, and the high degree of naturalism in field research boosts this form of validity.","Results from field research are typically more generalizable to the population being studied and better predict the target population's behaviors in real-world settings than results from laboratory experiments.","Field research enables psychologists to study topics and mental processes that cannot be faithfully or ethically abstracted in a constrained laboratory, such as the mental processes of violence and conflict or the experience of prolonged poverty.","Field research broadens research samples to include populations that are not typically recruited into laboratory research and broadens research settings to communities and world regions where laboratories do not exist.","Field experiments, one subtype of field research, export experimental control from the laboratory to the field, allowing researchers to test the causal impact of a treatment in the presence of other moving social environmental factors."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Field Research","topic":"Randomizing in the Field: Cluster, Block, and Waitlist Designs","visual_frame_description":"You sit at a conference table facing a factory floor diagram with sixty work groups of ten employees each, and a randomization console offers three switches labeled simple, cluster, and block. A timeline panel shows markets rolling into treatment in waves for the waitlist design, with a lottery drum on the side.","player_mission":"You are a research consultant helping a field team choose a randomization scheme for a textile factory experiment on participatory group meetings. Determine when simple individual randomization fails, decide whether cluster randomization or block randomization fits the research question and constraints, and advise on a waitlist design when withholding the treatment is not practically or ethically feasible. Justify each choice with the statistical consequences of the scheme.","key_concepts":["In a randomized experiment, a random procedure such as a coin flip determines whether a subject is assigned to a treatment or a control condition, so any difference observed between conditions after the treatments are administered can be attributed to the specific treatments.","Field researchers can randomly assign individuals, groups, or institutions to different treatment and control (or placebo) conditions in the field, and social psychologists have done so in national parks, factories, day-care centers, schools, and postwar communities.","Under cluster randomization, groups of individuals (the clusters) such as couples and classrooms are assigned to a treatment or a control condition instead of individual people.","Cluster randomization will generate more sampling variability than simple randomization if subjects within the same cluster tend to share common features that are related to the measured outcome.","Cluster randomization is sometimes necessary for theoretical reasons, because psychologists interested in group and community psychology often need to treat the group or the community rather than a single individual as their experimental unit.","Cluster randomization is sometimes necessary for practical reasons, because certain interventions such as television broadcasts and teaching curriculums can only be implemented on the cluster level, and treatments can also spill over between individuals who interact.","Block randomization is a procedure where participants are first partitioned into subgroups, called blocks or strata, and then complete random assignment occurs within each block.","Block randomization can address constraints from practical or ethical imperatives, such as an education program that requires how many participants with particular characteristics must be placed into a treatment condition.","Block randomization reduces sampling variability and ensures that certain subgroups are available for separate analysis, and it produces the most gain in estimation precision when the blocking variables strongly predict the outcome variables.","Because outcome variables cannot be observed until a study is conducted, researchers look at prior literature and field observations to predict which variables that are not affected by the manipulation are the strongest predictors of the outcomes.","In a waitlist design, also known as a stepped-wedge design or randomized rollout, every experimental unit will eventually be treated and random assignment determines the point in time at which each unit receives the treatment.","The waitlist design combines elements of within-subject and between-subject designs and enables researchers to extract statistically meaningful estimates from relatively small numbers of subjects, though for unbiased estimates assumptions about noninterference need to be made."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Field Research","topic":"Statistical Implementation: Spillover, Attrition, and Noncompliance","visual_frame_description":"You face a network diagram of participants with dashed influence arrows crossing between treatment and control nodes, labeled spillover, and a dropout funnel where subjects fall out at different rates by condition. A compliance ledger lists treated, untreated, and partially treated cases beside an instrumental-variables console.","player_mission":"You are a data auditor reviewing a field experiment's inference quality before publication. Check whether the noninterference assumption holds in the design, identify sources of attrition and how missing outcome data were handled, and verify how noncompliance with assigned conditions was measured and addressed. Recommend encouragement designs, inverse probability weighting, or bounds on the average treatment effect where the evidence is weak.","key_concepts":["The noninterference assumption holds that one person receiving a treatment should not affect the potential outcomes of another person, and it is one of the core assumptions needed to establish unbiased causal estimates.","Interference, also known as spillover, refers to the situation in which a participant's potential outcomes vary not only according to the experimental condition they receive but also according to the experimental conditions that other participants receive.","Spatial spillover takes place across different participants or experimental units in a field site, as when the causal effect of vaccination on an individual depends on whether the people around them are vaccinated.","Intertemporal interference happens within a participant or experimental unit in the dimension of time, as when participants remember past interventions or respond to future interventions differently as a result of past treatments.","Researchers can accommodate spillover with experimental designs that relax the noninterference assumption by randomly assigning varying degrees of secondhand exposure, and can use nonexperimental units to assess the nature of the spillover, such as its decay over geography or time.","Attrition occurs when a participant drops out of the research and their outcome data are missing, and when attrition systematically correlates with treatment assignment the remaining subjects no longer constitute random samples of the original participants.","When attrition systematically correlates with treatment assignment, a comparison of group averages becomes a biased estimator of the average treatment effect, and attrition can also make the sample less representative of the population the study tries to generalize to.","Attrition in field research takes various forms, such as participants being unwilling to fill out a post-treatment questionnaire or researchers losing track of participants who change addresses or names in long-term studies.","Researchers can minimize attrition by maintaining good administrative records, keeping good relations with those in charge of releasing information, drawing data from multiple sources, and using statistical methods such as inverse probability weighting and bounding the average treatment effect.","Noncompliance occurs when the actual experimental conditions participants experience do not match their assigned experimental condition, with one-sided noncompliance (failure to treat) meaning some assigned treatment participants do not receive the treatment and two-sided noncompliance also involving treated participants in the control condition.","When compliance rates are expected to remain low, researchers can consider an encouragement design and a placebo condition rather than a pure control condition, as in Nickerson's (2008) canvassing experiment that compared voters encouraged to vote with voters encouraged to recycle.","If the goal is to evaluate a program with natural variations in its failure to deliver the treatment, noncompliance is a feature of the program and not an inference issue, but when the goal is the average causal effect of the treatment, noncompliance narrows the scope of what can be estimated and reduces the precision of the estimates."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Field Research","topic":"Construal, Pilot Testing, and Non-WEIRD Field Research","visual_frame_description":"You walk through a factory floor strewn with cloth remnants while a tension-system diagram beside you lists driving and restraining forces around the trash cans. A golden coin decal glints on the floor as a construal meter aligns investigator and worker perspectives, and a world map highlights where psychology's participant pool actually comes from.","player_mission":"You are a design consultant for an anti-littering campaign in a textile factory where workers ignore rules and incentives. Map the driving and restraining forces of the littering behavior, use pilot testing and cognitive interviewing to discover participants' construal, and redesign the intervention around a shared construal rather than an off-the-shelf nudge. Then evaluate whether the sample is WEIRD and justify field research in non-WEIRD contexts against charges of low rigor.","key_concepts":["Construal is defined as individuals' interpretation of a stimulus, whether the stimulus is a survey question, a choice set, an experimenter, or a study environment.","Motivators that can affect an individual's engagement with and honesty in a research paradigm include demand characteristics, social desirability or strategic self-presentation, lack of trust in the researcher, level of education and literacy, and simple utilitarian motives such as payment incentives.","Contextual priming may affect individuals' construal processes, as in a study finding that voters were 0.5 of a percentage point more likely to vote in favor of increasing education spending when assigned to vote in a school versus a church.","Paluck and Shafir argue that field researchers need to achieve a shared construal with their participant population in order to design and deploy an intervention and its measurements in a way that better approximates the causal dynamics that lead to a behavior of interest.","Piloting typically refers to testing out a research paradigm before the actual research trial, and it can also mean taking time to investigate how stakeholders and participants understand the behavior in question and how the situation may affect their construal.","Cognitive interviewing is a technique in which participants think aloud, elaborate on their reactions to information of interest, and explain why they provide the responses they provide, and it is used to test perceptions of research materials.","Kurt Lewin suggested that individual behavior is a function of the individual's characteristics and their representation of the environment or the field, and that a behavior is maintained by a tension system of driving forces that motivate it and restraining forces that add resistance to it.","In a large textile factory in China, piece-rate workers were extremely motivated not to take seconds away from their work to throw trash into trash cans, although field observation and interviews showed that throwing trash away ultimately boosts productivity by preventing a pause when cleaners come through.","Because workers in the textile factory believed golden coins are an omen for fortune and luck that should not be touched by waste, researchers placed golden coin decals on the floors to create a countervailing motivation that changed the idea of the floor from a waste space to a space where waste was inappropriate.","Joseph Henrich and colleagues coined the term WEIRD for the observation that much of psychology and behavioral research is based on findings from societies that are Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, and Democratic.","A survey of the top psychology journals found that 96 percent of participants were from Western industrialized countries, which host only 12 percent of the world's population, and most field research on worker participation and democratic leadership is also conducted in Western democracies.","The trade-off between generalizability and internal validity is falsely imposed, because if appropriately designed and deployed, field research in non-WEIRD contexts can be just as rigorous as conventional lab research, or even more so, with the added benefit of external validity."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Field Research","topic":"Replicability, Technology, and Practical Guidance for Field Research","visual_frame_description":"You stand in a war room with a wall of six country flags above stacked study reports converging into a single meta-analytic forest plot, labeled metaketa. A globe beside you flickers between connected and dark regions to show who the Internet reaches, while a partnership contract and a data-quality checklist hover over a field-site briefing table.","player_mission":"You are the lead methodologist advising an early-career researcher who wants to launch an international field study on community policing. Choose a strategy for accumulating evidence across contexts, such as a meta-analysis or a coordinated metaketa study, and weigh the Internet as a delivery and data-collection platform given its advantages and its exclusions. Then draft a practical plan covering interdisciplinary collaboration, partnership agreements, local data-collection norms, and career safeguards before the project is funded.","key_concepts":["Field experiments typically test the efficacy of an intervention compared with a control or placebo in a specific context, but it can be difficult to know whether and when results from one context will generalize to another, and it can be challenging to compare across studies.","Since field experiments are generally much more expensive and time-consuming than lab experiments, there tend to be fewer studies on a given topic and often not enough studies already conducted to conduct a meta-analysis.","A meta-analysis is a useful tool for assessing the cumulative evidence within a domain and for assessing confidence in the generalizability and replicability of empirical findings and theoretical constructions.","A coordinated study, sometimes called a harmonized study, is an emerging trend in field research where multiple studies designed to test the same research question are conducted either sequentially or simultaneously in a range of contexts.","The metaketa initiative, led by the Evidence in Governance & Politics (EGAP) network, compiles a team of researchers who implement coordinated field research studies, agree on comparable interventions and measures, and analyze the results from each site in a meta-analysis.","A recent metaketa initiative conducted community policing studies in six countries (Brazil, Colombia, Liberia, Pakistan, the Philippines, and Uganda) and published findings in Science that community policing does not increase trust in the police or reduce crime in the global South.","Since the mid-1990s, the widespread adoption of mobile phones has made phone and SMS surveys possible in many parts of the world, and the expansion of the Internet makes digitized data collection possible, with tablets and audio and video recording devices replacing paper and pens in field interviews.","The Internet broadens the media through which treatments can be delivered, including text messages, push notifications, broadcast radio, webpage designs, and online voting, and it also broadens behavioral measurement, such as the analysis of police body camera footage.","Field research that relies on the Internet as a sole data source effectively excludes at least a third of the world's population, because around a third of the world's population has never used the Internet and about half do not own a smartphone.","Online interventions may be less immersive, less likely to grasp sustained attention, and less interactive than other types of field research, and online-only research precludes the study of face-to-face interacting groups and other intricate social processes that only manifest fully offline.","Field research often involves forming partnerships with nonprofits, NGOs, companies, governments, and local communities, and researchers should have explicit conversations up front and sign a formal or informal written agreement outlining each party's needs and responsibilities before entering a partnership.","When conducting international field research, researchers must learn local norms for data collection, work closely with community members to ensure the research is ethical and adapted for the context, and put measures such as high-frequency data checks, backchecks of a subset of interviews, and audio audits in place to ensure protocols are followed and data are high-quality."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm10","title":"Chapter 10: Survey Research","pages":"221-242","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Survey Research","topic":"What Is Survey Research? The Total Survey Error Framework","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a lecture screen with a population-to-sample pipeline diagram labeled representation process on one side and a construct-to-question pipeline labeled measurement process on the other. Four error cards float between the pipelines, while a clock and a padlock icon above the screen mark timeliness and availability as components of the Total Quality Framework.","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor preparing a lesson on the fundamentals of survey research. Define what makes a study a survey by identifying its common properties, distinguish biasing errors from variable errors, and sort the sources of error into representation errors, measurement errors, and processing errors using the Total Survey Error framework. Then evaluate trade-offs between accuracy, timeliness, and availability using the Household Pulse Survey as a worked example.","key_concepts":["Survey research is a commonly used research tool for understanding what members of a population think, feel, and do, and it is conducted by researchers, policy makers, journalists, and organizations to obtain a snapshot of a population.","Common properties of survey research are a well-defined population for which inference is intended, a scientifically drawn sample linked back to the population with a known probability of selection, a common data collection instrument of survey questions or other tasks, and methods of inference that allow conclusions about the population to be drawn from the sample.","Surveys can also be used to field experiments, such as the Time-Sharing Experiments in the Social Sciences (TESS), which lets researchers submit proposals to collect experimental data for free on a probability-based web panel.","Errors in survey research may be either biasing errors, which are systematic and make an estimate too high or too low relative to its true population value, or variable errors, which add noise to data by increasing standard errors and possibly attenuating measures of association.","The Total Survey Error perspective is an organizing framework that identifies different errors that may occur in surveys, and it is sometimes expanded into the Total Quality Framework, which adds timeliness and accessibility of the data under the heading of Fitness for Use.","Survey errors fall into two general groups: representation errors (errors of non-observation), which include coverage, sampling, and nonresponse errors, and measurement errors (errors of observation), which arise when answers do not adequately reflect true values.","The representation process consists of identifying a target population, developing a sample frame, drawing a sample, and recruiting respondents, and the four types of representation error arise from mismatches between these steps.","Coverage errors arise when there is a mismatch between a target population and the sample frame, sampling errors arise when only a subset of the sample frame is selected, and nonresponse errors occur when the selected sample and the final set of respondents differ on estimates of interest.","Respondents who fail to answer questions may need to have values imputed, and samples that do not fully observe the population may require weights, both of which can yield post-survey adjustment errors.","Construct validity issues occur when a measure or question does not adequately reflect the construct of interest, and measurement errors occur when the answer does not adequately (validly or reliably) reflect the true value for that question.","The U.S. Census Bureau's Household Pulse Survey (HPS), launched in April 2020 to understand how the COVID-19 pandemic was affecting Americans' everyday lives, prioritized timeliness and availability but made trade-offs in accuracy, yielding response rates generally lower than 10 percent and larger potential for non-sampling errors than traditional federal statistical surveys.","The HPS frame was the Census Bureau's Master Address File with complete coverage of the U.S. household population, but only 80 percent of addresses had appended email or cell phone contact information, producing coverage error, and post-survey adjustment weights were created to align the HPS with known population totals, possibly yielding adjustment errors."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Survey Research","topic":"Target Populations, Sample Frames, and Probability Sampling Designs","visual_frame_description":"You sit at a drafting table where three frame cards fan out: an envelope stack labeled Delivery Sequence File, a phone dial labeled RDD, and a laptop labeled probability panel, with an undercoverage gauge ticking beneath each. A selection console beside you offers SRS, systematic, cluster, and stratified switches, while a formula projector shows the design effect for a clustered sample mean.","player_mission":"You are a sampling consultant advising a researcher who wants to study U.S. adults living in households. Identify the target population and choose between the USPS Delivery Sequence File, a random digit dial frame, and a probability-based web panel, explaining what coverage errors each choice risks. Then draw the sample, deciding between simple random, systematic, cluster, and stratified designs and computing what the design effect implies for sampling variance.","key_concepts":["The first step in the representation process is identifying a target population, which requires clearly specifying who is of interest, in what areas, and for what time frame the researcher wants to make inference when the data are collected.","Sampling frames are lists used to identify potential members of a sample, and the frame for a survey is informed by the target population, the budget, and the desired mode of data collection.","Frames should either uniquely identify possible sample units (an element frame) or provide information about naturally occurring groups that can be used to identify unique sample units with additional sampling steps (a cluster frame).","In the Netherlands a population register with detailed demographic and family information on all people in the country is available for drawing samples, while the United States has no such register and relies on other frames to identify sample units.","When there is a mismatch between the frame and the target population on the main study variables, coverage error may occur.","The USPS Delivery Sequence File (DSF), the list of housing units to which the U.S. Postal Service delivers mail, is a frame used for address-based sampling (ABS) in which a sample of addresses is selected to reach members of the U.S. household population, and it can be used for national and local-area samples because addresses can be linked to geographies.","A random digit dial (RDD) sample uses telephone numbers as the frame, includes both landline and cell phone numbers to maximize coverage, and as of the end of 2021 about 69 percent of U.S. adults lived in wireless-only households reachable only on a cell phone.","Probability-based panels such as Ipsos's KnowledgePanel, the LISS panel in the Netherlands, and the GESIS panel in Germany start with a probability sample of the general population and inherit the coverage properties of the initial frame, and many attempt to overcome coverage problems by providing computer equipment or internet access to households that lacked it.","Probability samples are replicable because the sample selection step is known, and sampling error from probability samples is a well-defined variable error that arises because one selects a sample of size n rather than talking to the full population of size N.","In a simple random sample (SRS) each element on the frame has the same probability of selection and is selected individually using a random-number generator, whereas systematic samples draw only one random number and then select every kth unit where k = N/n, leaving the sample affected by the order of elements on the frame.","Cluster samples first select naturally occurring groups of elements and then often subsample within them, are used to reduce travel costs for in-person surveys, and elements within a cluster tend to be similar to each other, with the similarity measured by the rate of homogeneity (ROH), equivalent to the intraclass correlation coefficient in a multilevel model.","The design effect is the ratio of the sampling variance for an estimate using a complex sample design to the sampling variance for that estimate using a simple random sample of the same size, so clustered samples have deff greater than 1 (higher variance) while stratified samples generally reduce sampling error relative to an SRS of the same size (deff less than 1)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Survey Research","topic":"Nonresponse Error: Rates, Bias, and Data Collection Fixes","visual_frame_description":"You face a respondent funnel divided into three labeled flows: noncontact, refusal, and other nonresponse, with gatekeeper icons blocking the noncontact lane. A calculator on the desk evaluates RR1 and COOP1 formulas while a bias meter compares respondents and nonrespondents on the key outcome, and a design console offers incentives, shortened questionnaires, and mode switches.","player_mission":"You are a survey auditor reviewing an RDD study whose response rate fell to 6 percent. Classify the three nonresponse mechanisms behind the missing cases, compute and interpret AAPOR response and cooperation rates, and distinguish unit from item nonresponse. Then determine whether the low response rate necessarily implies nonresponse bias using the deterministic and stochastic bias models, and prescribe responsive or adaptive design strategies, incentives, or mode switches where bias is likely.","key_concepts":["The response rate is the proportion of the eligible sample that participated in the survey, and identifying the eligible sample is complicated when the frame contains ineligible units whose eligibility cannot be determined ahead of data collection, as when RDD samples contain business numbers or unanswered telephone numbers.","The American Association for Public Opinion Research (AAPOR) has developed its Standard Definitions with six different response rates that vary in how partial completes are included in the numerator and how units with unknown eligibility are treated in the denominator.","Unit nonresponse occurs when there is failure to measure any information about a sampled individual, whereas item nonresponse occurs when information is collected about some but not all of the questions in the survey, and although their statistical implications are similar, the mechanisms and the data collection or statistical fixes are different.","Nonresponse in household surveys is made up of three mechanisms: failure to contact (noncontact), refusal to participate, and other nonresponse, which is typically barriers due to the survey administration language or physical or cognitive disabilities.","Noncontact nonresponse is a function of the survey invitation not reaching the household, is easier to discern in interviewer-administered face-to-face surveys than in self-administered surveys, can be reduced through additional contact attempts and varying the time of day, and is amplified by gatekeepers such as restricted access to communities or call and spam blockers.","Refusals in interviewer-administered surveys often occur within the first moments of a request being administered, before a survey is even described, and are driven by general concerns such as not having time, not trusting the legitimacy of the request, or privacy and confidentiality concerns.","Survey cooperation decisions are considered a mix of reactions to design features seen as benefits with positive leverage versus costs with negative leverage, drawing on social exchange theory and leverage-salience theory, and survey organizations respond with monetary and nonmonetary incentives, shortened questionnaires, and mode switches.","Survey response rates to interviewer-administered surveys have been falling over the last four decades, with the Current Population Survey declining from 90.4 percent in 2012 to 72 percent in 2022 and Pew Research Center telephone response rates falling from 36 percent in 1997 to 6 percent in 2019, prompting a shift to self-administered modes.","High nonresponse rates do not necessarily mean there will be nonresponse bias, because under the deterministic model nonresponse bias of an unadjusted survey mean equals the nonresponse rate times the difference between respondents and nonrespondents on the survey characteristic of interest, and it is estimate-specific.","Groves and Peytcheva's (2008) meta-analysis of the association between response rates and nonresponse error showed no clear association between the two measures, with more heterogeneity in nonresponse error across estimates within a survey than between surveys with different response rates.","Groves (2006) introduced three conceptual models linking response propensity and the survey variable: the separate-causes model corresponding to Missing Completely at Random, the common-cause model corresponding to Missing at Random in which weighting adjustments can remove bias, and the survey variable cause model corresponding to Not Missing at Random in which estimates remain biased.","Responsive and adaptive designs tailor data collection approaches before or during data collection to different sample subgroups expected to vary on response propensity and key survey variables, often using subsampling of nonrespondents with changes in design features such as incentives and modes to balance the respondent pool."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Survey Research","topic":"Question Writing, Response Scales, and Questionnaire Pretesting","visual_frame_description":"You work at a whiteboard where question stems feed into a response-option mirror labeled match, and mismatched pairs glow red as the board flags them. A tool rack on the wall holds cards for QUAID, SQP, expert review, cognitive interview, eye tracking, split-ballot experiment, behavior coding, and field test, while a scale labeled acquiescence tilts as you edit wording.","player_mission":"You are a questionnaire designer building an employee climate survey. Draft question stems that communicate the desired response, replace agree-disagree scales with item-specific scales to reduce acquiescence, and convert check-all lists to forced-choice formats. Then assemble a pretesting package, choosing between expert review, QUAID or SQP, cognitive interviewing, split-ballot experiments, behavior coding, and a field test to catch breakdowns in the cognitive response process before data collection.","key_concepts":["Survey methodologists draw on a four-step cognitive response process model in which respondents comprehend the wording of a question, recall relevant information, make a judgment about whether the retrieved information is adequate and map it into the response categories, and may edit the answer to fit social norms, reduce social desirability, or fit other perceived expectations.","A first principle for questionnaire design is that the question stem should adequately communicate the types of response desired from the question, because a long history of experimental research shows the importance of question wording on the distributions of survey answers.","In a Schuldt et al. (2011) experiment, more people reported that the world's temperature phenomenon was happening when it was called climate change (74.0 percent) than global warming (67.7 percent), with the effect driven by Republicans (60.2 versus 44.0 percent) but not Democrats (86.4 versus 86.9 percent), showing that small wording changes can change survey responses.","Questions in which the stem projects a request for information that does not match the response categories are mismatches, and Smyth and Olson (2019) found that mismatched questions take longer to answer, vary in item nonresponse rates, and have different response distributions than matched questions, concluding that mismatches are detrimental and should be avoided.","Agree-disagree scales are prone to acquiescence bias, a tendency to say yes or agree to statements independent of their content, which yields systematic biases in estimates that are too positive and arises from satisficing shortcuts in the cognitive response process as well as interviewer-respondent rapport maintenance.","Repeated studies have shown that including both positive- and negative-valence items in a scale to counter acquiescence yields items that fail to measure a single underlying construct and creates confusion among respondents, so researchers instead favor construct-specific or item-specific scales that remove agree from the response options.","Item-specific questions yield responses with higher levels of reliability and validity than agree-disagree questions, but they require more processing time and take more space on self-administered questionnaires.","Forced-choice formats ask respondents to answer whether each item applies with a yes or no response, consistently yield higher rates of endorsement than check-all formats, and show longer and presumably more complete processing in response time data, so forced-choice response options generally yield better data quality.","Expert review is an inexpensive and common question evaluation method in which methodological experts focus on the form of the question and possible breakdowns of the cognitive response process while substantive experts focus on content and face validity, and having multiple experts yields better information than a single expert.","QUAID is a web-based tool that identifies five possible comprehension problems with a question: unfamiliar technical terms, vague or imprecise relative terms, vague or ambiguous noun phrases, complex syntax, and working-memory overload, while SQP codes questions on over thirty-five domains and uses meta-analysis of multitrait-multimethod experiments to estimate a question's reliability, validity, and overall quality.","Cognitive interviewing, usability tests, and eye tracking are lab-based pretesting methods in which respondents think aloud or answer follow-up probes, though the prevalence of problems in a population is not clear from the small samples and the methods require respondents to articulate their thoughts.","In a split-ballot experiment, the most common type of between-subject questionnaire design experiment, sample members are randomly assigned to one of a set of alternative question wordings, formats, or design features, as in the General Social Survey's ongoing experiments on spending on welfare versus assistance to the poor."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Survey Research","topic":"Modes, Interviewers, and the Big Survey Design Decisions","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a mode control board with four channel sliders labeled mail, web, telephone, and in-person, and a mixing panel that switches between concurrent and sequential (web-push) routing. A privacy curtain slides over the self-administered channels as sensitive items appear, while a timeline projector flips between one-shot, repeated, and panel designs over a cost gauge below.","player_mission":"You are the principal investigator designing a national health survey on sensitive behaviors. Choose between mail, web, telephone, and in-person modes and decide whether a concurrent or sequential mixed-mode design fits the target population, and justify how each choice affects disclosure, knowledge scores, and response-style biases. Then plan interviewer training under standardized interviewing, evaluate the risks of interviewer effects, and decide between cross-sectional and longitudinal designs given your research questions and budget.","key_concepts":["Modes of administration are divided into self-administered modes (mail and web surveys) that largely use visual forms of communication and interviewer-administered modes (telephone and in-person interviews) that primarily use aural forms of communication.","Self-administered modes are considered more private than interviewer administration, so respondents are more likely to disclose sensitive or socially undesirable behaviors and subjective phenomena in self-administered modes than in interviewer-administered modes.","Respondents to web surveys systematically score higher on knowledge questions than respondents to interviewer-administered modes, and aural versus visual modes experience different rates of selecting the first or last (or more positive) response options, so different modes of data collection yield different measurement biases.","Surveys increasingly use multiple modes of data collection, called mixed-mode surveys, which can be beneficial for participation and coverage when sample members have different mode preferences or access to different technology, and modes may be offered simultaneously (concurrent) or in successive contact attempts (sequential, sometimes called web-push when one mode is web).","Mixed-mode designs take advantage of the strengths of different modes, including computerization for navigation and reduced data entry costs, while mitigating weaknesses of individual modes such as lack of access to technology.","Interviewers introduce both variable and biasing errors into survey estimates and act as clustering agents that increase standard errors, with the interviewer effect rho measuring the homogeneity of responses for respondents interviewed by the same interviewer and the design effect due to interviewers increasing standard errors by a factor of (1 + (b - 1) * rho) where b is the average workload size.","Homogeneity in responses due to interviewers can arise because interviewers recruit similar types of respondents (nonresponse error variance) or because they obtain answers that are similar to each other (measurement error variance), and systematically different reports have been found on gender-related attitudes for male versus female interviewers and on race-related attitudes for Black versus White interviewers.","To mitigate interviewer effects, survey researchers train interviewers on standardized interviewing methods, which include reading the question exactly as worded, using nondirective probing and clarification, providing interpersonally neutral feedback, and recording answers exactly as provided, with the goal of giving the same stimulus to every respondent.","Despite the goal of standardization, Olson, Smyth, and Kirchner (2020) found that interviewers in two telephone surveys read questions exactly as worded only between 50 percent and 65 percent of the time, with substantial variation across questions and across interviewers.","Emerging modes of data collection augment survey questions with other measurements, including passively recorded smartphone location or social interactions, ecological momentary assessments that take daily measurements over text messages or apps for almost real-time information, implicit measures such as the 2008 ANES implicit-racism measure, consent to link administrative data, and live video interviewing, SMS texting, or virtual avatar interviewers.","Surveys may be administered once as stand-alone cross-sectional surveys, over time to different sets of respondents in repeated cross-sectional surveys, or over time to the same respondents in longitudinal panel surveys, and repeated cross-sectional surveys allow attitudes to be monitored over time, as with support for same-sex marriage rising from 27 percent of U.S. adults in 1996 to 71 percent in 2021.","Longitudinal surveys follow the same set of respondents over time to study within-person changes and how earlier information affects later outcomes, but they lose respondents to attrition over the course of the study, and the appropriate survey design is driven by both the research questions and the budget, since costs are the constraining factor on all aspects of a survey and have increased as response rates have fallen."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm11","title":"Chapter 11: Conducting Surveys and Experiments on the Internet","pages":"244-264","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Online Research","topic":"The Rise of Online Research and Its Benefits","visual_frame_description":"You stand before a lecture screen with a timeline stretching from 1998 to 2015, marking Project Implicit and the declining share of student samples in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology. Floating cards labeled accessibility, statistical power, sample diversity, and transparency orbit a scale that tips toward online data collection, while a preregistration stamp presses down on a stack of study protocols.","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor preparing a lesson on why psychological researchers moved their studies online. Trace the growth of internet research from the mid-1990s through the 2015 estimate that only 43 percent of samples were college students, and describe the benefits of online research: accessibility, efficient collection of large samples, greater sample diversity, remote participation, and easier sharing of study materials. Then argue how these advantages support replicability and generalizability while acknowledging the push for preregistration to reduce researcher flexibility.","key_concepts":["The possibility of conducting research on the Internet entered the purview of behavioral scientists in the mid-1990s, and social psychologists were some of the first to capitalize on this opportunity, such as with the creation in 1998 of Project Implicit, an online research laboratory devoted to implicit social cognition.","Gosling et al. (2004) found that 85 percent of samples in papers published in the 2002 volumes of the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology were composed of students, whereas Anderson et al. (2019) estimated that by 2015 the percentage of college student samples had declined to 43 percent.","In 2004, the Board of Scientific Affairs' Advisory Group of the American Psychological Association argued that the Internet has democratized data collection.","Conducting research online facilitates the efficient collection of large numbers of participants, which generally increases precision and statistical power, because collecting data from internet samples allows larger numbers of people to complete a study simultaneously.","Internet research provides an opportunity to overcome the sample homogeneity that has historically plagued behavioral-science research, since student samples who are more educated and liberal than the general population paint an inaccurate picture of psychological processes in various domains.","Behavioral-science samples, regardless of their source, tend to be overwhelmingly from Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, and Democratic (WEIRD) societies.","Participants can complete online studies remotely, which facilitates greater sample representativeness, because people with a disability who might not be able to travel to a physical lab space can participate.","Conducting studies online allows people to complete surveys and experiments remotely, which reduces challenges created by events that create societal upheaval, such as the spread of infectious diseases.","Online research facilitates sharing study materials, because such studies typically involve a computer program or survey file that can be uploaded to an online repository, which increases transparency and reproducibility in psychological research.","Psychologists began to consider Mechanical Turk (MTurk), a platform now commonly used to crowdsource online data collection, as a potential tool for research around 2010.","Bem (2011) reported evidence for precognition, which generated intense discussion about common practices in how psychological studies were conducted and reported, and Simmons et al. (2011) outlined how flexibility in data analysis could undermine the credibility of research claims.","Because internet research is relatively young and many topics do not possess a single and universally agreed upon best practice, the chapter encourages the use of preregistration to aid transparency and reduce researcher flexibility."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Online Research","topic":"Hosting, Programming, and Designing Online Studies","visual_frame_description":"A server rack shows four hosting options with cost and expertise gauges, while a code editor beside it toggles between server-side tabs (PHP, NodeJS, Django) and client-side tabs (JavaScript, jsPsych, PsychoJS). Four principle icons - linked rings, a single line, a magnet, and a shared lens - hover over a device preview where the study renders on a desktop, a phone, and a tablet beside a screen-reader icon.","player_mission":"You are an experiment designer helping a colleague build an online experiment from scratch. Choose a hosting option among an institutional server, a study-hosting service, purchased server space, or a personal computer, and select a programming route from platforms like Qualtrics, open-source LimeSurvey, reaction-time tools like Inquisit or Gorilla, or manual HTML with client-side JavaScript tools like jsPsych. Then review the study against the four guiding design principles of coherence, simplicity, immersion, and consistency, and check accessibility for smartphones, iPads, and screen readers.","key_concepts":["Any study that a researcher creates will need to be hosted on a server, and options include the servers of one's academic institution, a study-hosting service such as Qualtrics or Survey Monkey, purchased storage space on servers from companies such as Amazon, or setting up one's own personal computer as the server.","Using the servers of one's academic institution or a study-hosting service will typically be lowest in cost and maintenance, whereas setting up one's own computer as a server requires considerable technological expertise and can create security issues.","LimeSurvey is an open-source web application that researchers can use at no cost to design and host studies, while Inquisit and Gorilla allow researchers to program surveys and experiments with tools for measuring reaction time.","Study-hosting services such as Qualtrics often include their own platforms for designing studies, typically provide extensive and step-by-step instructions, and generally incur a financial cost, but they require very little programming knowledge.","Researchers can manually create their own study webpages using HyperText Markup Language (HTML), and manual coding remains the most cost-friendly option for programming studies online.","Server-side programming (e.g., PHP, NodeJS, Django) more directly connects the web browser to the server and is used to store and send information back to users, whereas client-side programming (e.g., JavaScript) is code that is downloaded and run on a participant's computer.","Existing tools that use JavaScript, such as jsPsych and PsychoJS, can be used to program experimental features such as random assignment.","Compatibility of client-side programming can change over time and render a study defunct, as happened when many browsers stopped supporting Adobe Flash software at the end of 2020.","Custom-designed programming and specialized software can often be inserted into surveys programmed on study-hosting services, for example by using JavaScript to program features within a Qualtrics survey, and a link to an Inquisit task or an Implicit Association Test can be embedded in a survey.","Four guiding principles have been outlined for research settings regardless of where a study is conducted (Nosek et al., 2002): studies should be coherent, simple, immersive, and consistent.","Presenting self-report items one at a time rather than all at once on the same page decreases missing data without sacrificing validity in research with internet volunteers (Nosek et al., 2012).","As of 2023, 90 percent of people in the U.S. own a smartphone, and methods that require participants to press keys on a keyboard may not translate well to smartphones and iPads, so researchers may need to program touch screen versions of the study."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Online Research","topic":"Auditing Data Quality: Attention, Bots, and Repeat Responders","visual_frame_description":"A dashboard shows a response-time histogram with several standard-deviation cutoffs, a missing-data heatmap, and an attention-check pass-fail table sorted by sampling source. A bot shield icon sits beside a captcha box, while a repeat-responder log tracks browser cookies and IP addresses, and a checklist recommends pilot runs and a ten-to-twenty-participant soft launch.","player_mission":"You are a data quality auditor reviewing responses from a compensated online experiment. Scan the response-time histograms, missing data counts, and response variability to flag mindless responders, then inspect where attention and instruction checks were placed and decide which participants to exclude from analyses. Investigate bot activity and repeat submissions, and recommend piloting and soft-launch procedures so problems are caught before full data collection.","key_concepts":["The quality of measurements collected online is often comparable to that collected from in-person research, with internal reliability, test-retest reliability, factor structure, and other assessments of measurement quality among commonly studied constructs tending to reach a magnitude considered acceptable.","Problematic responders who have little interest in providing genuine data can mindlessly click through studies that provide financial compensation, and they can be identified through metrics such as completing the study considerably faster than the average participant, having large amounts of missing data, showing little variability in responses, or showing strong divergences in response patterns from the overall sample.","No single metric can conclusively indicate mindless responding, but metrics of these types can be combined to filter out problematic responders.","Cognitively taxing studies can contribute to response bias and weaker measurement quality, even among participants who typically provide high-quality data.","The most common approaches for measuring participant attention are attention and instruction checks, which ask participants to complete tasks or answer questions that are often unrelated to the study at hand and are simple when afforded sufficient attention.","Because attention and instruction checks can alter the way participants approach the study, they should generally be placed after participants have completed the central aspects of the study.","Participants who fail attention and instruction checks can be excluded from analyses, which has the potential to increase statistical power by reducing measurement error.","Participants from crowdsourcing platforms such as MTurk are typically more likely to pass attention and instruction checks than are samples drawn from student populations and market research platforms.","Low-quality data in online research can be attributable to computer programs, frequently referred to as bots, that are developed to automatically complete studies, and one common approach for weeding out nonhuman bots is Captcha Verification, which requires participants to complete a task that is simple for humans but difficult for computer programs.","Researchers can capitalize on information that crowdsourcing and market research platforms obtain from participants, such as CloudResearch's currently available option to draw samples from an approved list of people who have been vetted and confirmed to be human responders who provide high-quality data.","Non-naivety among participants who repeat studies can bias and, in some situations, reduce effect sizes, and researchers are advised to use multiple prevention options simultaneously, since no single option will perfectly prevent repeat responders.","It is better if researchers learn about issues with their studies before fully launching them online, so the chapter recommends piloting with members of the researcher's lab and then conducting a soft launch that collects a small number of participants (e.g., ten to twenty) and asks whether anything was confusing or failed to present accurately."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Online Research","topic":"Recruiting Pools, Representativeness, and Generalizability","visual_frame_description":"A recruitment console displays four route cards with cost and pool-size meters, showing MTurk's roughly 30,000 active participants each month and AmeriSpeak's 43,000 households. Below, a generalizability triangle links three nodes - frequencies diverge, correlations generalize, and experimental effects depend on homogeneity - while probability and quota sampling toggles switch the demographic composition sliders for age and ideology.","player_mission":"You are a sampling consultant advising a researcher who wants U.S. adults for a paid political-attitudes study. Compare the four recruitment routes - self-advertising, crowdsourcing platforms like MTurk and Prolific, representative panels like AmeriSpeak, and market research platforms - and weigh costs, pool size, and fraud risks. Then judge whether the convenience sample you select can yield generalizable frequencies, correlations, and experimental effects, and choose between probability and quota sampling.","key_concepts":["The crowdsourcing platform that has to date been most extensively used in social and personality psychology research is MTurk, on which individuals who sign up to complete studies are called workers and complete human-intelligence tasks (HITs) for payment.","Crowdsourcing platforms generally have sizeable subject pools that are consistently available, and Litman and Robinson (2021) estimate that there are about 30,000 active participants on MTurk each month.","Prolific is a crowdsourcing platform more focused on academic research that has emerged and grown in popularity.","Representative panels such as KnowledgePanel and AmeriSpeak are typically recruited offline using procedures that optimize representativeness, such as random selection of households from the U.S. Census or addresses from the postal service, and as of February 2022 the AmeriSpeak panel was composed of about 43,000 households.","Time-Sharing Experiments for the Social Sciences (TESS) is a National Science Foundation-funded program in which researchers submit study proposals and those selected are then conducted using the AmeriSpeak panel at no cost to the researcher.","Market research platforms such as Prime Panels and Qualtrics Panels typically have the largest pool of participants, providing the advantage of being able to request very large samples or samples of target subgroups that are typically harder to acquire on crowdsourcing platforms, but the costs can be prohibitive for many researchers.","Participants can misrepresent themselves when completing an anonymous study to gain access to paid studies, and this tendency increases with greater financial compensation, so researchers are cautioned against using recruitment procedures that suggest certain criteria are needed for participation.","In 2000, 53 percent of White Americans said that they used the Internet whereas this was the case for only 38 percent of Black Americans, but as of 2021, 93 percent of White Americans and 91 percent of Black Americans report using the Internet.","Internet convenience samples tend to be more representative than U.S. student samples in various ways, such as in age and political ideology, and are mostly representative of the broader population in geographic location, but they tend to be younger and more liberal than the general population.","Probability sampling is an approach in which every participant in a population has, in theory, an equal chance of participating in the study, whereas quota sampling is a non-probability-based form of sampling in which researchers specify desired proportions of different groups.","Although frequencies from internet convenience samples typically cannot be generalized because they provide skewed estimates about exact prevalence, findings from correlational studies often can be, as subgroups of participants from convenience samples often behave similarly to individuals from these subgroups in representative online panels.","Internet convenience samples can also produce generalizable findings from experiments when there is homogeneity in the impact of the experimental manipulation across subgroups, whereas meaningful heterogeneity across subgroups could lead sample skew to overestimate or underestimate the manipulation's effect."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Online Research","topic":"Ethics, Dropout, and Data Security in Online Research","visual_frame_description":"A consent flow diagram branches into waiver and electronic-signature paths, while a debriefing pop-up appears on a closing browser window. A payment board shows Prolific's eight-dollar minimum and twelve-dollar recommended hourly rates, a dropout funnel charts where participants exit, and a security vault displays TLS, HIPAA, and GDPR badges beside a locked data file.","player_mission":"You are the principal investigator of an online study that uses deception and pays participants. Set up informed consent using a waiver of documentation with button-click consent, plan a debriefing that reaches participants even if they close their browser early, and check comprehension of the debriefing. Choose fair compensation, minimize dropout with the cocktail remedy, and protect data with encryption, deidentification, and compliance with HIPAA and GDPR.","key_concepts":["Broad ethical principles that guide scientific research, such as those laid out in the Belmont report, must be implemented when conducting research on the Internet, although the manner in which these guidelines are carried out online will sometimes diverge from in-person research.","Conducting a study online does not allow researchers to collect a participant's signature on a consent form as they typically would in the lab, so researchers can often request a waiver of documentation of informed consent that allows participants to consent through a procedure such as clicking a button on their computer screen.","An original signature on a consent form might be required under some conditions, such as when a study is deemed more than minimal-risk or when a parent needs to provide consent for their child, in which case the researcher would need to obtain an electronic or physical signature.","Standard procedures for the use of deception generally require researchers to debrief participants at the end of the study and inform them of exactly how they were deceived and why the deception was necessary, and to correct misperceptions that participants might have formed as a result of the deception.","Because participants can remove themselves from an online study with relative ease, participants who leave a study early will not receive debriefing information, so one approach researchers can take is to program text to display debriefing information if participants try to close their browser before completing the study.","Online studies could also reveal deception using a funneled approach, in which study measures probe whether the participant detected deception in a series of progressively overt questions and explanations.","The Project Implicit Health (PIH) website includes IATs that measure associations about self-injury and suicide and outlines empirical findings indicating that asking about suicide does not increase suicidal thoughts and instead increases the likelihood that a person will reach out for assistance.","As of this writing, the crowdsourcing platform Prolific sets a required minimum payment rate of eight dollars per hour and also provides a recommended rate of twelve dollars per hour, whereas the widely used crowdsourcing platform MTurk does not provide payment requirements.","It is rare for participants to leave in the middle of an in-person study, but dropping out is easy and more frequent when completing a study online since participants can simply close their browser, and systematic dropout tied to key individual difference variables or experimental manipulations can threaten internal validity, as in Zhou and Fishbach's (2016) finding that gender-based dropout created a confound that produced the false conclusion that imagining applying eyeliner caused people to weigh less.","One empirically tested approach for minimizing dropout is a cocktail remedy comprising prewarning, appealing to conscience, and personalization, which when used collectively halved the dropout rate in one study, although these approaches might also amplify self-selection toward other characteristics such as conscientiousness.","Researchers can use Transport Layer Security (TLS) or another form of HTTP encryption to protect data during transmission, and this type of encryption is automatically used among many survey-hosting platforms.","Data regulations guide data storage procedures, including HIPAA, which dictates how personally identifiable health information is to be maintained in the U.S., and the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), which is a data protection and privacy law covering the European Union and the European Economic Area."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm12","title":"Chapter 12: Methods for Studying Everyday Experience in Its Natural Context","pages":"266-295","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Everyday Experience","topic":"Capturing Life as It Is Lived: The Conceptual Rationale","visual_frame_description":"A conveyor belt carries everyday moments - a coffee chat, a bus ride, a quiet evening conversation - into a monitoring station labeled 'the lab comes to the participant.' Three target icons hover above a map of the day: prevalence tally, hypothesis scale, and hypothesis-finding telescope, while three domain lenses (exemplary, reconstructed, ongoing) focus on the same scene and a counter weighs a single major life event against a stream of tiny ones.","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor preparing a lesson on why everyday experience methods have matured into standard research tools. Explain that they are a paradigm, not a single instrument, aimed at capturing life as it is lived, and describe their three general research purposes: establishing the prevalence and properties of phenomena, testing theory-derived hypotheses, and discovering new hypotheses. Then lay out Reis's three domains of inquiry - exemplary, reconstructed, and ongoing experience - and explain why even minor, mundane events can matter as much as major life events.","key_concepts":["In the past three decades, methods known generically as everyday experience methods have matured from promising innovations into standard, widely used tools, referring not to a specific instrument or procedure but to a paradigm for studying social psychological phenomena as they occur in the ebb and flow of everyday life, to 'capture life as it is lived' (Bolger et al., 2003).","Among the popular examples of everyday experience methods are the experience sampling method (ESM; Hektner et al., 2007), ecological momentary assessment (EMA; Shiffman et al., 2008), daily diaries, and ambulatory assessments.","Everyday experience methods are akin to following participants through their day, systematically observing, questioning, or assessing them at relevant points, bringing the lab to the participant instead of bringing the participant to the lab.","Everyday experience studies have three general purposes: establishing the prevalence and properties of phenomena, testing theory-generated propositions, and helping researchers discover new hypotheses.","When recording immediately follows events, retrospection bias is diminished, resulting in relatively accurate accounts, at least from the respondent's point of view.","Solomon Asch (1952) taught that perception and action in the social world are determined by the situational context of behavior, writing that 'most social acts have to be understood in their setting, and lose meaning if isolated.'","Reis (1994) described three domains of inquiry - exemplary experience, reconstructed experience, and ongoing experience - each scrutinizing a phenomenon or process from a distinct perspective, and research programs incorporating all three perspectives are likely to be most informative.","Exemplary experience consists of studies in which behavior is observed in specific, restricted, or otherwise special settings, including laboratory experiments and observational studies carried out in uniform, often intrinsically relevant settings.","Reconstructed experience asks participants to evaluate, summarize, or otherwise describe in questionnaire or interview format their experiences with specific entities or in particular situations, and such global assessments often differ from online experience due to encoding, storage, retrieval, and evaluation processes.","Studies of ongoing experience focus on explicit, usually immediate, reports of activity and aim to maximize external validity by examining specific processes or phenomena within the stream of routine, voluntary activity.","Based on the assumption that ordinary evenings spent in quiet conversation or irritating days at work may be influential, especially when recurrent, everyday experience research explores the impact of minor, or mundane, events on outcomes such as emotional well-being, health, and social activity.","The vast majority of affects experienced in everyday life are low-intensity (Diener et al., 1985), and mundane events occur more often than major events, so their impact is more likely to be evident across repeated instances."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Everyday Experience","topic":"Three Protocols: Interval-, Signal-, and Event-Contingent Recording","visual_frame_description":"A control panel shows three recording stations side by side: a calendar clock for interval-contingent reports, a buzzing pager with random intervals for signal-contingent reports, and a card-slot for event-contingent records such as the Rochester Interaction Record. Above them a comparison table lights up rows labeled retrospection bias, time-series analysis, intrusiveness, and event frequency, while a decision needle swings between the three stations.","player_mission":"You are a methods consultant helping a researcher choose a sampling protocol for a study of daily mood and social interactions. Walk through the three-protocol typology proposed by Wheeler and Reis (1991) - interval-contingent, signal-contingent, and event-contingent recording - explaining how each works, what it is best for, and its drawbacks. Then apply Table 12.1 to recommend a protocol for a specific research goal, such as estimating the prevalence of mental states versus capturing rare, clearly defined events.","key_concepts":["Wheeler and Reis (1991) proposed a typology of three everyday experience protocols - interval-contingent, signal-contingent, and event-contingent recording - that differ primarily in the sampling frame used to obtain data.","With interval-contingent recording, sometimes called time-contingent recording, participants report their experiences at regular, predetermined intervals, typically representing theoretically or logically meaningful units of time such as day's end or every four waking hours.","By far the most common interval sampling unit is the day, as in the National Study of Daily Experiences (Almeida, 1997), in which a national sample of 1,484 adults completed telephone interviews about their daily experiences on eight consecutive evenings.","With signal-contingent reports, participants describe their activity at the moment a signal is delivered, most often by text message, pager, or email, and signals may follow fixed or random schedules or a combination of the two.","The original and best-known example of signal-contingent recording is the experience sampling method (ESM; Hektner et al., 2007), developed by Csikszentmihalyi and colleagues and first used to describe the 'ecology of adolescent experience' (Csikszentmihalyi et al., 1977).","If signals are random, or at least unrelated to participants' activities, and participants comply with instructions to respond immediately, signal-contingent data estimate the prevalence and distribution of activities and states over time.","Event-contingent recording requires a report whenever events matching a predetermined definition occur, as in the Rochester Interaction Record (RIR; Wheeler & Nezlek, 1977), which calls for completion of rating scales and descriptive items after every social interaction lasting at least ten minutes.","Key to event-contingent recording is unambiguous definition of the events to be described, as well as timeliness, because if participants are permitted to select events or it is unclear whether an event should be reported, systematic distortions are possible.","Interval-contingent methods minimize participant burden and are ideally suited to time-series analysis, for which the irregular gaps of signal-contingent and event-contingent recording add complexity, because the time span from one record to the next is constant.","A major disadvantage of interval-contingent recording is that events may be removed in time from reports, creating possible retrospection bias, and target variables may have fluctuated or occurred more than once during an interval, making them difficult to describe.","Signal-contingent recording tends to be more intrusive than event-contingent recording, because signals, even when inaudible to others, may interrupt ongoing activity, whereas event-contingent reports are provided after an event's natural completion.","Event-contingent sampling is most useful when the events are rare, or when variations within a class of events, some of which are rare, are of interest, because signal-contingent methods would capture too few instances of low-frequency events."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Everyday Experience","topic":"Auditing Protocol Design, Compliance, and Participant Management","visual_frame_description":"A project dashboard shows a weekly signal schedule with six beeps per day, a calendar with final exams and holidays crossed out, and an item palette offering open-ended boxes, Likert scales, and checklists. Beside it, a compliance graph tracks on-time response rates while a checklist flags hoarding and back-filling patterns, a training-calendar card marks two excluded practice days, and a confidentiality vault shows locked couple records.","player_mission":"You are a study manager auditing a planned experience sampling project before it launches. Check the sampling schedule against the meta-analytic norms of six assessments per day for seven days, verify that unusual occasions are avoided, and review instrument design choices among open-ended items, rating scales, and checklists. Then audit the participant plan: training days to eliminate initial elevation bias, incentives commensurate with burden, confidentiality safeguards, and protections against hoarding and back-filling.","key_concepts":["The choice among interval-, signal-, and event-contingent schemes should be based on two considerations: the question(s) being studied and the incidence of target behaviors.","A recent meta-analysis covering 496 research samples indicated that signal-contingent studies averaged six assessments per day for seven days (Wrzus & Neubauer, 2022), although eight to twelve signals per day for up to two weeks is also common.","Because event sampling is designed to obtain representative data, it is best to avoid unusual occasions such as final examinations, major holidays, or honeymoons, since non-random times may inadvertently confound research.","Computerized protocols that deliver prompts and record responses are now strongly preferred over paper-and-pencil administration, allowing researchers to time-link self-reports with other data, use branched protocols, record response latency, and upload data automatically for immediate monitoring of compliance.","Three kinds of self-report items are popular: open-ended questions, fixed-format rating scales, and checklists, which are used primarily for indicating which events within a series did and did not occur during a given interval.","Because excessive length inevitably degrades response quality (Eisele et al., 2022), once-daily reports should not exceed fifteen minutes, and more-than-daily reports should not exceed five minutes.","Event-sampling research inherently depends on participants' ability and willingness to comply with instructions, since diaries are usually burdensome and at least somewhat intrusive, and ambulatory devices are often uncomfortable.","Incentives should be commensurate with burden: not so large as to attract freeloaders but not so small as to only attract research-eager volunteers.","Protecting confidentiality and ensuring that participants are aware of safeguards is essential, especially when diaries ask about potentially embarrassing or illegal behaviors, and in couple studies it is vital that partners have no access to each other's records.","Some studies include one or two training days, whose data are excluded, which can help eliminate initial elevation bias, whereby reports of internal states, especially negative mental states and symptoms, tend to be elevated for the first few days of daily diary studies (Shrout et al., 2018).","Left unconstrained, a substantial number of participants, particularly undergraduates, may practice hoarding (providing multiple reports from memory at a single sitting) or back-filling (reporting on current events at a later time), so protocols should be designed to prevent these practices, for example by closing web links at predetermined times.","A meta-analysis by Vachon et al. (2019) found that compliance was higher when studies had fewer male participants, higher incentives, a longer interval between reports, and a fixed sampling scheme."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Everyday Experience","topic":"Retrospection Bias, Within-Person Effects, and the Analysis of Nested Data","visual_frame_description":"A split screen contrasts a raw momentary-report feed with a foggy global-retrospection bubble in which four sliders - recency, accessibility, sense-making, and state of mind - tilt the image. On the analysis side, a nested diagram stacks daily assessments inside person cards, with three question-type arrows pointing at between-person, within-person, and interaction cells, while a sine wave labeled 'weekly mood cycle' and a lagged arrow from time t-1 to time t scroll beneath.","player_mission":"You are a data analyst comparing momentary reports with global retrospections and planning the statistical analysis of an experience sampling dataset. First, explain the four heuristics - recency, accessibility, sense-making, and state of mind - that bias global recollection, and cite accuracy findings such as Wentland's 23 to 100 percent range. Then map the two-level structure of repeated assessments nested within individuals onto between-person, within-person, and cross-level interaction questions, and plan for lagged effects, temporal cycles, and spectral analysis.","key_concepts":["Most researchers agree that cognitive and motivational processes tend to bias responses to survey questions, and as Stone and Turkkan (2000) put it, 'It is naive to accept all self-reports as veridical.'","Wentland (1993) meta-analyzed studies that used objective criteria to evaluate the accuracy of people's reports about various circumstances, finding accuracy ranging from 23 percent to 100 percent and concluding that concrete, specific questions close in time to the event tend to be most accurate.","Recency: the more recent an event, the better it is recalled and the more likely it is to influence retrospection, as shown by Bernard et al. (1984), where fewer than half of informant-recalled social network contacts were remembered correctly but more recent events were recalled better than less recent ones.","Accessibility: more salient events, in terms of intensity, unusualness, or personal significance, tend to be more influential, so that summary ratings of daily or weekly emotion echo moments of peak intensity more than they reflect average levels (Neubauer et al., 2020).","Sense-making: compared to contemporaneous reports, remembered experiences tend to be interpreted in the context of beliefs about those experiences, as when women's recollections of their menstrual symptoms better resemble their general beliefs about such symptoms than their daily symptom reports (McFarland et al., 1989).","State of mind: retrospections, especially of affect and attitude, are influenced by mood at the time of report, and global summaries of mood tend to resemble current mood (Parkinson et al., 1995; Stone et al., 1993).","Furr (2009) went so far as to call instantaneous, in-the-moment reports the 'gold standard' for behavioral research, although recollections may still provide a different and valuable perspective on human behavior.","Typical daily experience designs yield two levels of data - repeated assessments nested within individuals - from which three general types of question can be posed: differences between individuals, variation within individuals, and interactions between the higher and lower levels.","Between-person processes ask whether certain behaviors differ as a function of certain characteristics of the individuals being studied, whereas within-person processes ask whether the behavior of an individual differs when certain circumstances are present compared to when they are absent.","Temporal precedence in sequentially structured data can support certain explanations while ruling out others (West et al., 2014): a lagged effect of variable X on variable Y is commonly tested by predicting Y at time t from X at time t-1, controlling for Y at time t-1, and is consistent with X causing Y but not with Y causing X.","Spectral analysis, a general class of methods for detecting cycles and rhythms in sequential data, is rarely applied in social-personality psychology, despite the prevalence of cycles such as the day, characterized by diurnal rhythms in mood, fatigue, and attentiveness.","Weekly cycles are well documented, accounting for 40 percent of the variance in daily mood in one study (Larsen & Kasimatis, 1990), with mood tending to be more positive and less negative on weekends than on weekdays."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Everyday Experience","topic":"Reactivity, Response Decay, and Integrating Event Sampling into a Research Program","visual_frame_description":"A double-pan balance weighs 'record-keeping changes behavior' against 'experience proceeds naturally,' with a decay gauge below showing falling event frequency and rising stereotype answers. In the background, a web of interlocking method nodes - laboratory experiments, surveys, and daily diaries - converges on a phenomenon core, while two five-item checklists label the circumstances in which event sampling is ill-suited and well-suited.","player_mission":"You are the principal investigator deciding whether everyday experience research fits a proposed research program. Weigh the reactivity concern - that record-keeping may alter the experiences being monitored - against evidence of little or no impact on some outcomes and improvements on others, and diagnose response decay signs such as diminished event frequency and ratings stereotypy. Then apply critical multiplism and multiple operationalism to position event sampling alongside laboratory experiments, and judge the five situations in which event sampling is ill-suited versus well-suited.","key_concepts":["Because ecological validity is a prime justification for daily event studies, researchers sometimes worry about reactivity - that the process of record-keeping itself may alter the experiences being monitored, as anticipation of the need to describe an event may affect behavior choices or people's experience of an event.","Some studies have indicated little or no impact of record-keeping on reports of depressed or anxious affect or suicidal ideation (e.g., Coppersmith et al., 2022; DeVuyst et al., 2019; Husky et al., 2014), whereas other studies have reported improvements in mood and mindfulness (Moore et al., 2016), decreased consumption of alcohol but not marijuana (Buu et al., 2020), and improvements in self-reflection among high-risk polydrug users (Roth et al., 2017).","Conner and Reid (2012) found that more frequent reports of momentary happiness were associated with lower ratings among participants higher in neuroticism and depressive symptoms, but increased reports of happiness among participants lower in neuroticism and depressive symptoms.","Reactivity is often indistinguishable from the general problem of response decay, in which repeated assessments alter the frequency or nature of reports, such as when boredom leads to less thoughtful responding, and one cause of response decay is participant burnout when protocols are overly burdensome or a study runs too long.","Diagnostic signs of response decay include diminished event frequency without apparent justification, more errors and missing data, and ratings stereotypy, such as logically inconsistent answers, changes in mean levels or variance across time, and increased use of scale anchors or midpoints.","Everyday experience data - nested within levels, serially dependent, and imbalanced in the number and variance of data points - necessitate relatively advanced statistical methods, so researchers should have a firm grasp of their data-analytic plan in the earliest stages of design and preparation.","Webb et al. (1966) introduced the idea of multiple operationalism: because all methods have strengths and weaknesses, the most useful perspectives on a phenomenon are provided by methods that complement one another's limitations, and methodological triangulation is the best way to prevent theoretical insights from being method-bound.","Houts et al. (1986) argued for 'critical multiplism,' which substitutes reliance on diverse alternative methods for perhaps naive faith in a single critical test or paradigm, noting that corroboration from 'aggressively sought alternative explanations' is needed to attribute findings to substantive rather than methodological features.","At least five situations seem ill-suited to daily experience research: when an effect is unlikely to be evident without careful control of other simultaneous processes; when it is necessary to create unusual, rare, or even implausible conditions; when ruling out spurious causation is paramount; when global, general impressions are the theoretical focus; and when participants are unable or unwilling to comply with sampling protocols and provide accurate reports.","Event sampling seems well suited to at least five circumstances: when it is desirable to observe phenomena in natural, voluntary, and spontaneous contexts; when retrospection and certain other biases are likely to produce misleading accounts; when relevant conditions cannot be created ethically or impactfully in the laboratory; when within-person and temporal patterns are likely to be revealing; and when ecological validity is foremost.","As Brewer and Crano argue in Chapter 6 of this handbook, construct validity is a property of research programs, not of individual studies, so the value of event sampling should be appraised in terms of how it complements other methods rather than in isolation.","Everyday experience studies can help fulfill social psychology's legacy of understanding how social behavior is embedded in situational contexts, as reflected in the popular construct of personality-situation interactionism, which holds that dispositions are reflected in the situations people select, in their differential reactions to existing situations, and in how one person's behavior may alter the situation for others."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm13","title":"Chapter 13: Mobile Sensing Methods","pages":"297-320","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Mobile Sensing","topic":"From the Decade of Behavior to Digital Trace Data: Why Mobile Sensing","visual_frame_description":"A smartphone and a smartwatch sit on a conveyor belt, feeding streams of glowing traces into a lab labeled 'the lab comes to the participant.' Behind them, a timeline walks from the 1990s ubiquitous-computing block to a 2012 manifesto scroll, past a camera badge and a microcassette recorder labeled EAR, while seven toggle switches labeled automatic, resource-efficient, passive, ecological, and standardized flip on above a balance scale weighing 'complement, not substitute' against 'not a panacea.'","player_mission":"You are a methods instructor introducing mobile sensing to a psychology seminar. Explain that mobile and wearable devices carry embedded digital sensors producing continuous streams of digital trace data, and that mobile sensing (also called passive sensing) collects those data in the background as participants go about their lives. Then trace the method's lineage from ubiquitous computing and Miller's smartphone psychology manifesto through experience sampling and its precursors (wearable cameras and the EAR), and list the seven properties that make mobile sensing a novel way to measure real-world behavior.","key_concepts":["Mobile and wearable devices are equipped with an array of embedded digital sensors that produce continuous streams of digital trace data, and these now effectively ubiquitous traces empower psychologists to study human behavior 'in vivo' and 'in situ,' a goal that gained momentum after the American Psychological Association's 'Decade of Behavior' (2000-2009).","This novel approach to field research is commonly referred to as mobile sensing, or passive sensing: participants install a research application on their mobile device, and the data are passively collected in the background as they go about their lives (Harari et al., 2016; Mehl et al., 2024).","Mobile sensing has its roots in the 1990s in the fields of ubiquitous, mobile, and pervasive computing (Krumm, 2010), and about a decade ago Miller (2012) published the seminal article 'The smartphone psychology manifesto,' characterizing smartphones as 'the most powerful, versatile, and intimate tools routinely carried by ordinary people.'","Because the study of actual real-world behavior was long neglected in social and personality psychology, which reduced its targets of investigation to the almost proverbial 'self-reports and fingermovements' (Baumeister et al., 2007), studies focusing on behavior remain underrepresented in leading journals today (Renner et al., 2020).","For the assessment of momentary thoughts and feelings in daily life, the experience sampling method is the clear gold standard in the field (Conner & Mehl, 2015), yet as a behavioral measure it ultimately assesses reflected behavior - displayed behavior as perceived and reported by the participant - and repeatedly assessing behavior can alter its display.","Precursors to mobile sensing that follow experience sampling's ecological sampling approach but passively pull in objective sensor information include wearable cameras that sample snapshots of participants' moment-to-moment environment (e.g., Brown et al., 2017) and the electronically activated recorder (EAR; Mehl, 2017), a portable audio recorder that intermittently records ambient sounds.","The EAR method has revealed, among other findings, that women are in fact not much more talkative in daily life than men (Mehl et al., 2007), that having more and more substantive daily conversations is robustly related to higher well-being (Mehl et al., 2010; Milek et al., 2018), and that people's daily language use can provide a window into their health-related gene expression (Mehl et al., 2017).","The EAR method's Achilles heel is that its hundreds of hours of audio data must, at least for the moment, be listened to and behaviorally coded by human coders, so EAR data are ultimately observational rather than unambiguously objective in nature.","Mobile sensing can collect an array of daily behaviors across a range of situations automatically, resource-efficiently (without human coders), passively, ecologically (sampling daily contexts representatively), and without relying on the participant as a 'reflected reporter,' but it complements rather than substitutes existing data collection approaches and is not a panacea for all behavioral measurement.","The range of devices to which sensing can be applied spans tablets, wearables such as smartwatches, smart home applications, car electronics, and even coffee machines, with 438 off-the-shelf wearables available between 2001 and 2016 to track activities, sleep, heart rate, and nutrition (Wac, 2018), and smart home system sensors can even infer inhabitants' respiration or heart rate from Wi-Fi radio frequency signals (Adib et al., 2015).","Smartphones take on a special role in this catalogue because almost everyone owns one and, combining alarm clock, calendar, communication, music player, navigation, and notebook functionalities in one device, it has become our most intimate daily companion, so it can provide an array of information about its users, the daily situations they encounter, and their everyday actions and interactions (Harari et al., 2016).","Mobile sensing data can also be collected to draw conclusions about daily behaviors that happen not directly on but in close proximity to the smartphone, such as sleep, which can be inferred from a silent, dark, and motionless device during night hours (Wang et al., 2014, 2018)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Mobile Sensing","topic":"Sensing Personality, Sleep, and Social Life: What Mobile Sensing Has Revealed","visual_frame_description":"A wall of six study cards lights up in turn: a Big Five hexagon fed by a stream of 15,692 indicator chips, a mood graph showing reciprocal arrows between smartphone use and adolescent well-being, a finger ring hovering over a sleep tracker, a four-channel communication dashboard (voice, calls, texts, apps), and a GPS map with mood-colored pins. A small print-out at the bottom reads 'duty, intellectuality, mating, positivity, sociality: predicted; adversity, deception, negativity: not.'","player_mission":"You are a research consultant briefing a lab on the empirical track record of mobile sensing in social and personality psychology. Walk through the major findings: personality prediction from smartphone behavior, smartphone use and affective well-being, sleep assessed via wearables and as a 'bodiless' smartphone sensor, sociability extracted from four communication channels, and movement and situation sensing. For each domain, name the study, the data types used, and the key result, ending with the caveat about which situation dimensions sensing data could and could not predict.","key_concepts":["One of the most comprehensive studies on daily behaviors extracted 15,692 indicators from smartphone sensing data collected and aggregated over four weeks, representing six different classes of behavior (app usage, music consumption, communication and social behavior, mobility, phone activity, and day-/nighttime activity), to predict the Big Five personality traits (Stachl et al., 2020).","In the Stachl et al. (2020) study, machine learning models predicted extraversion, followed by conscientiousness and openness (but less so agreeableness and emotional stability), relatively accurately from daily smartphone use, with higher extraversion predicted to the extent that participants showed more communication and social behavior.","Higher conscientiousness scores were predicted the earlier participants used their smartphones for the first and last time during the day, the lower the variation in the length of time they did not use their smartphones at night, and the more frequently they used more specialized apps such as weather and timer apps, with higher love-of-order and sense-of-duty values predicted the higher the charge level when the phone was disconnected from the charger (Stachl et al., 2020).","Marciano et al. (2022) examined reciprocal dynamics between smartphone use and adolescents' self-reported daily well-being over forty-five days and found that more frequent and longer smartphone use was related to higher well-being, that higher well-being was associated with longer subsequent smartphone use, and that changes in affective well-being lasted longer than changes in smartphone use.","Sleep research in daily life is challenging because the gold standard of objective sleep assessment, polysomnography, is hardly feasible in the field and self-reports of sleep are particularly susceptible to response bias, so mobile sensing via wearables constitutes a valuable alternative that provides access to physiological indicators collected in daily life (Sadeh, 2015).","The Oura Ring, a finger ring with integrated sensors, assesses and combines movement data, heart rate, heart rate variability, and pulse wave amplitude variability to infer sleep parameters, and Moshe et al. (2021) found, in a thirty-day study combining experience sampling, smartphone sensing, and Oura Ring data, that more total sleep time and more time in bed were related to higher depression scores.","The Oura Ring also proved effective at predicting COVID-19 infection on average 2.75 days before participants sought out diagnostic testing, with the algorithm heavily relying on the recently added skin temperature sensor (Mason et al., 2022).","Schoedel et al. (2020) investigated whether social jetlag patterns replicate when nighttime smartphone inactivity is used as a proxy for objective sleep duration and found that the compensatory weekend nightly resting pattern at the intra-individual level did not emerge in mobile sensing data, while at the inter-individual level individuals with shorter weekday nightly inactivity also had shorter weekend nightly inactivity.","Harari, Mueller, Stachl, et al. (2020), studying 1,078 young adults across four communication channels (detected in-person conversations via microphone data, phone calls, text messages, and communication/social media app usage), found that a substantial amount of the variance in smartphone-sensed social behaviors is attributable to inter-individual differences and that within-person daily social behaviors are very stable over time.","daSilva et al. (2021) used microphone logs to estimate conversation times and found that self-reported stress was associated with reduced social interaction the next day, whereas conversation times on a given day did not predict stress levels on the following day, and Kraemer et al. (2023) found that a higher frequency of communication app use was associated with higher negative affect when alone while more in-person conversations detected via microphone logs were linked to higher positive affect.","Mueller et al. (2020) found at the between-person level that participants who felt less lonely and less depressed had more irregular movement patterns and spent less time at home and more time in social places, and at the within-person level that participants felt less anxious on days when they moved longer distances and spent more time in social places but more anxious on days when they spent more time at work.","The accelerometer sensor collects information about the motion of the smartphone from which physical activities (e.g., sedentary versus walking versus driving) can be deduced (Wang et al., 2014), and Schoedel et al. (2023), using 1,356 objectively sensed mobile sensing cues collected over a fourteen-day window, found that momentary situation experience could be predicted for the dimensions duty, intellectuality, mating, positivity, and sociality but not for adversity, deception, or negativity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Mobile Sensing","topic":"Choosing Your Sensing Stack: Apps, Data Types, and Preprocessing","visual_frame_description":"A project dashboard shows three vendor cards: a do-it-yourself app under construction with a long-term maintenance arrow, an open-source toolkit with modular sensor blocks and a community support forum, and a commercial storefront with a one-study license stamp. Beside them, a stack of three data shelves labeled sensor logs, device status logs, and usage logs, and a pipeline diagram with two branches - a phone icon labeled on-device preprocessing and a server labeled off-device - with a privacy shield, a battery gauge, and a quality-control magnifier hanging between the branches.","player_mission":"You are a study manager auditing a planned mobile sensing project before launch. Review the three implementation options - self-developed app, open-source solutions, and commercial sensing services - and weigh their flexibility, cost, and sustainability against the study goals. Then audit the sensing design: which operating system to target, which of the three data categories (sensor logs, device status logs, usage logs) to collect, and whether feature extraction should happen on-device or off-device, including the privacy, battery, and quality-control trade-offs.","key_concepts":["There are three broad options for how researchers can implement mobile sensing: developing their own sensing app, relying on open-source mobile sensing solutions, or using commercially available mobile sensing app services, each with advantages and disadvantages to be weighed in light of the underlying research goals.","Developing a sensing app offers a great deal of flexibility - researchers can decide which types of data should be sensed and at what level of granularity - but psychologists usually need interdisciplinary teams with computer scientists or electrical engineers, and the app requires long-term commitment including continuous technical support and careful coordination with local data protection laws.","One of the most important design decisions is the operating system on which the app should run, because programming for both iOS and Android requires considerably more effort than for one of the two, and iOS tends to be much more restrictive for third-party apps than Android, so Android-based sensing can provide more, and more detailed, data.","The choice of operating system carries downstream consequences for sample composition, because operating system ownership tends to relate to sociodemographic characteristics: in Germany, Android users are relatively representative of the general smartphone-owning population whereas iOS users reflect a nonrepresentative subpopulation with respect to age and education (Keusch et al., 2020).","Open-source sensing apps such as AWARE and SensingKit are free of charge, usually designed in a modular manner so researchers can select the sensing modules they want, are generally well documented with bugs identified quickly thanks to their large communities, but offer no technical support in the classical sense and usually give researchers less leeway for implementing their own ideas.","Commercial mobile sensing apps such as Ethica, Movisens, and EARS are use-for-fee apps with a relatively simple researcher workflow and provided support, but the manufacturer specifies the scope of functions and the app is licensed as a one-time service for a specific study, making this option less sustainable in the long run as solutions often have a short half-life.","Figures 13.1 and 13.2 distinguish sensing data according to three categories: sensor logs from the physical sensors embedded in smartphones (e.g., accelerometer, ambient light, gyroscope, magnetometer, microphone, proximity, pressure, temperature), device status logs related to connectivity, location, and more specific functions (e.g., battery level, Bluetooth, booting, flight mode, GPS, headphones, Wi-Fi), and usage logs generated by the user's interaction with the device (e.g., apps, calendar, contacts, camera, phone calls and text messages, music, notifications, screen state).","The microphone can collect audio snippets from which the presence of human voice or conversation can be inferred to draw conclusions about whether a participant is alone or with other people (Wang et al., 2014), and Bluetooth protocols can be used to scan the user's environment for other Bluetooth-enabled devices, with repeatedly matched Bluetooth IDs (combined with self-reports) enabling the study of dyads or social networks (Ruegger et al., 2020).","Mobile sensing data in their raw version are usually stored as time-stamped data with a non-researcher-friendly structure (e.g., '2021-11-13 08:05:06.234 | Whatsapp | Move to foreground'), so they must be preprocessed into a usable format such as the average duration of communication app use per day, a step often referred to as creating features, that is, extracting meaningful variables.","Feature extraction can be done directly while the data are being acquired (on-device preprocessing), which is especially useful for sensitive data - for example, running a conversation classifier directly on the device instead of storing raw audio that could reidentify participants via their voices - but presupposes built-in and validated preprocessing algorithms that are computationally intensive and can easily be a battery burden for the device.","Off-device preprocessing, done after the data have been collected, involves more effort on behalf of the researcher but has the advantage that the raw data are available for quality control, reprocessing, and validation purposes, and the researcher can decide more flexibly, according to the study goals, how variables should be extracted to best align with the construct they are intended to measure.","Variables can be generated via simple rule-based data aggregation - for example, counting listened songs per day and aggregating them into the average number of daily songs listened to (Stachl et al., 2020) - or via more complex algorithms, such as pre-trained machine learning models applied on accelerometer, microphone, ambient light, and screen status logs to infer bedtimes and wake times (Wang et al., 2014)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Mobile Sensing","topic":"The Data Cocktail: Combining Sensing Streams, External Data, and Self-Reports","visual_frame_description":"A mixing console with five channels labeled Example 1 to 5 blends different streams - call logs, accelerometer and screen chips, a wearable heart trace, Spotify danceability bars, and a GPS pin with a mood slider - into five output meters showing 'daily call duration,' 'bed/wake times,' 'depression dynamics,' 'song danceability,' and 'mood in context.' A side panel shows a GPS pin enriching itself with restaurant, cafe, and bar labels, while a keyboard icon routes typed words through an on-device dictionary classifier.","player_mission":"You are a data analyst designing the feature layer of a mobile sensing study. Work through the five combination examples in panel C of Figure 13.2: a single phone-log feature, sensor logs combined with screen time for sleep, smartphone data enriched with wearable heart rate for depression, music logs enriched with an external database of Spotify audio features, and GPS combined with experience-sampled mood. Then explain how GPS can be enriched with location-type information and how sensing can trigger experience sampling prompts, and weigh keyboard-logging solutions for sensitive text data.","key_concepts":["Mobile sensing researchers have to decide whether data types should be used individually or in combination with other data types, and panel C of Figure 13.2 gives five examples from the literature illustrating the wide variety of psychologically meaningful variables that can be extracted from mobile sensing data alone or in combination with external data.","Example 1 illustrates the use of a single data type: the average daily duration of incoming calls can be extracted from the phone logs collected during the study period as a measure of sociability (Harari, Mueller, Stachl, et al., 2020; Montag et al., 2014), and the daily frequency of incoming calls can be related to the frequency of outgoing calls - for example in the working context - to learn about daily workload or power differentials between employees and managers.","Depending on the granularity of the sensing software, features can have higher resolution, such as extracting the duration of incoming calls not only on a daily level but also as time-of-day estimates, for example the average duration of incoming morning and evening calls (Harari, Mueller, Stachl, et al., 2020).","Example 2 shows sensor logs (accelerometer, ambient light, and microphone) combined with screen time to infer participants' bedtimes and wake times as a measure of sleep (Wang et al., 2014), which in turn can be used to examine the extent to which individuals live in accordance with a normative daytime social rhythm and how this affects social interaction patterns.","Example 3 combines different types of smartphone data (screen time and GPS locations) and enriches them with logs from external sensors (heart rate data from wearables) to investigate how self-reported depression manifests behaviorally and physiologically in college students' daily lives (Wang et al., 2018).","Example 4 combines smartphone data with an external database: Stachl et al. (2020) collected four weeks of music logs from smartphones and enriched the listened-to songs via an external database with audio features provided by Spotify, such as danceability, which could be used to investigate whether music selection changes depending on someone's mood (e.g., happy C-major versus sad A-minor songs) or on the presence and type of social interaction partners (e.g., high danceability and liveness indicating a social gathering or party).","Example 5 illustrates the combination of mobile sensing with experience sampling: Sandstrom et al. (2017) repeatedly assessed participants' mood states via experience sampling along with GPS information and found that positive affect was higher in more social types of situations.","GPS locations can be enriched with information about the type of location (e.g., restaurant, cafe, bar) and other details (e.g., popularity of the location, volume of visitors) to study social processes such as interpersonal perception in context, and external data sources that sensing data can be combined with include external sensor logs (e.g., car electronics, tablets, watches), external databases (e.g., geocoding, song text lyrics, social media data), and self-reports (diary studies, experience sampling, questionnaires).","Mobile sensing can be combined with experience sampling in an event-triggered way: if the smartphone detects that a person has made a call via the phone logs, experience sampling questionnaires could be triggered automatically asking about the type of relationship (friends, family, colleagues) or the social interaction in the past call, and a smartwatch detecting stress from heart rate and its variability could trigger notifications delivering an intervention to reduce the person's momentary stress level.","Text data are very sensitive data that participants are often not willing to share, so computer scientists have developed technical solutions to extract research-relevant information without storing raw text - for example, analyzing typed words directly on the device upon typing and assigning them via a dictionary-based text-analytical approach (LIWC) to psychologically meaningful categories such as personal pronouns, emotion words, or social words (Tausczik & Pennebaker, 2010; Bemmann & Buschek, 2020).","In the Human Screenomics project, Brinberg et al. (2021) captured screenshots of participants' smartphones every five seconds to extract high-resolution information on how participants interacted with their smartphone over the course of a day, allowing for a qualitative characterization of behavior beyond mere quantitative description.","In Jensen et al.'s (2022) Bluetooth project with veterans and their service dogs, the app registered a ping whenever the dog's Bluetooth-equipped collar and the veteran's phone were within thirty feet of each other, revealing that veterans spent more than 80 percent of their awake time in proximity to their dogs, and interestingly this quantitative marker of human-animal closeness did not explain variability in veterans' mental health whereas experience sampling-based qualitative ratings did."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Mobile Sensing","topic":"Five Bold Predictions Revisited: Where Mobile Sensing Is Going","visual_frame_description":"A projection screen shows five prediction cards being stamped one by one: 'At scale' with a partial stamp (a graph rising from 50 to 18,000 participants), 'Long-term' with a checkmark but a cross-sectional-analysis warning label, 'All-rounder platform' with a large red X, 'Daily lives' with a full check and an ecological-validity trophy, and 'High standardization' with a partial stamp and a researcher-degrees-of-freedom meter. A verdict banner at the bottom reads: 'solid yet early growth trajectory - it is ultimately about psychological measurement.'","player_mission":"You are the principal investigator deciding whether to invest in mobile sensing infrastructure for the coming years. Evaluate the method against the five predictions made by mobile sensing pioneers - data collection at scale, longer-term longitudinal data, an all-round research platform, closer access to people's daily lives, and high standardization of measurement - and for each one state whether the prediction has been realized, partially realized, or refuted, citing sample sizes, study durations, and the authors' conclusions. Then deliver your verdict on where the field should go, including the claim that mobile sensing is ultimately about psychological measurement.","key_concepts":["Prediction 1 holds that mobile sensing will enable data collection at scale and from global samples, which the authors define as sample sizes in the thousands that pose no data-analytic constraints with respect to statistical power, and based on existing studies they argue that the method has so far fallen short of this prediction.","Early mobile sensing studies commonly had samples of fifty or fewer participants, many current studies still collect samples on the order of 100 to 200 participants (e.g., Huckins et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022), some studies have achieved sample sizes beyond 500 often by combining datasets from different studies (Beierle et al., 2020; Schoedel et al., 2023; Stachl et al., 2020), and a few have broken through the thousand-participant mark, such as Servia-Rodriguez et al. (2017) with 18,000 participants and the UT1000 project by Wu et al. (2021) with about 1,500 participants.","Recruiting procedures have specific pitfalls: Kreuter et al. (2020) reported a response rate of only 16 percent even in a well-maintained annual household panel, partly because individuals are concerned about their data privacy while experiencing little added value when participating, since the sensing app is a pure tracking app designed to run unobtrusively in the background and to be remembered as little as possible (Keusch et al., 2019).","Phan et al. (2022) concluded that little to no cross-cultural mobile sensing research exists - with isolated exceptions, mobile sensing studies use samples from two different countries at most, usually only from WEIRD cultures - so mobile sensing has so far not delivered on the promise to facilitate a global reach for psychological research, even though about 66 percent of the world's population own a smartphone (Kemp, 2022; Taylor, 2023).","The authors conclude on prediction 1 that mobile sensing has the potential to achieve large and diverse samples, with the two biggest leverages lying in improving recruitment strategies and delivering added value to participants in these data-intensive study settings while ensuring that their spontaneous and habitual behavior remains minimally impacted by the sensing app.","Prediction 2 holds that mobile sensing will enable the collection of longitudinal data over weeks or months, and the authors see this as already being realized: although early studies typically captured short periods of days, today's studies report data collection periods ranging from one week (e.g., Ruegger et al., 2020) to several weeks (e.g., Schoedel et al., 2018) to across several terms (Huckins et al., 2020; Mack et al., 2021).","The limitation on prediction 2 is that although longitudinal data are being collected, the resulting behavioral and situational measures are all too often analyzed cross-sectionally and aggregated into simple average measures of habitual tendencies, leaving considerable untapped potential for modeling intra-individual processes with time series or continuous time modeling to study bidirectional effects over time (e.g., daSilva et al., 2021; Marciano et al., 2022).","Prediction 3 holds that mobile sensing will make smartphones an all-round, if not the central, platform for psychological research, and the authors judge that Miller's (2012) prediction that by 2025 smartphones would establish themselves as the data collection tool and that continued reliance on traditional methods would 'border on scientific malpractice' was 'beautiful in rhetoric but bad in accuracy.'","On prediction 3, the authors argue that mobile sensing is technically in its infancy, that it enables valuable 'contact-free' research via remote app deployment, instruction, data collection, and debriefing, but that studies requiring high standardization and controlled settings - for example, experimentally manipulating specific environmental aspects or tasks in which participants must not be distracted (perception experiments, intelligence tests) - will not be possible with mobile sensing.","Prediction 4 holds that mobile sensing brings researchers closer to studying people's daily lives, and the authors conclude it is clearly already doing so, capturing a wide range of human digital behavior right where and right when it happens, but they see a flipped order in current research - theory, or what can be studied, follows feasible measurement - exemplified by the original StudentLife study's heuristic approximation of studying behavior from GPS dwell time of at least 20 minutes in study areas (Wang et al., 2015), which lacked conceptual fidelity.","Prediction 5 holds that mobile sensing will facilitate the operationalization of constructs with high consistency and objectivity, and the authors only partially agree: while sensing data across all participants are subject to the same objective preprocessing rules, researchers have many degrees of freedom (how to bin the day, whether the weekend starts Friday at 5 p.m., 8 p.m., midnight, or Saturday at wake-up, or whether data from three or seven out of fourteen days suffice), and standardization is also limited by technical logging aspects such as sensors that differ in quality across manufacturers and operating system adaptations.","The authors' conclusions for the future are that mobile sensing research would benefit from more precision in documentation and transparency in reporting preprocessing decisions (e.g., preregistration; Wrzus & Schoedel, 2023), the gradual development of behavioral measurement reporting standards (Trull & Ebner-Priemer, 2020), and the evaluation of how robust findings are against variation in preprocessing decisions (Schoedel et al., 2020), and they summarize that mobile sensing has made great progress yet has a long way to go, since mobile sensing is ultimately about psychological measurement."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm14","title":"Chapter 14: Language Research in Social Personality Psychology","pages":"322-347","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Language Analysis","topic":"Cleaning and Preprocessing Language Data: From Raw Words to Analyzable Text","visual_frame_description":"A glowing workbench displays a raw text file whose characters are being pulled through a cleaning pipeline: garbled tokens are repaired by a spell-checker, a grate labeled stop words sifts out the, and, and of, while a lemmatizer folds going, goes, and gone into go. Behind the bench, a stack of category buckets fills with words while a sign reads 'the more words analyzed, the more trustworthy the interpretations.'","player_mission":"You are a data analyst preparing a messy corpus of diary entries for quantitative analysis. The raw text is riddled with OCR errors, missing spaces, and mixed formats, and you must decide what to keep. Identify function words as the stylistic skeleton of language, mark them as stop words when content is the target, but preserve them when style signals such as pronouns matter, then lemmatize content words and assemble a clean bag-of-words file that reflects the probabilistic logic of word counting.","key_concepts":["The most common computerized text analysis approach is the bag-of-words method, in which a computer program calculates the percentage of words in a text that may be positive, negative, causal, perceptual, and so on, with no regard for syntax or word order.","Style in language is defined by function words: grammatical words such as conjunctions, articles, and pronouns that make up the syntactic structure of sentences, which do not stand on their own and have little independent meaning outside the context of an utterance.","Function words are more numerous than most people realize: the sixty most commonly used words in the English language are all function words, and altogether function words make up more than half of spoken and written language.","In the Corpus of Contemporary American English, for example, the top ten most frequent words are the, be, and, a, of, to, in, I, you, and it (Davies, 2008).","Historically, most text analysis researchers removed function words as a preprocessing step, which is why they are often called stop words in computer science, and removing them is wise when researchers are studying the content of speech.","Because function words are supporting words that connect or refer to content rather than communicating meaning on their own, they receive less conscious attention than content words while people read and speak (Bell et al., 2009; Rayner, 1977).","Content words, which include nouns, regular verbs, and most modifiers, define what a person is saying rather than how they are communicating, and together they make up less than half of conversational language, with content words among the fifty most frequent words making up only about 2 percent of language use.","Word counting is a probabilistic enterprise: some words in a text may be misclassified, but on average a truly angry person would use other anger terms, meaning that the more words that are analyzed in a text, the more trustworthy the interpretations become.","Lemmas are root words (for example, the lemma go includes going, goes, and gone) and are used in cases where particular word senses are less important than the basic meaning of the word.","The logic of text analysis rests on the assumption that the ways people pay attention to their world are reflected in their language, so a person who is self-focused will use more I-words, people who feel connected to a group should use more we-words, and people interested in others will use people-related names and pronouns.","It is useful to analyze text both with and without function words even in cases such as topic modeling, because whether a topic includes social or self-referential pronouns can help contextualize its meaning.","A person who refers to themselves relatively frequently is not, as many assume, egotistical or narcissistic, but may be neurotic, humble, or honest depending on the context (Holtzman et al., 2019; Kacewicz et al., 2014; Newman et al., 2003; Tackman et al., 2019)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Language Analysis","topic":"Dyads and Dialogue: Turn-by-Turn Language in Conversation","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen chat interface shows two speaker panes with color-coded function words pulsing in sync, and a convergence meter rises and falls along a turn timeline below. Outcome tags such as confession, impasse, rapport, and hostility flicker above the timeline as the meter peaks, while a sidebar lists dyadic measures including language style matching and emotional synchrony.","player_mission":"You are a conversation analyst transcribing a tense negotiation between two partners and must track their language turn by turn. Compute how much the speakers converge on function word frequencies over the conversation, decide whether the language alignment signals rapport or hostility given the context, and predict the interaction outcome. Then extend your analysis to dyadic similarity measures to advise whether the pair is cooperating or heading for impasse.","key_concepts":["Turn-by-turn analyses treat each utterance as its own text, either alone or nested within a broader text, and are useful in studies of interpersonal influence over the course of a conversation.","Ho and Hancock's (2019) language-as-context approach to deception detection uses verbal action cues within utterances to indicate speakers' communication goals, and contextualized approaches are especially valuable because dishonesty manifests differently across contexts such as opinion spam, secrecy, and online dating profiles.","Using a turn-by-turn measure of language style matching, Richardson and colleagues (2014) found that interrogations were more likely to end in confessions when the suspect converged increasingly with the interrogator over time, rather than vice versa.","Language style matching (LSM) is a measure of dyadic language similarity that focuses on function word frequencies (Ireland & Pennebaker, 2010), and other dyadic measures include emotional synchrony, syntactic alignment, narrative arcs, and holistic measures of similarity encompassing both content and style.","In social psychology, language alignment tends to be related to positive outcomes in friendly or neutral interactions, including emotional support, romantic interest, perceived psychotherapeutic effectiveness, and peaceful hostage negotiation outcomes.","In contrast, during conflict or competition, dyadic language convergence reflects negative processes such as hostility in spousal conflict discussions and impasse in negotiation, suggesting that language coordination amplifies underlying social processes rather than being uniformly positive.","Linguistic similarity between spouses also increases over time for romantic couples (Brinberg & Ram, 2021), and dyadic language analysis often focuses on close relationships such as romantic couples and parent-child dyads, as well as deception and collaboration in experimental games that assign strangers to be partners.","Analytic frameworks like the actor-partner interdependence model (APIM) can assist in measuring how people influence and are influenced by their partner's language use, and such approaches work equally well with dyadic or paired independent samples such as separate interviews with spouses.","In conflict, second-person pronouns like you and your reflect a negative, accusatory style, especially when used in sync by both sides of a dialogue (Simmons et al., 2005; Timmons et al., 2021).","First-person plural pronouns like we and our reflect either high status or self-other overlap, and people cope more effectively with health challenges if their partners use we at high rates when discussing their health, indicating communal coping (Rentscher et al., 2017; Rohrbaugh et al., 2012).","Experimental dialogue achieves a high degree of ecological validity by studying conversations between real-life friends or spouses, as in Gottman and Levenson's (2000) research on romantic couples' conflict conversations and speed-dating experiments in which all participants are single and interested in finding dates.","The CANDOR corpus includes over 1,600 video call transcripts totaling over 7 million words along with survey data on conversation quality and audio and video data (Reece et al., 2022)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Language Analysis","topic":"Auditing Text Analysis Methods: Validity, Errors, and Safeguards","visual_frame_description":"A forensic review board displays the original anger dictionary highlighting the word critical inside rows of system error logs, with red margin notes flagging the automated messages. A second screen shows a balance scale weighing theory-based dictionary methods against data-driven machine learning, while a checklist of safeguards - reading subsamples, Benjamini-Hochberg correction, blinding, and context coding - stamps itself across the bottom.","player_mission":"You are a methods auditor reviewing a published claim that Americans spiked in anger after 9/11 based on pager message counts. Inspect the dictionary-based pipeline, discover that the anger category matched automated network error messages containing the word critical, and re-run the analysis with corrected categories. Write a failure report that weighs top-down versus data-driven approaches and recommends safeguards such as reading raw texts, controlling false discovery rates, and attending to reliability and context.","key_concepts":["Top-down language analysis involves methods that are in service of an idea in the mind of the researcher, such as theories or content themes, and the most common top-down approach is dictionary-based text analysis, in which a computer program searches a text for words matching its internal lists and outputs frequencies or percentages for each dictionary category.","Although data-driven methods of text analysis are primarily quantitative, they virtually always use some qualitative measures, such as humans labeling training data that are then used to build language-based models.","Back and colleagues (2010) analyzed over 400,000 pager messages sent the morning of the 9/11 terrorist attacks and initially reported a spike in anger references, but closer inspection revealed that most of the anger words were automated error messages sent by overwhelmed networks that included the word critical, which was part of LIWC's anger category at the time.","The original 9/11 pager authors later published a failure analysis discussing how the misinterpretation occurred and providing recommendations for avoiding such errors in large linguistic datasets, illustrating the importance of reading texts in addition to analyzing them quantitatively (Back et al., 2011).","Word- or lemma-level analyses involve many exploratory tests, so these methods typically report only correlates that survive after controlling for false discovery rates and family-wise error, and the Benjamini-Hochberg method is commonly used because it is more flexible and less conservative than the Bonferroni strategy of dividing the p-value criterion by the number of hypotheses tested.","Early projective methods such as Rorschach (1921) inkblots and the thematic apperception test suffered from methodological limitations, including poor reliability across stimuli and judges and, against Rorschach's explicit advice, a frequent lack of blinding that resulted in experimenter effects and false positives (Wood et al., 2000).","Most of LIWC's dictionaries are top-down in that they were initially created by researchers for an a priori research aim rather than being emergent properties of a text sample, although they are also informed by data such as word-level correlates and word embeddings.","All dictionary-based emotion measures are limited by individual and cultural differences in emotional expressiveness, since many people repress emotional expression and even expressive people often show rather than verbalize their emotions, and for these reasons no sentiment analysis method approaches perfect accuracy.","Most emotional-language measures modestly predict theory-consistent outcomes such as distress, depression, mood, well-being, and relationship quality, and this apparent paradox may mean that emotional language maps onto chronic mood states more than it reflects in-the-moment emotions.","With a sufficiently large and representative sample of naturalistic behavior, statistical safeguards such as replication and controlling for multiple comparisons are for the most part obviated by techniques such as cross-validation, and large datasets allow for self-replication on a held-out sample.","In topic modeling, it is important to pay attention to the data first and any subjective labels second, because topic labels will always be more subjective and less informative than the words that make up the topics themselves.","EAR recordings require human involvement and oversight: even if recordings are automatically transcribed, people are needed for coding the social context of language samples, since personality manifests differently across social contexts (Mehl, Robbins, & Holleran, 2012)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Language Analysis","topic":"The Dictionary Matrix: Categories, Word Vectors, and Topic Models","visual_frame_description":"A glowing grid displays rows of dictionaries and columns of psychological constructs, with word chips snapping into their cells as you match them, from LIWC's I category to MFD2.0's Sanctity and EMPATH's Envy. Beside the grid, a vector field shows arrows from the seed word plan linking to commitment and decision, while three rotating gears labeled LDA, MEM, and LSA spin above a corpus of open-ended essays.","player_mission":"You are a psychometrician assembling a dictionary matrix for a new study on morality and well-being. Match psychological constructs to word lists from LIWC, the Moral Foundations Dictionary, EMPATH, PERMA, and Prospection, and place each category into the matrix grid. Then extend the matrix with word embeddings, judging whether seed words like plan extend to commitment and decision via co-occurrence, and decide which topic-modeling approach - LDA, MEM, or LSA - fits your corpus best.","key_concepts":["LIWC dictionaries include categories such as I (with example words I, me, mine, my), Tone_neg for negative emotional tone (crying, idiot, anxious, yuck), and Prosocial (advise, support, kindness, care) (Pennebaker et al., 2022).","The updated Moral Foundations Dictionary 2.0 includes categories such as Loyalty (patriot, ally, betray, traitor), Fairness (equals, trust, mooch, robber), and Sanctity (sacred, clean, trash, infected) (Frimer et al., 2017).","EMPATH is a word-embedding-based dictionary created by computer scientists that includes 200 pre-validated topics such as aggression, tourism, and blue-collar work, with categories including Art (dance, exhibition, design, paint), Envy (discontent, flaunt, yearn, resent), and Stealing (heist, mobster, liable, swindle), plus code for generating novel dictionaries (Fast et al., 2016).","The PERMA dictionaries from the World Well-Being Project include categories such as Relationships (love, baby, birthday, loveu), Meaning (life, world, wisdom, journey), and Engagement (watching, night, fun, awesome) (Schwartz et al., 2013).","The Prospection dictionary includes categories for Present (justwant, waitingfor, ilove, bored), Past (was, saw, did, spent), and Future (tomorrow, tonight, gonna, wait), and both PERMA and Prospection lexica are weighted both positively and negatively (Schwartz et al., 2015).","Word embeddings, or word vectors, are mappings of words onto dimensions, and word-embedding measures of semantic similarity are based on usage across text samples, according to the distributional hypothesis that words used in similar contexts have similar latent meanings independent of their definitional similarity (Wittgenstein, 1953).","The most common word-embedding methods analyze how word frequencies correlate with each other across text samples and then reduce those correlations to word vectors akin to factors in principal-components analysis, resulting in term-dimension matrices such as word2vec or GloVe (Mikolov et al., 2013; Pennington et al., 2014).","In the chapter's example, three-dimensional vectors for rat (1, 1, 0) and horse (1, 0, 1) share the first dimension (possibly representing animal) while differing on the second and third, and the mean absolute difference of 0.667 measures their semantic distance.","When using word embeddings to build dictionaries, researchers start with theoretically central seed words; for example, a planning dictionary would start with variants of plan and their direct synonyms such as scheme and strategize, and the LIWC-22 dictionary workbench shows that commitment and decision are semantically near planning without being synonymous with it.","Latent semantic analysis (LSA) uses singular value decomposition, a data reduction technique similar to traditional factor analysis, on word co-occurrences to calculate semantic similarity between texts and to extract meaning from texts, with early applications that automatically retrieved information and assessed writing assignments (Foltz, 1996).","Topic modeling automatically identifies coherent themes in a text, typically based on word co-occurrences that disregard syntax or word order, and the most commonly used approaches are based on latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA; Blei et al., 2003).","The meaning extraction method (MEM) is a simple and intuitive topic-modeling method based on factor analysis (Chung & Pennebaker, 2008), and in both MEM and LDA, humans are required to interpret the word clusters that make up each topic, with labels ranging from straightforward (medical references) to abstract (loneliness)."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Language Analysis","topic":"The Future of Text Analysis: Generative Models, Big Data, and Ethics","visual_frame_description":"A command deck overlooks streams of social media and pager data flowing into a giant transformer core glowing blue, with labels word2vec, BERT, and GPT-4. Ethics dials labeled false positives and bias feedback loop require balancing as the core generates sample texts, while a wall chart ranks predictive accuracy of language models against self-report scales and a banner reads 'they need us as much as we need them.'","player_mission":"You are the principal investigator of a language lab deciding its agenda for the next decade. Evaluate whether to adopt transformer-based generative models, plan a large-scale naturalistic data collection with self-replication, and set ethical guardrails for AI-written text. Compare the predictive power of GPT-style models against self-report scales, draft a collaboration plan with computer scientists, and win the boss fight by designing a study in which language serves as independent variable, dependent variable, and mediator in the same sample.","key_concepts":["Within social psychology, language is unique in that it can be an independent variable, a dependent variable, and a mediator in the same sample, depending on the analytic approach and the research questions, and analyzing words allows researchers to track speakers' psychological state, their framing of their message, and how readers or listeners interpret the message.","Using big-data methods, it is possible to determine how large groups change in their thinking styles, emotional experiences, and social connections in response to personal or shared upheavals such as relationship breakups, COVID, the death of a loved one, or cultural events (Ashokkumar & Pennebaker, 2022; Seraj et al., 2021).","Transformer-based models are dramatically improving linguistic prediction models, and Kjell and colleagues (2022) found that transformer-based models of open-ended responses to questions about well-being predicted self-reported well-being as accurately as the self-report scales predict themselves, exceeding the scale's mean inter-item correlations and test-retest reliability.","Sap and colleagues (2022) showed that sequentiality, an index of how predictable an utterance is based on those that came before it, is stronger in imagined and retold stories than in autobiographical stories told for the first time, suggesting that individuals' stories converge with the cultural average as they retell and reconstruct their experiences.","Charlesworth and colleagues (2022) analyzed word embeddings in books published over the last 200 years and found that time has tempered explicit racist and nationalist prejudices, but the valences associated with social groups have remained mostly unchanged.","Generative language models allow comparisons between how people talk and how the culture talks, and transformer-based models are revealing how language evolves over time and demonstrating that the ceiling for the predictive power of words is higher than was previously suspected.","The recent introduction of ChatGPT as a free and publicly accessible chat agent brought generative language models to the forefront of the Internet's consciousness and introduced new practical and ethical questions about using AI to make art, complete homework, write academic papers, and learn to communicate more effectively.","Knowing that a supportive message such as a condolence email was written or cowritten by AI may undermine perceived empathy, even though transformer-based conversational agents can help people respond more empathetically to requests for support in peer-to-peer therapy (Liu, Mittal, et al., 2022; Sharma et al., 2023).","Large datasets allow for self-replication on a held-out sample, and questionable research practices such as flexible exclusion criteria, atheoretical fishing, and p-hacking can be forgiven if the unexpected findings are found again in independent samples with appropriate statistical power.","As we develop systems that increasingly emulate prescient beings and can make more accurate predictions of behavior, researchers must avoid the fallout of false positives, such as being inaccurately labeled a security risk, and ethically make use of accurate predictions while testing empirically what could go wrong when language-based findings are applied at scale.","Researchers should pay special attention to avoiding a bias feedback loop when creating models or generating language trained on inherently biased naturalistic language, for example by learning to prevent chatbots from recapitulating the racism and sexism they find on the Internet (Hovy & Prabhumoye, 2021).","Language research is challenging and requires interdisciplinarity and, for some methods, a bare minimum of computer programming knowledge, yet it is easy to test the waters of computerized text analysis because many tools require virtually no training to use and even creating a novel dictionary can be accomplished quickly with the right tools."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm15","title":"Chapter 15: Collecting Digital Footprints in the Wild","pages":"349-376","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Digital Footprints","topic":"Match or Mismatch: What Dating Data Really Reveals","visual_frame_description":"A data dashboard splits in two: the left panel shows a heatmap grid of OkCupid reply probabilities by the race and gender of sender and receiver, while the right panel shows cheerful self-report survey answers about what people say they look for in a partner. The two columns refuse to agree, and cells light up as you match each reported preference to the behavior that contradicts it.","player_mission":"Review the OkCupid response-rate data and compare people's self-reported dating preferences against the actual behavior recorded in their digital footprints. Rebuild the response-probability grid by sender and receiver demographics, then write up what the gap between survey answers and observed choices says about response bias.","key_concepts":["Digital footprints are typically generated by people going about their business in natural environments, rather than in the artificial setting of a lab, which gives them high ecological validity.","Self-reports tend to capture explicit, conscious, and motivated opinions rather than real behaviors and preferences, and people find it hard to admit to socially undesirable preferences.","In 2020, about half of new couples met online, so dating platforms now record an enormous amount of real romantic behavior.","Dating app users spend about ninety minutes per day on average on the platform, generating hundreds of swipes and tens of messages.","Studies conducted on dating website data found that physical attractiveness is the most important factor in romantic choices, closely followed by income (Hitsch et al., 2010).","When sending an initial message to a white woman on OkCupid, Black men have a 21 percent chance of a response, compared with 29 percent for White men (Rudder, 2009).","When sending an initial message to a Black man, Black women hear back 37 percent of the time, compared with 51 percent for White women (Rudder, 2009).","The outsized role of physical beauty in online dating may partly stem from the difficulty of judging character from a dating profile.","People may honestly believe that they do not care about others' race (or are too embarrassed to admit it), yet their digital footprints prove otherwise.","Digital footprints can typically be collected retrospectively, days, weeks, or even years after they were left behind, capturing spontaneous behavior.","Following people around to observe their actual romantic advances is prohibitively expensive, time-consuming, and virtually guarantees the alteration of participants' behavior."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Digital Footprints","topic":"Designing Experiments in the Digital Wild","visual_frame_description":"A study-design studio offers three selectable design cards, each unfolding into a live funnel showing participants flowing into treatment and control arms. Miniature replays of the Facebook voting experiment, the court fine reminder trial, and the hurricane study play in the background as you lock in your design.","player_mission":"You are advising a team that wants to test whether tailored messages change real-world behavior. Pick the right design (RCT, natural experiment, or quasi-experiment) for each research question, allocate participants into treatment and control conditions, and defend your choices against reviewers.","key_concepts":["In 2020, Google, LinkedIn, and Microsoft alone conducted about 20,000 randomized controlled trials, which they commonly refer to as A/B tests.","In a typical A/B test, a randomly selected fraction of users accessing a website are presented with an alternative version of it, and observing changes in their behavior reveals the effect.","Bond et al. (2012) randomly divided the 61 million U.S. Facebook users active on the day of the 2010 congressional elections into three groups to test the effectiveness of voting encouragement messages.","Including pictures of friends who had already voted in the voting message increased the likelihood of voting by 0.4 percent, and the friends of those users were also more likely to vote.","An RCT by the Behavioral Insights Team at the UK Cabinet Office showed that texting people to remind them to pay court fines worked, and that messages including the recipient's name were particularly efficient.","The court fine study led the Court Service to adopt the approach, saving about 30 million pounds and 150,000 bailiff interventions annually.","Egebark et al. (2021) posted twelve fictitious dating profiles, randomly assigned active users to receive invitations, and found that educated men preferred uneducated women while educated women sought educated men.","Breza et al. (2021) randomly divided U.S. zip codes into experimental and control conditions and used Facebook ads to encourage staying home over the holidays, reducing infections by about 3.5 percent.","In natural experiments, people are randomly (or quasi-randomly) split into groups due to processes outside the experimenters' control, such as a hurricane's path.","Phan and Airoldi (2015) studied nearly 65,000 students affected by Hurricane Ike and found that directly affected students formed stronger social bonds in its aftermath.","Matz et al. (2017) used the Facebook Ads platform to test psychological targeting of the kind employed by Cambridge Analytica, and targeting people with personality-aligned ads increased sales by about 50 percent.","People who liked extroverted pages were 77 percent more likely to buy a cosmetic item when exposed to the extrovert-tailored ad than when shown the introvert-tailored one."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Digital Footprints","topic":"The Data Hunter's Toolkit","visual_frame_description":"A circular tool rack displays seven acquisition cards: existing data sets, building your own app, collaborating with an existing app, APIs, web scraping, participant uploads, and foundational models. Each card flips over to reveal an effort-versus-yield gauge, and the myPersonality success story plays as a tutorial reel.","player_mission":"A research group needs to obtain millions of digital footprints. Walk through the seven acquisition strategies, estimate cost, time, and risk for each, and build a sourcing plan that matches the study's budget and ethical constraints.","key_concepts":["Before attempting to collect your own data, ensure that the data you are looking for do not already exist, as existing data sets save time, expense, and the participants' burden.","A quickly growing number of publicly available data sets can be found on repositories such as Wikipedia's list of datasets for machine-learning research, ICPSR, Kaggle, and Google Dataset Search.","Governments host enormous data portals, including over 300,000 data sets on data.gov and over 1.5 million on data.europa.eu.","David Stillwell's myPersonality app, published on Facebook in 2007, went viral and amassed over 6 million users before it was discontinued in 2012, and Google Scholar returns over 1,600 papers when searching for the project.","The myPersonality app incentivized honest responding by offering extensive feedback on scores rather than financial rewards, because dishonest respondents would merely waste their time.","Creating your own app to collect research data is a risky and difficult endeavor, as apps require constant updating and compete for the audience with tech companies that have much larger budgets.","When collaborating with existing apps, researchers should make sure not to agree to terms limiting their scholarly freedom, such as giving up control over whether results may be published.","The API provided by X (formerly Twitter) is one of the most widely used in psychological research, and anyone can register for free on X's developer page to download tweets and profile data.","When APIs are unavailable, researchers may use web scraping, and Richard Landers et al. (2016) provide an excellent tutorial aimed at psychologists.","Landers et al.'s scraping code took approximately twenty hours to download over 165,000 posts from the Healthboards.com website, and the authors deliberately slowed their script to wait two seconds after recording each post.","Many platforms, including Google, X, Facebook, Apple, TikTok, and 23andme, allow users to download archives of their own digital footprints, an approach that should be used only when accessing participants' data through an API is impossible.","Large pre-trained models such as GPT-3 make it possible to study digital footprints without ever collecting them, and personality perceptions of public figures extracted from GPT-3 correlated at r = 0.7 to 0.8 with traditional survey methods (Cao & Kosinski, 2024)."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Digital Footprints","topic":"Footprint Forensics: Reading the Signals","visual_frame_description":"A forensic grid lights up like a flight-connection board: footprint types (usage logs, language, mobile sensors, images) on one axis and predicted attributes (personality, well-being, mobility, emotion) on the other. Each successful match traces a glowing line, and a confidence meter shows external validity, reliability, and the correlation with questionnaire scores.","player_mission":"Your task is to classify the digital footprint left in each evidence locker, match it to the psychological attributes it reveals, and load the correct analysis tool for the format. Build a predictive model and defend it against a skeptical reviewer who questions the quality of the data.","key_concepts":["Usage logs take the form of diary-like records filled with time-stamped entries, such as “Michal Kosinski listened to Lady Gaga's song Poker face at 3:00pm on January 1, 2022.”","Facebook Likes, one of the simplest types of usage log, reveal individuals' political and religious views, relationship status, substance abuse, race, sexual orientation, well-being, intelligence, and personality (Kosinski et al., 2013).","Bank statements have been shown to be linked with well-being (Matz et al., 2016), purchase records can be used to predict pregnancy (Hill, 2012), and edits on a movie's Wikipedia page can predict its popularity and commercial success.","Digital footprints solve the two historical problems of language research: communication is now easily recorded in natural settings, and computing power has fueled powerful but easy-to-use language analysis tools.","Mobile devices such as smartwatches, fitness trackers, and smartphones are packed with precise sensors that record physical movement, mobility patterns, social interactions, and events such as coughing or brushing teeth.","Combining cartographic data with GPS coordinates reveals a user's location (such as workplace, home, or shopping center) and the particular routes they traveled to get there.","Combining data from accelerometers and microphones reveals behaviors such as clapping, vacuuming, or taking out the trash, and phone usage logs combined with other sensors can reveal sleep patterns.","Speech-to-text technology can extract text from audio files, visual object recognition algorithms can detect and label objects within images, and widely available facial detection software can label emotional expressions.","Kosinski (2017) used over 135,000 Facebook profile images and a facial detection algorithm to test claims linking facial width with antisocial behavior, finding the approach as accurate as manual coding but vastly faster, saving about 600 work hours.","Big Five personality scores can be derived from blog posts, Facebook status updates, tweets, website browsing logs, search queries, music collections, Facebook Likes, and mobile phone usage.","Personality estimates based on digital footprints show high external validity, high test-retest reliability, and high concurrent validity, and they highly correlate with conventional questionnaire-based scores (R = 0.56).","Youyou et al. (2017) collected Facebook data from over 50,000 pairs of friends and romantic couples and showed that partners and friends do have similar personalities once personality is measured from digital footprints rather than self-reports."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Digital Footprints","topic":"Big Data, Big Responsibility","visual_frame_description":"A dark war-room screen shows a live risk map of the lab's planned data collection: platform-change alerts scroll past, a policy-compliance panel flashes, consent zones are drawn around public versus private data, and a de-anonymization counter ticks up as location points accumulate on a map.","player_mission":"You are the ethics reviewer for a lab that wants to scrape millions of public profiles, and the clock is running. Inspect each threat in the risk dossier, weigh the proposed safeguards, and decide whether each study can proceed, must be revised, or must be rejected.","key_concepts":["Digital platforms are constantly re-engineered and updated, altering their users' behavior and the data they generate, and these changes often occur unannounced.","In December 2007, a seemingly minor change to Facebook's status update prompt shifted users from self-obsessed to other-focused updates, showing how platform changes can alter the data that users generate.","The Google autocomplete suggestion for the search term starting with “arejews” in December 2016 was not evidence of a spike in anti-Semitism but the result of a small group of anti-Semitic websites deliberately manipulating Google's PageRank algorithm.","Platform policies typically restrict the ways in which data can be collected and used, even if it is publicly available or a user consented to its recording, and Facebook made several attempts to halt Kosinski's research citing such policies.","In the digital wild, the boundaries between public and private data are often vague: dating profile information, while often publicly available, is treated as personal, and people commonly pretend not to know facts gleaned from dating website profiles.","Data volunteered by study participants often contains information contributed by others, such as images featuring other people or comments left on participants' profiles, so the boundaries of participants' consent are unclear.","A typical subset of a single individual's digital footprints contains more data than they could manually review, and the author's own data captured by Google services amounts to about eight gigabytes per day.","Even a small subset of someone's digital footprints is virtually guaranteed to be unique, and any four locations from one's geolocation history, along with timestamps, can identify a person the way a fingerprint does (de Montjoye et al., 2013).","Research has shown that date of birth, gender, and zip code allow for identifying nearly 90 percent of the U.S. population when matched with large publicly available data sets (Sweeney, 1997).","Just because data can be obtained does not imply that it is legal or ethically appropriate to use it for research (Schultze & Mason, 2012).","Open science practices make digital footprint research harder: journals and funding agencies are less tolerant of scientists who do not share data, yet footprints are difficult to review and anonymize and may contain data pertaining to nonparticipants.","Digital footprints' robustness and high ecological validity make them virtually impossible to anonymize, yet researchers must carefully weigh the potential social benefits of their contributions against the potential risks to participants."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm16","title":"Chapter 16: Behavioral Observation and Coding","pages":"378-402","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Behavioral Coding","topic":"Observing and Coding: What It Is and Why It Matters","visual_frame_description":"A research lab whiteboard is split into two columns: one labeled behavioral observation, listing a researcher watching and hearing actions in real time, and one labeled behavioral coding, showing assistants turning those actions into numbers in a spreadsheet. Icons of a handheld video camera, a clipboard, and a computer with NLP gear float nearby, with a balance scale weighing strengths against challenges.","player_mission":"Define behavioral observation and behavioral coding, then contrast manual coding with automated coding done by computer programs. Build a checklist of the method's strengths (direct measurement, low interference, temporal frequency, portability) and its three main challenges (measurement difficulties, labor intensity, time intensity).","key_concepts":["Behavioral observation is the process that occurs when a researcher sees or hears the actions of a person or multiple persons.","Behavioral coding is the process that occurs when other people systematically document those actions in quantitative form.","Manual coding stands in contrast to automated coding, which uses tools from computer science such as natural-language processing rather than people to quantify behaviors.","The chapter focuses on observation of direct behaviors and actions of a person, rather than the products or outcomes of a person's behaviors.","The primary strength of behavioral observation and coding is that it explicitly measures the variables of interest to researchers.","Self-reports about behavior are subject to numerous biases and do not show a one-to-one correspondence with real behavior.","Behavioral observation requires relatively low levels of interference, reducing social desirability biases and experimenter effects.","Behavior can be assessed with a high degree of temporal frequency, often continuously throughout a study or at multiple timepoints.","Behavioral data can be recorded in a wide range of settings, including outside the lab, often with a simple handheld video camera.","Behavioral coding is not immune to measurement difficulties, including whether a code is reliable and whether it is a true measure of the behavior.","Behavioral observation and coding are labor-intensive because multiple research assistants must be hired, trained, and supervised throughout the coding process.","The first guiding principle is to lead with your conceptual question and not build a research question around behaviors that are easy to record."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Behavioral Coding","topic":"Designing Observation: Settings, Recording, and Code Types","visual_frame_description":"A settings dial rotates from a city park (naturalistic), to a living room with a no-devices rule (quasi-naturalistic), to a lab with couples discussing conflicts (analog), to a participant talking with a confederate (experimental). Beside it, two code ladders stand side by side: one with presence-absence checkboxes and one with a 1-to-7 intensity scale, plus two clocks labeled macro (one summary rating) and micro (every five seconds).","player_mission":"Walk through the continuum of observation approaches, from naturalistic to quasi-naturalistic to analog to experimental manipulation with confederates. Then decide between topographical and dimensional codes and between macro and micro systems, judging which code types fit the behavior's observability and variability.","key_concepts":["Observation and coding can occur live in real time or later from documentation such as videotape, audio recording, or a text transcript.","Recordings afford flexibility because they can be viewed repeatedly, slowed down for temporal precision, and used for secondary data analysis.","Behavioral observation occurs in environments ranging from tightly controlled settings to completely naturalistic contexts.","With naturalistic observation, researchers exert no influence over participants or the setting, giving the approach ecological validity and relevance.","Quasi-naturalistic observation preserves ecological validity while placing some constraints, such as telling families no devices or no leaving home during a two-hour observation window.","With analog observation, researchers use more control to construct in the lab situations of the kind in which the behaviors of interest occur.","In experimental manipulation designs, researchers may control the interaction partner through a confederate or even a virtual confederate.","Behaviors and constructs vary in observability, and in general more overt behaviors are easier to code and to achieve reliable estimates for.","Topographical codes judge whether a behavior is present or absent and are useful when there is a clear, meaningful distinction between presence and absence.","Dimensional codes capture the intensity of a behavior on a scale, such as rating negative affect on a scale of 1 to 7 during a conflict conversation.","With both code types, variability in measurements is important because low variability can impair inter-rater reliability estimates.","Macro coding systems involve global or overall codes made over longer lengths of time, while micro systems involve more frequent and fine-grained coding."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Behavioral Coding","topic":"Building and Managing the Coding Team","visual_frame_description":"A personnel chart shows junior research assistants clustered around a lead coder, with a 25 percent overlap badge on a pie chart of the coding workload. A five-step staircase labeled scheme, self-test, teach, practice, and independent coding rises behind them, while a drifting arrow warns that ratings can change over time, and a wall sign reads: randomize, don't binge-code, ask questions.","player_mission":"Select coders who are conscientious and blind to the hypotheses, decide how many coders you need and how much they should overlap, and choose what study information coders may see. Then run the five-step coder training process and lead the team with weekly meetings that minimize coder drift.","key_concepts":["Coders should not have extensive knowledge of the study hypotheses and research objectives, and they are often junior members of the research team such as undergraduate research assistants.","Coders should be conscientious, organized, and detail-oriented, and they must follow coding instructions closely without applying their own interpretations of behavior.","Researchers generally worry most about generalizability when codes involve more subjectivity, and in those instances they try to have a larger number of coders.","You must have some overlap among coders in order to assess inter-rater reliability, and often people recommend 25 percent overlap.","The more your coders overlap and the less you rely on any one single coder, the better.","Researchers should think about coder overlap at the level of coding units or time intervals rather than participants, dyads, or groups, so reliability estimates are not based on agreement on only a subset of participants.","Generally, the less information coders have about the specific study hypotheses, the fewer chances there are for their judgments to inadvertently conform to or refute those hypotheses.","When outcomes are at the level of the dyad or group, coders need access to all interactants' behavior, but with individual-level outcomes coders' judgments of one person can be affected by information about that person's interaction partners.","In the first step of training, researchers develop an initial coding scheme that describes all the behaviors they want documented and how they want them documented.","Lead researchers test the coding scheme themselves and develop a coding manual that includes answers to likely questions and specifics about how borderline or questionable cases should be coded.","Coders first practice on example observations in small groups and then independently code new example participants, and researchers only proceed once coders have reached an acceptable level of inter-rater reliability.","Coding teams should meet weekly to minimize coder drift, and although disagreements are discussed, ratings are not updated or resolved because that would destroy their independence."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Behavioral Coding","topic":"Coding Logistics and Reliability Statistics","visual_frame_description":"A video editor timeline shows multiple camera angles, color-coded coding passes for questions, smiles, and emotional tenor, and visual cue markers at segment boundaries. A calculator splits into two branches: one counting nominal codes through Cohen's kappa with Landis-Koch benchmarks, and one feeding ordinal ratings into ICC formulas, with a checklist reading remove mistakes, match the statistic to your final analysis measure, and report the variant used.","player_mission":"Set up your recording and coding workflow: test your recording system before launching, decide how much of the observation to code, use coding passes, and document codes in spreadsheets or specialized software. Then select and compute the right reliability statistic, weighing Cohen's kappa against intraclass correlation coefficients.","key_concepts":["Researchers should fully test out a recording system and coding scheme before officially collecting data, and have their team code pilot recordings before launching the study.","When recording, researchers should consider multiple camera angles, high-quality video with proper zoom and lighting, separate audio recording devices, and visual cues to mark the beginnings and ends of tasks.","With longer tasks, researchers often select shorter intervals to code under the assumption that those segments are representative of longer periods of time, an approach known as thin-slicing.","Coding passes are separate instances of processing a recording for particular codes, such as marking task start and stop times on one pass and questions on another.","When structuring coding passes, the goal is to get as much information from coders as you can with as little effort as possible, and coding related variables together in one pass is often efficient.","Spreadsheet programs are cost-effective, require minimal training, and have easily manipulable data, while specialized software like Datavyu or Noldus Observer keeps all information about a recording in one place.","When two or more people code behavioral observations, researchers must assess inter-rater reliability, the degree to which those coders agree in their ratings.","Inter-rater reliability is important because many behavioral codes have some degree of subjectivity, and it matters for statistical power and validity since unreliable measurements are poor representations of theoretical constructs.","Cohen's kappa is appropriate when the final analyzed behavioral outcome is measured on a nominal scale, and it assesses agreement while adjusting for agreement expected due to chance.","Cohen's kappa values range from -1 (complete disagreement) to 1 (complete agreement), with values from 0.6 to 0.8 considered substantial and 0.8 to 1 almost perfect agreement.","The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) is used for variables measured on ordinal, interval, or ratio scales and is rooted in generalizability theory, which decomposes variance into sources such as participant, coder, and their interaction.","When calculating ICCs, researchers must decide whether coders are random or fixed effects, whether the same or different coders coded each participant, whether absolute agreement or consistency is needed, and whether the unit of analysis is average or single measures."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Behavioral Coding","topic":"Analyzing Coded Data and Open-Science Practices","visual_frame_description":"A final analysis dashboard shows three gauges labeled reliability, aggregation, and nonindependence, with pipelines feeding averages across coders, summed frequencies across codes, and sums across time. Around it float a robot arm coding video frames, a transcript with dictionary keywords highlighted, and an open-science shelf holding a preregistration form, a transparency report card, and a Databrary repository box.","player_mission":"Tackle the three major analytic issues in behavioral data: inter-rater reliability, aggregation of coded responses, and nonindependence of measurements. Then navigate the modern toolkit of automated coding, text analysis, preregistration, transparency, and open data, deciding how each applies to your coding project.","key_concepts":["There are three major analytic issues relevant to behavioral data: inter-rater agreement and reliability, aggregated coded responses, and potential nonindependence of measurements.","Reliability is the extent to which a measurement can be reproduced, and unreliable measurements make it harder to detect true relationships among variables.","If all coders coded all participants, averaging across coders can allow you to incorporate the number of coders into your reliability calculation, which can boost reliability estimates.","Researchers can aggregate responses across codes, for example summing frequencies of fussing, crying, and gaze aversion to create a more general measure of infant negative engagement.","When aggregating across codes, you should make sure the behavioral codes hang well together by assessing internal consistency, for example with a variance decomposition approach.","Aggregating responses across time, such as summing the number of times people smiled over an entire conversation, can lead to more reliable estimates and a more parsimonious analysis.","Nonindependence exists when outcomes cannot be considered fully independent of one another, as with repeated measures over time within a person or measures from people linked in a dyad, family, or team.","Traditional statistical approaches like ANOVA and linear regression assume independent observations, so they often cannot be used when analyzing coded behavioral data.","Automated coding trains computer models to recognize behaviors of interest based on a subset of information, and one example uses computer vision and machine learning to code facial affect intensity.","One drawback of automated approaches is that they generally require substantial expertise in computer science, and it can be challenging to code macro behaviors in ways congruent with human perceptions.","Because behavioral observation and coding are iterative, researchers who want to preregister should outline the general stages of their process and how they will make decisions during each stage, since preregistration is not all-or-nothing.","Behavioral coding often receives just a few sentences in methods sections, and the authors encourage transparency about decisions such as how coders were trained and how coder drift was handled, even if only in a supplement."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm17","title":"Chapter 17: Automaticity and Implicit Measures","pages":"404-425","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Implicit Measures","topic":"Four Features of Automaticity: Unintentionality, Efficiency, Controllability, Awareness","visual_frame_description":"A four-chamber machine hall, each chamber labeled with one criterion: an instruction card flips for unintentionality, a clock and a digit-loading conveyor run for efficiency, a stop-sign lever stands for uncontrollability, and a masked subliminal flash room represents unconsciousness. A central control panel warns 'test features separately,' while two conveyor belts labeled formation and expression run in opposite directions under a sign reading 'operating conditions, not operating principles.'","player_mission":"Compare the four criteria of automatic processing - unintentionality, efficiency, uncontrollability, and unconsciousness - and design an experimental manipulation that tests each one. Then apply the pragmatic rules for studying automaticity: test each feature independently, distinguish operating conditions from operating principles, guard against null-effect inferences, and separate the formation from the expression of mental representations.","key_concepts":["The four basic criteria of automatic processing are unintentionality, efficiency, uncontrollability, and unconsciousness (Bargh, 1994).","The unintentionality criterion refers to the question of whether a given process operates in the absence of a goal to start the process.","A common approach to studying unintentionality is to compare the emergence of a given effect under different task instructions, for example with and without explicit impression-formation instructions.","The efficiency criterion refers to the question of whether a given process operates when the amount of invested or available processing resources is small.","The modal approach to studying efficiency is to compare the emergence of a given effect under conditions of different processing resources, such as varying time pressure or requiring a concurrent secondary task.","The uncontrollability criterion is concerned with goals to alter or stop a process, and a common approach is to investigate the effectiveness of instructions to alter or prevent a given effect.","Evidence that control instructions are ineffective merely demonstrates that the underlying process is uncontrolled, but it is insufficient to conclude that the process is uncontrollable, because participants may have used an ineffective control strategy.","The unconsciousness criterion refers to the question of whether a given process operates in the absence of conscious awareness, and empirical tests require further specification of what exactly is claimed to be outside awareness.","Effects of unconscious stimuli can be studied by comparing their effects when presented supraliminally versus subliminally, with subliminal presentations typically involving very short target presentations and masking stimuli, or the alternative technique of continuous flash suppression.","The different features of automaticity do not overlap, so each feature has to be tested independently and the umbrella term automatic remains ambiguous if the particular sense is not specified.","Statements about automaticity features specify only the operating conditions under which a process operates, not the operating principles by which the process translates environmental inputs into behavioral outputs.","It is important to distinguish between the processes involved in the formation of mental representations and the processes involved in the behavioral expression of mental representations."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Implicit Measures","topic":"Implicit Versus Explicit Measures and the Implicit Association Test","visual_frame_description":"A split screen shows two mirrored worlds: on the left, a hand filling out a self-report questionnaire beside a smiley rating scale, and on the right, a White face and a positive word snapping together on one key while a Black face and a positive word snap together on another key with a timer icon. A branch diagram below grows five variant nodes labeled single-category, single-attribute, recoding-free, single-block, and brief IAT, each tagged with an internal-consistency badge.","player_mission":"Contrast implicit measures, which capture automatic responses, with traditional self-report measures, which capture nonautomatic responses. Then explain the logic of the Implicit Association Test, including its two combined binary categorization tasks, its compatible and incompatible critical blocks, and its difference-score interpretation, and evaluate the trade-offs of its procedural variants.","key_concepts":["A shared feature of the instruments called implicit measures is that they capture automatic responses that may differ from the nonautomatic responses captured by traditional self-report measures.","The most frequently used task among the listed implicit measures is the implicit association test (IAT), which consists of two binary categorization tasks that are combined in a manner that is either compatible or incompatible with a to-be-measured psychological attribute.","In a race IAT, participants respond to positive words and pictures of White faces with one key and to negative words and pictures of Black faces with another key in one critical block, with the category-to-key assignments reversed in the other critical block.","The rationale underlying the IAT is that quick and accurate responses are facilitated when the key mapping is compatible with a participant's preference, but impaired when the key mapping is preference-incompatible.","The difference in participants' speed and accuracy in the two critical blocks is typically interpreted as an index of their relative preference for one category over the other, depending on the calculation of the difference score.","By using evaluative attribute dimensions, the IAT can be used to assess relative preferences between any pairs of objects or categories, such as White versus Black people, men versus women, or Coke versus Pepsi.","The evaluative attribute dimension of the IAT may be replaced with a specific semantic dimension to assess relative semantic responses, such as stereotypical responses linking men and women to the concepts career versus household.","The IAT typically shows estimates of internal consistency that are comparable to those of traditional self-report measures.","A common concern about the IAT is that its task structure is inherently comparative, so it is not possible to calculate separate indices for evaluations of individual target concepts such as Black people and White people.","The presentation of compatible and incompatible trials in separate, consecutive blocks can distort measurement scores through various sources of systematic error variance.","Procedural variants of the IAT include the single-category IAT and the single-attribute IAT for individual target concepts or attributes, the recoding-free IAT and the single-block IAT, and an abbreviated brief IAT.","A downside of the new IAT variants is that they tend to undercut desirable characteristics of the standard IAT, in that most variants show lower internal consistencies, with the single-category IAT being the exception that has demonstrated its usefulness in a considerable number of studies."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Implicit Measures","topic":"Priming and Misattribution Tasks: Evaluative and Semantic Priming, AMP, SMP","visual_frame_description":"A conveyor line of trial sequences shows a brief prime flash, then a target word or neutral Chinese ideograph, then a black-and-white pattern mask, each step lit in a different color with a millisecond counter above. A spreadsheet beside the line scores internal-consistency bands for evaluative priming (low), the AMP (high), and the SMP (moderate), with a warning tag reading 'intentional prime evaluation possible' near the AMP station.","player_mission":"Work through the family of sequential-priming instruments: the evaluative priming task, semantic priming with lexical-decision and semantic-decision tasks, the affect misattribution procedure (AMP), and the semantic misattribution procedure (SMP). For each instrument, describe the trial procedure, the kind of response it measures, and its psychometric strengths and weaknesses.","key_concepts":["The evaluative priming task employs the basic procedure of sequential priming to assess evaluative responses, in which participants are briefly presented with a prime stimulus followed by a positive or negative target word.","In the typical evaluative-decision version of the evaluative priming task, participants are asked to quickly determine whether the target word is positive or negative by pressing one of two response keys.","A major problem of the evaluative priming task is its low internal consistency, which rarely exceeds estimates of 0.50.","Semantic priming with a lexical-decision task uses meaningful words and meaningless letter strings as target stimuli, and participants' task is to determine as quickly as possible whether the letter string is a meaningful word or a meaningless nonword.","In semantic priming with a lexical-decision task, the extent to which a prime stimulus facilitates quick responses to a meaningful target word indicates that the prime is associated with the semantic meaning of the target word.","Because lexical classifications tend to be substantially faster than evaluative or semantic classifications, priming effects in lexical-decision tasks are often in the range of only a few milliseconds and are particularly prone to measurement error.","Semantic priming with a semantic-decision task includes only meaningful words as target stimuli, with participants asked to categorize the target words in terms of their semantic rather than evaluative meaning.","In the affect misattribution procedure (AMP), participants are briefly presented with a prime stimulus that is followed by a brief presentation of a neutral Chinese ideograph, which is then replaced by a black-and-white pattern mask.","In the AMP, participants indicate whether they consider the Chinese ideograph visually more pleasant or less pleasant than the average ideograph, and the typical finding is that neutral ideographs are evaluated more favorably after positive than after negative primes.","A major advantage of the AMP is that it shows larger effect sizes and estimates of internal consistency that are comparable to those of traditional self-report measures, making it one of the most valuable alternatives to the IAT.","An important caveat regarding the AMP is that participants may sometimes base their responses on intentional evaluations of the prime stimuli instead of the neutral ideographs, which can undermine the implicit nature of the task.","The semantic misattribution procedure (SMP) is a modified version of the AMP that is amenable to the measurement of semantic responses, for example guessing whether Chinese ideographs refer to a male or a female name, as in research on gender stereotypes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Implicit Measures","topic":"Response Interference, Approach-Avoidance, and Relational Tasks","visual_frame_description":"A multi-screen control room, each monitor running one task: a space bar with a 600-millisecond deadline for the GNAT, colored beer words for the EAST, a joystick labeled pull and push for approach-avoidance, a four-key grid for the SPF, Santa Claus gift pictures for the AIP, and true and false buttons for the RRT. A wall chart titled Table 17.1 lists each task's underlying mechanism - response compatibility, misattribution, or stimulus compatibility - next to its range of internal-consistency estimates.","player_mission":"Tour the remaining instruments of Table 17.1: the go/no-go association task (GNAT), the extrinsic affective Simon task (EAST) and its identification variant, approach-avoidance tasks, the sorting paired features (SPF) task, the action interference paradigm (AIP), the implicit relational assessment procedure (IRAP), the relational responding task (RRT), and the weapon identification and shooter tasks. For each instrument, record the underlying mechanism, the kind of response it captures, and its internal-consistency estimates.","key_concepts":["The go/no-go association task (GNAT) was inspired by the basic structure of the IAT with an attempt to make the task amenable for the assessment of responses toward a single target concept rather than relative preferences between two target concepts.","GNAT trials typically include a response deadline of about 600 milliseconds, and error rates are analyzed by means of signal detection theory, with differences in sensitivity scores between the two pairings of go trials interpreted as an index of responses to the target concept.","In the extrinsic affective Simon task (EAST), participants categorize white positive and negative words in terms of their valence and colored target words in terms of their color, and faster responses to a colored word combined with a positive response are inferred to indicate a positive response to the object.","The identification-EAST (ID-EAST) presents target and attribute words in upper and lowercase instead of different colors, a procedural modification that helped to increase the relatively low internal consistency of the original EAST.","The rationale underlying approach-avoidance tasks is that positive stimuli facilitate approach reactions and inhibit avoidance reactions, whereas negative stimuli facilitate avoidance reactions and inhibit approach reactions.","Congruency effects in approach-avoidance tasks depend on the evaluative meaning that is assigned to a particular motor action, so carefully designed instructions with unambiguous response labels are important to avoid misinterpretations of the resulting scores.","The sorting paired features (SPF) task uses combinations of two simultaneously presented stimuli and four response options to break the four response dimensions that are confounded in the standard IAT into separate indices.","The action interference paradigm (AIP) was developed for research with very young children, and in one application children were faster in stereotype-compatible than stereotype-incompatible gift assignments, which was interpreted as evidence for spontaneous gender stereotyping in children.","The implicit relational assessment procedure (IRAP) has a unique characteristic in that it was designed to capture relational rather than associative responses, allowing it to differentiate beliefs such as I am good from I want to be good.","The relational responding task (RRT) was developed to address the high complexity of the instructions and the overall length of the IRAP, capturing relational responses in a simpler and more efficient way by asking participants to indicate whether statements about the relation between objects are true or false.","In the weapon identification task, participants briefly presented with a Black or White face prime must indicate whether a masked target picture showed a gun or a harmless object, and the common result is that harmless objects are more frequently misidentified as guns when the face prime was Black than when it was White.","In the shooter task, participants press a shoot button when the target person holds a gun and a no-shoot button when the target person holds a harmless object, and the common finding is that participants are more likely to respond shoot for unarmed Black targets than for unarmed White targets."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Implicit Measures","topic":"Pragmatic Issues, Validity, and Interpretation of Implicit-Measure Scores","visual_frame_description":"A seven-drawer filing cabinet, each drawer labeled with one pragmatic issue, with the IAT and AMP drawers glowing green for high consistency while other drawers show amber or red. On top of the cabinet, a decision tree splits absolute versus relative interpretations of implicit scores, and a stamp reading 'replicate with a different-mechanism measure' rests on the desk beside a portrait of the quad-model's four processing branches.","player_mission":"Confront the pragmatic issues in research using implicit measures: internal consistency, test-retest stability, implicit-explicit measure confounds, process-pure assumptions and computational modeling, formation versus expression of representations, prediction of behavior, and divergent underlying mechanisms. Then weigh the metric of implicit-measurement scores and decide when absolute versus relative interpretations of the scores are justified.","key_concepts":["The only tasks that consistently show high internal consistency are the IAT and the AMP, whereas some other tasks show moderate estimates that may be deemed acceptable yet suboptimal from a psychometric view, and others show clearly unsatisfactory internal consistencies.","Even instruments with high internal consistency have shown comparatively low test-retest stabilities, with correlations in the range of 0.40 to 0.50.","The combination of high internal consistency and low test-retest stability suggests that a considerable portion of variance in responses on implicit measures reflects transient states rather than stable traits.","When comparing responses on implicit and explicit measures, it is important to avoid confounds between the type of measure and the specific materials used in the two kinds of measure, because such confounds render interpretations of dissociations ambiguous.","There are no process-pure measures, because even responses on implicit measures reflect a mixture of multiple distinct processes, which prohibits direct inferences of underlying mental processes from observed responses.","The quad-model is a multinomial processing tree (MPT) model that quantifies the contributions of four qualitatively distinct processes to IAT performance: activation of an association (AC), detection of the correct response (D), overcoming associative bias (OB), and guessing (G).","Implicit measures constrain processing conditions only during the expression of mental representations, so dissociations between implicit and explicit measures do not have any direct implications for the role of automatic processes in the formation of mental representations.","Because the internal consistency of a given measure sets an upper limit for its relation with another measure, it seems unrealistic to expect strong predictive relations for implicit measures with low estimates of internal consistency.","The principle of measurement correspondence suggests that implicit measures should show stronger predictive relations when the processing conditions of the to-be-predicted behavior converge with the processing conditions imposed by implicit measures, such as unintentional behavior under conditions of low elaboration.","Different implicit measures are based on different underlying mechanisms, including response compatibility, misattribution, and stimulus compatibility, so it seems prudent to replicate effects observed on one implicit measure with another implicit measure that is based on a different underlying mechanism.","Implicit-measurement scores are often used to draw diagnostic inferences about individuals or populations, but both the size and the direction of the scores are affected by incidental features of the stimuli, rendering absolute interpretations of the scores problematic.","Most research questions in social and personality psychology do not require absolute interpretations of implicit scores, but instead are based on relative differences between measurement scores compared across different groups or across different individuals."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm18","title":"Chapter 18: Social Neuroendocrinology","pages":"427-451","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroendocrinology","topic":"The Neuroendocrinology Primer: HPA and HPG Axes, Oxytocin, and Hormone Ratios","visual_frame_description":"A three-panel anatomy gallery: the first panel shows a hypothalamus linked through the pituitary to the adrenal glands with cortisol and DHEAS glowing at the cortex, the second links the pituitary to testes and ovaries with testosterone and estradiol, and the third shows oxytocin and vasopressin confined to the brain, unable to cross the blood-brain barrier. A ticker tape at the bottom flashes 'no context-free invariants' and 'hormones are physiologically stingy' while tags reading stress, aggression, and cuddle hormone spin in place with strikethroughs.","player_mission":"Trace the cascade of each neuroendocrine axis, from the hypothalamus down to the end organs: CRH and ACTH for the HPA axis, GnRH, LH, and FSH for the HPG axis, and central release for oxytocin and vasopressin. For each hormone, note its biological function, its main psychological correlates, and the pitfalls of popular labels like the stress hormone, the aggression hormone, and the cuddle hormone.","key_concepts":["The HPA axis is one of two primary stress systems, the other being the sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) system, which is fast acting and associated with increases in catecholamines like epinephrine.","HPA axis activation starts with the hypothalamus, which stimulates the release of corticotrophin-releasing hormone (CRH), which then stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to release adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which travels to the adrenal glands and stimulates the release of cortisol and DHEAS.","Compared with the SAM system, the HPA is slower to respond, takes longer to shut off, and generally requires greater stimulus or context intensity to activate.","In the meta-analysis by Dickerson and Kemeny (2004), situations that reliably activated the HPA were distressing, effortful, performance-based situations, which the authors labeled social evaluative threat (SET), and HPA activation requires tasks that are active rather than passive and that last minutes rather than seconds.","Cortisol's primary biological role is to metabolize glucose, and running a marathon can spike cortisol levels to more than five times typical levels with circulating cortisol staying elevated for a number of days.","Cortisol is best perceived as a stingy hormone that only reluctantly varies from its strong diurnal pattern, veering off its daily pattern only for something unusual or uncertain, or a situation requiring physical or mental effort beyond quotidian activities.","Most secreted cortisol is bound to proteins in the blood, and because unbound (free) cortisol can easily enter cells by passive diffusion, cortisol can be measured in saliva, with blood and saliva cortisol correlating highly, above 0.90.","DHEA is an androgen prohormone that, in contrast to catabolic hormones like cortisol, builds up tissue and promotes muscle growth, and DHEA (with its sulfated form DHEAS) is the most abundant circulating steroid hormone in the human body.","About 98 percent of circulating DHEA is the sulfated form DHEAS, which does not have a strong circadian cycle and represents a stable index of adrenal activity, whereas DHEA varies over the day and might better reflect responses to an acute stressor.","DHEA levels vary along the lifespan, peaking around the mid-twenties and decreasing in subsequent decades, so that by the eighth decade DHEA levels are at about 20 percent of their peak levels.","The HPG axis regulates sex steroid hormones and starts at the hypothalamus, which releases gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), which stimulates the anterior pituitary to produce luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), which stimulate the gonads to release testosterone from the testes and estradiol and progesterone from the ovaries.","Adult men have approximately fifteen times greater testosterone levels than women, and the dual-hormone hypothesis (Mehta & Josephs, 2010) proposes that when cortisol is low and testosterone is high then dominance is higher, whereas when cortisol is high it reverses the testosterone-dominance association."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroendocrinology","topic":"Measuring Hormones: Basal Levels, Diurnal Levels, and Reactivity","visual_frame_description":"A day-long timeline strip runs across the scene with a cortisol curve rising steeply at 7 AM into a peak marked CAR, descending through the day with small meal bumps, and bottoming out in the late afternoon where a laboratory icon is docked. Above the strip float three labeled bins - basal (morning urine and hair), diurnal (wake-sleep slopes), and reactivity (baseline to post-task changes) - with a baseline sample icon positioned immediately before a stress task icon on the timeline.","player_mission":"Decide how to time hormone collection for a study by working through the three temporal bins: basal levels, diurnal levels, and reactivity. For each bin, specify which samples to collect, how time of day changes interpretation, and how to operationalize baseline, including where in the day to run a cortisol stress study and how long participants should relax before the first sample.","key_concepts":["Researchers typically focus on three temporal bins for hormones: basal levels, diurnal levels, and reactivity.","Basal levels capture circulating hormones that represent typical or trait-like levels, and they are often best determined from samples that represent cumulative hormonal levels obtained from morning urine, feces, or hair.","Basal cortisol levels are used as part of the multisystem measure called allostatic load (McEwen, 1998), which attempts to consider multiple biological systems to determine cumulative wear and tear, with higher basal cortisol levels indicating poorer health.","Higher basal testosterone is correlated with more dominance and higher social standing, and testosterone is a popular hormone for capturing basal levels.","Among healthy humans, cortisol levels are at their highest in the morning hours and decrease over waking hours, with notable meal bumps throughout the day, and fluctuations from the expected diurnal pattern may indicate underlying psychological or physical health problems.","Depressed individuals tend to have a flatter diurnal cortisol pattern, with morning and afternoon cortisol levels tending toward a flatter trajectory compared with the large negative slope among nondepressed individuals (Adam et al., 2017).","The cortisol awakening response (CAR) is the expected sharp peak of cortisol from waking levels to thirty minutes after waking, representing an approximately 60 to 75 percent increase in cortisol in healthy adults.","Kunz-Ebrecht et al. (2004) observed that the CAR was greater on workdays than on weekend days and that a lower SES position was associated with larger CARs, underscoring the importance of cortisol surging in anticipation of a workday.","To capture reactivity, researchers calculate changes in cortisol from baseline to following the stressor, and reactivity values can be positive, indicating an increase, no change, or a decrease, which is difficult to interpret when hormones have a negative slope throughout the day.","Because cortisol has a strong diurnal pattern, researchers interested in acute cortisol changes conduct their studies in the mid- to late afternoon hours so they are targeting the nadir of waking cortisol levels.","Typical advice is to have participants relax for between twenty and thirty minutes before taking the baseline sample, showing a neutral-content or relaxing video during this period, and to obtain the baseline sample immediately prior to exposure to the target task.","In Miller and Maner (2010), men who sniffed the T-shirts of ovulating women showed testosterone levels that were the same or decreased relative to baseline, with only the non-ovulating T-shirt condition showing a significant decrease in testosterone, contrary to popular misinterpretation that men showed increases."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroendocrinology","topic":"Capturing Hormones: Saliva, Urine, Hair, and Study-Day Control","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory intake desk displays a clipboard with the twelve intake questions of Table 18.1, from wake time and caffeine to last period date and birth control use. Beside the desk, three collection stations are set up: a drool tube with a straw labeled gold standard, a salivette cotton roll under a cortisol tag, and a hair sample cut from below the cranial bone with a ruler marking one centimeter per month of growth.","player_mission":"Build a complete hormone collection protocol: draft study-day instructions delivered at least twenty-four hours before the lab visit, write the intake interview questions modeled on Table 18.1, and choose a collection method for a given research question, weighing saliva (salivettes, drool tubes, swabs), twenty-four-hour urine, and hair cortisol, including the covariates each design must record.","key_concepts":["All bodily fluids contain hormones, but not all hormones are apparent in every bodily fluid, and researchers assess hormones from saliva, urine, feces, hair, and blood.","At least twenty-four hours prior to the study, participants can receive study-day instructions that encourage a good night's sleep and normal meals but avoiding foods with active cultures like yogurt, which can bind to saliva and interfere with some hormone assays, and two hours before the study start time, to refrain from caffeine, smoking, alcohol, tobacco or marijuana, large meals, brushing teeth, and vigorous exercise.","Upon arrival at the lab, participants complete a standard intake form (Table 18.1), which in many cases confirms adherence to study-day requirements, including questions about wake time, caffeine, yogurt, exercise, sleep schedule, illness, medications, alcohol and tobacco, and, for women, the first day of the last period and birth control use.","Saliva is a reliable, relatively simple, and cost-effective approach to measuring hormones, and most of the common steroid hormones can be reliably assayed from saliva, with cortisol levels in saliva correlating highly with blood.","Cortisol is not affected by salivary flow rate, which can decrease during states of high stress, so as long as a minimum amount of saliva is obtained, saliva from highly stressful situations can yield reliable and valid data.","When obtaining saliva samples, participants should rinse their mouth with water upon lab arrival to clear out possible food or other contaminants, but wait at least five minutes after the rinse before providing a sample, and researchers should limit water consumed during the study since water might dilute the saliva samples.","Salivettes are cotton rolls that participants chew until moist and then hold under the tongue for several minutes, providing a set collection time, but they are inaccurate for testosterone and DHEA assays because the synthetic cotton interferes with the anabolic steroids.","The passive drool approach, in which participants generate saliva and drool through a straw into a two-milliliter tube, is considered the gold standard of saliva collection because it yields the purest saliva without any interference from the collection method.","Collecting twenty-four-hour urine can provide an indication of accumulated hormones, and the most common tests are for UFC (urine-free cortisol), with the first urination of the day made into the toilet and urine collected in specimen containers for the remaining twenty-four hours.","Hair cortisol provides a measure of long-term HPA activity reflecting months of exposure, with about one centimeter of growth per month, so six centimeters of hair reflects the past five to six months of cortisol exposure, and hair is collected from below the cranial bone at the back of the head.","Hair cortisol reflects unbound (free) cortisol so it has fewer confounds than blood and saliva, and hair samples do not decompose like body fluids, though color-treated hair and daily washing might reduce cortisol levels and cumulative cortisol provides no information on the CAR or reactivity.","Health behaviors affect basal levels and hormone reactivity: night-shift workers will have dysregulated cortisol levels, the number of hours awake is strongly linked to the negative slope of cortisol, vigorous exercise just prior to a sample increases several hormones, and habitual exercisers tend to have lower cortisol and higher DHEA and testosterone."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroendocrinology","topic":"Manipulating and Assaying Hormones: Pharmacological, Quasi-Experimental, and Lab Standards","visual_frame_description":"A pharma aisle with shelves labeled intranasal sprays for oxytocin and vasopressin, a 0.5 milligram sublingual testosterone tablet, and a transdermal gel tube, with a prescription pad reading IND approval required. Next to it, a wet-lab bench displays assay plates in duplicate, a spreadsheet with specificity, sensitivity, and accuracy columns, and a shipping box packed with samples on dry ice under a tag reading 'assay all samples from a study in batch.'","player_mission":"Select a hormone manipulation strategy for a causal research question, choosing among increasing hormone levels (intranasal sprays, tablets, transdermal gels), suppressing release (the dexamethasone suppression test), and quasi-experimental approaches (patient samples and natural variations). Then set up the assay pipeline: pick a lab, batch samples, ship on dry ice, and interpret specificity, sensitivity, accuracy, and inter- and intra-assay coefficients of variation.","key_concepts":["Pharmacological interventions allow researchers to increase hormone production with intranasal sprays, tablets, injections, and transdermal gels, and intranasal sprays are typical approaches for hormones like oxytocin (OT) and arginine vasopressin (AVP).","There is considerable controversy regarding whether intranasal oxytocin sprays directly alter the targeted brain regions (Nave et al., 2015), but there is more direct support that intranasal AVP increases AVP levels in cerebrospinal fluid.","To obtain pharmacological interventions like oxytocin intranasal spray, a researcher typically has to engage an MD as a collaborator for the prescriptions overseeing the protocols, and when oxytocin experiments first started in the U.S., researchers were required to submit an IND (investigational new drug) application to the FDA.","Single-dose testosterone administration developed and validated by Van Honk and colleagues uses a 0.5 milligram dose delivered sublingually to women, and these studies reveal effects including increased risk taking, reductions in vigilance, and increased cardiac responses to angry faces.","The dexamethasone suppression test (DST) is a pharmacological challenge developed to test for Cushing disease, in which a single dose of one milligram of dexamethasone is ingested just prior to sleep, and among healthy people DST suppresses cortisol the following day while individuals with greater HPA dysregulation are non-suppressors.","Goetz et al. (2014) first suppressed testosterone levels with an injection of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), an antagonist that reduces variability in baseline testosterone, and then examined how a single dose of 100 milligrams of transdermal testosterone modulated threat responses using fMRI.","Researchers can study patient samples with known hypo- or hyper-levels of a specified hormone, such as people with Cushing's disease who have very high cortisol from overactivation of the HPA and people with Addison's disease who have very low cortisol from damage to the adrenal glands.","Men being treated for testicular cancer typically undergo a medical castration that temporarily reduces circulating testosterone, offering a pre-post design for examining how directly testosterone might be implicated in processes like risky decisions.","Exploiting naturally varying hormone levels can provide insight from a within-subject perspective, as in Jacobs et al. (2015), who scanned women during the early follicular phase when estradiol is low and again in the late follicular phase when estradiol is at its peak.","Typically the assay lab is chosen prior to collecting data to confirm that the lab conducts the target assays and how much biological product to collect, and the recommendation is to send all samples from a particular study in batch, assayed at the same time, shipped on dry ice with a quick delivery time.","Assay validity is determined by specificity (maximizing detection of the target hormone while minimizing cross-reactivity), sensitivity (the lowest dose of an analyte that can be distinguished from a sample containing zero analyte), and accuracy (comparing an observed value to a known amount, acceptable between 90 and 110 percent).","Precision is estimated with the coefficient of variation (CV%), calculated as the average of all replicated measures on a sample divided by the standard deviation multiplied by 100, with two types - inter-assay CV% and intra-assay CV% - and for both the expected percentage to be labeled acceptable is less than 10 percent."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroendocrinology","topic":"Analyzing Hormone Data and Hormones in Social and Personality Psychology","visual_frame_description":"A data pipeline starts with raw values flowing into a skew icon, then branches into a natural-log transform station and an AUC machine with a trapezoid formula that splits into two outputs, AUC-G labeled total production and AUC-I labeled peak changes. A wall of five case folders - Mendes 2007, Ratner 2013, Park 2022, Akinola and Mendes 2014, and Creswell 2005 - each open to reveal its hormone assay results on a line chart.","player_mission":"Run a full hormone analysis workflow: decide how to handle skewed distributions, choose between AUC-ground and AUC-increase as summary indices, and handle missing data with expectation maximization or multilevel modeling. Then apply hormones to real social psychology questions, walking through the intergroup, discrimination, power and status, values, and emotions studies to identify which hormone was measured and what was found.","key_concepts":["Even after careful collection of samples and control of time of day and context, most hormone levels are not normally distributed because of the pulsatile, dump-and-release nature of most hormones, and some researchers will natural-log transform raw hormone values prior to analyzing.","Calculating the area under the curve (AUC) can represent overall hormone levels over a set amount of time, and AUC is calculated with two sources of information: the hormone levels and the time between the hormone measurements (Pruessner et al., 2003).","AUC-ground (AUC-G) represents total hormone production over all from an event, whereas AUC-increase (AUC-I) is more sensitive to peak changes in a hormone because the trapezoid formula subtracts out baseline levels to capture reactivity.","Khoury et al. (2015) compared fifteen different summary indices of cortisol using PCA and found two components accounting for 79 to 93 percent of the variance, one capturing total hormone production that mapped well to AUC-G and the other representing changes in hormone levels mapped to AUC-I.","Missing hormone data can be addressed with different techniques, such as the expectation maximization algorithm used by Lindsay et al. (2018) to replace missing data prior to calculating AUC, or abandoning the AUC approach and using multilevel modeling, which does not require complete data.","In Mendes et al. (2007), White participants completed a standard TSST with either all White evaluators or all Black evaluators, and the interaction between IAT racial bias and interracial context was significant for DHEAS and the anabolic balance ratio but not for cortisol, with more egalitarian participants evaluated by Black evaluators showing higher anabolic balance.","Ratner et al. (2013) examined Black and Latina participants' in-group pride and observed that participants who held more positive personal attitudes about their ethnic or racial group showed higher levels of baseline DHEA, with no significant differences for cortisol.","Park et al. (2022) administered forty international units of intranasal oxytocin or saline placebo to Black participants before an interaction with a White partner, and in the positive feedback condition oxytocin compared with placebo was associated with greater trust behavior in a public-goods game, greater liking of the partner, and more adaptive challenge physiologic reactivity.","In Akinola and Mendes (2014), police officers with higher perceptions of social standing within the police department showed greater testosterone reactivity (r = 0.27) during a mock interview, and male undergraduates assigned to a higher status role showed greater increases in testosterone reactivity than males assigned to a lower status position.","In Kraus and Mendes (2014), male participants randomly assigned to change into low-class clothing showed a significant decrease in testosterone relative to men who changed into upper-class clothing and to men who did not change clothing.","In Creswell et al. (2005), participants who affirmed their personally important values just prior to completing the TSST showed lower cortisol across the three post-task saliva samples (at twenty, thirty, and forty-five minutes after the start of the stress task) than participants in the control condition.","Akinola and Mendes (2008) found that among participants who were socially rejected, increased negative emotions were negatively correlated with basal levels of DHEAS at r = -0.50, and changes in negative emotions were not correlated with DHEAS in the social acceptance or control conditions."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm19","title":"Chapter 19: Multivariate Neuroimaging in Social and Personality Psychology","pages":"453-469","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroimaging","topic":"From Voxels to Patterns: Foundations of Univariate and Multivariate Neuroimaging","visual_frame_description":"A scanning console in a dim laboratory where a brain hologram is sliced into a three-dimensional grid of glowing voxels. On one wall, two heat maps labeled FFA and PPA light up for faces versus houses, while a second screen strips away the average magnitude to show a lattice of unique distributed patterns, and a final projection draws an arrow from activation back to a mental process labeled reverse inference.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundations: define the voxel as the unit of neuroimaging measurement, explain how classic univariate contrast analyses located the fusiform face area and the parahippocampal place area, retrace Haxby et al.'s (2001) multivariate challenge to the FFA story, and separate forward inference from reverse inference as the key interpretive frames for MVPA.","key_concepts":["A voxel is a volumetric pixel at one place in the brain that is used as the unit of measurement in neuroimaging, and traditional fMRI analysis fits a model at each voxel to estimate the response to each type of stimulus one at a time.","Researchers have consistently found that one area, the fusiform face area (FFA), tends to show the most activation to faces, whereas another area, the parahippocampal place area (PPA), tends to show the most activation to houses and places.","Kanwisher et al. (1997) demonstrated with standard univariate methods that a portion of the right ventral temporal lobe called the fusiform gyrus was reliably and robustly active to faces more than any other visual object category, and early studies claimed this region was a category-specific brain module for facial processing.","In a seminal paper, Haxby et al. (2001) used a multivariate approach and found that distributed patterns of activity within the FFA could accurately dissociate multiple object categories, meaning the region can distinguish categories from each other on the basis of their activity pattern.","Whereas blurring across all voxels within a region reveals a quantitative difference (more activation for faces than other objects), examining all the individual voxels reveals a qualitative distinction in the unique patterns of activity, showing that general object categorization is also represented in patterns distributed across voxels in the FFA.","Both the univariate (Kanwisher et al., 1997) and the multivariate (Haxby et al., 2001) findings about the FFA have been replicated dozens of times and are well-established effects, despite having seemingly contradictory interpretations (Duchaine & Yovel, 2015).","Traditional univariate analysis is powerful and appropriate when the research question is which part of the brain responds more to faces than to other categories, whereas multivariate pattern analysis (MVPA) asks whether information related to a psychological process can be statistically decoded from patterns of activity distributed across voxels.","MVPA does not make assumptions about the direction (more or less activation compared to resting brain activity) or the strength of the responses, but rather allows the patterns of activation to inform the representation of the perceptions being studied.","Univariate methods ask about the location of the activation and multivariate methods ask about the patterns of activation, and because the mean signal is removed in multivariate methods, they provide independent information about the nature of brain activation.","MVPA methods provide answers to questions about not only which parts of the brain are involved in a given mental process, but also the implementation of mental processes within them, offering greater detail and sensitivity than univariate methods for informing theory.","Reverse inference is the problem of inferring backwards that, when a pattern of activation is apparent in the data, a given mental process is being engaged, and because there is no simple one-to-one mapping of activation within a region to complex social cognitive processes, mere observation of activation is not sufficient to infer that a particular mental process is engaged (Poldrack, 2011).","Because MVPA quantifies how strongly a pattern of activity correlates with a mental process, it provides a framework for developing a formal means to implement reverse inference, and therefore has greater promise for informing social and personality psychological theorizing than univariate approaches."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroimaging","topic":"Location Versus Pattern: A Debate Between the Univariate and MVPA Frameworks","visual_frame_description":"A divided debate hall with two podiums: the univariate researcher stands before a brain map with single hot-spot regions highlighted, while the MVPA advocate gestures at a pointillist pattern map with voxels arranged like a constellation. Between them a rotating display flips the question from 'Which parts of the brain are more active?' to 'Where does information dissociate?', and a banner overhead reads 'massively parallel processing system.'","player_mission":"Moderate a debate between a traditional univariate researcher and an MVPA advocate. Force them to defend which research questions each framework can answer, how the frameworks differ in their assumptions about the direction and strength of activation, and whether the shift from magnitude differences to distributed patterns changes how social and personality psychologists generate hypotheses.","key_concepts":["The contrast between the results from univariate and multivariate approaches simply indicates that the classes of methods address different questions (Jimura & Poldrack, 2012).","Within the traditional univariate framework, a researcher might ask which parts of the brain are more active for perceiving humans compared to animals, whereas within an MVPA framework the question would instead be where information in the brain dissociates perceiving humans from animals.","MVPA is an umbrella term used to capture a variety of methods that use brain responses distributed across multiple voxels to make inferences about mental representations and other psychological processes.","Multivariate approaches can, in principle, be applied to any neuroimaging or psychophysiological modality where simultaneous recordings are captured from multiple sites, although the chapter focuses mostly on fMRI methods.","The brain is a massively intricate parallel processing system, and complex cognitive and behavioral phenomena almost never have a simple one-to-one mapping with individual regions of the brain (Cacioppo et al., 2003).","A portion of the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex is implicated in processing both physical pain and social pain (Eisenberger et al., 2003), yet Woo et al. (2014) used MVPA to show that these two conditions are reliably distinguished based on patterns of voxel activation even when they have similar activation across all the voxels on average.","Although the FFA has a greater average response to faces than to other categories, MVPA methods can accurately detect the percept of other object categories within the same region due to their distributed patterns (see Figure 19.1).","Haxby et al. (2001) was not the first paper to deploy multivariate methods in fMRI (see McIntosh et al., 1996), but that paper is often cited as the one that pioneered MVPA and was the basis for many of the advancements that followed.","The focus away from magnitude differences toward distributed activity patterns requires a fundamental rethinking of how to ask questions and generate hypotheses in social and personality psychology, because the MVPA framework makes no assumptions about the direction or strength of responses.","Effectively applying these methods requires a greater degree of sophistication in understanding both quantitative methods and neurobiological systems, and each approach can only be as useful as the psychological theories and paradigms on which it is based.","Thoughtfully and intentionally bringing multivariate approaches into social and personality psychology research will not only help advance psychological theories, but in turn help neuroscientists better understand the organization of the brain based on our best understanding of social cognition, personality, and behavior.","By the early 2000s, fMRI had come to dominate the landscape of high-profile studies of the brain basis of social cognition because it had better spatial resolution than electroencephalography and better temporal resolution than positron emission tomography, sitting as a compromise between the two."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroimaging","topic":"Cross-Validation and Overfitting: Auditing an MVPA Classification Study","visual_frame_description":"An inspection floor with four run-lanes of a paradigm, three lanes glowing as training data while a fourth lane is held out for testing, with the held-out lane rotating in each iteration. A monitor streams average accuracy scores with a standard deviation bar, and a red alarm labeled 'overfitting to noise' blinks near a server rack of support vector machines and random forest models.","player_mission":"Act as a methods auditor for an MVPA classification study. Verify the train-then-test procedure with held-out runs, check that accuracies are aggregated across iterations and tested against chance with non-parametric methods, flag overfitting risks in the machine learning choices, and judge whether within-subject designs justify the data demands of classification.","key_concepts":["MVPA classification studies aim to test whether patterns of brain activation can reliably classify, or dissociate, two or more mental states or stimulus types (Wagner et al., 2019).","MVPA classification turns the typical fMRI equation around: instead of using stimulus categories to predict brain responses, classification approaches use the multi-voxel brain response patterns as the independent variable to make predictions about the stimulus categories.","Contemporary MVPA classification studies use machine learning algorithms, though early classification work used familiar statistical methods such as correlation analysis, linear discriminant analysis, and logistic regression.","The most commonly used machine learning methods in MVPA classification studies include support vector machines, naive Bayes, and random forest models, and in practice there are usually not large differences in the conclusions drawn from using any of the standard machine learning algorithms (Haxby, 2012).","One concern with machine learning methods is that increased sensitivity to the underlying signal also increases vulnerability to overfitting responses to noise, because if an algorithm detects a pattern in the data it will leverage that pattern as part of its prediction even if the pattern is unrelated to the true signal.","Common practice is to make inferences on the basis of an out-of-sample prediction model, where classification algorithms are first trained on subsets of the data (e.g., 75 percent of it) before being evaluated on independent portions of the data left out of the original analyses (e.g., the remaining 25 percent).","In a typical experiment with four separate runs of the paradigm, the classification algorithm is trained to dissociate the categories in three runs before being tested for accuracy on the held-out run, and this train-then-test procedure is repeated iteratively with each run serving as the testing dataset.","The classification results are aggregated across each iteration and reported as average accuracy scores for each participant, with the standard deviation indicating variation in accuracy across runs.","Final statistical analyses are typically performed with a non-parametric t-test against chance-level performance, and because classification accuracies are typically not normally distributed, non-parametric methods that do not assume normality are important for robust inferences.","Classification algorithms often leverage within-subject designs to capitalize on the large quantity of data per subject and control for person-to-person variation, so they do not necessarily require greater sample sizes than univariate methods, though they may require more data or runs per subject (Coutanche & Thompson-Schill, 2012).","Hassabis et al. (2014) used MVPA classification to accurately identify which of four novel individuals a participant was imagining based on activity patterns in a portion of the medial prefrontal cortex, demonstrating that multivariate patterns of activity can not only dissociate thinking about people versus not thinking about people, but also identify information about specific individuals.","Parkinson et al. (2014) trained an MVPA classifier on one domain and tested it on the other two (spatial, temporal, and social distance), and found that the right inferior parietal lobule, an area thought to be involved in sensorimotor transformations of information, showed consistent cross-domain classification across all three domains."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroimaging","topic":"Representational Similarity Analysis: Building the Neural Dissimilarity Matrix","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory workbench where brain response patterns stream from the scanner into a grid that fills with colored cells, forming a triangular neural dissimilarity matrix flanked by three miniature matrices labeled all-face model, human-face model, and hybrid face model. A slider labeled region scope sweeps from small regions to whole networks while warning lights flash for 'nested data' and 'collinearity.'","player_mission":"Build an RSA pipeline from raw patterns to model comparison: calculate correlation distances among voxel-wise response patterns within a region, construct the neural dissimilarity matrix, choose item-level versus category-level granularity, and formally compare competing theoretical similarity models in a regression framework, watching for region scope and statistical pitfalls.","key_concepts":["Representational similarity analysis (RSA) is a procedure for measuring the conceptual distances among categories by quantifying the similarities of their voxel-wise activity patterns (Kriegeskorte, Mur, & Bandettini, 2008).","RSA abstracts away from the fMRI signal into similarity space (distance or correlation matrices of voxel-by-voxel responses), allowing researchers to quantitatively describe the relationships among each of the categories and test hypotheses about similarity derived from behavioral data or theoretical models.","A neural dissimilarity matrix is created by calculating the correlation distances (e.g., 1 minus Spearman r) among patterns of responses to each stimulus within a given brain region, and it indicates the magnitude of the difference in neural response between the categories (Figure 19.2a).","Once neural dissimilarity matrices are calculated, dissimilarity values between pairs of stimuli can be related against predicted similarity structures derived from competing theoretical models (Figure 19.2b) or separate behavioral data, and the models can then be formally compared in a regression framework to determine which one most accurately describes the data.","Classification methods are not optimal for directly comparing hypothesized models (Popal et al., 2019), because if theories suggest that human and animal faces should be more related to each other than they are to inanimate objects, classification accuracies alone cannot address this question.","In an early RSA paper, Kriegeskorte, Mur, Ruff, et al. (2008) compared human fMRI data with single-cell electrical recordings in monkeys who viewed the same objects and found a strong correspondence between the two species, suggesting relatively preserved object categories that are shared across species and not modified by language.","RSA methods make it possible to calculate similarity matrices at the item level (Mumford et al., 2014) as well as at the category level by aggregating across multiple trials within categories, and the decision between them depends on study design, the amount of data collected, and the details of the hypothesis.","Similar to behavioral paradigms, the more trials per condition there are, the greater the power will be to estimate responses to that condition and to generalize to other exemplars from those conditions.","An RSA framework allows dissimilarity measures to be calculated at any scale, including small regions, large regions, and even non-adjacent areas analyzed together (Jolly & Chang, 2021), but including too many areas that are not involved in processing the stimuli will distort the similarity matrices, while narrowing down too much may not capture how distributed regions work together.","Statistical issues to be addressed when using similarity matrices as the basis for modeling include nonindependent and nested data structures, collinearity of competing models, and the appropriate threshold for the significance of a correlation within a matrix.","Thornton & Mitchell (2017) used RSA to demonstrate that specific, familiar individuals elicited consistent patterns of similarity across participants in brain areas previously shown to be important for social cognition, and neural similarity patterns dovetailed with similarity patterns from theories of social cognition such as the stereotype content model and Big Five personality traits.","Stolier & Freeman (2016) found that participants' biases in the perception of social categories (race, gender, and emotional expression) were reflected in the similarity structure of the fusiform gyrus and orbitofrontal cortex, and using the model comparison approach they showed the effects held even after accounting for lower-level visual similarities between facial stimuli."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Neuroimaging","topic":"Whole-Brain Signatures and the Frontier: Biomarkers, EEG, dMRI, and the Future of MVPA","visual_frame_description":"A control room facing a giant whole-brain weight map that glows across every voxel, with validation meters for sensitivity and specificity above 94 percent. On side screens, an EEG cap flashes microstates across sixty-one channels, a white-matter tractogram rotates for a self-esteem model, and a social network graph links students whose brain-to-brain similarity predicts relationship strength.","player_mission":"Take on the final challenge: build and validate a whole-brain biomarker from training on one sample to cross-validation on independent participants, prove its specificity against related constructs using the pain signature and the PINES model, and extend the multivariate logic beyond fMRI to EEG microstates, dMRI personality models, and social network designs.","key_concepts":["Among the most robust findings across years of neuroscience research is the lack of functional specificity of individual brain regions, and most mental processes, especially those social psychologists are interested in, depend on activity in numerous brain regions rather than mapping one-to-one onto single areas.","Biomarker or neural signature analyses capitalize on broadly distributed activation patterns to generate whole-brain predictive models of mental state representations, clinical diagnoses, or individual differences, and when executed carefully and validated appropriately these models provide quantitative, falsifiable predictions that tend to generate greater effect sizes and specificity than other methods (Kragel et al., 2018).","Unlike typical MVPA classification analyses that are cross-validated across runs, whole-brain biomarker models are trained within a subset of individuals and generate a predictive model that can be applied to completely independent groups of participants.","Biomarker studies allow for generalizability across different participant demographics, different scanners, and even different experimental paradigms, and once a signature is trained it is possible to inspect the model to see which parts of the brain contribute most strongly to its predictive accuracy and the direction of their relationships to the outcome.","Wager et al. (2013) built a whole-brain predictive model of physical pain using a regression-based machine learning algorithm based on every voxel in the brain, and the signature showed very high sensitivity and specificity (greater than 94 percent) in predicting pain intensity level, which they then cross-validated in an independent sample of participants.","The neurological pain signature was specific to increases in the experience of pain intensity and not to the anticipation of pain, the recall of previous pain, or other mental states known to activate similar brain networks such as social rejection.","Chang et al. (2015) developed a whole-brain biomarker called the picture-induced negative affect signature (PINES), which was sensitive at predicting the level of negative affect evoked by a given image and was specific to the perception of images, since it did not predict affective responses to pain or vice versa, and the results could not be reduced to any individual brain structure within the signature.","The signatures derived from these procedures produce whole-brain images of statistical parameters called voxel-wise weight maps that can be saved in standard neuroimaging formats and applied to any data aligned to the same coordinate space, providing a framework to combine signatures from different psychological constructs into integrative predictive models at multiple levels of abstraction (Woo et al., 2017).","Parkinson et al. (2017) combined social network analysis with MVPA by recruiting 275 first-year MBA students, converting social network analysis metrics into dissimilarity values and relating them to the similarity of multivariate fMRI responses using RSA, and found that each social network metric was spontaneously encoded in different brain regions.","Guthrie et al. (2022) ran a round-robin fMRI study with twenty groups of six participants and found that similarity in brain-to-brain responses within regions previously implicated in mentalizing and social motivation (e.g., the dorsal medial prefrontal cortex and anterior insula) consistently predicted social relationship strength between pairs of individuals.","Multivariate EEG methods include Hundrieser et al.'s (2021) MVPA classification of participants' binary yes/no agreement with moral statements based on spatiotemporal patterns from sixty-one electrode channels, and microstate ERP analysis, which identifies stable configurations of global electric brain activity across all EEG electrodes in a data-driven fashion (Cacioppo et al., 2014).","Diffusion MRI (dMRI) is a structural neuroimaging technique that measures anatomical white matter connectivity between regions, and because its relatively static nature makes it well suited for studying stable characteristics, Chavez et al. (2022) built a biomarker model of individual differences in self-esteem from dMRI that generalized across time, scanners, and subject demographics, and similar effects are seen in models of Big Five personality traits that generalize across samples (Stendel & Chavez, 2023)."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm20","title":"Chapter 20: Measurement: Reliability, Construct Validation, and Scale Construction","pages":"471-500","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Measurement","topic":"The Seven Steps of Scale Construction: From Items to Composite Scales","visual_frame_description":"A blueprint table with seven numbered shelves, each holding a different artifact: a compass labeled prediction versus explanation, a dictionary entry for the target construct, a box of candidate items, a clipboard for administering them, a matrix of inter-item correlations, a scored scale, and a certificate of validation. Beside the table a single test item is projected as four concentric bands labeled general, group, specific, and error variance.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundations: walk through all seven steps of Table 20.1 from choosing between prediction and explanation through validating the resulting scales, distinguish predictive validity from construct validity, survey the kinds of items and open-source item pools a builder can draw on, and decompose item variance into its general, group, specific, and error components.","key_concepts":["A scale is typically formed by combining two or more items thought to measure the same construct into a single composite scale, which is done to increase the reliability and generality of the resulting scale.","The seven steps toward constructing scales are: (1) choosing between prediction and explanation, (2) specifying the construct(s) to measure, (3) choosing items thought to measure those constructs, (4) administering the items, (5) examining the structure and properties of composites of items, (6) forming, scoring, and examining the scales, and (7) validating the resulting scales (Table 20.1).","Early tests of individual differences such as the Strong vocational interest test (Strong, 1927) and the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Hathaway & McKinley, 1943) were developed to predict real-world outcomes such as vocational choices and psychopathology, with the emphasis upon predictive validity.","A drastic change from an emphasis upon predictive validity came with the emergence of construct validity as a way of furthering psychological theory (Cronbach & Meehl, 1955; Loevinger, 1957), which to Cronbach and Meehl meant evaluating what psychological qualities a test measures.","Convergent validity implies high correlations with theoretically related concepts, whereas discriminant validity assumes low correlations with theoretically non-related concepts.","Items form the basic unit of scales and need to be written clearly and unambiguously with a number of response alternatives, and typical items may be single words, phrases, sentences, or even vignettes.","Items can measure emotional states such as energetic arousal or tension (e.g., from the motivational state questionnaire), conventional personality traits such as extraversion and neuroticism, normal activities, or cognitive ability (Table 20.2).","It is very important to have subject matter experts as well as the targeted end users screen items for relevance, understandability, and subtle forms of bias, because lack of understandability will not prevent participants from responding, albeit randomly.","A useful compendium of 3,320 personality items was released to the public as the International Personality Item Pool (Goldberg, 1999), and a larger set of sources for 10,984 items with the actual items for 4,878 public-domain items is available as the Database of Individual Differences (Condon, 2019).","The lexical hypothesis, first proposed by Galton and emphasized by Allport and Odbert (1936), Norman (1963), and Goldberg (1990), is that any individual differences that are important in a society will be encoded in its lexicon.","The variance of a single item may be thought of in terms of four separate parts: general variance associated with all items in a scale, group variance associated with some but not all items, specific variance associated just with that item, and error reflecting instability of the construct from moment to moment (Figure 20.1).","Ever since Spearman (1904b) aggregated classroom performance and Binet averaged responses to simple tasks to form composite measures of intellectual ability, psychologists have been constructing composite scales by taking the sum of batches of individual items."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Measurement","topic":"Prediction Versus Explanation: A Debate Over What Validity Should Mean","visual_frame_description":"A debate hall with two podiums facing a judge's bench: on one side a slide of the MMPI and the Strong tests with known-group hit rates, on the other a nomological web of convergent and discriminant correlations around a latent construct. A screen overhead toggles between two banners: 'A test is valid if it predicts' and 'Constructs are to traits as statistics are to parameters.'","player_mission":"Moderate a debate between an empirical-keying researcher and a construct-validity advocate. Force them to defend whether a scale should maximize correlations with a criterion or the internal structure of a construct, confront Loevinger's argument that predictive accuracy is inadequate, weigh the causal account of validity from Borsboom et al. (2004), and settle how Goldberg's (1972) comparison of homogeneous and empirical keying should guide modern scale construction.","key_concepts":["The first question the investigator needs to address is to what purpose the scale to be developed will be employed, weighing the advantages and disadvantages of the approach that emphasizes predictive validity and the approach that emphasizes construct validity.","Leading researchers argued that every different criterion required a separate validation study, which was a source for many a dissertation but did not seem to produce notable cumulative wisdom.","Loevinger (1957) pointed out that because predictive validity can vary with each criterion of interest, the simple notion of validity as predictive accuracy is inadequate.","To Loevinger (1957), constructs are to traits as statistics are to parameters, and she suggested that there are two contexts for defining validity to be recognized: administrative and scientific.","According to Loevinger (1957), there are essentially two kinds of administrative validity, content and predictive-concurrent, but there is only one kind of validity that exhibits the property of transposability or invariance under changes in administrative setting, which is the touchstone of scientific usefulness: construct validity.","The standard treatment of construct validity considers what a test measures by its pattern of correlations: does it have high correlations with what theory says it should (convergent validity) and low correlations with theoretically unrelated constructs (divergent validity).","Borsboom et al. (2004) emphasized that the validity of a test reflects a causal statement: a test is valid for measuring an attribute if variation in the attribute causes variation in the test scores.","Yarkoni and Westfall (2017) attacked the emphasis on construct validity by noting that machine learning models can be accurate but not transparent as to why particular items work in the model, and they challenged the view that latent variables are necessary for psychological research.","Condon et al. (2020) and Mottus et al. (2020) emphasize the power of the item rather than of the scale to predict outcomes, although the use of items does not make for parsimonious theory and scale construction.","In a comparison of empirical and homogeneous keying (factor-analytic item sorting) procedures, Goldberg (1972) concluded that emphasizing homogeneous keys is probably better for easily predicted criteria but that empirical keying was superior for harder-to-predict criteria.","With the advent of larger samples, the advantage of empirical keying over the traditional Big Five constructs becomes much more obvious (Revelle et al., 2021).","Selecting items with the highest loadings on the factors to maximize internal consistency is an unsupervised learning procedure, whereas choosing items with the highest predictive validity for a criterion is a supervised learning procedure."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Measurement","topic":"Administering and Auditing: Data Quality in Web-Based Scale Administration","visual_frame_description":"An auditing desk facing a live survey dashboard where response times flash green for attentive participants and red for impossibly fast responses. A queue of respondent profiles streams by, flagged with tags such as 'wide awake AND sleepy', 'straight-liner', and 'impossible range', while a histogram of Mahalanobis distances grows on a side monitor and a double-entry keying station reconciles two independent transcriptions.","player_mission":"Act as a methods auditor for a new online scale administration. Verify that the target population and sample size fit the number of items per factor, screen for bots, careless responders, and straight-lining using validity questions and psychological antonyms, and check the transcription, range-checking, and Mahalanobis-based outlier detection steps before the data are allowed into analysis.","key_concepts":["An unappreciated part of scale construction is the actual administration of the items, which involves considering the target population for the scale and its presumed limits of generalizability, such as age, educational, or occupational constraints based on the difficulty or complexity of the content of items.","Required sample size and the number of items per factor appear to be inversely related: if the number of items per factor is small, a relatively large sample size may be needed, whereas a much smaller sample size may be adequate if the number of items per dimension is large.","About 400 participants are needed to adequately recover a five-factor structure of the twenty-five items of the BFI data set (five items per factor), but only 300 participants are needed to recover five factors from the 135 items of the SPI (twenty-seven items per factor).","The authors agree with the suggestion (Clark & Watson, 1995) that 300 participants is a good goal, but in the era of web-based data collection, while more is always better, the increase in benefit is a function of the square root of the sample size.","When administering items it is important that participants are fully engaged, and with web-based testing it is useful to know why participants are taking the test, such as whether bots are trying to defraud the researcher or whether answers are given by MTurk workers paid by the item.","Screening for too-rapid responding, inconsistent responding, and failure to answer validity questions (e.g., an item that states 'Answer 3 to this item'), and looking for patterns of responses such as straight-lining, ski-sloping, and long strings of identical responses, are recommended (Meade & Craig, 2012; Reyes, 2020; Ward & Meade, 2018).","A recurring problem when giving tests is that some unknown percentage of subjects give dishonest answers, either intentionally to deceive or due to a lack of involvement (careless responding), and Johnson (2005) also includes linguistic incompetence as a threat to item validity.","One solution to careless or deceptive responding is to include psychological antonyms (Goldberg & Kilkowski, 1985), because people who say they are both wide awake and sleepy are presumably not paying attention.","If answered by pencil-and-paper forms, data often need to be transcribed to a machine-readable form by hand entry, and the data should be entered twice by independent workers and the results compared automatically, since hand entry is a surprising source of error.","Once entered into a machine-readable format, basic descriptive statistics allow for the detection of impossible responses (the range of responses should not exceed the maximum minus the minimum possible response), which can be found using the describe function within the psych package in R.","Automatic outlier detection techniques include examining the Mahalanobis distances for the responses of each respondent, and automatic outlier detection can be performed using the outlier function within the psych package in R.","With Likert-like data, outliers may take the form of impossible responses, nonconforming responses (e.g., answering 'agree' to one item and 'disagree' to the same item worded differently later in the scale), or implausible responses (e.g., answering 'yes' to 'I have never told a lie')."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Measurement","topic":"The Multi-Trait Multi-Method Matrix: Convergent and Discriminant Validation","visual_frame_description":"A spreadsheet-like grid on a wall with two labeled blocks: Self-reports and Peer reports, each with five trait rows. The diagonal cells glow with reliability coefficients, the between-method diagonal band lights up with strong convergent correlations near 0.7, and the cross-trait cells stay dim, while a magnifying glass reveals an average correlation of 0.41 inside the peer-report block flagged 'methods factor?'.","player_mission":"Build a multi-trait multi-method matrix from self-report and peer-report data: place internal consistency reliabilities on the diagonal, convergent validities between the self-report and peer-report blocks, and discriminant correlations everywhere else. Interpret the Zola et al. (2021) SAPA results, verify that same-trait correlations across methods are high while cross-trait correlations are low, and diagnose the methods factor implied by the peer-report block.","key_concepts":["The standard treatment of construct validity considers what a test measures by its pattern of correlations, and stronger evidence for validity may be found by comparing measures across methods (Campbell & Fiske, 1959).","Methods are typically means of assessment, such as self-report, peer reports, behavioral observations, or physiological measures, and each method is thought to have contaminating variance such that two traits measured with the same method might correlate because of the shared method.","Trait measures across methods should correlate only to the extent that both are measures of the same trait (Campbell & Fiske, 1959).","Zola et al. (2021) report the correlations between self-reports and peer reports for about 900 web participants who nominated anonymous raters to rate them on eight traits, as part of the SAPA project (Table 20.8).","The SAPA project used a massively missing-data structure in which some 158,631 participants responded to random subsets of items in the SAPA personality inventory (Condon, 2018).","In the multi-trait multi-method matrix, the main diagonal shows internal consistency estimates of reliability, the sub-diagonal between self-reports and peer reports shows the convergent validities, and the other correlations show discriminative validity (Table 20.8).","All correlations in the MTMM matrix reported by Zola et al. (2021) are corrected for reliability, and the polychoric correlation matrix of the items is available as the zola dataset in psychTools.","The strong agreement between self-reports and peer reports of the same traits (average convergent correlations of 0.72) and low mean correlations with the self-report measures (0.18) or the other peer reports (0.09) show the appropriate pattern of convergent and discriminative validity.","The fact that the peer reports correlate among themselves with an average correlation of 0.41 shows less discriminative validity for peer reports than for self-reports and suggests the presence of a methods factor.","In the Zola et al. (2021) example, same-trait correlations across methods are high (e.g., 0.78 for agreeableness, 0.79 for conscientiousness, and 0.76 for extraversion), whereas cross-trait correlations between different traits are much lower.","Convergent validity implies high correlations with theoretically related concepts, whereas discriminant validity assumes low correlations with theoretically non-related concepts.","If scales include overlapping items, the scoreOverlap function in the psych package gives statistics that correct for the artificial inflation of the correlations for overlapping scales."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Measurement","topic":"The Final Pipeline: Factor Structure, Reliability, and Cross-Validated Scale Building","visual_frame_description":"A control room with a two-screen console: the left screen shows the Schmid-Leiman path diagram of two correlated factors resolving into a general factor with omega subscripts, and the right screen runs a bestScales loop with ten fold lanes lighting up in turn. A large scoreboard tracks derivation versus cross-validation accuracy, with the fifty-item model's gap flashing red under a banner reading 'capitalize on chance?'.","player_mission":"Take on the final challenge: run the complete scale-construction pipeline on the GERAS gender data. Compare EFA and PCA loadings and residuals, apply the Schmid-Leiman transformation and omega analysis to find general factor saturation, score composites with unit-weighted means, and use bestScales with k-fold cross-validation and bootstrap resampling to build an empirically keyed ten-item scale that maximizes validity without overfitting.","key_concepts":["Principal components and factor analysis are conceptually very different: components represent sums of items while factors are models of the items, so items are reflective indicators of the factors but formative indicators of the components, and as the number of items increases the results of the two models converge.","In Table 20.5, the loadings (RC1 and RC2) are slightly larger for the principal components solution than for the factor solution (MR1 and MR2), but the residuals are larger for the components solution.","A major problem in exploratory factor analysis is what the optimal number of factors to extract is, and the likely worst way to answer this is asking how many eigenvalues are greater than one (the so-called little-jiffy criterion), with parallel analysis (Horn, 1965) comparing the eigenvalues of the observed solution to eigenvalues of random data of the same number of variables and subjects.","The bifactor model allows two major loadings for each item, one on a general factor and the second on a group factor, and bifactor solutions can be found using the bifactor rotation applied to the EFA solution, as can a transformation of the loadings (Schmid & Leiman, 1957) from a correlated-factors model.","McDonald (1999) introduced two model-based estimates of reliability based upon the factor structure of the items, which Zinbarg et al. (2005) relabeled as omega g for the general factor saturation and omega t for the total reliable variance.","Alpha tends to underestimate the test-retest reliability and is a large overestimate of the general factor saturation (omega g) of the test, and the authors do not encourage the use of alpha, including it for historical reasons only.","Although some have proposed factor scores as the appropriate way to compute scores, simple sum scores have been shown to be just as good, if not better, primarily because unit-weighted scores are actually sample-independent, whereas factor score estimates are weighted sums whose weights are based upon a particular sample (Widaman & Revelle, 2022).","Reverse-scoring an item is equivalent to subtracting the item from the highest option plus the lowest option (e.g., for items that range from 1 to 6, a reversed item is subtracted from 7), and the authors recommend keeping the items in their original direction and allowing the scoring program to do the reversals.","A well-known danger of simple empirical item selection is the tendency to capitalize on chance, and the solution is cross-validation: develop scales on one sample and then validate the scales on a different sample, such as splitting a sample into a derivation sample and a validation sample (Cureton, 1950).","Generalizations of cross-validation include k-fold resampling and bagging (bootstrap resampling with aggregation): with k = 10 there is a 90 percent derivation sample and a 10 percent cross-validation sample, while bagging forms bootstrap samples that typically extract 1 - 1/e = 63.2 percent of the subjects for the derivation sample and validates on the remaining 1/e (out-of-bag) sample.","In the GERAS example, an empirically keyed set of ten items found using bestScales showed a cross-validated validity of 0.69 that agreed with the results from 100 bootstraps, whereas the model using all fifty items overfitted, dropping from 0.78 in derivation to 0.64 in cross-validation.","Eagly and Revelle (2022) showed that aggregating items improves prediction particularly if the aggregated items share little variance, and although internal consistency helps make a scale understandable from the point of view of construct validity, it reduces the predictive validity of a scale."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm21","title":"Chapter 21: Advanced Psychometrics","pages":"502-530","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Psychometrics","topic":"Four Prototypic CFA Models of Twelve Item Responses","visual_frame_description":"A whiteboard diagram of four path models labeled 1a through 1d, with boxes representing measurable item responses and circles representing inferred latent variables, arrows flowing from latent circles to item boxes in the first model, a web of four correlated circles in the second, a tall circle feeding four facet circles in the third, and a general circle plus facet circles all pointing directly at item boxes in the fourth, with a caption reading 'the congeneric item response model.'","player_mission":"Audit the four confirmatory factor analysis models depicted in Figure 21.1, which model twelve item responses with increasing complexity: model 1a, a single latent variable as common cause; model 1b, four correlated latent factors; model 1c, a second-order factor model; and model 1d, a bifactor model. For each model, note the number of estimated parameters and state which models justify ordering participants on a single general dimension.","key_concepts":["Confirmatory factor analysis is a statistical model of correlations among measured variables, such as survey item responses, that makes use of latent variables (Millsap, 2011, 29).","In Figure 21.1, variables in boxes can be measured, whereas variables in circles or ovals are inferred latent variables.","In model 1a, a single latent variable theta is modeled as the cause of variation and covariance of the item response data, with item responses expected to be correlated because they all have the same common cause.","Each item response Xij is represented as a linear function of a person-specific latent variable theta with an item-specific intercept and slope, and the residual term describes the difference between the item response and the response model (Equation 21.2).","In psychometrics, the equation of model 1a is called the congeneric item response model, a generalization of the parallel-measure model that assumes all items have the same intercept, slope, and item error variance.","Model 1a assumes that the residuals are uncorrelated; if a pair of residuals had been predicted to be correlated, they would be connected by a double-headed arrow in the figure.","Model 1b has four latent variables, each accounting for the covariation among three distinct item response variables, and the latent variables may be correlated as shown by double-headed arrows between every pair.","Model 1c replaces the six correlations among the four latent variables in model 1b with a second-order factor model, in which a fifth latent variable explains a portion of the variance of each of the four first-order factors.","Model 1d, called a bifactor model, represents item responses as a direct function of two distinct processes, the general factor process and one of the facet processes, and the facet latent variables are uncorrelated after adjustment for the general factor.","The bifactor model is much less parsimonious than the second-order factor model, requiring thirty-six parameters to be estimated compared to twenty-eight for the twelve-item second-order model 1c.","All but model 1b justify the ordering of participants according to a theoretically interesting dimension, with model 1a representing the construct directly and models 1c and 1d representing it as a general factor.","For models 1c and 1d, the average item response is contaminated by facet variation that is distinct from the variation of the central general construct."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Psychometrics","topic":"Estimation, Fit Functions, and Model Comparison","visual_frame_description":"A seminar room with a projector showing three equations: ULS as a trace of squared differences between S and Sigma(xi), GLS with a weighting by S inverse, and ML involving log determinants of the matrices, with a running graph on the side that shows RMSEA, CFI, and TLI values and a table of likelihood ratio statistics L1 and L2 with a difference arrow labeled LDelta.","player_mission":"Moderate a statistical methods panel comparing the three fitting functions in Table 21.2: unweighted least squares (ULS), generalized least squares (GLS), and maximum likelihood (ML). Force the panel to explain how each function compares the sample covariance matrix S to the fitted matrix Sigma(xi), when each estimator is preferred, and how the ML fit value is turned into a chi-square statistic used to test nested models.","key_concepts":["The key to estimating factor loadings and factor variances and covariances is to compare the sample variance-covariance matrix S to the fitted variance-covariance matrix Sigma(xi), where xi is the list of unknown parameters of the specific CFA model.","The most common fitting functions used in CFA estimation are unweighted least squares (ULS), generalized least squares (GLS), and maximum likelihood (ML), which yield ULS, GLS, and ML estimates of xi.","ULS provides decent estimates without assumptions about the original data, but GLS and ML are more efficient estimates if the item data are distributed as multivariate normal.","Because self-report item responses in personality and social psychology will rarely be normally distributed, it is best to think of the fitting functions as alternative approximations.","All three fitting functions in Table 21.2 equal zero for perfect fit and take positive values when fit is not perfect.","CFA programs estimate parameters with iterative methods: an initial guess of xi is made, Sigma(xi) is compared to S with a fitting function, the guesses are updated to improve fit, and the steps are repeated until a solution is obtained.","The root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) is a widely used index of absolute fit, with values smaller than 0.06 indicating acceptable fit and values greater than 0.10 indicating inadequate fit (Hu & Bentler, 1999).","The comparative fit index (CFI) and the Tucker-Lewis index (TLI) reflect comparative fit against a reference model asserting that none of the item responses are correlated; best-fitting models have values greater than 0.95, and models with values less than 0.90 are considered inadequate.","CFI compares the model of interest and the reference model with a difference operator, whereas TLI compares them with a ratio operator.","When evaluated at the ML estimate values, the ML fitting function can be transformed into a likelihood ratio statistic that has a chi-square distribution with nu = J(J+1)/2 - wk degrees of freedom, where wk is the number of estimated parameters in model version k.","For two nested models, where one model contains a subset of the parameters of a more elaborate model, the ML difference statistic LDelta = L1 - L2 is distributed as chi-square on the difference in degrees of freedom nuDelta = nu1 - nu2 when response variables are approximately multivariate normal.","Standard errors of the estimates can be used to construct confidence bounds and significance tests of whether loadings are essentially zero, and modern programs can provide robust standard errors that do not assume multivariate normal data."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Psychometrics","topic":"Four Levels of Measurement Invariance Across Groups","visual_frame_description":"A four-tier courtroom diagram, with each tier labeled 'configural invariance,' 'metric invariance,' 'scalar invariance,' and 'strict invariance,' and an inspection table beside it showing which item parameters (factor structure, loadings, intercepts, error variances) are locked down at each tier, with a verdict stamp reading 'partial invariance allowed' over a note about sum and average scores.","player_mission":"Act as a psychometric auditor reviewing evidence for measurement invariance across two groups. Walk through the hierarchy of four invariance levels - configural, metric, scalar, and strict - and for each level state which parameter is constrained equal across groups, which baseline model it is compared against, the degrees of freedom of the ML difference test, and the consequences of a violation for comparing group scale scores.","key_concepts":["When a measure seems to have the same psychometric meaning across key groups, we say that it has measurement invariance, and this evidence is also informative about construct generalization.","The psychometric literature on measurement invariance dates back to Meredith (1993), who laid out formal definitions of the levels of invariance.","The least restrictive level is configural invariance, which addresses the question of whether the factor structure of the items is the same in the two groups.","The second level is metric invariance, which asks whether the factor loadings are the same between groups that have similar configural factor structures.","The third level is scalar invariance, which checks whether the item intercepts are the same in the two groups after adjusting for the level of the latent variable of interest.","The final level is strict invariance, which asks whether the error variances of items are the same in the two groups, assuming scalar invariance.","To assess configural invariance, one usually carries out factor analyses of each group separately, and reasonable fit in both groups is consistent with configural invariance.","Measurement invariance methods for binary or categorical items are often called differential item functioning (DIF), and such items are modeled with logit or probit item response theory rather than the covariance-based approaches in this chapter.","If the strict invariance does not hold but scalar invariance does, the observed item means are unbiased, but the variability differences can lead to biased inference.","If scalar invariance does not hold but metric invariance does, then the groups can be compared in a structural equation context, but the sum or average item scores might be biased.","If metric invariance does not hold, then group comparisons are obscured by the relative importance of one group of items over another.","If a measure does not have configural invariance in the different groups, the comparison of the groups is nearly impossible to interpret."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Psychometrics","topic":"Numerical Example of CFA: Fitting Three Models to the OPP Data","visual_frame_description":"A data analyst's desk with a questionnaire of twenty-five OPP items scored 1 to 5, a spreadsheet showing the 325 unique statistics of the sample covariance matrix S, and three model fit cards side by side: model 1a with RMSEA 0.117 and CFI 0.759, model 1c with RMSEA 0.077 and CFI 0.897, and model 1d with RMSEA 0.073 and CFI 0.916, with an arrow and a note reading 'LRDelta(21) = 139.7, p < 0.001.'","player_mission":"Reanalyze the numerical example of Section 21.5, fitting the single-factor, second-order, and bifactor models to twenty-five items of the openness to political pluralism (OPP) measure collected from 400 MTurk workers. Work through the fit statistics of Table 21.5, the unstandardized and standardized loadings, the two cross-loading items, and the interpretation of the second-order regression coefficients, then judge whether the bifactor model's best fit outweighs its interpretive complexity.","key_concepts":["Openness to political pluralism is the tendency of an individual to obtain and engage with political information from a variety of perspectives, measured by Shrout et al. (2022) with twenty-five items.","The twenty-five OPP items were answered on a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree) and were constructed to represent four theoretically relevant facets: pluralistic motivation (eleven items), emotional tolerance (five), intellectual tolerance (five), and nonrigidity (four).","Twelve of the items were worded such that agreement reflected higher OPP, and the remaining thirteen items were worded such that agreement reflected lower OPP; responses to the reverse-worded items were rescaled to be consistent with the positively worded items.","Four hundred MTurk workers from the U.S. answered these questions in 2018, with an average age of 36.8 years, 51 percent identifying as male, and Cronbach's alpha and omega (total) for the scale both equal to 0.95.","The CFA analyses can be carried out using only the sample variance-covariance matrix S, which has 325 (25 x 26 / 2) unique statistics to be fit, without needing the original item response data.","The single-factor model (model 1a) did not fit the variance-covariance matrix well, with an RMSEA of 0.117, larger than the warning cutpoint of 0.10, and CFI and TLI far from the warning cutpoint of 0.90.","Model 1c, the second-order factor model, specified four latent variables corresponding to the facet item clusters plus a second-order latent variable predicting variation in the four first-order factors, and two items (items 6 and 16) were allowed to load on two facets based on preliminary data.","The second-order model improved fit dramatically over model 1a, with RMSEA 0.077, CFI 0.897, TLI 0.885, and an LR difference of 862.7 on 6 degrees of freedom (p < 0.001).","In model 1c the standardized regression coefficients relating the four facets to the second-order OPP factor varied from 0.76 to 0.91, providing strong evidence that the second-order factor was related to the four facets.","The bifactor model 1d estimated twenty-one additional parameters relative to model 1c, and its fit was again improved, with RMSEA 0.073, CFI 0.916, and TLI 0.898, and an LR statistic 139.7 smaller than model 1c's (p < 0.001).","In the bifactor model, the loadings on the general factor were all positive and significant with a median of 0.63, whereas the loadings for the facets adjusted for the general factor were notably smaller, with a median of 0.35 versus a median of 0.78 in model 1c.","The bifactor model 1d provided the best fit of the three models, but its interpretation is more nuanced because the facet factors become essentially residual sources of covariation that are not theoretically as important as the general factor."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Psychometrics","topic":"Testing Measurement Invariance of OPP Across Gender Groups","visual_frame_description":"A command-center wall with two scree plots of eigenvalues showing parallel-analysis curves for males and females, two scatterplots of factor loadings with congruence statistics 0.995 and 0.736, a fit table for bifactor models fit separately by gender, and a summary table showing the configural model (150 parameters, LR 2176.17), metric model (LRD 22.428, p = 0.554), scalar model (LRD 44.968, p < 0.006), and strict model (LRD 62.675, p < 0.001).","player_mission":"Lead a full invariance-testing investigation, Section 21.9 style, comparing men (n = 204) and women (n = 195) on the twenty-five OPP items. Run the exploratory factor analyses with scree plots and parallel analysis, the Geomin-bifactor rotation with factor congruence statistics, the confirmatory bifactor fit for configural invariance, and then the sequence of metric, scalar, and strict invariance models in Table 21.11, interpreting each chi-square difference test and the false-discovery-adjusted follow-up tests.","key_concepts":["The invariance example compared men (n = 204) and women (n = 195), and because one person chose not to report gender, the total sample size was 399 rather than 400.","The average OPP scale score was 3.70 for men and 3.65 for women, with no striking gender differences for individual items, leading the authors to proceed with formal testing for invariance.","Scree plots of the eigenvalues from exploratory factor analyses, together with parallel analysis describing the expected decrease of eigenvalues under chance association, suggested that a two-factor solution in both groups was probably appropriate.","The two-factor EFA solutions in each group were rotated with a Geomin-bifactor rotation that yields one factor with positive loadings for all items and a secondary uncorrelated factor.","The factor congruence statistic, the sum of products of male and female loadings divided by the square root of the product of the sums of squares, was 0.995 for the general factor, where 1.00 indicates perfect concordance.","The confirmatory bifactor model fit separately in each gender, with the fit for the female sample slightly worse than for the male sample, provided enough support for configural invariance to move on to check the other levels of invariance.","The metric invariance model, which constrained the loadings for males and females to be the same while leaving intercepts and error variances group-specific, estimated 126 parameters and produced a chi-square difference of 22.43 on 24 degrees of freedom (p = 0.554), so metric invariance stood unchallenged.","The scalar invariance model, which constrained intercepts and slopes across groups while estimating the female latent variable mean, was rejected by the chi-square difference test (LDelta(24) = 44.97; p < 0.006).","One intercept stood out with an approximate z of 2.03: the intercept for item 7, 'I am willing to engage in discussions with individuals who hold opposing political views,' was 4.04 for men but 3.84 for women.","Men seemed to have larger intercepts for items in the pluralism openness facet, whereas women seemed to have larger intercepts for the nonrigidity facet, and when the fifteen items in these two facets were taken into account, there was no indication of intercept differences in the remaining ten items.","The strict invariance model, constraining the item error variances to be the same in men and women, significantly worsened fit (LDelta(25) = 62.68; p < 0.001), with the error variance for item 19 nearly twice as large for males as for females (0.70 versus 0.36).","Men had larger residual variances than women for five of the six intellectual tolerance items, and when those six items were allowed to have gender-specific error variances, the omnibus strict invariance test was no longer significant (LDelta(19) = 29.19; p < 0.06)."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm22","title":"Chapter 22: Dealing with Repeated Measures: Design Decisions and Analytic Strategies for Over-Time Data","pages":"532-563","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Repeated Measures","topic":"Choosing a Repeated-Measures Design: Frequency, Timing, and Spacing","visual_frame_description":"A design-studio whiteboard with five cards from Table 22.1, each showing a design name, its definition, and concrete examples such as a survey link completed at every negative interpersonal event, a nightly bedtime survey for two weeks, and random text-message prompts during the day. Beside the cards hangs Figure 22.1, showing identical data captured well by measurement timepoints in the top row and missed by poorly timed timepoints in the bottom row, with a checklist labeled 'frequency, spacing, timeframe, timing of assessments.'","player_mission":"Audit the five common repeated-measures designs in Table 22.1 - event-contingent, daily diary, ESM/EMA, pre-post, and longitudinal - and assign each study scenario to its best-fitting design. Then review the frequency and timing decisions in section 22.2.1, checking that the timescale of measurement matches the timescale of the phenomena, spacing is chosen deliberately, and multiple frequencies are allowed where they are useful.","key_concepts":["Over-time, longitudinal, and repeated measures are all terms referring to data with repeated measurement of the same variables within the same unit (e.g., person, family, team, company).","Common types of repeated-measures designs (Table 22.1) include event-contingent, daily diary, experience sampling method (ESM), ecological momentary assessment (EMA), pre-post, and longitudinal designs.","In an event-contingent design, participants provide data (self-report, behavioral, or physiological) in response to a particular event, such as completing a survey every time they experience a negative interpersonal event.","A daily diary design typically involves a single daily assessment, such as participants sent a link each night for two weeks and told to complete the survey right before bed.","Experience sampling method (ESM) and ecological momentary assessment (EMA) are used interchangeably for methods with multiple assessments per day for a number of days, with the goal of capturing snapshots of natural life such as social interactions in the past thirty minutes.","Pre-post designs gather data before and after an event, which can be a naturally occurring event or some type of manipulation or intervention, such as lab tasks completed before and after a one-month online intervention.","Although all of these designs are longitudinal, in practice the term is often used for designs with fewer repeated measures spanning longer periods of time (often months or years), as opposed to intensive longitudinal designs like daily diary or ESM studies.","One critical design aspect is ensuring that the timescale of your repeated measures matches the timescale of the phenomena you are studying, and ideally you choose measurement timepoints that accurately reflect the underlying pattern of temporal change.","If you are examining change across timepoints, equal spacing between repeated measures typically allows for a more parsimonious analytic model than unequal spacing, though you may have theoretical or practical reasons for unequal spacing.","Researchers may end up with unequal spacing due to the nature of their variables, such as a Monday-through-Friday daily diary study in which weekends can be marked as missing data points, even though weekends are not missing at random and could be meaningfully different.","If your variable of interest is slow-moving, you may want to spread your measurements out over a longer period of time, whereas fast-changing processes like heart rate can be assessed every few seconds and slower ones like salivary cortisol are spaced further apart.","You can have different kinds of data collected at different frequencies, such as tracking college students across four years of college while conducting an event-contingent study during exam week at the end of each academic year."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Repeated Measures","topic":"Power and Sample Size for Over-Time Data","visual_frame_description":"A war room with a large chart from Figure 22.2 showing effective sample size for 100 participants with ten repeated measurements across ICC values from 0 to 1, with the curve falling steeply from near 1000 at ICC 0 to near 100 at high ICC. A whiteboard lists the design effect equation ingredients - total sample size, average number of repeated measures per person, and ICC - next to a plan with two levels labeled 'people' and 'days' for a daily diary study and three levels for an ESM study.","player_mission":"Run the power-planning session for a new intensive longitudinal study. Decide how many people and how many repeated measures are needed at each level of data, weigh the similarity of repeated measures via the ICC and the design effect equation, decide whether between-person variability matters for your questions, and plan for missing data and attrition before launch.","key_concepts":["Statistical power calculations are not as straightforward with longitudinal data as with cross-sectional data because you have to decide on sample size for each level of data, such as how many people and how many days for a daily diary study, or how many times a day as well for an ESM study.","You will generally increase power more by increasing the number of people rather than the number of repeated measures (Bolger & Laurenceau, 2013; Snijders & Bosker, 2012).","One factor that affects power is the similarity between repeated measures within a person: the more similar repeated measures are, the less unique information you get with each additional measure.","The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) represents the proportion of the total variance in an outcome that is explained by between-person variability in mean levels, with an ICC of 1 for stable variables like adult height and an ICC near 0 for uncorrelated measures like random numbers generated each day.","Ultimately, the more correlated the repeated measures are within a person, the less statistical power you have to detect a relationship with another variable that is also collected repeatedly.","In addition to nonindependence between repeated measures within a person, you must also think about whether the people in your sample are independent of each other, because if you are studying dyads or groups you may have to account for the correlation between people in the dyads or groups in your statistical models.","There is less statistical power to find a significant relationship between two variables measured in a sample of 200 sibling pairs (400 individuals) than in a sample of 400 individuals who are not related to each other in any way.","Modeling between-person differences in within-person effects requires larger samples than modeling the average within-person effect, because the sample size for those between-person questions is the number of people in your sample, not the number of repeated measures.","If you have a two-level model, the design effect equation uses your total sample size, the average number of repeated measures per person, and the ICC to calculate what is known as the effective sample size.","The effective sample size tells you what the sample size would be for a simple random sample with independent observations that has the same precision of estimates (and therefore statistical power) as your two-level sample with nested data.","If you have 1,000 data points from 100 participants providing ten days of data and a very high ICC, you can think of that as having a sample size that is effectively much smaller than 1,000, and the design effect equation quantifies exactly what much smaller means.","You should think through potential missingness in your data because it will affect statistical power for a given sample size, and to minimize attrition you could offer a bonus (e.g., additional pay or a lottery prize) to people who complete at least 80 or 90 percent of the timepoints."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Repeated Measures","topic":"Nonindependence and Data Structure: Nested Versus Crossed","visual_frame_description":"A forensic review room with a large copy of Figure 22.3 on the wall: on the left, a three-level nested model of momentary reports (level 1) nested within days (level 2) nested within individuals (level 3), and on the right, a cross-classified model in which daily reports are nested within both individuals and days at level 2. An inspection table lists every clustering factor of the audited dataset with checkboxes for 'nested' and 'crossed,' and a sticky note reads 'even ICC = 0.10 matters.'","player_mission":"Act as the structural auditor for a longitudinal dataset and determine whether nonindependence exists, identify every level of data, and classify the clustering factors as nested or crossed. Review whether the outcome was truly measured repeatedly per person, check for disaggregation problems, and judge whether ICC levels even as small as 0.10 force you to model the nonindependence.","key_concepts":["Nonindependence in repeated-measures data exists when you have potentially correlated errors in your outcome variable, and you need to account for it if the variable you are predicting has been measured more than once within the same person.","In traditional statistical approaches, like a typical OLS regression framework, all predicted data points are assumed to be independent, and correlated repeated measures within the same person violate this assumption, which leads to biased standard errors and degrees of freedom (Fox, 2015).","Even a small amount of nonindependence can change standard errors in ways that dramatically affect statistical significance, so the safest bet is simply to use statistical models that allow one to adjust for nonindependence, regardless of how much exists.","Simulation studies suggest that nonindependence matters even for ICCs as small as 0.10 (Vajargah & Masoomehnikbakht, 2015).","If your outcome variable is measured only once per person - for example, a change score, a person-level variable measured once, or an aggregated score from repeated-measures data - then your data points are independent and you can use more traditional statistical approaches.","Predicting a score measured once per person from a score measured repeatedly, so that you have multiple rows of data for the same person but the same outcome score on every row, is known as disaggregation and can lead to tests that are too liberal or too conservative.","Often longitudinal models have two levels of data: repeated measures (seconds, days, years) nested within individuals, where repeated measures represent the lowest level of data (level 1) and the individual is the clustering factor (level 2).","If you collected ESM data with multiple measurements each day and multiple days of data, you have three levels: momentary reports (level 1) nested within days (level 2), which are nested within individuals (level 3).","Data are considered nested when one factor occurs only within a particular grouping of another factor, such as when a person's daily reports come from that person only in a diary study.","Data are considered crossed when observations are nested within multiple clustering factors simultaneously, which can occur with longitudinal data when time itself is modeled as a clustering factor, as when daily reports of stress are nested both within individuals and within days of the week.","In the crossed daily-stress example, a participant's stress on Monday is likely to be more similar to someone else's stress on Monday than to their own stress on Saturday, creating nonindependence within days as well as within individuals.","If assessments are collected at set times that meaningfully differ from each other (e.g., morning, afternoon, night), time of day may also be a clustering factor, because two participants' feelings in the morning may be more similar to each other than one participant's feelings in the morning and the afternoon."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Repeated Measures","topic":"Analytic Strategies: MLM, Alternatives, and Random Effects","visual_frame_description":"A tactical command table with the Table 22.2 definitions of fixed effects, random effects, random factors, and ICC displayed on one screen and a decision tree on another: 'heterogeneity of interest?' leading to MLM, 'few timepoints with cross-classification?' leading to time-as-covariate, and 'many timepoints?' warning against timepoint-as-variable SEM. On a third screen, Figure 22.4 shows three panels of participant lines - random intercepts only, random slopes only, and both - each fanning out around the average regression line.","player_mission":"Select the analytic approach for nonindependent data among multilevel models, fixed-effects approaches, repeated-measures ANOVAs, marginal models/GEE, and SEM, then build an MLM specifying random factors, random intercepts, random slopes, random covariances, and a residual variance-covariance structure. Justify each modeling decision against the questions of interest, heterogeneity, and the structure of the data.","key_concepts":["Multilevel models (MLMs), also known as mixed-effects models, random-effects models, or hierarchical linear models, account for the multiple levels and nested or crossed structure of the data.","MLMs take advantage of the unique aspects of repeated-measures data by modeling fixed effects (i.e., average responses) and random effects (i.e., variability around the average response), making MLM a great tool if you are interested in understanding heterogeneity in effects.","One approach other than MLM is accounting for nonindependence within clusters through fixed effects, such as including your clustering factor as a categorical covariate, in the way researchers treat country as a categorical covariate when participants are nested within countries.","If you have repeated-measures data with only a few timepoints (e.g., two to five) and you think you have cross-classification where time is a clustering factor, you might consider treating time as a categorical covariate to make comparisons between particular timepoints.","Repeated-measures ANOVAs are fairly easy to implement with only a few timepoints, but they assume homogeneity of covariances between timepoints, cannot handle missing data (relying on listwise deletion), cannot handle different numbers of timepoints across participants, and cannot incorporate time-varying predictors.","Marginal models, generalized estimating equations (GEE), population-averaged models, and cluster-robust standard errors are all terms that refer to an alternative approach that directly adjusts residuals (and thus corrects bias in standard errors) without modeling random effects.","Researchers with longitudinal data may also be interested in structural equation models (SEMs) to capture change over time, with common examples including the latent growth curve model, which looks at change across all timepoints, and cross-lagged models, which look at change from one timepoint to the next.","SEM can handle nonindependent data because it allows you to specify correlated errors between repeated measures, and it provides more flexibility for model specification, but with intensive longitudinal data with many timepoints a model that treats every timepoint as a separate observed variable can get unwieldy quickly.","You can choose whether or not to model random effects, but you must have at least one random effect (typically a random intercept) to have a multilevel model, and there are three types of random effects: random intercept, random slope, and random covariance.","The variance estimate for the random intercept indicates how much variability in the outcome variable there is in the sample around the fixed intercept, so the size of the random effect can provide information about how generalizable the fixed effect is.","You can only model random slopes for time-varying variables, because time-invariant measures, such as those measured only once per person, cannot have random effects since you cannot calculate individual slopes for each person.","With repeated-measures data there are almost always time-related correlations in residuals, known as autocorrelation, and if you ignore them you risk biased standard errors for your fixed effects, which can produce Type I errors; the most common error structure is a first-order autoregressive structure."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Repeated Measures","topic":"Centering, Moderation, Mediation, Time, and Directionality","visual_frame_description":"A mission-control wall with six panels: a centering diagram showing each person's raw scores collapsing around their own mean to create a within-person centered variable, a moderation tree with cross-level and within-person branches, a mediation path diagram with random effects on the 'a' and 'b' paths, a timeline with time variables coded 0-27 sequentially and by day, a lag table showing a lag of two deleting two timepoints for every missing day, and a pre-post panel labeled 'Lord's paradox' contrasting change scores with residualized change.","player_mission":"Lead the full analytic campaign: unconfound within- and between-person effects with person-mean centering, run moderation and mediation at the correct level, structure time variables for equal or unequal spacing, model over-time patterns with growth curves, and test directionality with lagged models. Finish by deciding between change score and residualized approaches for pre-post group designs and check for Lord's paradox.","key_concepts":["Longitudinal data confound within- and between-person effects, and without properly centering the data you cannot tell whether the fixed effects in your MLM results are due to between-person effects, within-person effects, or both.","You can unconfound within- and between-person effects by person-centering your data: getting each person's average score for a predictor and subtracting it from each of their repeated measures to create a within-person centered variable that captures fluctuations relative to each person's average.","With your time variable it is especially important to make sure that zero is meaningful; often people choose to center on the first timepoint, so that the intercept represents people's starting point, and the key is to be thoughtful about what zero represents because it influences how you interpret your fixed and random effects.","Cross-level moderation occurs when you look at whether a within-person effect varies as a function of some between-person variable, such as whether the daily within-person effects of belonging on mood are different for people who tend to have higher or lower levels of belonging.","A mediator of within-person changes in mood must also be within-person, so you typically want to separate out within- and between-person effects and conduct mediation analyses at the appropriate level with the correctly centered variables.","If you have equally spaced timepoints, creating a variable to represent time is simple (e.g., 0-6 for a seven-day diary), but with unequal spacing you have to decide whether to model timepoints by the number of the timepoint (0, 1, 2, 3) or by distance from baseline (0, 1, 6, 12).","When you have event-contingent data with unequal spacing, you could average across time bins (e.g., calculate average events for each person for each of four weeks), or treat time as an ordering variable that lists each event sequentially relative to a point of interest.","The most common approaches for modeling change over time include growth curve models in which time is entered as a predictor in the model, allowing you to examine mean levels via the intercept as well as change over time via the slope for your time variable.","You generally cannot unconfound within- and between-person effects for time itself, because everyone typically has the same scores on time at each timepoint (i.e., 0-13 for a fourteen-day diary) and thus the same between-person means and deviations, so time is usually centered on a timepoint of interest such as the starting point or midpoint.","Lagged models predict a variable at one timepoint from a variable at a prior timepoint to assess directionality, and these models should adjust for the outcome variable measured at the same time as the predictor, in order to predict change over time and adjust for correlations between the outcome measured at different timepoints.","You will lose data as your lags get longer: a lag of one is associated with one lost timepoint, a lag of two with two lost timepoints, and this data loss is compounded by any missing data, so a fourteen-day daily diary study with a lag of two and two missing timepoints has only eight timepoints with both predictor and outcome values.","When comparing groups in a pre-post design, predicting T2 while adjusting for T1 as a predictor (the residualized approach) can produce results different from a change score approach, a phenomenon known as Lord's paradox, and with pre-existing group differences at T1 you are best off using the change score approach."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm23","title":"Chapter 23: The Design and Analysis of Data from Dyads and Groups","pages":"565-600","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Dyadic Analysis","topic":"Nonindependence, Variable Types, and Distinguishability","visual_frame_description":"A terminology laboratory with cards on the walls: one card defines nonindependence as correlated scores within dyads or groups, a second shows the MLM random intercept version versus the correlated-errors version, and a three-column table lists within-group, between-group, and mixed variables with examples like talk time, task ambiguity, and relationship trust. A sorting rack labeled 'distinguishable vs. indistinguishable' holds examples including husbands and wives, identical twins, and lab groups formed by random assignment.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundational terminology section: define nonindependence and explain why it is a social psychological phenomenon rather than a statistical nuisance, classify variables as within-dyad, between-dyad, or mixed, identify the level at which outcomes are measured, and sort dyads or groups into distinguishable versus indistinguishable cases. Then check each definition against the worked examples in section 23.1, such as talk time in four-person groups, gender in marital research, and identical twins.","key_concepts":["Standard statistical methods such as analysis of variance and multiple regression require independence between observations, yet many social psychological concepts (e.g., attraction, imitation, person perception, and communication) intrinsically involve two persons.","Because observations from social data do not refer to a person but to multiple persons embedded within a social context, the key assumption of ANOVA and regression - that scores of different units are uncorrelated once predictors are controlled - is routinely violated in dyadic and group research.","Social psychologists should treat interdependence not as a statistical nuisance to be controlled, but rather as an important social psychological phenomenon meriting study, and a survey of papers published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology found that only 11 percent involved the study of actual dyads or groups.","In the standard multilevel modeling formulation, nonindependence is modeled as a variance in the intercepts, so that to the extent there is more variance in average responses across groups than from person to person within groups, the scores within groups are correlated.","Nonindependence can be negative, as when an outcome is structured so that one group member's high score (e.g., talking a great deal) by necessity forces other members' low scores, and if negative nonindependence is modeled as a variance, data analysis programs can generate error messages.","An alternative and more general specification is to have the errors of members in the same group be correlated rather than to include a random intercept, and because that correlation can be negative, this correlated-errors approach makes negative nonindependence possible.","A within-group variable varies across group members but yields the same average score for every group, as with gender in a study of four-person groups comprising two men and two women, whereas a between-group variable varies from group to group but is the same for all members of the same group, as with randomly assigned task ambiguity.","A mixed variable varies both within and between dyads or groups, as with relationship trust in romantic partners, where the two partners may differ in trust and some couples have higher average trust than other couples.","Measurements in dyadic research can be at the individual level, with each member having their own score, or at the dyad level, in which case the dyad is treated as the unit of analysis as if it were the participant; group data can also be measured at the dyad level within the group, producing twelve dyadic ratings in a four-person group.","Members of a dyad or group are conceptually distinguishable if a meaningful variable (e.g., gender or family role) differentiates them, and they are indistinguishable or exchangeable if there is no systematic way to order the scores, as with gay and lesbian couples, identical twins, or groups formed by random assignment in the laboratory.","Distinguishability matters because systematic differences in means, variances, and covariances may exist across roles, and by demonstrating that members are statistically indistinguishable the researcher can estimate a simpler model with fewer parameters and have greater power in statistical tests.","For same-sex couples in close-relationship research, the authors strongly recommend including all couples and adding gender of the respondent as a control variable rather than discarding the same-sex couples, because discarding their data is both statistically and ethically problematic."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Dyadic Analysis","topic":"Statistical Methods and the Three Models of Dyadic Data","visual_frame_description":"A briefing room with two lecterns labeled 'MLM' and 'SEM': the MLM lectern shows a random-intercept equation with persons nested in dyads, and the SEM lectern shows two structural equations with X1 and X2 predicting Y1 and Y2 and correlated disturbances. On the main screen, Figure 23.1 displays three path diagrams side by side - the APIM with X1 and X2 each determining both Y1 and Y2, the CFM with latent X and Y variables linked by one path, and the MIM with Y1 and Y2 mutually affecting one another - each with a caption and a popularity bar chart underneath.","player_mission":"Host a methods briefing comparing multilevel modeling (MLM) and structural equation modeling (SEM) for dyadic and group data, then walk the team through the three major models of dyadic data: the actor-partner interdependence model (APIM), the common-fate model (CFM), and the mutual-influence model (MIM). For each model, state when it applies, how it is estimated, what the key paths mean, and when one of the other models would be more appropriate.","key_concepts":["The two major methods that dominate the analysis of dyadic and group data are multilevel modeling (MLM) and structural equation modeling (SEM), each with advantages and disadvantages for analysis.","SEM treats the dyad or group as the unit of analysis with each member representing a different variable, so that groups of four persons with five different variables yield twenty variables in the dataset, and X1 and X2 are exogenous and correlated while e1 and e2 are correlated disturbances.","SEM is much easier to employ with distinguishable members than with indistinguishable members, because for indistinguishable dyads numerous equality constraints (e.g., b1 = b2, a1 = a2) sometimes numbering in the hundreds must be created.","Advantages of SEM over MLM include easy specification of multi-equation models such as mediation, chi-square testing of overall model fit, a convenient pictorial representation, the fact that only SEM can be used for common-fate and mutual-influence models, and better handling of missing data via full-information maximum likelihood (FIML) rather than listwise deletion.","Advantages of MLM include built-in routines in most statistical packages, specification that is familiar to researchers versed in regression and ANOVA, historical dominance in dyadic and group publications, and the ability to estimate models with small samples whereas SEM requires at least 100 or more dyads or groups.","In the actor-partner interdependence model (APIM), one person's score on a predictor or causal variable X influences not only that person's score on the outcome Y (the actor effect) but also the partner's score on the outcome (the partner effect), and the model works for both distinguishable and indistinguishable dyads.","The APIM is by far the most popular of the three models, having been used in 1,503 papers published from 2005 through 2021, whereas the common-fate model was used only forty-two times and the mutual-influence model only a handful of times during the same period.","In the common-fate model (CFM), the covariation between the two X variables and the two Y variables is assumed to be due to two latent or unmeasured variables, and the key theoretical path runs from latent X to latent Y; estimation of the CFM requires SEM, and the basic model fixes the loadings of one member to one and sets the freed loadings of the other member equal to each other.","In the mutual-influence model (MIM), X1 affects Y1 but not Y2, X2 affects Y2 but not Y1, and Y1 and Y2 mutually affect one another; the model requires SEM for estimation, and to identify it the X variables, called instrumental variables, are assumed to have actor effects but no partner effects.","The MIM requires strong actor effects, because if the actor effects are weak the estimates of the mutual-influence paths have very high standard errors and are essentially uninterpretable, and when dyad members are indistinguishable the two reciprocal paths are set equal but they may differ when members are distinguishable.","The major difficulty in implementing the MIM is finding viable instrumental variables, and one possibility is to use prior values of X and Y, such as using emotions felt the day before as instruments to measure simultaneous effects between emotions felt on the current day in a study of emotional contagion.","The APIM, CFM, and MIM can be blended together in hybrid models, as when Wickham and Macia (2019) discussed hybrid APIM and CFM models and Herzberg (2013) provided an example of a hybrid model with both APIM and mutual-influence components."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Dyadic Analysis","topic":"Power in Dyadic APIM Analyses","visual_frame_description":"A power-analysis cockpit with a console showing the apimpower app inputs - alpha 0.05, actor effect 0.20, partner effect 0.10, r_xx 0.50, r_ee 0.30, and N = 100 - with readouts of power 0.777 for the actor effect and 0.275 for the partner effect. A wall chart reproduces Table 23.1 with power values for the actor and partner tests across r_xx and r_ee ranging from -0.75 to 0.75, and a second screen shows Table 23.2 for distinguishable dyads with N = 75, r_xx = 0.3, and r_ee = 0.4.","player_mission":"Audit the power budget for a planned APIM study. Using the apimpower app's logic, specify alpha, the actor and partner effect sizes as beta weights, the correlation between dyad members' X variables (r_xx), the correlation between their residuals (r_ee), and the number of dyads, then trace how each factor moves power. Handle the distinguishable case with the difference and pooled tests, and judge when a design using singles outperforms an N-dyad design.","key_concepts":["There is an app called apimpower (Ackerman & Kenny, 2016) that is very helpful in determining power for cross-sectional APIM analyses involving indistinguishable or distinguishable dyads, and it permits researchers to either estimate power given the sample size or estimate sample size given the desired level of power.","The apimpower app uses actor and partner effects measured as beta weights (i.e., standardized regression coefficients) for all power computations, and if either Cohen's d or a partial correlation is used, they are converted by the app into beta coefficients.","Power is based on a pooled-regression approach using the Satterthwaite approximation for degrees of freedom, and to determine power the non-central t distribution is used, which only requires the non-centrality parameter (the actor or partner effect divided by its standard error) and the degrees of freedom.","Power to detect actor and partner effects for indistinguishable dyads is a function of alpha (the Type I error rate), the size of the actor effect, the size of the partner effect, r_xx (the correlation between the dyad members' X variables), r_ee (the correlation between the dyad members' residuals), and sample size.","As a simple example, if alpha is 0.05, the actor effect is 0.20, the partner effect is 0.10, r_xx is 0.50, r_ee is 0.30, and there are 100 dyads, the power to detect the actor effect is estimated as 0.777 and the power to detect the partner effect as 0.275.","Power to detect actor and partner effects increases as N, the number of dyads, increases and also as alpha increases, but power is lowered by collinearity of the X variables: raising r_xx from 0.50 to 0.80 drops the power of the actor effect from 0.777 to 0.518 and the power of the partner effect to 0.171.","The power of the test of the actor effect increases from 0.777 to 0.919 if the beta effect size for the partner effect is raised from 0.10 to 0.50, because increasing the size of the other effect reduces the error variance, which results in an increase in power.","A fifth and least intuitive factor affecting power is the product of r_xx and r_ee: power increases if this product is very positive but decreases if the product is very negative, a finding about the sign of r_ee that has not been previously noted in the literature.","Sometimes only one member of a dyad has Y measured while both members' X variables are measured - such people might be called singles - and it is possible to perform an APIM analysis using only singles, with the only thing that cannot be estimated being the correlation of the errors.","With indistinguishable dyads, when the product of r_xx and r_ee is negative, the design with two N singles has more power than an N-dyads design, so power is not always greater with N dyads than with two N singles.","For distinguishable dyads, apimpower offers two additional tests: power to detect differences in actor and partner effects for the two members (a1 - a2 = 0 and p1 - p2 = 0), which are essentially tests of whether the distinguishing variable moderates the effects, and power to detect pooled actor and partner effects ((a1 + a2)/2 = 0 and (p1 + p2)/2 = 0).","In the distinguishable case the power levels in the tests of actor and partner effects are much lower than in the indistinguishable case, so that in essence the sample sizes for the effects are doubled in the indistinguishable case, and differences in the variances of X and E across members (as in patient-caregiver dyads) can dramatically affect power."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Dyadic Analysis","topic":"The Partition of Nonindependence and Over-Time Dyadic Analysis","visual_frame_description":"A matrix-shaped wall of panels: the first panel shows Figure 23.2, the decomposition of nonindependence for the Acitelli et al. (2013) data with standardized paths of -0.120 and -0.150 and an overall correlation of 0.231; the second panel reproduces Table 23.5, the variance-covariance matrix of random effects (tau) for the dyadic growth-curve model; and the third panel contrasts three model cards - growth curve with time as predictor, stability-and-influence with lagged Y as predictors, and over-time APIM with lagged X as predictors - each listing its random effects and centering notes.","player_mission":"Lay out the full analytic matrix for interdependence: first decompose the total nonindependence in an APIM into spuriousness due to actor-partner effects and the correlation of the X variables, then route longitudinal dyadic designs to the dyadic growth-curve model, the stability-and-influence model, or the over-time APIM. For each model, specify the tau matrix of random effects, the centering strategy, and the estimation method (MLM, SEM, or DSEM).","key_concepts":["Because the name of the APIM contains the term interdependence, it is surprising that researchers almost never examine how much of the nonindependence is explained by actor and partner effects, but methods to partition nonindependence in the APIM have been developed by Kenny (2015) and Dwyer et al. (2017).","There are two ways in which the APIM explains nonindependence: first, X1 is a common cause of Y1 and Y2 and X2 is a common cause of Y1 and Y2 (spurious or confounding effects a1p2 and a2p1), and second, the correlation of the X variables contributes a1a2r_xx and p1p2r_xx.","The total explained variance in the indistinguishable case is 2ap + (a2 + p2)r_xx, and it is very possible that these quantities can be negative, so that the APIM can explain nonindependence only through the combination of actor and partner effects, never through either alone.","In the Acitelli et al. (2013) example, commitment measured a year and a half earlier served as X and anxious attachment measured at follow-up as Y, the correlation between the two outcomes was 0.231, spuriousness explained 0.036 (15.7 percent) and the correlation between the partners' commitment scores explained 0.022 (9.3 percent), for a total of 25.0 percent of the overall correlation explained.","In the complex case with a second mixed variable Z and covariates C1 and C2, there are six different sources of correlation between Y1 and Y2 explained by the APIM, presented in Table 23.3, and with two mixed predictors and two covariates in Table 23.4 the nonindependence was totally explained by the APIM (99.7 percent) while the covariates added essentially nothing.","The decomposition of the APIM is much more complicated and less intuitive than the decomposition of mediation, but it merits consideration because the APIM is fundamentally an attempt to understand how interdependence - the I in APIM - is explained.","What sets over-time dyadic analysis apart from cross-sectional dyadic analysis is the need to account for temporal nonindependence in addition to dyadic nonindependence, and the two levels in over-time dyadic models are the dyad at the upper level and observation within the dyad at the lower level.","It is typically a mistake to treat over-time dyadic data as a three-level multilevel model with observations nested in persons nested in dyads, because observations from both dyad members are simultaneously collected at each timepoint, so time is crossed with person and not nested.","In the dyadic growth-curve model, time is the predictor variable, a minimum of three timepoints is required, and the intercept and slope coefficients have a subscript j indicating that these effects can vary across dyads, producing four random variables (two intercepts and two slopes) in the tau matrix.","The covariance between the dyad members' intercepts captures an important source of dyadic nonindependence - the degree to which the two members' predicted values for the outcome at time zero are correlated - and the covariance between their slopes reflects whether the two members are changing at the same rate.","In the stability-and-influence model, participants' current scores are predicted by both their own (stability) and their partners' (influence) previous scores from a prior timepoint, the model looks very similar to the cross-sectional APIM except that lagged scores are used as predictors, and a serious endogeneity problem arises because lagged outcomes serve as both predictors and outcomes.","The over-time APIM uses another variable X at time t - 1 as the predictor instead of lagged values of Y, and with person-mean centering of X and grand-mean centering of the person means it yields four effects: within-person actor, between-person actor, within-person partner (spillover or influence), and between-person partner effects."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Dyadic Analysis","topic":"Group Studies, the Social Relations Model, and Measurement Invariance","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom with a six-person team roster (Art, Beth, Carol, Doug, Ed, Felicia) on the screen, where Table 23.6 shows how Art's perception of Beth's helpfulness equals the group mean plus Art's actor effect plus Beth's partner effect plus the unique relationship effect. A second screen displays Table 23.7 with SRM relative variances for leadership, personality judgments, liking, romantic attraction, and metaperception, and a third shows the invariance checklist with correlated residuals between members' same indicators.","player_mission":"Take command of a final case conference: analyze group data with the traditional MLM averaging approach, the group APIM, and the GAPIM, then decompose a round-robin dataset with the social relations model (SRM) into group mean, actor, partner, and relationship components, interpret SRM variance partitions, and finally run the three-level measurement invariance checklist (configural, loading, intercept) for dyadic constructs.","key_concepts":["In the traditional multilevel model for group data, the lower-level predictor variable is averaged across all of the group members to create an upper-level predictor, and a key question is whether the effect of that variable occurs at the lower level (the person) or the upper level (the group).","In the group APIM, a person's outcomes are a function of both one's own inputs and the average of one's partners' inputs, so the actor effect has the same definition as in dyadic APIM, but the partner effect is the effect of the average of the other group members' inputs on the person's outcome.","Group researchers typically examine actor effects but often ignore partner effects, yet it is the partner effects that capture interdependence within groups, because their presence indicates that a person's response depends on the characteristics and behaviors of other group members.","Kenny and Garcia (2012) proposed the group actor-partner interdependence model (GAPIM), which assesses interactions such as whether the actor is similar to others in the group and whether other members are similar to each other, which can be combined into a diversity effect.","Common-fate models have also been proposed for group data, in which the X and Y measurements for each member are assumed to be indicators of latent variables, a model that Bonito et al. (2016) call the latent group model.","In a round-robin design, the prototypical SRM design, every member of the group interacts with or rates every other individual in the group, so a group of four individuals (A, B, C, and D) yields twelve dyadic measures (AB, AC, AD, BA, BC, BD, and so on).","In the social relations model (SRM), each dyadic score is a function of four effects: the group mean, the actor effect, the partner effect, and the relationship effect, and the SRM provides a general framework from which both social behavior and interpersonal perception can be studied.","In the SRM, the actor effect is the tendency for a person to exhibit a consistent level of response across all interaction partners and the partner effect is the tendency for others to be consistent with a particular partner, and these meanings differ from the APIM, where actor and partner refer to the effect of a measured variable on another variable in one specific relationship.","The relationship effect is at the dyad level and reflects the unique combination of two individuals after removing the group mean and their individual-level tendencies, it can be viewed as the interaction between actor and partner, and it can be separated from error only if there are multiple measures of the same underlying construct or replications over time.","In the SRM variance partitions of Table 23.7, target variance was dominant for leadership (60 percent, meaning group members agree on who is the leader), relationship variance was dominant for liking (64 percent), and perceiver variance was dominant for metaperception (64 percent, correlating negatively with social anxiety and positively with self-perceptions).","The SRM is a multilevel model that may have as many as five different levels (observation, measure, dyad, individual, and group), it was for many years estimated by a variant of analysis of variance such as the TripleR package, but it is increasingly being estimated by multilevel modeling programs and the web-based app SRM-R (Wong et al., 2024).","Many social cognition studies are SRM studies in which participants take on the role of actor and stimuli take on the role of partner, such as the Correll et al. (2002) shooter study, and although the design is not reciprocal the model is still the SRM."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm24","title":"Chapter 24: Random Factors and Research Generalization","pages":"602-620","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Random Factors","topic":"Random Factors, Fixed Factors, and the Generalization Question","visual_frame_description":"A terminology laboratory with two bins labeled 'Fixed Factor' and 'Random Factor': target gender sits in the fixed bin while participants and target photographs sit in the random bin. A whiteboard diagrams Jane's design with 25 participants rating 20 targets (10 male, 10 female) for perceived leadership potential, and a two-panel display contrasts the participant-means t-test (generalizing to participants, same targets) with the target-means t-test (generalizing to targets, same participants).","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's core distinction: define fixed and random factors and the two kinds of generalization (over participants and over targets), then apply them to Jane's study of leadership ratings of target photographs. Walk through Jane's initial paired t-test on participant means and the separate analysis of target means, and judge what each analysis permits inference to.","key_concepts":["In Jane's study, 25 participants judged 20 target photographs, half male and half female, on perceived leadership potential, and her data set was initially organized by participant with each participant being a different row in the matrix.","A factor is treated as fixed when the researcher has no interest in generalizing beyond the particular levels included in the study, whereas a factor is treated as random when generalization to other levels of the same factor is desired.","Jane's initial analysis was a paired t-test on the two mean scores from each participant (averaging across targets), which treated participant as the random factor and permitted inferences to larger samples of participants but only for the same twenty target photographs.","Jane proposed a second analysis computing a mean judgment score for each target (averaging across all twenty-five participants) and then conducting a two-sample t-test on the twenty target means, permitting inferences to larger samples of targets but only for the same participants in her original study.","The conjunction of Jane's two separate analyses is not equivalent to the mixed-model analysis that incorporates both random factors and uses the full design and data set in one analysis with multiple sources of error variation.","In the mixed-model analysis that treats both participants and targets as random, the t statistic associated with the gender difference is 2.134, which is statistically significant but considerably smaller than it was in Jane's initial analysis in which participants were the only random factor.","Jane's earlier conclusion was that she could be confident of finding a gender difference in ratings for new samples of participants, but only using the same twenty target photographs as in the original study.","The mixed-model analysis treating both participants and targets as random permits inference to new samples of both participants and targets that she might use, sampling them from the same populations as the current samples.","In most studies, participants are the random factor, but it is not at all unusual to have at least one other random factor that varies in a particular study, be that factor stimulus items, accomplices, partners, or the like.","When a study has more than one random factor, traditional ANOVA and t-test procedures are generally not appropriate because they typically permit only one random factor and treat any others in a data set as fixed.","Appropriate mixed-models analyses are necessary in order to assure that generalizations can be reached across other levels of all random factors that might have been used, rather than only across one of them.","In Jane's design, the effect of target gender was the fixed factor of interest, while participant and target photograph were the two random factors to which generalization was sought."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Random Factors","topic":"Mixed Models: Specification and Output in Figure 24.1","visual_frame_description":"A briefing room with Figure 24.1 projected on the main screen: the top line shows the model syntax Y ~ X plus the random terms (1|T) and (1 + X | P), and below it a numbered output table lists the random variances 0.762 (participant means), 0.145 (participant gender differences), 1.135 (target means), and 0.970 (residual). A second panel shows the 0.31 correlation between the two participant-level random components and the fixed effects with grand mean 4.634 and gender effect 0.542.","player_mission":"Host a methods briefing on the lme4 mixed-model syntax and output for Jane's data. Explain each part of the model specification (the fixed part Y ~ X, the random intercept for target (1|T), and the random intercept and slope for participant (1 + X | P)), then walk the team through every number in the output: random variances, standard deviations, the correlation between random components, and the fixed-effect estimates.","key_concepts":["Models that permit multiple sources of error variation simultaneously are typically called mixed models, and they required Jane to think about her data a bit differently than she had until now.","In the model specification shown in Figure 24.1, Y ~ X gives the fixed part of the model, meaning the outcome Y may vary as a function of X (gender of target) with the grand mean or intercept being implicit in the model.","The term (1|T) specifies that the model intercept (mean) varies as a function of target, and the term (1 + X | P) specifies that both the intercept and the X slope (gender difference) vary as a function of participant.","The residual variation in the model is implicitly part of it, and it is equivalent to the participant-by-target-within-gender interaction, which could be estimated separately only if there were replications with two observed scores for each participant-by-target combination.","The output first gives the random variances due to participant means (0.762) and participant gender differences (0.145), showing more variation in average ratings from participant to participant than in participants' gender differences.","To the right of these two variances, the output gives their square roots, that is, the standard deviations of these two random effects, and then continuing to the right it provides the estimated correlation (0.31) between the two random components.","The estimated correlation of 0.31 indicates that those participants who give higher mean ratings on average across the targets also tend to give judgments that manifest a larger male-minus-female difference in perceived leadership potential.","The next line of the output provides the estimated variance associated with target means (1.135), and interestingly there is more variance in judgments due to targets than due to participants.","The output estimates residual variation in the individual observations over and above all the previously discussed fixed and random effects as 0.970, and this includes variation due to the interaction of participant and target within gender.","The fixed-effects part of the output gives the grand-mean rating (4.634) and the gender effect (0.542), which when doubled is the male-female mean judgment difference because with effect coding of +1 and -1 the effect must be doubled to obtain the mean difference.","The t statistic (2.134) associated with this gender difference is statistically significant, but now considerably smaller than it was in Jane's initial analysis in which participants were the only random factor in the analysis.","Mixed-model programs differ in how they determine the degrees of freedom, resulting in usually small differences in the p values, and the same syntax could just as easily have been used in other packages such as SPSS, Stata, or SAS."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Random Factors","topic":"The Ubiquity of Second Random Factors","visual_frame_description":"An evidence board organized into four sections: 'Stimuli, Vignettes, and Scenarios' (McArthur 1972, the risky shift with the CDQ, Higgins et al. 1977 priming, Belmi and Schroeder 2021), 'Accomplices' (Word et al. 1974, Stephenson and Wicklund 1984), 'Groups and Dyads' (Goenka and Thomas 2019, Meagher 2015, Eastwick et al. 2007), and 'Measures' (Cronbach et al. 1972, the Beck depression inventory). Each card shows the study, its second random factor, and a red stamp where the published analyses treated that factor as fixed.","player_mission":"Audit the research literature for studies that actually contain two or more random factors but were analyzed as if participants were the only one. For each classic study - McArthur's covariation test, the risky-shift research, priming studies, accomplice studies such as Word et al., group and dyad studies, and multi-item measures - classify the second random factor and state what generalization was foreclosed by treating it as fixed.","key_concepts":["Studies with more than one random factor are found throughout social and personality psychology, even if their accompanying analyses have typically acted as if participant were the only random factor.","The most common examples of second random factors occur in studies like Jane's with multiple stimuli or events to which participants respond, and the section is organized by the nature of the second random factor found in the studies reviewed.","McArthur's (1972) test of Kelley's covariation principle studied attributions made for twelve different behaviors (e.g., 'John laughs at the comedian'), and her study had potentially two random factors - participants and behaviors - to which researchers would want to generalize.","McArthur was well ahead of her time because she reported how often each effect was present for each participant across behaviors and how often it was present for each behavior across participants, thus recognizing the two factors as random even though she did not have the tools to treat them both simultaneously.","In the risky-shift research using the choice dilemma questionnaire, Myers and Lamm (1976) showed that whether or not the risky shift was found depended on the specific items in the CDQ, with three of the original items consistently showing cautious shifts rather than risky ones.","Because the dilemmas in the risky-shift research were never treated as a random factor, no one asked whether the results would generalize across larger samples of dilemmas, and it turned out that group decision making is characterized not by risky shifts but by shifts in the direction of group norms that depend on the specific items used.","Reanalyses with mixed models by Wolsiefer et al. (2017) revealed that, for at least some of the priming tasks used to assess automatic or implicit attitudes, results may not generalize across both participants and the priming stimuli that might be used.","Belmi and Schroeder (2021), using mixed-model analyses that generalized across both participants and targets, concluded that participants viewed targets in a more utilitarian and objectifying way when the targets appeared in a work context than when the same targets appeared in a non-work context.","In accomplice studies such as Word et al. (1974), both participants and accomplices should be treated as random factors, but the interviewers (accomplices) were not treated as random, so the results can only be generalized to the specific accomplices included in the study.","With such small numbers of accomplices as in Word et al. (1974) (three White and two African American interviewers), it probably would not have been feasible to treat them as random, and the numbers of levels of the random factors jointly constrain power in mixed-model analyses (Westfall et al., 2014).","In Stephenson and Wicklund's (1984) study of the contagion of self-focus, treating the non-instructed dyad member as random also treats the other member of the dyad as random, because the unit in the analysis is effectively the yoked pair of participants.","A random-effects meta-analysis treats experimenter (or equivalently the study) as a random factor, and Cronbach and colleagues' (1972) generalizability theory underlies the modern realization that measures contain multiple sources of variation, yet items within a scale are in practice almost always treated as fixed."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Random Factors","topic":"Designs with Two Random Factors: Crossing, Nesting, and Power","visual_frame_description":"A matrix wall of panels: the first shows Jane's design with participant crossed with target gender and target photograph nested under gender, and a second contrasts a design with both random factors crossed with a bearded-versus-clean-shaven fixed factor against one with both nested under gender. A third panel contrasts the counterbalanced design and the subset design (60 stimuli, each participant responding to 10) with power bars, and a corner panel reproduces the round-robin honesty design with two twelve-person groups.","player_mission":"Lay out the full design matrix for one fixed factor and two random factors, following Judd et al. (2017): map every combination of crossing and nesting of each random factor with the fixed one and with each other, then budget statistical power. For each design - fully crossed, targets nested under gender, each participant with a unique set of targets, students nested under teachers, and the round-robin honesty study - specify the mixed model and which variance components enter the error term.","key_concepts":["Judd et al. (2017) provided a systematic treatment of design possibilities to examine the effect of one fixed factor with two random factors, and each random factor can be crossed with the fixed factor of interest or nested under its levels.","In Jane's case, participant was crossed with the fixed factor of target gender (i.e., each participant judged both male and female targets), but target photograph was nested under the fixed factor (i.e., each target was either male or female).","The two random factors themselves can be crossed with each other or one nested under the other: in Jane's study every participant judged every target, making these crossed random factors, whereas if each participant judged only a unique set of targets, targets would be nested within participants.","The correctly specified mixed model should include effects of all factors, both fixed and random, and all interactions of factors that are crossed with each other, a specification called the maximal or full specification (Barr et al., 2013).","In the teacher-student example, where students are nested under teachers, the mixed-model specification has only three sources of variation - the fixed effect of teacher experience, random variance due to teachers, and random variance due to students within teachers - because none of the interactions among these factors can be assessed.","Random factors that are crossed with the fixed factor contribute two sources of variation to the data that are separately estimable - variance due to mean differences between the levels of the random factor and variance due to the interaction of the random factor with the fixed one - and only the latter contributes to the error in testing the fixed factor.","For random factors that are nested under levels of the fixed one (as targets are in Jane's design), variance due to mean differences and variance due to the interaction with the fixed factor are confounded and cannot be separately estimated, so the variation due to mean differences influences the test of the fixed factor.","Tests of the fixed factor will generally be more powerful with random factors that are crossed with the fixed factor than with random factors nested within it, especially when there are large differences attributable to the individual levels of the random factor.","Power is determined jointly by the number of sampled units of both random factors and by the magnitude of the variance attributable to them both, so it is not possible to focus exclusively on one random factor in a quest for high power (Judd et al., 2017; Westfall et al., 2014).","In a subset design with sixty stimuli where each participant responds to only ten of them, retaining the full sixty stimuli in the design results in substantially more power than if all participants responded to the same ten stimuli, because what affects power more than the total number of observations is the number of sampled units or levels of both of the random factors.","A counterbalanced design (Judd et al., 2017), in which participants and stimuli are each randomly divided into two groups and each participant responds to each stimulus once in one condition or the other, is nearly as powerful as the fully crossed design with only half the observations and without repeated responses of participants to individual stimuli.","In a round-robin design with two twelve-person groups where each participant also serves as a target, variance in honesty ratings is due to the fixed self-presentation instruction (or group), participant (rater), target (ratee), and the participant-by-target interaction, and if there were more than two groups, groups would also need to be treated as random."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Random Factors","topic":"Random Factors Between Studies: Heterogeneity, Meta-Analysis, and Replication","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom with a forest plot of the Vesely and Klöckner (2020) meta-analysis of social desirability and environmental behavior (average r = 0.11, SD = 0.05, 95 percent of studies between 0.06 and 0.16) and a scatterplot of the Linden and Hönekopp (2021) finding that domains with larger average effects have more heterogeneity (r = 0.49 across 150 meta-analyses). A wall banner asks 'One true effect?' and quotes the chapter's conclusion that a particular study is designed to reveal one of many realities.","player_mission":"Take command of a final strategy session on generalization beyond a single study. Explain why random factors that vary only between studies cannot support generalization from one study's data, trace the two sources of variation in effect sizes in meta-analysis (sampling error and effect size heterogeneity), and lay out the implications for power analyses, registered replication reports, and the notion of a single true effect.","key_concepts":["Appropriate generalization can be accomplished for random factors that vary within any given study, but for random factors that do not vary within a study, generalization from study data is not feasible.","The only way one can see whether factors that vary only between studies are responsible for variation in observed effects is by looking across studies, and one can purposively design replication studies to capture effects of random variables such as experimenter, location, and so forth.","In a meta-analysis there are two sources of variation in effect sizes across studies: sampling error within a given study, which derives from the random factor(s) that vary in that study, and effect size heterogeneity induced by unknown random variables that vary between studies (Kenny & Judd, 2019).","Whenever effect sizes show more variability than is expected from sampling error alone, a random-effects meta-analysis is called for, and it yields an estimate of the variability in effect sizes in a research domain over and above sampling error.","Vesely and Klöckner (2020) found an average correlation of 0.11 between social desirability and stated environmental behaviors with a standard deviation of 0.05, suggesting that about 95 percent of studies would have effect sizes between 0.06 and 0.16.","Kenny and Judd (2019) observed that research areas with larger average effect sizes have greater levels of heterogeneity, and a study of 150 meta-analyses by Linden and Hönekopp (2021) found a 0.49 correlation between the magnitude of the average effect size in a domain and the degree of heterogeneity in the obtained effect sizes.","When there is in fact a substantial effect, many random factors that vary between studies result in its being larger or smaller, but when there is little to no effect on average, those random factors will result in little variation in its estimation.","It is probably incorrect to talk about one 'true' effect in a domain, because there may be an average effect but there are probably multiple 'true' effects that vary systematically across many uncertainly known moderators.","It is misguided to try to design the one perfect, high-powered replication to definitively verify an effect, because the effect size found even in that perfect replication is likely specific only to the particular levels of those random factors that have been held constant in that study.","A better approach is to allow many different replication attempts that systematically vary a possible moderator between studies, so that one can both get the average effect size and observe its variability, as in registered replication reports (e.g., Simons et al., 2014).","Effect size heterogeneity, if present in a domain, necessarily complicates power computations in the planning of studies, because the actual true effect may be larger or smaller than anticipated, and power computations that consider a range of true effect sizes are recommended (Kenny & Judd, 2019; McShane & Böckenholt, 2014).","A particular study is designed not to discover some unchanging and universal reality but to reveal one of many realities, and original and replication studies will vary in what they find not only because they may be poorly done or reported but also because of random factors that vary and cannot be fully controlled."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm25","title":"Chapter 25: Combining Statistical and Causal Mediation Analysis","pages":"622-651","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Mediation","topic":"Statistical Mediation: The Simple Mediation Model and Its Paths","visual_frame_description":"A terminology laboratory with a path diagram like Figure 25.1 on the main screen: X on the left feeding into M and Y, M feeding into Y, with the a1, b1, and c1-prime paths labeled and a dashed overlay showing optional covariate paths U1 through UJ. A side panel scrolls the two estimating equations, and a calculator bar demonstrates a worked example where a1 equals 2 and b1 equals 3, producing an indirect effect of 6.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundation: define statistical mediation analysis as an analytical process using OLS regression or SEMs, and walk through the two regression equations that define the a-path, b-path, and direct effect. Compute the indirect effect as the product a1 times b1, show how the indirect and direct effects sum to the total effect, and extend the equations to include covariates, referencing the path diagram in Figure 25.1 throughout.","key_concepts":["Mediation analysis has become a staple of research in social and personality psychology because it is useful for testing questions about how or why effects occur.","One example given in the chapter is that increasing growth mindset can improve performance through the proposed mechanism of increased attention to mistakes (Schroder et al., 2017).","Statistical mediation analysis is focused on conducting statistical inference, that is, testing whether a quantity is nonzero at the population level, whereas causal mediation analysis is focused on causal inference, evaluating whether a sample value is an unbiased estimate of a causal effect at the population level.","Statistical mediation analysis is an analytical process which typically involves estimating linear models using ordinary least squares (OLS) regression or structural equation models (SEMs), and these models provide an estimate of the indirect effect.","The indirect effect is of greatest interest for testing a mediation hypothesis because it quantifies the effect of an independent variable on an outcome variable through a specified mediator variable, such as the effect of X on Y through M.","Two regression equations represent the effects of interest: one predicts the mediator from the independent variable (M equals a0 plus a1X plus a residual), and the other predicts the outcome from both the mediator and the independent variable (Y equals c0-prime plus c1-prime X plus b1 M plus a residual).","In the first equation, the coefficient a1 represents the predicted difference in M for two cases that differ by one unit on X, and residuals are typically assumed to be normally distributed with a mean of zero and an unknown variance.","In the second equation, the coefficient c1-prime represents the expected difference in Y between two cases that differ by one unit on X but have the same score on M, and b1 represents the predicted difference in Y for two cases that differ by one unit on M but have the same score on X.","The indirect effect is quantified by the product of a1 and b1 (a1 times b1), and in a worked example where a1 equals 2 and b1 equals 3, a one-unit difference on X results in a two-unit difference on M, which results in a six-unit difference in Y.","The indirect effect and the direct effect sum together to a quantity called the total effect (a1 b1 plus c1-prime equals c1), and the total effect can be estimated using a separate regression equation that predicts Y from X alone.","When covariates are included in all three estimating equations, the indirect and direct effects still sum to the total effect, but this is not true if different covariates are used in different equations, which can make the indirect effect very difficult to interpret.","Statistical mediation analysis was popularized through the seminal paper by Baron and Kenny (1986) and has become a staple for researchers interested in evaluating processes, increasing in use in social psychology over the last forty years."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Mediation","topic":"Inference for Indirect Effects: Methods That Work and Methods to Avoid","visual_frame_description":"A briefing room with a two-column verdict board: the left column, stamped 'Works', lists percentile bootstrap CIs, Monte Carlo CIs, distribution of the product, and joint significance with software badges (PROCESS, psych, Rmediation); the right column, stamped 'Avoid', lists BC and BCA bootstrap CIs, the Sobel test, and causal steps, each with a red flag noting elevated type I error, low power, or the overly conservative total-effect requirement.","player_mission":"Host a methods briefing on how to make inference about indirect effects. Explain why inference is needed at all, then present the four inferential methods that perform well in simulation studies: percentile bootstrap confidence intervals, Monte Carlo confidence intervals, the distribution of the product, and the joint significance test. Then run through the three methods simulation research says to avoid, the bias-corrected and bias-corrected-and-accelerated bootstrap CIs, the Sobel test, and the causal steps approach, and say exactly what goes wrong with each.","key_concepts":["If at the population level the indirect effect is zero, this means that M is not a mechanism through which X affects Y, so inference about indirect effects from a sample is needed to provide evidence for or against research questions about mechanisms.","Based on a variety of simulation studies, four methods seem to perform very similarly in most circumstances: percentile bootstrap confidence intervals, Monte Carlo confidence intervals, the distribution of the product, and the joint significance test.","The percentile bootstrap is a non-parametric method where thousands of bootstrap samples are generated by sampling from the original sample with replacement, the indirect effect is estimated in each resample, and specific percentiles of that distribution define the bounds of the confidence interval.","Percentile bootstrap confidence intervals are examined to see if they include zero, and if they do not, the null hypothesis that the indirect effect is zero is rejected, though the CIs also provide information about magnitude and uncertainty beyond this dichotomous decision.","The Monte Carlo CI is a semi-parametric method that simulates the sampling distributions of the a1 and b1 estimates and then multiplies pairs of samples together to generate an estimated sampling distribution for the product a1 b1.","The distribution of the product method shares the same assumptions as the Monte Carlo CI but uses mathematical functions rather than simulation to generate confidence intervals, and software is available in the Rmediation package for R and in SAS.","The joint significance test relies on discrete hypothesis tests for the individual components a1 and b1, and if both tests are significant at a pre-specified alpha level, then the null hypothesis that the indirect effect is zero is rejected.","The joint significance test performs well with respect to making correct inferential decisions but does not generate a direct quantification of the indirect effect, and its advantage is that any program which can do OLS regression can conduct it.","The bias-corrected (BC) and bias-corrected-and-accelerated (BCA) bootstrap CIs suffer from elevated type I error rates, such that when a researcher sets their alpha level at 0.05 they may actually be operating under a type I error rate more like 0.07 or 0.08.","The Sobel test, sometimes called a delta method, assumes the indirect effect has a normal sampling distribution, which holds only in very large samples of over 2,000, a size not common in social and personality psychology, and it has low power and performs worse than other methods.","The causal steps approach proposed by Baron and Kenny (1986) uses a four-step process, and its requirement that the total effect be significant is often criticized as overly conservative because the total effect may not be distinguishable from zero when the direct and indirect effects have opposite signs.","The distinction between complete and partial mediation from the causal steps method is criticized because complete mediation relies on accepting the null hypothesis, and simulation research shows the causal steps method is very low in statistical power, leading many researchers to abandon it in favor of the joint significance test."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Mediation","topic":"From Cross-Sectional Critiques to Causal Assumptions and Sensitivity Analysis","visual_frame_description":"An evidence board with three panels: the first shows a timeline of critiques ending in the statistic that about 43 percent of Journal of Personality and Social Psychology articles published from 2018 to 2020 included at least one mediation analysis; the second reproduces Figure 25.2 with panels A, B, and C labeling U as a confounder, a collider, and a mediator; the third reproduces Figure 25.3 with four dashed paths marking confounders U1 through U4 against Pearl's four assumptions, plus a sensitivity dial showing the residual correlation rho.","player_mission":"Audit the practice of mediation analysis in psychology: document the critiques of applying statistical mediation to completely cross-sectional data, then trace how causal mediation analysis answers them through the potential outcomes framework, SUTVA, and the confounder-collider-mediator distinctions in Figure 25.2. Lay out Pearl's four assumptions for identification of the pure and total natural indirect effects (Figure 25.3), state which assumptions randomization of X satisfies, and explain what sensitivity analysis can and cannot tell a researcher.","key_concepts":["Perhaps the most common criticism of mediation analysis concerns the type of data it is commonly applied to, completely cross-sectional data, where X, M, and Y are observed variables measured all at the same time.","The issues with applying mediation analysis to completely cross-sectional data are twofold: mediation is an inherently causal process, and mediation is an inherently longitudinal process, though these two issues are linked and do not constitute two separable problems.","Mediation is a hypothesis about causal effects, but statistical mediation analysis alone cannot support causal claims, and even when X is randomly assigned there is ambiguity in the causal order of M and Y such that experimental data still cannot provide unambiguous evidence of causal indirect effects (Bullock et al., 2010).","The primary distinction between statistical and causal mediation analysis is that the latter is concerned with defining the assumptions under which the indirect effect is truly estimating a causal effect, a process typically called identification.","The theoretical development of causal mediation methods occurred in mathematics, statistics, and computer science rather than applied fields like psychology, and the chapter follows the general approach proposed by Pearl (2001), which relies on the potential outcomes framework (Rubin, 2005).","In the potential outcomes framework, each individual has their own potential outcome scores for each condition, the individual causal effect Y(X=1) minus Y(X=0) is impossible to observe because each participant is only assigned to one condition, and the average of this causal effect over the population would be the average treatment effect (ATE).","A confounder is a variable which causally affects both the group and the outcome, and when confounders are not taken into account it may look like there is a causal relationship between X and Y when in fact the relationship is confounded, so U needs to be included in the statistical model to provide an unbiased estimate.","A collider is a variable which is caused by both X and Y, so the causal pathways from each variable collide at it, and conditioning on a collider can cause bias in the estimate of a causal effect, just as controlling for mediators can bias the estimate because variability due to the indirect effect is misattributed.","The stable unit treatment value assumption (SUTVA) has two important implications: no spillover, meaning an individual's treatment response does not depend on the treatment of other cases, and consistency, meaning there are not multiple kinds of the treatment which are different from one another and would lead to different potential outcomes.","Pearl (2001) outlined four assumptions for identifying the indirect effects: no unmeasured confounders of the effect of X on Y, no unmeasured confounders of the effect of M on Y conditioned on covariates and X, no unmeasured confounders of the effect of X on M, and no measured or unmeasured confounders of the effect of M on Y which are affected by X.","A confounder of the M-Y relationship that is affected by X is troublesome because if it is measured and included as a covariate, part of the indirect effect (X to U4 to M to Y) will not be included in the indirect effect estimate, but if unmeasured it will bias the M-Y relationship, and it can be described as an alternative mediator.","Sensitivity analysis aims to provide information about whether the indirect effect can still be identified under a certain amount of confounding, typically by quantifying the largest possible correlation between the residuals for which the indirect effect is still identified, and it is not designed to provide direct support for assumptions but rather additional information to evaluate their plausibility."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Mediation","topic":"Integrating Both Frameworks: Planning, Conducting, and Reporting Mediation","visual_frame_description":"A workflow wall with four lanes: 'Plan' shows the four-assumption checklist with a DAG tool and power bars labeled 'tests of indirect effects tend to be low in power'; 'Conduct' shows a decision tree for testing the XM interaction (Vo et al., 2020) before fitting the mediation model and a covariate justification sheet; 'Report' shows a paper skeleton annotated with the AGReMA statement (Lee et al., 2021); and 'Preregister' shows an Open Science Framework submission card with planned sample size, alpha level, variable roles, inferential method, and sensitivity benchmarks.","player_mission":"Lay out an integrated workflow that applies tools from both statistical and causal mediation analysis at every stage of a study. For planning, match study designs to the four identification assumptions, add directed acyclic graphs and power analysis, and schedule preregistration; for conducting, set the rules for covariates, the XM interaction, and sensitivity analyses; for reporting, apply the AGReMA-aligned structure for the introduction, methods, results, and discussion sections.","key_concepts":["Researchers planning on conducting mediation analysis should not feel the need to choose between statistical and causal mediation analyses because the two methods are complementary and can be used in concert to generate research findings.","An important contribution of the causal mediation analysis approach is clearly articulating the assumptions required for the identification of indirect effects, and evaluating the feasibility of these assumptions in advance can ensure a stronger study.","A study where X is randomly assigned will help meet two of the four assumptions of mediation analysis, and other design-based approaches include generating versions of the treatment that do and do not affect the mediator (Bullock & Green, 2021) and designs proposed by Imai et al. (2013) which involve manipulation of the mediator.","One tool for identifying confounders is directed acyclic graphs (DAGs, Pearl 1995), and researchers should identify potential confounders of the relations in the model, make sure to measure them, and evaluate how to include them in the model.","A growing body of research on statistical power in mediation analysis suggests that tests of indirect effects tend to be low in statistical power, for both statistical mediation analyses and causal mediation analyses, and the interaction term in causal mediation can cause additional concern because interactions typically have small effects.","Researchers should consider preregistering their plan using a service like the Open Science Framework or AsPredicted, because preregistration creates a time-stamped, publicly accessible record of the research plan, and many journals in social and personality psychology offer badges to papers with preregistered studies.","Preregistrations of mediation analyses can include the planned sample size, alpha level, role of different variables in the analysis, inferential method and any important specifications for that method, and plans for sensitivity analysis including benchmarks for correlations deemed acceptable.","Because mediation analysis has many potential researcher degrees of freedom, especially the possibility of swapping variables around in their roles as outcomes, mediators, and covariates, researchers should avoid iterating through different variables in different roles unless the process will be explicitly described as exploratory.","Common practice in psychology is to not include interactions unless directly hypothesized, whereas proponents of causal mediation analysis suggest including the XM interaction by default (Vo et al., 2020), either by testing it prior to fitting the mediation model or by using it as a robustness check with a preregistered plan.","Researchers should include covariates that they believe could be potential confounders of the effects in the model, and sensitivity analyses should be conducted for any paths which, based on the experimental design, could be confounded, even when X is randomly assigned.","The AGReMA statement (A Guideline for Reporting Mediation Analyses, Lee et al., 2021) provides reporting guidance, and the introduction should present prior theoretical evidence supporting the proposed causal order of the variables, with any research suggesting potential confounders discussed there as well.","In the results section, APA recommendations state it is not sufficient to report only p-values, so coefficient estimates and confidence intervals should be reported, and the a and b paths need to be reported separately and together as the indirect effect so that the alignment of results with hypothesized patterns of effects can be evaluated."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Mediation","topic":"The Future of Mediation: Latent Variables and Longitudinal Models","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom with two projections: the first shows Figure 25.4, a latent variable mediation model with observed X in a rectangle, latent variables for M and Y in ovals with J and K observed indicators in rectangles, and residuals in circles; the second shows Figure 25.5 with panels A, B, and C for the cross-lag panel approach, the multilevel approach with random b1j coefficient, and the latent growth curve approach with latent intercepts and slopes for the mediator and outcome. A banner reads 'Integrating latent and longitudinal models with causal inference is the frontier'.","player_mission":"Take command of a strategy session on where mediation analysis in psychology is heading. Explain why measured variables are best seen as indicators of latent variables, how SEMs handle the measurement model and the structural model, and why using summary scores without accounting for measurement error can bias indirect effect estimates (Figure 25.4). Then chart the three longitudinal approaches, the cross-lag panel, multilevel, and latent growth curve models (Figure 25.5), and issue the call to integrate these developments with causal inference methods.","key_concepts":["Mediation analysis with latent variables has been an area of great growth in recent years, but the integration of this literature with the literature on causal inference is still very nascent (Gonzalez & Valente, 2022).","Many constructs important to the theory of social and personality psychology are not directly observable, they are latent, and typically researchers use a scale with multiple items all designed to tap into the latent variable.","The theoretical model behind multi-item measures is that each student has some unobservable interest in STEM (broadly) and that interest then causes them to respond the way they do on the questions, a process modeled through a factor model where a latent variable causes each item response.","Models of just the measurement processes are frequently called factor analyses, and models with structural relationships, where one latent variable predicts another, are called SEMs, which can be fit in programs such as lavaan in R, Mplus, and AMOS.","Using summary scores of scales (such as a sum or mean of the items) does not account for the possibility of measurement error, and not accounting for measurement error can bias the estimate of the indirect effect as well as increase uncertainty around the estimate (Hoyle & Kenny, 1999).","Mediation models in particular can behave in a complex manner when the mediator is latent because the mediator is both a predictor and an outcome, and researchers may want to leverage latent variable modeling to increase power without increasing sample size, though latent variables will not always improve power because of additional uncertainty.","Recent work has consistently demonstrated that mediation analysis with cross-sectional data provides biased estimates of the longitudinal process as it unfolds (Maxwell & Cole, 2007), because the underlying ideas of mediation are that one variable causes another, but how much time is needed for an effect to take place is unclear.","In the cross-lag panel approach, the simplest point of entry into longitudinal mediation, X is measured or assigned at time 1 and M and Y are measured at times 1, 2, and 3, and the indirect effect of X1 on Y3 through M2 is estimated with M1 and Y1 serving as covariates in the model because they are potential confounders.","In multilevel mediation, the model is described by the levels of each variable such that an experimental longitudinal design is a 2-1-1 model because X is assigned to the person and does not vary across time, while M and Y are measured at each time point.","A particular advantage of multilevel modeling is that each participant can be allowed to have their own intercept and slope in the model, called random effects, so the a-path, the b-path, or both can be random, and when both paths are random they can covary, a covariance that must be taken into account when estimating the indirect effect.","Monte Carlo confidence intervals are recommended for inference for indirect effects in multilevel mediation because bootstrapping approaches are complicated by the nesting of the data, and for 1-1-1 models indirect effects can occur at level 1 and/or level 2, called contextual effects.","Latent growth curve approaches to mediation require SEMs, allow for a random intercept and slope for both M and Y, and typically offer three possible indirect effects (intercept-to-intercept a0 b0, intercept-to-slope a0 b2, and slope-to-slope a1 b1), so researchers must clearly specify which indirect effect aligns with their hypothesis."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm26","title":"Chapter 26: Mathematical and Computational Models","pages":"653-676","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Computational Modeling","topic":"Kinds of Models and Their Purposes: A Taxonomy from Marr's Levels to Table 26.1","visual_frame_description":"A museum hall of model exhibits: seven display cases labeled statistical, descriptive, measurement, structural, algorithmic-level, computational-level, and baseline, each holding an example artifact such as a general linear model equation, an ex-Gauss distribution curve, an SDT plot, and a path diagram. A three-story escalator at the back carries labels computational, algorithmic, and implementational, and an interactive kiosk asks the visitor to place each model on the level where its explanation is formulated.","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundations: sort the kinds of models from Table 26.1 (statistical, descriptive, measurement, structural, algorithmic-level, computational-level, and baseline models) and match each kind to its primary purpose, example, range of applications, and degree of commitment to psychological interpretations. Then classify each model example according to Marr's computational, algorithmic, and implementational levels of analysis and explain why the term 'computational' is used in two different senses.","key_concepts":["Marr (1982) distinguished a computational, an algorithmic, and an implementational level of analysis, arguing that explanations of the operation of complex systems such as the human mind should be specified at each of these three levels.","The computational level of analysis describes the problem faced by the system and why it is solved in an abstract, mathematical way, describing competent and, if there is a means to evaluate the goodness of the solution, optimal behavior.","Models at the algorithmic level specify the concrete algorithm that the system uses to solve the computational-level problem, whereas models at the implementational level specify how this algorithm is implemented in the medium that does the processing, for example in the human brain.","Most computational models can be classified as providing explanations at the algorithmic level, and some, such as the PDP model, approach an implementation-level explanation inasmuch as they are inspired by analogies with neuronal processing.","It is confusing that the term 'computational' denotes both a level in Marr's taxonomy and a category of models, because computational models are usually algorithmic-level or implementational-level explanations, whereas computational-level explanations usually correspond to mathematical models other than computational ones.","Statistical models identify the major sources of variability in data without imposing or presupposing a psychological interpretation of such factors, and they are typically based on general-purpose models such as the general linear model.","Descriptive models aim to provide a concise summary of the observed distribution of data in terms of a few parameters without aiming at psychologically interpretable explanations, and one example is the ex-Gauss model for reaction time data used by Sui and Humphreys (2013) to show that self-face perception forms a unique perceptual category.","Measurement models serve to define dependent measures from which typical confounding factors are partialled out according to a substantive rationale, and they provide measures of latent variables, as when signal detection models provide estimates of sensitivity and criterion settings.","Structural models serve to specify and test hypotheses about the causal structure underlying the interrelations of a set of variables, as in a simple path analysis model in which A influences B, B influences C, and there is no direct influence of A on C.","Baseline models provide upper or lower baselines for behavior based on a modeled set of competencies, for example a model that implements only the capability to respond based on random guessing to show that additional capabilities are required to account for the observed responses.","In computational models, the model predictions emerge from the interaction of several directly specified computational steps whose repeated execution in simulations samples the implicitly defined predicted distributions of data, whereas the signal detection model directly and explicitly defines the predicted distributions of data.","Models are often used as instantiations of verbal theories, in which theoretical assumptions are made so precise that the model formalizes major tenets of the theory and generates quantitative predictions for observed data, and they are also used to discriminate between competing theoretical accounts."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Computational Modeling","topic":"Signal Detection Theory: Disentangling Sensitivity from Criterion in Social Categorization","visual_frame_description":"A firing-range simulator viewed through an SDT overlay: two overlapping bell curves labeled 'Distribution for B stimuli' and 'Distribution for A stimuli' with a movable vertical criterion bar labeled c, and shaded regions marking P(miss) and P(false alarm). A readout shows d' as the separation between the curve means, and as the player drags the criterion or the curves, target silhouettes with and without guns flash across the range and response tallies update.","player_mission":"Play the measurement analyst in the shooter paradigm: given hit rates and false alarm rates for Black and White targets, decide whether the observed race effect reflects changes in sensitivity d' or in criterion setting c, and defend the disentangling rationale of the SDT measurement model. Then propose selective-influence manipulations, such as a memory-load manipulation for convergent validity and a base-rate manipulation for discriminant validity, that would validate d' as a measure of discriminatory ability.","key_concepts":["The basic signal detection model (Kellen & Klauer, 2018) claims that the frequencies of signal reports among trials with and without a signal are distributed according to a binomial distribution with probabilities that are functions of the two model parameters sensitivity, d', and criterion setting, c.","Sensitivity (d') quantifies how well the observer can discriminate between trials with and without a signal, whereas criterion setting (c) quantifies the observer's relative preference for responding that a signal is present versus responding that it is not present.","Decision makers condense the information in the stimulus display into one numerical value encoding the strength of evidence for an A rather than a B stimulus, and across stimuli and trials these evidence values are assumed to be normally distributed with a lower mean for B stimuli than for A stimuli, with a criterion c placed on the axis separating decisions for A from decisions for B.","For the problem of minimizing expected costs when false alarms and misses are penalized, expected costs are minimized by an information-processing system that follows an SDT model with appropriate parameter values, so the SDT model provides a computational-level account of this problem.","In Correll et al.'s (2002) shooter paradigm, participants are to decide to shoot if a target person carries a gun and not to shoot if the target holds a harmless object, and SDT analyses reveal a stronger bias in criterion settings to prefer shoot decisions for Black targets than for White targets.","Signal detection models are often used as measurement models because they provide estimates of sensitivity and criterion settings that are unconfounded from participants' preferences for one of the response options, for two-alternative forced-choice data in which participants try to discriminate between trials of two kinds.","Depending upon the task context, sensitivity can be grounded in different psychological variables, such as sensory acuteness when soft sounds are to be discriminated from no sound, memory performance when studied items are to be discriminated from new items, or reasoning ability when valid logical arguments are to be discriminated from invalid ones.","Criterion settings can reflect any factor that leads to an overall preference for one response option over the other, such as motivation to prefer one response option, knowledge and use of differential base rates of type A and type B stimuli, or implicit response biases.","In the shooter task context, the goal of using the signal detection model as a measurement model is to determine whether the observed effect of race on shoot decisions reflects an effect of race on the participants' ability to discriminate guns from tools or an effect on their response biases captured by criterion settings.","Evidence for convergent validity of d' can be obtained by correlating parameter values with independently obtained indices of discriminatory ability, for example by showing that d' values correlate with test scores from established tests of reasoning ability when d' is believed to reflect reasoning ability in a given task context.","Asking participants to memorize a seven-digit number while working on the modeled task should depress discriminatory ability in that task, so a memory-load manipulation that affects the parameter for discriminatory ability would provide evidence for convergent validity of that parameter.","Base-rate manipulations or manipulations of the relative costs of false alarms and misses, which promote a higher or lower criterion for judging that a signal is present, typically do not affect d', and a manipulation that fails to affect d' provides evidence for discriminant validity of d' as a measure of discriminatory ability disentangled from criterion-setting processes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Computational Modeling","topic":"Evaluating Models: Auditing Fit, Flexibility, Consistency, Competitiveness, Validation, and Generativity","visual_frame_description":"A regulatory review chamber where a model is displayed as a stack of sliders: one slider labeled 'goodness of fit' balances against another labeled 'flexibility,' while badges for AIC, BIC, NML, DIC, and WAIC hang on the wall as penalty weights. A check-list panel lists the other audit criteria, and a stamp at the bottom offers the verdicts 'passes' or 'needs stronger evidence' after each criterion is assessed against the data patterns the model addresses.","player_mission":"Act as a model auditor for a newly proposed model: weigh its goodness of fit against its flexibility, decide whether a saturated model can support substantive conclusions, apply model selection indices such as AIC, BIC, NML, DIC, and WAIC, and audit the model across the remaining criteria of consistency across applications, competitiveness against extant models, psychological validation of its parameters, and generativity. Issue a verdict on whether the new model earns its place next to the established one.","key_concepts":["A model that does not fit the data is usually dismissed or improved upon, but the amount of support a model can receive from a good fit needs to be assessed in relation to the complexity or flexibility of the model.","Models that are so flexible that they can account for any conceivable set of data are often called saturated models, and for them goodness of fit is trivially achieved and uninformative, although they can still provide useful measurement models as exemplified by process dissociation and SDT models.","There is a trade-off between the simplicity of a model (its lack of flexibility or its parsimony) and its goodness of fit, so that a good fit is the more impressive the simpler the model, and a model that qualitatively fits an observed interaction between four means using fifteen adjustable parameters is less impressive than one that also fits the shape of the distribution of data values in each condition.","Parameter counting is only a heuristic for assessing flexibility, but it is used in formal indices for model selection, such as AIC and BIC, which add a penalty based on the number of model parameters to a measure of badness of fit so that models differing in flexibility become more comparable.","NML (normalized maximum likelihood) is a model selection index that goes beyond parameter counting by taking into account what the parameters are used for, and in a Bayesian framework DIC (deviance information criterion) and WAIC (widely applicable information criterion) serve similar purposes.","In Bayesian models one needs to specify prior distributions for each model parameter, which involves structural choices as to the family of distributions from which the prior is chosen, and in PDP models structural choices concern the number and organization of nodes and whether or not there is a link between any two nodes.","Concerns about model flexibility can be mitigated if the model succeeds in accounting for data from different studies using the same structural choices and mostly the same parameter values, and if structural choices change from study to study without preregistration, modelers should provide a justification for why the conclusiveness of model fits is not compromised.","A special case of evaluating consistency across applications is cross-validation, in which one portion of the data is used for developing the model and estimating parameters while the remainder is used to validate the model with final structural choices and parameter estimates held fixed.","Comparing a new model with possible competing models and showing that it provides better fit and/or strikes the better compromise between fit and flexibility goes beyond a confirmatory research strategy toward one that considers explicit alternatives, and this is the idea behind the criterion of competitiveness.","Model parameters are first and foremost mathematical entities whose values are usually numbers devoid of a priori meaning, so the verbal labels attached to parameters (such as 'sensitivity' or 'guessing tendencies') need to be validated as would any psychological measurement.","A series of selective-influence studies is sometimes implemented in which convergent and discriminant validity is assessed for each model parameter that is to be interpreted as a measure of a psychological process, as exemplified by Klauer and Wegener (1998).","Generativity means that a model may still be useful and fruitful even if it provides only coarse fits to data and its verbal labels are poorly validated, as long as it predicts new effects and inspires empirical research to take new avenues, as in the case of a model that generates predictions via simulations."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Computational Modeling","topic":"The Bayesian Marginal Model: Reading the Contingency Matrix of Illusory Correlations","visual_frame_description":"A floating two-by-two contingency table with counts 18, 6, 6, 2 in the cells and row and column marginals highlighted in yellow; the cell interiors are shaded light gray to signal that updating occurs on the marginals only. Flanking the table are two histograms of phi correlations labeled Prior and Posterior, the first centered on zero with P(phi > 0) = .50 and the second shifted right with P(phi > 0) = .72, while a side panel displays the rule of succession formula p-hat = (k+1)/(N+2).","player_mission":"Work through the two-by-two contingency matrix of group membership and desirability: verify that the joint correlation (phi) is zero while the marginals are skewed, then trace how a uniform prior on the underlying probabilities is updated by Bayes's rule on the marginals only, yielding a posterior distribution of phi correlations biased toward positive values. Pit the Bayesian marginal model against the rule of succession, decide which account survives the p(min) smaller than 0.5 pattern, and check the model's generativity in the one-cell and prior-knowledge extensions.","key_concepts":["In Hamilton and Gifford's (1976) seminal study, participants were presented with desirable and undesirable behaviors of members of a majority and a minority group, where the majority group was represented by more behaviors than the minority group and there were more desirable than undesirable behaviors overall, with equal proportions of desirable and undesirable behaviors in each group.","The correlation between group membership and desirability across the presented behaviors is often quantified in terms of the phi correlation, a correlation coefficient tailored to two-by-two contingency tables, which is zero for the stimulus contingency table shown in Figure 26.2.","Participants typically judge the majority group to have exhibited proportionally more desirable behaviors than the minority group, and because the correlation across stimuli is in fact zero, the perceived correlations have been called illusory correlations or pseudocontingencies.","Pseudocontingency perceptions have been shown to reflect the skew in the marginals, independently of whether joint observations are actually presented, and according to Fiedler et al. (2007) a pseudocontingency perception arises when the distributions of two attributes in a group are skewed, so that skew in the same direction produces the appearance of a positive correlation and skew in opposite directions mimics a negative relationship.","In the Bayesian marginal model, the cell frequencies in the stimulus display are considered samples from an underlying multinomial distribution with probabilities p, and a prior distribution of these probabilities encodes prior beliefs about the range and distribution of possible underlying probabilities.","Based on the stimuli shown and their frequencies, the prior distribution is updated using Bayes's rule, which is a universal inference rule that is statistically normative and therefore only supports valid statistical inferences, and updating occurs not on the joint bivariate frequency counts but only on the marginals.","A uniform prior, in which any set of values for the probabilities is as likely as any other set, is a standard choice to describe a learner who is ignorant a priori, and it implies a prior distribution of phi correlations that is centered on zero and not biased toward negative or positive correlations.","After learning the marginals, the posterior distribution of the probabilities implies a posterior distribution of phi correlations that is biased toward positive values, accounting for the perceived positive phi correlations that participants express in their responses.","The Bayesian marginal model thereby shows that the marginals contain valid statistical information about the probabilities that likely generated them and that the perception of positive correlations is in fact a valid and rational statistical inference, making the model a formal version of Fiedler et al.'s (2007) pseudocontingency heuristic.","The rule of succession, originally proposed by Laplace, states that proportions are estimated as p-hat = (k+1)/(N+2), where k is the frequency of desirable behaviors in the group and N is the total number of behaviors shown for the group, and Costello and Watts (2019) showed that it predicts differential regression to the mean and can thereby account for illusory correlations.","Bott et al. (2021) pointed out that the rule of succession also predicts that both estimates should be larger than 0.5, so that regression to the mean cannot explain assessments in which the more frequent behavior in a group is perceived as the less frequent one, and using versions of the Bayesian marginal model and the rule of succession extended by one adjustable concentration parameter, the qualitative patterns could be fit by the Bayesian marginal model but not by the rule of succession.","The Bayesian marginal model is generative because it can also be applied when the presented information differs qualitatively from the standard two-by-two contingency table, as when participants are shown behaviors from only one cell of the contingency table, or when there is prior knowledge about the two attributes that can be coded in the choice of the prior."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Computational Modeling","topic":"Parallel Distributed Processing and the Dynamic Interactive Model of Person Construal","visual_frame_description":"A control-room console showing the layered network of Figure 26.5: cue-level nodes labeled White cue, Black cue, Angry cue, and Happy cue feed into category-level nodes, which connect to stereotype-level nodes for hostile Black and neutral White and a higher-order racial-prejudice node with a race task demand input. Green links are excitatory and red links inhibitory, a time-step counter advances toward 150 while activation pulses flow upward through the layers, and output bars beside the White and Black category nodes update with each simulated trial.","player_mission":"Take command of Freeman and Ambady's (2011) localist PDP network: configure the cue, category, stereotype, and higher-order level nodes and their excitatory and inhibitory links, set the connection weights, and run simulations of race categorization for ambiguous faces across 150 time steps. Verify that the model recovers the interaction of prejudice level and facial emotion from Hugenberg and Bodenhausen (2004), audit the structural and quantitative degrees of freedom, and evaluate competing accounts in terms of guessing-based alternatives and the model's generativity.","key_concepts":["Parallel distributed processing (PDP) models are inspired by neurons and their connections via synapses, and they comprise a number of simple neuron-like processing units, each with an activation state, that are connected with other units by excitatory and inhibitory links.","Units that interface with the environment are called visible units and can receive activation from the environment, whereas units that have no contact with the environment are called hidden units, and units are typically organized in layers.","A network in which the flow of activation is strictly directional, proceeding from a lowest level to successively higher levels, is called a feed-forward network, whereas a network in which activation can proceed backwards from higher to lower levels is called a recurrent network.","The units' activation states are updated in discrete time steps based on the inputs they receive via the connections according to simple formulas, and noise can be entered at different stages, for example by adding random noise to each unit's activation at each time step, which defines a stochastic process over the activation states.","In abstract connectionist networks, hidden units have no pre-specified meaning and connection weights are learned by providing feedback to the model in learning trials, whereas in localist networks units are given meanings by the modeler that a priori constrain the links and connection weights, and the models proposed by Freeman and Ambady (2011) are of this kind.","Because of the distributed nature of processing, damage to a network results in graceful degradation rather than abrupt breakdown, distinctions between processing, representations, and memory become blurred, and processing does not need to invoke propositional content, symbolic inference rules, or heuristics.","In PDP models, the flow of activation is often computed until the activation states of the different units stabilize, and such stable states can be considered consistent with the set of excitatory and inhibitory links, which is why such network models are often called constraint satisfaction models.","Hugenberg and Bodenhausen (2004) presented racially ambiguous happy and angry faces and asked participants to classify each face as either Black or White, finding that level of prejudice did not reliably affect the classification of happy faces but highly prejudiced participants classified angry faces as Black more frequently than did participants with low levels of prejudice.","Freeman and Ambady's (2011) dynamic interactive theory of person construal models this interaction with two stereotype-level nodes, one representing the 'Black is hostile' stereotype and one the 'White is neutral' stereotype, which receive activation from a higher-order racial-prejudice node that receives external input when modeling data from high-prejudice participants and no input for participants low in prejudice.","In the model, the angry cue and happy cue nodes receive external input when modeling trials with angry and happy faces respectively, the Black cue and White cue nodes received equal amounts of input in each simulated trial to model the fact that the shown faces were ambiguous with respect to race, and activation flow was computed for 150 consecutive time steps with the categorization response determined by whichever of the White and Black category-level nodes ended up with the higher activation level.","The model uses structural parameters, the number and nature of nodes and the presence or absence of excitatory and inhibitory links between them, as well as quantitative parameters given by the connection weights, which were set intuitively by the authors based on their intuitions about stimulus and task features, so the balance between four means and a large number of parameters could be improved if the model accounted for a larger number of patterns.","PDP models are probably the most frequent kind of computational model used in social and personality psychology, and other kinds of computational models in the field comprise agent-based models, in which simple processing units act autonomously following simple rules, and production system models such as ACT-R (Anderson, 1983) and CLARION (Sun et al., 2005)."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]},{"id":"rm27","title":"Chapter 27: Meta-analysis","pages":"678-703","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Meta-analysis","topic":"Foundations and History: From Vote Counting to Effect Size Synthesis","visual_frame_description":"A timeline gallery with exhibit cases starting in the 1950s (combining probabilities across studies), passing a 1977 psychotherapy-outcome display, and ending at a wall of modern weighted models; each case labels a distinct era of the method. Four icons hover above the gallery - a map, a sum symbol, a comparison scale, and a magnifying glass over a bias gauge - while a graffiti wall reading 'mega-silliness' is being repainted by workers carrying buckets labeled 'apples and oranges' and 'garbage in, garbage out.'","player_mission":"Scrub the chapter's foundations: define meta-analysis as the quantitative synthesis of results across multiple studies, sort its history from vote counting through Smith and Glass (1977) to the modern weighted models, distinguish meta-analysis from systematic review and narrative review, and identify the four core affordances of mapping, summarizing, comparing, and assessing bias. Then defend the method against the classic complaints of 'mega-silliness,' 'apples and oranges,' and 'garbage in, garbage out,' and decide when a research question is focused enough to be meta-analyzed.","key_concepts":["Meta-analysis refers to the quantitative synthesis of results across multiple studies, and the term became widely known in psychology when Smith and Glass (1977) reviewed a large number of psychotherapy outcome studies using meta-analytic methods.","Early efforts at quantitative reviews used vote counting, which usually took the form of tabulating the numbers of statistically significant and nonsignificant studies, but this approach is relatively uncommon because it is insensitive and its conclusions can be seriously biased by variations in the sample sizes of the studies being tabulated.","In contrast to narrative and vote-counting reviews, meta-analyses mainly extract and analyze effect sizes that capture the direction and magnitude of findings, and the value of effect sizes is that they are a standardized metric - common ones being standardized mean differences such as Hedges' g and Cohen's d and the Pearson correlation - that permits comparison of outcome magnitudes across studies whose original measurement metrics were not identical.","Meta-analysis and systematic review are distinct concepts because one can happen without the other: one can do a systematic review without a meta-analysis, and one can do a meta-analysis without a systematic review, as when a researcher conducts several studies and performs a mini meta-analysis to summarize them.","When aiming to quantitatively synthesize results beyond the authors' own work, a high-quality systematic search and its documentation should usually precede the meta-analysis, with features such as careful explanation of the inclusion criteria, databases searched, search terms, and numbers of studies included and excluded.","To conduct a meta-analysis the researcher must have a focused research question, which generally means the effect of interest can be estimated with a single degree of freedom, such as comparisons of two means, planned contrasts, trends tested within a set of means, single-degree-of-freedom interaction effects, or linear correlations.","Studies that produce only unfocused results such as multi-degree-of-freedom omnibus F-ratios are generally not amenable to standard meta-analytic practices, and squared indices should be used with caution because they may obscure whether a result is focused or omnibus and they lack a sign, which is a serious problem for meta-analysis.","Meta-analytic techniques can be usefully applied to even a few studies, because pooling evidence across two or more studies can offer greater precision in estimating the effect of interest than any individual study, though tests of differences in effects across studies should be interpreted cautiously if based on a small number of studies.","The four core functions of systematic reviews and meta-analyses are mapping (carefully describing what has and has not been done in a field), summarizing (answering the overall research question), comparing (detecting moderators - ways the basic effect varies with features of study methodology or population), and assessing bias.","Early critics questioned the legitimacy of meta-analysis, calling it an exercise in mega-silliness and complaining that it combines apples and oranges by lumping disparate methodologies under the same conceptual heading, but researchers now agree that a key advantage of meta-analysis is being able to combine evidence across multiple studies.","If there are sufficient studies, the meta-analyst should perform moderator analyses to see whether different operational definitions of key variables matter, and meta-analysts should carefully consider biases throughout project design and interpretation, including bias in the search and selection of studies, the methodological quality of studies, and the representativeness of the studies available (usually called publication bias or selective-reporting bias)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Research Methods | Meta-analysis","topic":"Fixed Versus Random Effects: The Great Model Choice Debate","visual_frame_description":"A judge's chamber with three lecterns labeled 'Fixed-effects,' 'Weighted random-effects,' and 'Unweighted,' each holding an assumption card and a model equation; a balance scale tips as the player moves sliders for statistical power and generalization to new studies. A wall screen shows one study contributing four effect sizes (verbal and physical aggression crossed with two scales), with three buttons labeled 'segregate,' 'average,' and 'robust variance estimation' that light up the consequences of each decision.","player_mission":"Take the role of a meta-analyst deciding among the fixed-effects model, the weighted random-effects model, and the simple unweighted approach for a database of narcissism-aggression correlations: state what each model assumes about the population of studies, explain how weighting enters each approach, and weigh statistical power against generalizability to new studies before issuing a model verdict. Then decide how to handle studies that contribute multiple effect sizes, choosing between segregating the effects, averaging within studies, and robust variance estimation, and justify your choice to a skeptical editorial board.","key_concepts":["Fixed-effects models assume that there is one true effect underlying all studies in the database, which means that all variation between studies is due solely to sampling error.","A random-effects model assumes that there is variance due to factors other than sampling error, with attendant greater generalization to new studies, reflecting its more ambitious inferential goals of generalization to new and not identical studies.","Fixed-effects analyses harnessed study size in calculating confidence intervals and p-values, yielding much higher statistical power because N was effectively participants within studies, not studies, and a quickly growing dogmatism about the fixed-effects model meant most meta-analyses were done that way for over twenty years.","Heterogeneity among effect sizes beyond what would be expected based on sampling error is the rule, not the exception, in published meta-analyses, raising serious questions about the appropriateness of a fixed-effects analysis, which generalizes only to the very same studies but with new participants rather than to new studies that might vary in other respects.","The prevailing habit as of this writing is the weighted random-effects model, which weights studies as a function of the between-studies variation and within-study uncertainty and often has less statistical power than the fixed approach.","Unweighted models assign equal weight to each effect size, avoiding the potential biasing effects of sample size being confounded with effect size moderators, though at the cost of reduced statistical power compared to weighted models.","Not weighting by sample size avoids the risk of accidentally confounding sample size with other methodological features such as study design or population characteristics, a problem of biased interpretation that can make weighting a threat to validity because one is actually giving more weight to certain kinds of studies, not simply those with larger sample size.","Inherently, none of these models is more correct than any other; one's choice has to depend on the database as well as on one's goals, and analysis choices are not entirely either-or, as one can usually do things in multiple ways and compare them.","Traditional meta-analysis models typically assume that the effects are independent of each other, but honoring this assumption is a challenge when a single study contributes multiple effect sizes, because if left unaddressed the independence assumption is violated and the meta-analyst may find significant results when no true effect exists, yielding high false-positive rates.","A commonly used approach for handling multiple effect sizes per study is to average effect sizes within studies to generate one effect size per study, which is inappropriate if one is interested in heterogeneity within studies but can be sensible if the meta-analysis focuses on study-level moderators or if there are no meaningful conceptual differences between the several effects from one study.","Robust variance estimation provides appropriate meta-analytic standard errors and significance tests even in the presence of multiple effect sizes per study, and it relaxes the requirement of earlier multivariate approaches that the user specify exactly the correlations between outcomes within a study, generating inferences that are still appropriate even if the exact correlations are unknown.","The latest methodological developments, including small-study adjustments, tend to become available first in the R software environment, which supports an active community of open-source developers, and meta-analysts should carefully document their code and make it publicly available upon publication so that others can reproduce the findings."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Methods | Meta-analysis","topic":"Heterogeneity and Moderators: Auditing Why Effects Vary","visual_frame_description":"An inspection laboratory with a large histogram of effect sizes that splits into two overlaid curves labeled 'true underlying effects' and 'sampling error,' plus a gauge labeled tau (with tau-squared in parentheses) that ticks up as the player drags studies across the room. A moderator workbench holds scatterplots of effect size against moderator variables with meta-regression lines, a warning light flashes 'underpowered' when the number of studies is small, and a shelf of graph templates displays forest and rainforest plots beside a prediction-interval band.","player_mission":"Act as an inspector auditing a meta-analytic database: check that study characteristics are described with frequencies and that correlations among potential moderators, especially between sample size and study characteristics, are examined and controlled; estimate the between-studies heterogeneity parameter tau and tau-squared and use prediction intervals to describe the overall magnitude of variability. Then run moderator analyses and meta-regression while controlling for methods confounders, and judge whether the moderator tests have enough power, limiting confirmatory analyses to a small number of moderators theorized to be most important a priori.","key_concepts":["The meta-analyst should describe study characteristics in terms of frequencies or percentages of studies occurring within every coded category, and correlations among potential moderators can be substantial and, if left unaccounted for, can seriously bias the interpretation of moderator effects.","One correlation that is rarely reported is that between study sample size and study characteristics, and this confound is potentially biasing because of the ubiquitous use of sample size in weighting formulas, so meta-analysts must ascertain and appropriately control for correlations between study size and study characteristics.","The standard deviation of the observed effect size distribution is a simple metric for describing variation in effect sizes, but it conflates two sources of variability: differences in true underlying effects across studies and sampling error for each study, and it could mislead the meta-analyst into thinking effects are quite variable when the variability is actually due to chance alone.","The between-studies heterogeneity, sometimes referred to by the Greek letter tau, aims to estimate the standard deviation of the true underlying effects across studies rather than of the noisy effect size estimates, and it describes how much psychological effects substantively vary, independent of the sample sizes used to estimate those effects.","An estimated heterogeneity of zero means that the observed variance in effect size estimates can be attributed to chance alone, and common meta-analysis software applications routinely provide this parameter in model output or provide the variance tau-squared, which can be converted to tau by taking the square root.","One of the most informative aspects of a meta-analysis is understanding how specific study characteristics can explain variability in effect sizes, and these moderator analyses are analogous to tests of moderation in primary research, which are typically addressed by looking at interaction effects.","In investigating moderators, the meta-analyst must remember the earlier point about confounds, because studies that use verbal or physical aggression might differ in other ways than just the type of aggression measured, for example in the proportion of female participants, so meta-analysts need to maintain the same vigilance for confounders that they would have in primary research.","The analyst can account for confounds through regression-based approaches that include multiple predictors in the same model, often called meta-regression, and should consider always controlling for methods confounders such as study design and effect size computation details that are not central to the substantive research questions but could contaminate the results of interest.","Moderator analyses are often underpowered, especially for small numbers of studies, and when conducting a large number of moderator tests the analyst could find a stray significant moderator and herald that result as a main finding when that result might just be due to chance.","Following Tipton et al.'s (2019) recommendations, analysts should distinguish confirmatory and exploratory analyses, limiting the confirmatory analyses to just a small number of moderators theorized to be most important a priori.","Authors can help readers understand the overall magnitude of heterogeneity through prediction intervals, which can answer questions such as what the middle 95 percent of true underlying effects across studies is, and then, to describe specific sources of heterogeneity, authors should return to their guiding research questions to examine moderators.","Figure 27.2 shows one way to visualize the effect sizes from each study and their uncertainty using a rainforest plot, which aims to improve on more traditional displays called forest plots, and data points with longer, lighter shading indicate less precise estimates (smaller samples) relative to data points with shorter, darker shading."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Meta-analysis","topic":"The Search and Screening Matrix: Tracing the PRISMA Flow","visual_frame_description":"A giant conveyor-belt assembly line with hoppers labeled 'Database searches 11,324,' 'Citation tracking 7,118,' and 'Gray literature 40' feeding a chute of unique records, which passes two screening stations where records drop into chutes labeled by exclusion reason until 142 reports and 98 studies reach the end bin. Hanging above the line is a PRISMA flow diagram template with the Identification, Screening, and Included stages lighting up in sequence, and a monitor at the end shows the Open Science Framework upload screen for data files and analysis scripts.","player_mission":"Run the literature search and screening pipeline of a meta-analysis on children's gender stereotypes about academic abilities: log citations arriving from database searches, citation tracking, and gray literature, move records through abstract screening and full-text screening with specific exclusion reasons recorded and counted, and embed dual screening with machine-learning prioritization so that errors of judgment are reduced. End by filling in the PRISMA flow diagram from identification through screening to inclusion, and publish the extracted data and analysis scripts so others can reproduce the findings.","key_concepts":["A prototypical meta-analysis project has four phases: finding the literature, quantifying (coding) characteristics of the chosen studies, extracting effect sizes that describe the direction and magnitude of findings, and, finally, analyzing and reporting on the extracted data.","Over time, expectations have become stricter for the search process and its reporting, and although occasionally a meta-analyst can justify using only published studies, this practice is now rare, with unpublished work found through systematic methods, search engines, direct inquiries to authors, and postings on listservs or similar outlets.","Screening for eligibility typically happens in two stages: abstract screening to eliminate obviously irrelevant studies based on the abstract alone, and full-text screening to make final eligibility decisions, and this step can be arduous, sometimes involving the review of thousands of citations.","Methodologists commonly recommend developing a written protocol to guide decisions at each screening stage, conducting training sessions on the protocol, and meeting regularly as a screening team, and discussion in the early stages often produces refinements of the inclusion and exclusion criteria.","Embedding dual screening, in which two humans screen the same citation, throughout the process can help assess and resolve differences in interpretation and reduce the chances of erroneous judgments, though not every record needs to be dual-screened to realize these benefits, as a reviewer could randomly assign 30 percent of citations to be dual-screened.","Machine learning tools such as Abstrackr, a web-based tool for abstract screening, can help by prioritizing dual screening for the records that are more likely to be relevant while reverting back to single human screening for less relevant records.","Figure 27.1 shows a PRISMA flow chart from an ongoing meta-analysis on children's gender stereotypes about academic abilities, in which database searches identified 11,324 records, citation tracking identified 7,118, and gray literature searches identified 40, with the chapter text reporting 18,490 citations identified in total through the three search methods.","In the flow chart, 18,482 abstracts were screened with 17,242 excluded, 1,240 full-text reports were to be retrieved with 160 unavailable, 1,080 full-text reports were screened with 938 excluded for specific recorded reasons such as being qualitative only or not empirical, having no sample of children, having no ability stereotype measure, having an ineligible response structure, or having insufficient statistics, and the final included set comprised 142 reports representing 98 studies.","The PRISMA guidelines play a critical role in improving transparent reporting by helping authors comprehensively note key decisions, and they include a two-page checklist for reporting methods details and figure templates for reporting the flow of studies during eligibility screening, while the Meta-Analysis Reporting Guidelines (MARS) are generally less extensive than the PRISMA guidelines.","A codebook describes the characteristics that coders should extract from eligible studies, and the variables to be coded fall into two overlapping categories: those that describe the database (populations, designs, measures, and so on) and the subset of those that are intended to be examined as theoretically meaningful moderators based on a priori reasoning.","Reliability of coding the selected studies can be based on coding the entire database or a sufficient sample of studies using two or more coders, and this dual coding should begin early and continue throughout the review process rather than being saved for the end, with disagreements on categorical variables generally adjudicated by consensus.","Meta-analysis authors should publish the extracted data and analysis scripts on a repository such as the Open Science Framework website or the Interuniversity Consortium for Political and Social Research website, and although meta-analyses in psychology commonly report the extracted effect size data, they rarely provide the code used to analyze the data, which is a critical limitation because both are vital to reproducing the findings."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Research Methods | Meta-analysis","topic":"Publication Bias: Battling the File Drawer","visual_frame_description":"A war room with a giant filing cabinet whose drawers labeled 'file drawers' leak out blank pages as an unseen hand stamps 'significant' on only some results, while a gauge reads 1.54 for top psychology journals. Four weapon racks labeled 'published vs. unpublished,' 'small-study effects,' 'p-value models,' and 'sensitivity analyses' hold a funnel plot, a PET-PEESE regression equation, a selection-model diagram, and a failsafe-N counter, and a final screen asks whether the range of plausible adjusted estimates still differs from zero.","player_mission":"Take command of a meta-analysis under attack from selective reporting: trace how small, nonsignificant, or counterintuitive findings vanish into researchers' file drawers at both the publication level and the results level, and quantify the severity of the problem using the estimate that statistically significant results in the expected direction are 1.54 times as likely to be reported in top psychology journals. Then deploy the four families of bias methods - comparing published versus unpublished studies, small-study effects with funnel plots and PET-PEESE, p-value-based selection models and p-curve, and sensitivity analyses such as the failsafe N - and issue a verdict on whether the meta-analytic conclusions survive as sensitivity analyses rather than as definitive corrected estimates.","key_concepts":["Results that are very small or nonsignificant, or that run counter to accepted wisdom, may remain unpublished in the researchers' proverbial file drawers, and these types of omission can occur at both the publication level (entire studies not being published) and the results level (studies being published but not all outcomes or analyses from them), so if the published literature only contains statistically significant results, the meta-analyst may form biased conclusions.","Kvarven et al. (2019) compared the results of fifteen meta-analyses, potentially subject to selective reporting, to large-scale, multi-laboratory preregistered replications on the same psychological topics, and found that average effect sizes were systematically larger in the meta-analyses than in the preregistered replications, which selective reporting in the meta-analyzed primary literature may partly explain.","Mathur and VanderWeele (2021) estimated the severity of selective reporting bias across several fields and found that for top psychology journals, statistically significant results in the expected direction were 1.54 times as likely to be reported relative to nonsignificant results or those in the unexpected direction, with the estimated severity of bias larger for psychology than for other fields such as medicine.","Approaches for investigating selective-reporting bias largely fall into four families of methods: comparison of published and unpublished studies, methods based on small-study effects, methods based on p-values, and sensitivity analyses without estimating bias.","Published studies generally include peer-reviewed journal articles or edited book chapters, whereas unpublished studies generally include master's theses, dissertations, unpublished manuscripts, and investigators' unpublished data sent on request, and failing to find a published-unpublished difference should be interpreted cautiously because the retrieved unpublished results may not resemble the universe of all unpublished results and may themselves contain selective reporting at the results level.","A large family of methods rests on the assumption that smaller studies might show larger effects in the published literature due to selective reporting, because small studies with small observed effects fail to reach statistical significance and are therefore not published, and early methods focused on detecting such bias through funnel plots of effect sizes graphed against some function of the study size.","The precision-effect test (PET) and precision-effect estimate with standard error (PEESE) extrapolate to a theoretical population of studies with infinite sample size by including the effect size standard error or variance as predictors in a meta-regression model, on the assumption that studies with infinite sample size always yield statistical significance if a true effect exists.","A key limitation of the small-study methods, including both funnel plots and more recent regression-based approaches, is assuming that the only explanation for small-study effects is selective-reporting bias, and the trim-and-fill procedure performs poorly in methodological research despite being widely used in practice.","Selection models explicitly model that reporting decisions often depend on the p-value and adjust meta-analytic mean estimates for bias, showing favorable methodological performance across many contexts relevant to meta-analysis in psychology, and they have become more widely used in recent years thanks to software such as the weightr R package and the selmodel function in the metafor R package.","P-curve analysis discards findings of p greater than .05 and explicitly does not aim to make inferences about them, giving it fundamentally distinct inferential goals from selection models, and it can be substantially biased if one's inferential goal is to estimate population means for all studies conducted on a topic.","The file-drawer N or failsafe N estimates the number of unpublished file-drawer studies with results that are, on average, zero that would have to exist to render the obtained meta-analytic average nonsignificant, and methodologists have noted that it suffers from limitations such as the arbitrariness of defining a large file-drawer N and questionable assumptions about the average effect size of unretrieved studies.","Bias adjustment methods are not a panacea and should not be viewed as providing definitive estimates of true corrected effects, especially when effect heterogeneity is large; they are appropriate as sensitivity analyses that help the meta-analyst and readers understand how sensitive the results are to the chosen set of assumptions and modeling strategies, and the meta-analyst should consider the range of plausible adjusted estimates to better understand the robustness of results."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Peer-reviewed methodological handbook reflecting established best practice and current standards (replication, validity, design) in social and personality psychology. Broadly ac..."}}]}]};
